/* Generated by scripts/build-book-studies.py. */
const bibleBookStudies = [
  {"id": 91000, "title": "The Family Into Which Jesus Came", "subtitle": "God's promise through generations", "text": "Matthew 1:1–17", "chapters": [[39, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Matthew begins by naming Jesus Christ as the son of David and the son of Abraham. Then he traces the family line forward from Abraham. Isaac is followed by Jacob, then Judah and his brothers. At several points Matthew mentions women as well as fathers and sons. Perez and Zerah are named with Tamar. Rahab is named with Boaz, and Ruth with Obed. Ruth's son Obed is followed by Jesse, the father of David.", "From David the record goes through the house of Judah's kings. Solomon is named as the son David had by Uriah's wife. The names that follow include Hezekiah, Manasseh and Josiah. Matthew brings this part of the record to the carrying away to Babylon. A family whose members had held a throne is now marked by a national loss.", "The record continues after the exile. Jeconiah is followed by Shealtiel and Zerubbabel, and more names carry the family line towards Joseph. When Matthew comes to Joseph, the form of the record changes. He calls Joseph the husband of Mary and says that Jesus, who is called Christ, was born of her.", "Matthew groups the names into three stretches of fourteen generations: Abraham to David, David to the exile, and the exile to Christ. The grouping helps the reader remember the turns in the family's history. The Gospel will now move from the names of generations to what took place in the house of Joseph and Mary."], "lesson": "The exile could seem to mark the end of David's house. Matthew keeps tracing the line beyond it. God's faithfulness can outlast the losses that make his promises seem remote.", "question": "What does Matthew gain by keeping the exile in the family record?"},
  {"id": 91001, "title": "Joseph Takes Mary Home", "subtitle": "Obedience when plans change", "text": "Matthew 1:18–25", "chapters": [[39, 1]], "start": 18, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Mary was pledged to marry Joseph. Before they came together, she was found to be expecting a child. Matthew tells the reader from the beginning that the child was conceived by the Holy Spirit. Joseph did not yet have that explanation. He had to decide what to do with the news as he understood it.", "Joseph was a righteous man, and he did not want to make Mary a public example. He intended to put her away privately. While he was considering what to do, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream and addressed him as a son of David. The angel told him not to be afraid to take Mary as his wife, because the child she carried was from the Holy Spirit.", "The instruction also concerned what would happen after the birth. Mary would have a son, and Joseph was to name him Jesus, because he would save his people from their sins. Matthew connects the birth with the prophet's words about a child called Emmanuel, which means God with us. The promise gives the child's coming a significance beyond the immediate trouble in Joseph's household.", "When Joseph woke, he followed the angel's instruction and took Mary home as his wife. He did not have relations with her before she gave birth. When the son was born, Joseph gave him the name Jesus. The man who had been preparing to end the engagement now took responsibility for carrying out what the Lord had entrusted to him."], "lesson": "Joseph was prepared to act without making Mary a public example. When God gave him a different course, he acted on what he had heard.", "question": "Which instructions did Joseph carry out, and what do they show about his trust?"},
  {"id": 91002, "title": "The Visitors From the East", "subtitle": "Two responses to a king", "text": "Matthew 2:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After Jesus was born at Bethlehem, wise men from the East arrived in Jerusalem asking where the King of the Jews had been born. They had seen his star and had come to worship him. The question troubled Herod and, Matthew says, all Jerusalem with him. Herod gathered the chief priests and scribes and asked where the Christ was to be born. They named Bethlehem and gave him the words of the prophet about a ruler who would shepherd Israel.", "Herod then met the wise men privately and asked when the star had appeared. He sent them to Bethlehem to search carefully. When they had found the child, he said, they should report back so that he too might go and worship. The visitors departed, and the star went before them until it stood over the place where the child was. Seeing it, they rejoiced greatly.", "Inside the house they found the child with Mary his mother. They fell down, worshipped him and opened their treasures, offering gold, frankincense and myrrh. The journey that had begun with a question in the capital ended with them bowing before a child in a house.", "Herod had asked them to return, but God warned them in a dream against doing so. They went back to their own country along another road. Herod had obtained an answer about where the ruler would come from. The visitors had gone there, given their gifts and left by the way God directed them."], "lesson": "Herod learned where the Christ would be born and used that knowledge to protect his power. The visitors used what they learned to seek and worship Jesus. Consider where your knowledge of Scripture is taking you.", "question": "What differences do you see between Herod's questions and the visitors' actions?"},
  {"id": 91003, "title": "The Flight to Egypt and the Return", "subtitle": "God's care amid danger and grief", "text": "Matthew 2:13–23", "chapters": [[39, 2]], "start": 13, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After the visitors had gone, an angel appeared to Joseph in a dream. He was to take the child and his mother to Egypt and stay there until he received further word. Herod would search for the child to kill him. Joseph got up, took them that night and left. They remained in Egypt until Herod died. Matthew connects their time there with the prophet's words about God calling his son out of Egypt.", "Herod realised the visitors had departed without reporting back. Enraged, he ordered the killing of the boys in Bethlehem and the surrounding district who were two years old or younger. He used the time he had learned from the visitors to set the age. Matthew recalls Jeremiah's picture of Rachel weeping for her children and refusing comfort because they were gone. The escape of one family sits alongside the grief of others.", "After Herod's death, an angel told Joseph in Egypt that those seeking the child's life were dead. Joseph took the child and his mother back towards Israel. On hearing that Archelaus ruled Judea in his father's place, he was afraid to go there. Another warning in a dream directed him away, and he withdrew into Galilee.", "The family settled in Nazareth. Matthew associates that place with the words of the prophets about Jesus being called a Nazarene. The chapter ends with the family finding a place to live after leaving at night and changing course again on the way home."], "lesson": "Matthew records both the child's deliverance and the grief of the bereaved families. God's work in history gives room for lament as well as gratitude.", "question": "Why does Matthew include the cries of the grieving families in the account of Jesus' escape?"},
  {"id": 91004, "title": "A Voice in the Wilderness", "subtitle": "Repentance that bears fruit", "text": "Matthew 3:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["John the Baptist came preaching in the wilderness of Judea. He called people to repent because the kingdom of heaven was near. Matthew presents him as the voice Isaiah spoke of, calling for the Lord's way to be prepared. John's clothing was made of camel's hair, and he wore a leather belt. His food was locusts and wild honey.", "People left Jerusalem, Judea and the country around the Jordan to hear him. In the river they were baptised by him while confessing their sins. The wilderness became a place to speak openly about conduct people might otherwise have kept to themselves.", "When many Pharisees and Sadducees came, John asked who had warned them to flee from coming wrath. He called for fruit consistent with repentance. They should not rely on saying Abraham was their father. God could raise children to Abraham from stones. John spoke of an axe already laid at the root of trees that failed to bear good fruit.", "John then directed their attention to someone who would come after him. John used water and called for repentance. The coming one was mightier, and John said he was unworthy even to carry his sandals. He would baptise with the Holy Spirit and with fire. John closed with the image of someone clearing a threshing floor, gathering the wheat and burning the chaff. His preaching prepared the crowd for a person with authority beyond his own."], "lesson": "John's warning reaches people who rely on their religious ancestry. Repentance becomes visible in the life that follows confession.", "question": "What would count as fruit of repentance in a situation you are facing?"},
  {"id": 91005, "title": "Jesus Comes to the Jordan", "subtitle": "The Father's beloved Son", "text": "Matthew 3:13–17", "chapters": [[39, 3]], "start": 13, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["The crowds had come to John at the Jordan confessing their sins. John had told them that someone greater was coming after him. He had spoken of being unworthy to carry that person's sandals. Matthew now records who came to the river. Jesus travelled from Galilee to John and asked to be baptised.", "John resisted. He told Jesus that he himself needed baptism from him and asked why Jesus was coming to him. That question brings the earlier preaching into the encounter. The man who had baptised the crowds now spoke of his own need before the one standing in front of him.", "Jesus told him to allow it for the present because it was fitting for them to fulfil all righteousness. John consented. Matthew leaves the exchange there and turns to what happened as Jesus came up from the water. The heavens opened, and Jesus saw the Spirit of God coming down like a dove and alighting on him.", "A voice from heaven declared that this was God's beloved Son, in whom he was well pleased. John had questioned whether he should baptise Jesus, and Jesus had answered by speaking of righteousness. The voice from heaven says who Jesus is as he begins the work Matthew will go on to recount. The next account takes him from the water into the wilderness, carrying that declaration into the time of testing."], "lesson": "Jesus willingly took the course that fulfilled righteousness. The Father's declaration identifies the one whose life and teaching Matthew now follows.", "question": "What does the Father's declaration tell you about the person John baptised?"},
  {"id": 91006, "title": "The Test in the Wilderness", "subtitle": "Trusting God under pressure", "text": "Matthew 4:1–11", "chapters": [[39, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After his baptism, Jesus was led by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil. He fasted for forty days and forty nights and was hungry. The tempter approached him at the point of that hunger. If he was the Son of God, the tempter suggested, he could command the stones to become bread. Jesus answered from Scripture that a person lives by every word that comes from God, as well as needing bread.", "The devil then took him to the holy city and set him on a high point of the temple. Again he challenged Jesus to act if he was God's Son. This time he used words from Scripture, appealing to a promise that God's angels would bear him up. Jesus brought another command to bear on the proposal. God must not be put to the test.", "On a very high mountain the devil showed him the kingdoms of the world and their glory. He offered them in exchange for Jesus falling down and worshipping him. Jesus told Satan to go away and replied from Scripture that worship and service belong to the Lord God alone.", "The devil left, and angels came and ministered to Jesus. Matthew records their arrival after Jesus had refused to force a display of their protection. Through hunger, an appeal to a biblical promise and an offer of power, Jesus continued to answer from what God had said."], "lesson": "Each offer invited Jesus to take something by abandoning obedience. His answers show that a verse must be read in keeping with what God has commanded.", "question": "How does Jesus' second reply help you recognise a misuse of Scripture?"},
  {"id": 91007, "title": "The First Followers by the Lake", "subtitle": "The call to follow Jesus", "text": "Matthew 4:12–25", "chapters": [[39, 4]], "start": 12, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus heard that John had been put in prison and withdrew into Galilee. He left Nazareth and settled in Capernaum beside the sea, in the region associated with Zebulun and Naphtali. Matthew remembers Isaiah's words about light coming to people who sat in darkness. Jesus began to preach, calling for repentance because the kingdom of heaven was near.", "Walking beside the Sea of Galilee, Jesus saw two brothers, Simon Peter and Andrew. They were casting a net into the water because that was their work. He called them to follow him and said he would make them fishers of people. They left their nets at once and followed.", "A little farther on he saw James and John, sons of Zebedee. They were in a boat with their father, mending their nets. Jesus called them as well. They left the boat and their father and followed him. Those first calls came in the middle of work the four men already knew how to do. The promise Jesus made would take them into work they still had to learn.", "Jesus travelled throughout Galilee, teaching in synagogues, preaching the good news of the kingdom and healing diseases. People brought sufferers to him from as far away as Syria. Crowds followed from Galilee, the Decapolis, Jerusalem, Judea and beyond the Jordan. The men who had left their nets now accompanied Jesus as his teaching and healing drew people from many places."], "lesson": "The fishermen's response had an immediate cost. Jesus gave them a new purpose whose full meaning they would learn by accompanying him.", "question": "What did the four men leave, and what did Jesus say he would make of them?"},
  {"id": 91008, "title": "The People Jesus Calls Blessed", "subtitle": "A visible life of faith", "text": "Matthew 5:1–16", "chapters": [[39, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus saw the crowds, he went up the mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him, and he began to teach. He called the poor in spirit blessed because the kingdom of heaven belonged to them. He spoke of people who mourn and will be comforted, of the meek who will inherit the earth, and of those who hunger for righteousness and will be filled.", "Jesus continued with the merciful, the pure in heart and the peacemakers. He attached promises to those ways of life: mercy, sight of God and the name of God's children. He included people persecuted for righteousness and spoke directly to his hearers about what to remember when people reviled them because of him. The prophets before them had also faced persecution.", "Then Jesus compared his hearers to salt. Salt that has lost its savour can no longer do its work. He gave a second picture, a city on a hill which cannot be hidden. People light a lamp to put it on a stand where it gives light to the house. Covering it with a measure would defeat the purpose of lighting it.", "Jesus applied the lamp to the lives of his disciples. Their good works were to be visible enough that others would glorify their Father in heaven. The people he had called blessed were now asked to let their conduct be seen for what it showed about God."], "lesson": "The blessings correct the way we measure a good life. The light Jesus describes draws attention to the Father through the conduct of his people.", "question": "Which of the blessings would change the way you judge a difficult situation?"},
  {"id": 91009, "title": "Before You Bring Your Gift", "subtitle": "Reconciliation and the heart", "text": "Matthew 5:17–26", "chapters": [[39, 5]], "start": 17, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After speaking of visible good works, Jesus addressed how his teaching related to the law and the prophets. He had come to fulfil them. He gave weight even to the smallest details and spoke of the responsibility of people who both do and teach God's commands. The righteousness of his hearers must go beyond that of the scribes and Pharisees.", "Jesus began with the command against murder. He brought anger and words of contempt into the discussion. A person might never have struck a fatal blow and still need to answer for how he regarded and addressed another person. Jesus' examples take the hearer from an outward offence towards conduct that can help a quarrel grow.", "He pictured someone bringing a gift to the altar who remembers a brother has something against him. The worshipper should leave the gift there and go first to be reconciled. Then he can return and make the offering. What he remembers at the altar gives him something to attend to before completing his act of worship.", "Jesus gave a second picture of two people on their way to court. He urged agreement while they were still on the road, before the matter passed through the judge and the officer and ended in prison. Both examples identify a moment when a hearer still has room to act. One is the moment of remembering a grievance. The other is the remaining distance before reaching the judge."], "lesson": "Jesus connects worship with how we treat people. A serious attempt to make peace can reveal whether we have taken his words about anger to heart.", "question": "What unfinished matter would Jesus' picture of the worshipper bring to your attention?"},
  {"id": 91010, "title": "Faithfulness in Desire and Speech", "subtitle": "Integrity without loopholes", "text": "Matthew 5:27–37", "chapters": [[39, 5]], "start": 27, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus next took up the command against adultery. He spoke of the look that is directed towards lust and brought the heart into view. What another person could see of someone's conduct was only part of the matter. Jesus asked his hearers to reckon with what they were allowing themselves to desire.", "He used severe images of an eye or a hand that causes a person to stumble. He pictured giving up something precious rather than letting it lead the whole person to ruin. The force of the illustration is in the seriousness of what is at stake. A person who sees a source of temptation has a decision to make about what place to give it.", "Jesus then spoke of putting away a wife. His hearers knew the provision for a written notice of divorce. He addressed the consequences for a wife dismissed by her husband and the person who marries her, giving an exception involving sexual immorality. The act of writing a notice does not exhaust the question of responsibility.", "He turned from marriage to the reliability of words. His hearers knew the duty to keep oaths made to the Lord. Jesus told them to avoid oaths that appealed to heaven, earth, Jerusalem or even one's own head. A person's yes or no should be dependable. The passage ends by putting truthfulness into the simplest words people use with one another."], "lesson": "Faithfulness reaches into the desires we entertain and the words others must rely on. Jesus calls for decisive action against habits that make unfaithfulness easier.", "question": "Where would a truthful yes or no be better than a promise dressed up to sound convincing?"},
  {"id": 91011, "title": "When an Enemy Needs Your Prayer", "subtitle": "Love patterned after the Father", "text": "Matthew 5:38–48", "chapters": [[39, 5]], "start": 38, "end": 48, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus recalled the rule about an eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth. He then gave his hearers a series of situations in which they might feel entitled to answer in kind. He spoke of a person struck on the cheek, of a lawsuit over a garment and of someone compelled to go a mile. In each picture, he asked the hearer to consider a response beyond returning the injury or doing the minimum demanded.", "He went on to speak of giving to someone who asks and of receiving a person who would borrow. These are ordinary occasions when another person's claim can feel like an interruption. The situations are different, but each places a choice in the hands of the person hearing Jesus' words.", "Jesus then spoke of loving enemies and praying for persecutors. He pointed to the Father, who makes his sun rise on evil and good people and sends rain on just and unjust alike. The illustration locates the example for love in what the Father gives every day to people whose conduct differs.", "He asked what was distinctive about loving only those who love us or greeting only those who belong to our own circle. Even people his hearers looked down on could do as much. The passage closes with the Father's perfection as the measure. Jesus has moved from a single blow or demand to the kind of love his followers are to practise towards people who oppose them."], "lesson": "Jesus calls disciples to act beyond the circle of people who repay kindness. Prayer for an enemy places that person's welfare before God and tests our desire for revenge.", "question": "Whom could you pray for by asking God for a specific good in that person's life?"},
  {"id": 91012, "title": "What the Father Sees in Secret", "subtitle": "Giving, prayer and fasting", "text": "Matthew 6:1–18", "chapters": [[39, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus asked his disciples to consider who they were trying to please when they gave, prayed or fasted. A person could make a show of giving and receive public praise for it. Jesus described an act of giving carried out so quietly that one hand did not know what the other was doing. The Father who sees in secret would see the gift.", "He raised the same question about prayer. Some chose visible places where others could see them. Jesus directed his hearers to go into a room and shut the door. He also warned against piling up words under the assumption that numbers would make God listen. Their Father knew what they needed before they asked.", "Then he gave them a prayer. It begins with the Father's name, his kingdom and his will. It asks for daily bread, forgiveness as the people praying forgive others, and deliverance from temptation and evil. After the prayer Jesus returned to forgiveness, giving it direct weight in how his hearers dealt with people who had wronged them.", "When he turned to fasting, he described people making their faces look miserable so everyone would notice. His followers were to wash and care for their appearance as they usually would. Their fast could then remain a matter between them and the Father who sees in secret. The three instructions leave plenty for a disciple to do where there may be no audience at all."], "lesson": "The prayer Jesus gave orders our desires around the Father before naming everyday needs. A private practice of mercy or prayer can expose how much we depend on being noticed.", "question": "How might the order of the Lord's Prayer change what you ask for today?"},
  {"id": 91013, "title": "Treasure and Tomorrow", "subtitle": "Trusting the Father for daily needs", "text": "Matthew 6:19–34", "chapters": [[39, 6]], "start": 19, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus spoke about two places in which treasure could be stored. On earth it could be damaged by moth or rust, or taken by thieves. Treasure in heaven was beyond those losses. He joined the choice of treasure to the direction of the heart. He then spoke of the eye as a lamp for the body and warned about what happens when the light within is darkness.", "He gave his hearers another picture: one servant trying to serve two masters. A divided attachment would show itself in how the servant loved and held to one while despising the other. Jesus applied it plainly to the service of God and the service of money.", "The subject of money led into the needs that make people anxious. Jesus spoke of food, drink and clothing. The birds did not sow, reap or store food in barns, yet their heavenly Father fed them. The lilies did not labour or spin, yet Solomon in his glory was not clothed like one of them. The Father who clothed grass that lasted so briefly knew his people's needs.", "Jesus told his hearers to seek first God's kingdom and righteousness. He brought the teaching back to the day they had in front of them. Tomorrow would have its own concerns, and today had enough trouble of its own. The passage moves from where people store their treasure to what they give first place when real needs come to mind."], "lesson": "Jesus connects anxiety about provision with what has first claim on the heart. Trust allows us to attend to today's duties while placing tomorrow before the Father.", "question": "What would seeking God's kingdom first change about one decision concerning money or time?"},
  {"id": 91014, "title": "The Speck and the Beam", "subtitle": "Honest self-examination and generous prayer", "text": "Matthew 7:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus began with a warning about judging. The judgement and measure his hearers applied to others would be applied to them. Then he pictured someone noticing a speck in a brother's eye while failing to notice a beam in his own. The person with the beam even offered to remove the speck. Jesus told him to deal with the beam first so that he could see clearly enough to help his brother.", "Jesus gave another warning about discernment. He pictured giving what was holy to dogs or throwing pearls before pigs. What was given could be trampled and the giver attacked. The image adds a different kind of care to the warning about judgement. The person offering help still has to pay attention to the situation.", "Jesus then told his hearers to ask, seek and knock. He pictured a child asking a parent for bread or a fish. A parent would not answer by handing over a stone or a serpent. If parents who fell short in other ways knew how to give good gifts, their Father in heaven would give good things to those who asked him.", "He drew the instruction towards daily conduct by telling them to do for others what they wanted others to do for them. He connected this with the law and the prophets. The listener has been asked to examine his own approach to correction, trust the Father's generosity and put himself in the place of another person."], "lesson": "Correction should begin with honest attention to our own conduct. The Father's generosity also shapes how we approach the needs of others.", "question": "What would removing the beam first change about a conversation you need to have?"},
  {"id": 91015, "title": "Two Builders in the Storm", "subtitle": "Hearing that becomes obedience", "text": "Matthew 7:13–29", "chapters": [[39, 7]], "start": 13, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["As Jesus brought the sermon towards its close, he spoke of two gates. The broad road was easy to enter and many went down it, but it ended in destruction. The narrow way led to life. He warned that false prophets could arrive looking like sheep while being dangerous within. Fruit would show what kind of tree stood before the hearer.", "Jesus then spoke of people who would call him Lord and point to impressive deeds done in his name. He said that entering the kingdom belonged to those who did his Father's will. A name spoken publicly and deeds cited in one's own defence could leave the question of obedience unanswered.", "He set two builders before the crowd. A wise man heard his sayings and acted on them, like a man building on rock. Rain came, streams rose and winds beat against the house. It stood because it had been founded on rock. A foolish man heard the same words and failed to act, like a builder who set his house on sand. The same forces beat on it, and its fall was great.", "When Jesus finished speaking, the crowds were astonished at the way he taught. Matthew says they heard authority different from that of their scribes. The people had heard the same sermon together. The picture Jesus left them with concerned what each person would go on to do with what he had heard."], "lesson": "The storm exposes a difference that was already present in how the builders chose their foundations. Jesus links a sound foundation to hearing his words and doing them.", "question": "Which instruction from the Sermon on the Mount have you heard often enough to put into practice now?"},
  {"id": 91016, "title": "A Man Reaches Out, a Soldier Asks", "subtitle": "The authority and compassion of Jesus", "text": "Matthew 8:1–17", "chapters": [[39, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus came down from the mountain, large crowds followed him. A man with leprosy approached and knelt before him. The man did not question whether Jesus had the power to cleanse him. He put his request in terms of whether Jesus was willing. Jesus stretched out his hand, touched him and said he was willing. The man was cleansed at once. Jesus told him to show himself to the priest and offer what Moses had commanded.", "In Capernaum a centurion approached Jesus about a servant who lay at home paralysed and in great distress. Jesus said he would come and heal him. The officer said he was unworthy to have Jesus under his roof. He understood what an order could do: one soldier went when told to go and another came when told to come. He trusted Jesus to speak the word from where he stood.", "Jesus marvelled and told those following him that he had not found such faith in Israel. He spoke of people coming from east and west to sit with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, and warned about presuming on a place at the table. Then he sent the centurion on his way. The servant was healed that hour.", "In Peter's house Jesus saw Peter's mother-in-law sick with fever. He touched her hand, the fever left and she rose and served him. In the evening people brought more sufferers. Matthew connects his care for them with Isaiah's words about bearing infirmities and sicknesses."], "lesson": "The man with leprosy appealed to Jesus' willingness. The centurion relied on the authority of his word. Both brought a real need before him.", "question": "What did the centurion understand about authority that shaped his request?"},
  {"id": 91017, "title": "The Cost of Following and the Storm", "subtitle": "Discipleship when safety is uncertain", "text": "Matthew 8:18–27", "chapters": [[39, 8]], "start": 18, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus saw the crowds around him, he gave orders to cross to the other side of the lake. Before he set off, a scribe offered to follow him wherever he went. Jesus answered by speaking of foxes with holes and birds with nests, while the Son of Man had nowhere to lay his head. His reply gave the man a picture of what travelling with him could involve.", "Another disciple asked for leave to go first and bury his father. Jesus told him to follow and leave the dead to bury their own dead. Matthew does not say how either man answered. The next people he describes are the disciples who followed Jesus into the boat.", "A violent storm arose on the lake. Waves covered the boat, and Jesus was asleep. The disciples came to wake him and cried for the Lord to save them because they were perishing. He asked why they were so afraid and spoke of their little faith. Then he stood and rebuked the winds and the sea.", "A great calm followed. The people in the boat marvelled and asked what sort of man this was, since even the winds and sea obeyed him. Their question carries the account beyond relief at reaching calm water. They had set out with the man who spoke of having nowhere to lay his head, and they were discovering an authority they had not expected."], "lesson": "The call to follow Jesus is given before the storm arrives. In the boat the disciples discover how much more they have to learn about the one they follow.", "question": "How do Jesus' words before the crossing prepare you to read what happened on the lake?"},
  {"id": 91018, "title": "Two Men Among the Tombs", "subtitle": "When deliverance unsettles a town", "text": "Matthew 8:28–34", "chapters": [[39, 8]], "start": 28, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus reached the other side of the lake, two men came out of the tombs to meet him. They were so fierce that people could not pass along that road. Matthew brings the crossing straight into this encounter. The men cried out to Jesus as the Son of God and asked whether he had come to torment them before the appointed time.", "Some distance away a herd of pigs was feeding. The demons begged Jesus to send them into the pigs if he drove them out. He told them to go. They left the men and entered the herd. The whole herd rushed down the steep bank into the sea and died in the water. The men who tended the pigs fled to the town.", "In the town, the herdsmen reported what had happened, including what had happened to the men who had been possessed. Matthew keeps both parts of their report together. The herd's loss was plain, and the power that had held the two men had been driven out. People in the town received news of both before coming out to meet Jesus.", "The whole town came out. When the people saw Jesus, they begged him to leave their district. Matthew ends there without telling us what the two men said afterwards or giving a reason for the townspeople's request. Their appeal to Jesus to go is the last word in the scene."], "lesson": "A community can witness a powerful act and still want distance from Jesus. The town's response invites us to examine what we are anxious to protect when his authority disturbs familiar arrangements.", "question": "What happened to the two men, and what did the townspeople ask Jesus to do?"},
  {"id": 91019, "title": "Forgiveness, Healing and a Place at the Table", "subtitle": "Mercy for those who need it", "text": "Matthew 9:1–13", "chapters": [[39, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus crossed back by boat and came to his own town. People brought him a paralysed man lying on a bed. Seeing their faith, Jesus addressed the man as a son and told him to take courage because his sins were forgiven. Some scribes said to themselves that Jesus was blaspheming. He knew their thoughts and asked why they were thinking evil in their hearts.", "Jesus asked which was easier to say: that the man's sins were forgiven, or that he should rise and walk. Then he gave the people present something they could see. To show that the Son of Man had authority on earth to forgive sins, he told the man to rise, take up his bed and go home. The man rose and went home. The crowds marvelled and glorified God.", "As Jesus went on from there, he saw Matthew sitting at the tax booth. Jesus told him to follow, and Matthew rose and followed him. Later, as Jesus sat at a meal in a house, many tax collectors and people regarded as sinners came and ate with him and his disciples.", "The Pharisees asked the disciples why their teacher ate with such people. Jesus heard and answered with the picture of a physician. It was the sick who needed one. He told his hearers to go and learn what God meant by desiring mercy and spoke of his calling to sinners. The man on the bed and the people at the table each give the reader a place to consider the mercy Jesus came to exercise."], "lesson": "Forgiveness addresses a need the people bringing the man may have overlooked. At Matthew's table Jesus shows why the company of sinners fits his mission of mercy.", "question": "How do Jesus' words to the paralysed man help explain why he ate at Matthew's table?"},
  {"id": 91020, "title": "A Question About Fasting", "subtitle": "Recognising the time and the occasion", "text": "Matthew 9:14–17", "chapters": [[39, 9]], "start": 14, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["John's disciples came to Jesus with a question about fasting. They fasted, and so did the Pharisees. Why did Jesus' disciples go about their days without keeping the same practice? Matthew has just described Jesus eating with tax collectors and sinners. The question now concerns another difference that people could see in the conduct of those around him.", "Jesus asked them to think of a wedding. As long as the bridegroom was with the wedding party, it would be out of place for his companions to mourn. The days would come when he was taken away from them. Then they would fast. Jesus gave fasting a place in the days to come and explained why the present moment called for a different response.", "He gave another example from a familiar task. A patch of new cloth sewn onto an old garment could pull away and make the tear worse. Then he spoke of new wine in old wineskins. The skins would burst, the wine would spill and the skins would be ruined. New wine belonged in new skins so that both could be preserved.", "John's disciples had asked why one company followed a practice that another did not. Jesus answered by placing himself in the picture. They needed to consider who was present and what that presence meant before deciding what his disciples should be doing."], "lesson": "Jesus explains a religious practice in light of his presence and the occasion. The question encourages us to consider whether our habits express a living response to him.", "question": "How does the bridegroom image explain why the disciples' conduct differed?"},
  {"id": 91021, "title": "Interrupted on the Way to a Child", "subtitle": "Faith when hope seems gone", "text": "Matthew 9:18–26", "chapters": [[39, 9]], "start": 18, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["While Jesus was speaking, a ruler came and knelt before him. His daughter had just died. He asked Jesus to come and lay his hand on her, believing that she would live. Jesus got up and followed him, and his disciples went with him.", "On the way, a woman came up behind Jesus. She had suffered from bleeding for twelve years. She told herself that if she could touch his garment she would be made well. She reached for its edge. Jesus turned, saw her and told her to take courage. He addressed her as daughter and said that her faith had made her well. Matthew says she was healed from that hour.", "Jesus continued to the ruler's house. Inside were musicians and a crowd making a commotion. He told the people to go out and said the girl was sleeping. They laughed at him. After the crowd had been put out, he went in, took her by the hand and the girl rose. News of what had happened spread through the district.", "The two encounters share a journey. The ruler had come openly to ask Jesus to touch his child. The woman had approached from behind and touched the edge of his garment. Jesus stopped to speak to her and continued to the child, entering the house despite the response of the people who had gathered there."], "lesson": "Jesus had time to address the woman personally even on the way to the ruler's house. The account gives attention to two sufferers whom others might have passed over or given up for dead.", "question": "What do Jesus' words to the woman tell you about the way he received her?"},
  {"id": 91022, "title": "Opening Eyes and Seeing the Harvest", "subtitle": "Compassion that asks for workers", "text": "Matthew 9:27–38", "chapters": [[39, 9]], "start": 27, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["As Jesus went on from the ruler's house, two blind men followed him and called for mercy, addressing him as the Son of David. They came to him inside a house. He asked whether they believed he could do what they were asking. They said they did. He touched their eyes, spoke to them about their faith, and their eyes were opened.", "Jesus warned them sternly that no one should know what had happened. They went out and spread the report through the district. As they were leaving, others brought a man who could not speak and was afflicted by a demon. When the demon was driven out, the man spoke. The crowds were amazed and said they had never seen anything like it in Israel. The Pharisees gave a very different account, attributing his work to the ruler of demons.", "Matthew then follows Jesus through cities and villages. He taught in the synagogues, proclaimed the good news of the kingdom and healed the sick. When he saw the crowds, he had compassion because they were distressed and scattered, like sheep without a shepherd.", "Jesus turned to his disciples and described a harvest too plentiful for the few workers available. He told them to ask the Lord of the harvest to send workers into it. The crowd's condition had moved him to compassion, and he gave the disciples a way to begin sharing in his concern."], "lesson": "Jesus sees more than a crowd. His compassion notices its condition and gives the disciples something specific to pray for.", "question": "Who in your community comes to mind when you read about sheep without a shepherd?"},
  {"id": 91023, "title": "The Twelve Are Sent Out", "subtitle": "Giving freely what you have received", "text": "Matthew 10:1–15", "chapters": [[39, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus had just told the disciples to pray for workers. Now he called twelve disciples and gave them authority over unclean spirits and diseases. Matthew names them, beginning with Simon Peter and his brother Andrew, and James and John, the sons of Zebedee. The list includes Matthew the tax collector, Simon the Canaanite and Judas Iscariot, whom Matthew identifies as the one who would betray him.", "Jesus sent the Twelve out with instructions for this journey. They were to go to the lost sheep of Israel. As they went, they were to announce that the kingdom of heaven was near. The work included healing the sick, cleansing people with leprosy, raising the dead and driving out demons. They were to give freely because they had received freely.", "They were to travel without taking gold, silver or spare clothing as provisions. Jesus said a worker was worthy of his food. On arriving in a town or village, they should look for a suitable household and remain there until they left. The household's welcome gave them a place from which to carry out the work.", "Jesus also prepared them for refusal. If a house or town would not receive them or listen to them, they were to leave and shake the dust from their feet. He took that refusal seriously, comparing the coming judgement with Sodom and Gomorrah. The instructions give the disciples something to do both when a door opens and when it stays shut."], "lesson": "Their authority was a gift and their service was to bear the same character. The instructions join generosity with dependence on God and on the hospitality of others.", "question": "How would receiving freely affect the way the disciples treated people who could give them nothing?"},
  {"id": 91024, "title": "Speaking When Opposition Comes", "subtitle": "Courage under the Father's care", "text": "Matthew 10:16–33", "chapters": [[39, 10]], "start": 16, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus told the Twelve that he was sending them out like sheep among wolves. He called for the wisdom of serpents and the innocence of doves. He warned them that people would hand them over to councils and bring them before governors and kings. Those hearings would give them an occasion to bear witness.", "When they were handed over, they need not be anxious beforehand about what to say. In that hour the Spirit of their Father would speak through them. Jesus also warned of opposition closer to home: relatives would turn against one another and his followers would be hated on account of his name. He called them to endure, telling them to move to another city when persecuted in one.", "A disciple could expect to be treated as his teacher had been treated. If people had spoken against the head of the house, they could speak in the same way of those who belonged to him. Even so, Jesus told his messengers to speak openly what they had learned from him. There was a limit to what people who could kill the body could do.", "He brought their attention to the Father's care. Sparrows sold for very little, yet their Father knew what happened to them. Even the hairs on the disciples' heads were numbered. Jesus ended by addressing whether they would acknowledge him before others. The people he had warned about hearings and hostility could carry that assurance into the moment when they had to speak."], "lesson": "Jesus prepares his followers for costly witness and assures them of the Father's knowledge of each one. His teaching gives courage a firmer basis than the hope that people will approve.", "question": "What does the sparrow illustration contribute to Jesus' instructions about public witness?"},
  {"id": 91025, "title": "A Cup of Cold Water", "subtitle": "Allegiance and welcome", "text": "Matthew 10:34–42", "chapters": [[39, 10]], "start": 34, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["As Jesus finished speaking to the Twelve about their mission, he warned of divisions that could enter even a household. A person might find relatives opposed to following him. He said that even the love owed to parents or children could not take first place over allegiance to him. He spoke of taking up a cross and of losing one's life for his sake and finding it.", "Then he turned to a second group of people in the same mission: those who would receive his messengers. A person who welcomed them welcomed Jesus, and a person who welcomed Jesus welcomed the one who had sent him. What appeared to be an ordinary reception of travelling people could carry that significance.", "Jesus spoke of someone receiving a prophet because he was a prophet, and of someone receiving a righteous person because he was righteous. Each welcome would have its reward. He came at last to a small thing a person could do even with very little to offer. Someone who gave one of the little ones a cup of cold water because he was a disciple would not lose that reward.", "The instruction ends with water in a cup. Earlier in the speech, the apostles had been told to go out and rely on hospitality. Here Jesus gives meaning to a simple act of the person on the other side of the door."], "lesson": "The passage places demanding loyalty and ordinary hospitality in the same mission. Help offered quietly to a disciple can express real allegiance to Jesus.", "question": "What kind of welcome is within your reach even if you have little to give?"},
  {"id": 91026, "title": "John Sends a Question From Prison", "subtitle": "Taking uncertainty to Jesus", "text": "Matthew 11:1–19", "chapters": [[39, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After giving instructions to the Twelve, Jesus went on to teach and preach in their towns. John the Baptist was in prison. Hearing about the works of Christ, he sent disciples to ask Jesus whether he was the one who was to come or whether they should look for another.", "Jesus told the messengers to report what they heard and saw. Blind people were receiving sight. The lame walked, people with leprosy were cleansed, the deaf heard, the dead were raised and good news was preached to the poor. He added a blessing for the person who did not stumble on account of him. The messengers had something concrete to take back into the prison.", "When they left, Jesus spoke to the crowd about John. He asked what they had gone into the wilderness to see. John was no reed wavering in the wind and no man dressed for a palace. He was a prophet and more than a prophet, the messenger sent to prepare the way. Jesus spoke of John's place in what God had been doing, telling those with ears to hear to listen.", "Then he compared the generation to children in the marketplace who complained that others would neither dance to a tune nor mourn at a lament. John had come with an austere manner of life and been accused of having a demon. The Son of Man came eating and drinking and was criticised for his company. Jesus let the hearers examine why they had found reasons to dismiss both."], "lesson": "John's question went directly to Jesus. Jesus answered by directing attention to his works, while exposing the habit of finding an excuse to reject every messenger.", "question": "What evidence did Jesus tell John's disciples to carry back to him?"},
  {"id": 91027, "title": "An Invitation to the Weary", "subtitle": "Repentance and rest", "text": "Matthew 11:20–30", "chapters": [[39, 11]], "start": 20, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus began to rebuke towns where many of his mighty works had been done because they had not repented. He named Chorazin and Bethsaida. If Tyre and Sidon had seen what those towns had seen, he said, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes. He also addressed Capernaum, comparing its accountability with that of Sodom. The works he had done had given these towns a reason to change their conduct.", "At that time Jesus thanked his Father, Lord of heaven and earth, for revealing things to little children that were hidden from the wise and understanding. He acknowledged the Father's good pleasure in this. What he had said to the towns makes the thanks worth hearing carefully. A place could have seen much and still fail to receive what was being shown to it.", "Jesus said all things had been given to him by his Father. He spoke of the Father knowing the Son and the Son knowing the Father, and of the Son revealing the Father to others. Then he turned to people carrying heavy burdens and called them to come to him for rest.", "He asked them to take his yoke and learn from him, describing himself as meek and lowly in heart. The rest he promised would be found under his teaching. His invitation names both the burdens people already carry and the person from whom they can learn a different way to bear their lives."], "lesson": "The invitation to rest comes from the same Jesus who calls the towns to repent. Rest is found in coming under the teaching of a gentle Lord.", "question": "What does Jesus ask weary people to do after they come to him?"},
  {"id": 91028, "title": "Two Sabbath Disputes", "subtitle": "Mercy and doing good", "text": "Matthew 12:1–14", "chapters": [[39, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["On a Sabbath Jesus and his disciples were passing through grainfields. The disciples were hungry and began to pluck grain and eat. When some Pharisees saw, they accused the disciples of doing what was unlawful on the Sabbath. Jesus answered by recalling David, who was hungry with his companions and ate the consecrated bread ordinarily reserved for priests.", "He also asked whether they had read about the priests who worked in the temple on the Sabbath and were held guiltless. He spoke of someone greater than the temple and appealed to God's desire for mercy. If his critics understood that, he said, they would not have condemned the guiltless. He declared the Son of Man Lord of the Sabbath.", "Jesus went into their synagogue. There he found a man with a withered hand. The people questioning him asked whether it was lawful to heal on the Sabbath, looking for a charge to bring against him. Jesus asked what any of them would do if a sheep fell into a pit that day. They would take hold of it and lift it out. A person was worth more than a sheep.", "Jesus said it was lawful to do good on the Sabbath and told the man to stretch out his hand. He did, and it was restored like the other. The Pharisees went out and consulted about how to destroy Jesus. Matthew gives the reader two uses of that day to consider: restoring a man's hand and taking counsel against the one who restored it."], "lesson": "Jesus makes the suffering person visible in a dispute about rules. His questions call us to examine whether our judgements reflect God's mercy.", "question": "How does the example of the sheep challenge the way the critics looked at the man?"},
  {"id": 91029, "title": "A Healing and an Accusation", "subtitle": "Words reveal the heart", "text": "Matthew 12:15–37", "chapters": [[39, 12]], "start": 15, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Knowing of the opposition, Jesus withdrew. People followed him, and he healed them and told them to keep his identity quiet. Matthew saw in this the servant Isaiah described, gentle in the streets and careful with people like bruised reeds. The prophet's words also look beyond Israel to the nations placing hope in his name.", "Then a man who was blind and unable to speak was brought to Jesus. The man was afflicted by a demon, and after Jesus healed him he could both see and speak. The people wondered whether Jesus was the Son of David. The Pharisees heard and claimed he drove out demons by the ruler of demons.", "Jesus answered that a kingdom divided against itself would come to ruin. If Satan cast out Satan, how could his kingdom stand? If Jesus drove out demons by the Spirit of God, then God's kingdom had come upon them. He pictured someone entering a strong man's house only after binding the man who guarded it. He also warned about speaking against the Holy Spirit, giving grave weight to what the Pharisees were saying.", "He turned to a tree and its fruit. Words come from what fills the heart, as fruit comes from a tree. People would answer for even their careless words. The scene began with a man receiving speech, and it closes with Jesus asking the people who already had speech to consider what their words revealed."], "lesson": "The crowd and the Pharisees saw the same healing and spoke about it differently. Jesus warns us to take seriously the heart from which our judgements and accusations come.", "question": "What did the Pharisees say about the healing, and how did Jesus answer their explanation?"},
  {"id": 91030, "title": "A Sign and a Family", "subtitle": "Responding to the light already given", "text": "Matthew 12:38–50", "chapters": [[39, 12]], "start": 38, "end": 50, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Some scribes and Pharisees told Jesus they wanted to see a sign from him. He answered by speaking of Jonah. As Jonah was three days and nights in the great fish, the Son of Man would be in the heart of the earth. The people of Nineveh would stand as witnesses against the present generation because they had repented at Jonah's preaching. Jesus said that someone greater than Jonah was there.", "He also recalled the queen of the South, who came a great distance to hear Solomon's wisdom. His hearers had someone greater than Solomon before them. In both examples, Jesus asked people looking for a new sign to consider how others had responded to the opportunity already given them.", "Jesus then described an unclean spirit leaving a person and later returning to find its former home empty, swept and put in order. It brought back seven spirits worse than itself, and the person's final condition was worse than the first. He applied that picture to the generation hearing him.", "While Jesus was still speaking, his mother and brothers stood outside wanting to speak to him. When someone brought the message, he asked who his mother and brothers were. Pointing towards his disciples, he said that whoever did his Father's will was his brother, sister and mother. The passage closes by putting the response to God's will into the language of belonging."], "lesson": "The passage asks what we do with the light we have already received. Jesus describes a close relationship with him in terms of doing his Father's will.", "question": "How does Nineveh's response challenge the people asking for another sign?"},
  {"id": 91031, "title": "The Sower and Four Soils", "subtitle": "How the word takes root", "text": "Matthew 13:1–23", "chapters": [[39, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["That day Jesus went out of the house and sat beside the sea. A crowd gathered so large that he got into a boat and sat there while the people stood on the shore. He began telling them about a man who went out to sow and scattered seed across ground where it would meet different conditions.", "Some seed fell on the path and birds ate it. Some fell where the soil was shallow over rock. It came up quickly, but when the sun rose it withered for lack of root. Other seed fell among thorns, which grew up and choked it. Seed that fell on good ground bore fruit in different amounts. Jesus called on those with ears to hear.", "The disciples asked why he spoke to the crowd in parables. Jesus answered about what had been given to the disciples to understand. He described people who could hear and see without taking in what was before them. Then he blessed the disciples' eyes and ears for what they were seeing and hearing.", "He explained the soils. The word could be snatched away when someone failed to understand. It could be received eagerly and abandoned under pressure, or choked by worries and the deceitfulness of riches. The good ground pictured people who hear, understand and bear fruit. The parable gives the hearer time to ask what happens after the first moment of hearing."], "lesson": "The seed meets different conditions. Jesus gives us a way to name what can stop the word from bearing fruit after our first interest in it.", "question": "Which obstacle in Jesus' explanation deserves particular attention in your own life?"},
  {"id": 91032, "title": "Wheat, Weeds and Quiet Growth", "subtitle": "Patience while God brings the harvest", "text": "Matthew 13:24–43", "chapters": [[39, 13]], "start": 24, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus told of a man who sowed good seed in a field. While people slept, an enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and left. As the crop came up, the servants noticed the weeds and asked where they had come from. Hearing that an enemy had done it, they offered to pull them up. The owner warned that they might uproot the wheat at the same time. He told them to let both grow until harvest.", "Jesus gave the crowd two more pictures of the kingdom. A mustard seed was small when sown and grew large enough for birds to lodge in its branches. A woman hid leaven in flour until it worked through the whole amount. Both images ask the hearer to pay attention to a beginning that could easily be overlooked.", "When Jesus left the crowd and went into the house, the disciples asked him to explain the weeds. He named the Son of Man as the sower and the world as the field. The good seed stood for children of the kingdom and the weeds for children of the wicked one. The enemy was the devil, the harvest the end of the age and the reapers angels.", "He described the judgement at harvest and the righteous shining in their Father's kingdom. The servants' question had been about what to pull out as soon as they saw it. Jesus gave the final separating a time and agents of its own."], "lesson": "The farmer's restraint teaches patience with a world in which good and evil are still mingled. The mustard seed and leaven encourage faith when the kingdom's work seems small.", "question": "What work does the farmer reserve for harvest, and what does that teach about judgement?"},
  {"id": 91033, "title": "Hidden Treasure and a Familiar Town", "subtitle": "Recognising what stands before you", "text": "Matthew 13:44–58", "chapters": [[39, 13]], "start": 44, "end": 58, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus compared the kingdom of heaven to treasure hidden in a field. A man found it, hid it again and in his joy sold what he had to buy the field. A merchant looking for fine pearls found one of great value and also sold all he had to buy it. One man came upon treasure in the ground. The other knew what he was looking for and recognised the pearl when he found it.", "Then Jesus spoke of a net cast into the sea that gathered fish of every kind. When it was full, people drew it ashore, kept the good fish and threw the others away. He applied it to the separation at the end of the age. When the disciples said they understood, Jesus compared a teacher instructed in the kingdom to a householder who could bring out both new and old things from his treasure.", "After finishing these parables, Jesus went to his own country and taught in the synagogue. People were astonished at his wisdom and mighty works. They asked whether he was the carpenter's son and named his mother and brothers. His sisters, they said, were still among them. What they already knew about his family made it hard for them to receive what they were hearing.", "They took offence at him. Jesus spoke of a prophet being without honour in his own country and house. Matthew says he did few mighty works there because of their unbelief."], "lesson": "The people in the parables recognised value and acted decisively. Jesus' neighbours let their familiarity with his family shape how they received him.", "question": "What difference do you see between the buyers in the parables and the people in Jesus' hometown?"},
  {"id": 91034, "title": "The Death of John the Baptist", "subtitle": "Courage when truth is costly", "text": "Matthew 14:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When reports about Jesus reached Herod, he said to his servants that this was John the Baptist risen from the dead. Matthew then goes back to explain why Herod spoke of John that way. Herod had arrested and bound John because John told him it was unlawful for him to have Herodias, the wife of his brother. Herod wanted to kill him but feared the crowd, who regarded John as a prophet.", "At Herod's birthday celebration, Herodias's daughter danced before the company and pleased him. He swore to give her whatever she asked. Prompted by her mother, she requested John's head on a dish. The king was grieved, but his oath and the people sitting at the meal mattered to him. He commanded that her request be granted.", "John was killed in prison. What had been requested was brought to the girl, and she took it to her mother. Herod's promise, spoken during a celebration, had been carried out against a prisoner who had challenged his conduct. The company before whom he made the promise had helped shape what he was prepared to do.", "John's disciples came for his body, buried him and went to tell Jesus. Matthew shows the outcome first in Herod's fearful explanation of the reports about Jesus, then at the close in the grief and duties of the disciples who had to bury their teacher."], "lesson": "Herod allowed public embarrassment to outweigh what he knew he was about to do. John's costly witness makes the weakness behind Herod's display of authority plain.", "question": "Which pressures influenced Herod, and where do similar pressures arise in ordinary decisions?"},
  {"id": 91035, "title": "Bread for the Crowd", "subtitle": "Compassion when resources look small", "text": "Matthew 14:13–21", "chapters": [[39, 14]], "start": 13, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus heard what had happened to John, he withdrew by boat to a deserted place apart. The people heard and followed him on foot from the towns. When he came out and saw the large crowd, he had compassion and healed their sick. The place he had chosen for withdrawal became a place where many people received his attention.", "Evening came. The disciples pointed out that the place was remote and the hour late. They suggested sending people into the villages to buy their own food. Jesus told them the people did not need to leave and asked the disciples to give them something to eat. They answered that they had only five loaves and two fish. Jesus told them to bring the food to him.", "He had the crowd sit on the grass. Taking the loaves and fish, he looked up to heaven, blessed the food and broke the loaves. He gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the crowd. The men who had first looked to the surrounding villages for provision were now passing food among the people themselves.", "Everybody ate and was satisfied. The disciples collected twelve baskets of what remained. Matthew gives the number as about five thousand men, besides women and children. The five loaves and two fish had looked insufficient when the disciples counted them. At the end of the account there was enough to feed the crowd and enough remaining to gather."], "lesson": "The disciples first saw a problem to send elsewhere. Jesus asked them to bring what they had and made them participants in caring for the crowd.", "question": "What changed when the disciples brought their limited food to Jesus?"},
  {"id": 91036, "title": "Peter Steps Out of the Boat", "subtitle": "Calling on Jesus when fear rises", "text": "Matthew 14:22–36", "chapters": [[39, 14]], "start": 22, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After the crowd had eaten, Jesus sent the disciples into the boat to go ahead of him. He dismissed the people and went up the mountain by himself to pray. By evening he was still there alone. The boat was out on the lake, battered by the waves because the wind was against it.", "Late in the night Jesus came towards the boat, walking on the sea. The disciples were terrified and cried out, supposing it was a spirit. Jesus spoke to them at once. He told them to take courage, identified himself and told them not to be afraid. Peter asked Jesus to command him to come if it was really him. Jesus told him to come, and Peter climbed out of the boat and walked towards him on the water.", "When Peter noticed the strength of the wind, he became afraid and began to sink. He cried out to the Lord to save him. Jesus immediately stretched out his hand and caught him, then asked why he had doubted. When they got into the boat the wind ceased. Those in the boat worshipped Jesus and confessed that he truly was God's Son.", "They crossed to Gennesaret. When the people there recognised Jesus, they sent word through the district and brought the sick to him. They asked to touch even the edge of his garment, and those who did were made well. Matthew ends the crossing with recognition and renewed activity on the other shore."], "lesson": "Peter had enough trust to step out and enough need to cry for help. The account draws our attention to the hand Jesus extended and the confession the disciples made.", "question": "What did Peter do when he began to sink, and what did the others come to say about Jesus?"},
  {"id": 91037, "title": "Clean Hands and an Unclean Heart", "subtitle": "God's command above custom", "text": "Matthew 15:1–20", "chapters": [[39, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Scribes and Pharisees came from Jerusalem and asked why Jesus' disciples broke the elders' tradition by eating bread without washing their hands. Jesus responded with a question about a command from God. Honour was due to father and mother, yet their tradition permitted someone to declare what a parent might have received from him a gift devoted to God. The declaration could then excuse the person from giving the parent help.", "Jesus said they had set aside God's command for the sake of their tradition. He cited Isaiah's words about people honouring God with their lips while their hearts were far from him and treating human commands as doctrine. He then called the crowd and spoke directly to the question of what defiles a person.", "He said that what goes into the mouth does not defile a person. What comes out of the mouth does. The disciples told him the Pharisees were offended. He answered with the image of a plant his Father had not planted and of blind guides leading the blind. Peter asked him to explain the saying.", "Jesus distinguished what happens to food from what comes out of the heart. From the heart come evil thoughts, murder, adultery, sexual immorality, stealing, false testimony and slander. These are the things he said defile a person. The original question had been about hands at a meal. His answer took the disciples back to how a person treats parents and to what comes from within."], "lesson": "A religious custom becomes harmful when it makes room for neglect of a clear duty. Jesus points to the heart as the place where the deeper problem must be addressed.", "question": "Which duty did Jesus say the tradition could undermine, and how did it happen?"},
  {"id": 91038, "title": "A Persistent Mother and Another Hungry Crowd", "subtitle": "Mercy beyond familiar borders", "text": "Matthew 15:21–39", "chapters": [[39, 15]], "start": 21, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus withdrew to the region of Tyre and Sidon. A Canaanite woman cried out to him as the Son of David and begged for mercy because her daughter was severely afflicted by a demon. He did not answer her at first. The disciples asked him to send her away. Jesus spoke of being sent to the lost sheep of Israel. The woman came nearer, bowed before him and asked him to help her.", "Jesus used the image of children's bread that should not be thrown to the dogs. The woman answered that even the dogs ate crumbs falling from their masters' table. Jesus commended her great faith and told her that what she desired would be done. Matthew says her daughter was healed from that hour.", "Jesus went on beside the Sea of Galilee and sat on a mountain. People brought him many who were sick or disabled, and he healed them. When the crowd saw people who could not speak beginning to speak, the lame walking and the blind seeing, they glorified the God of Israel.", "After three days with the crowd, Jesus told his disciples he had compassion because the people had nothing to eat and might faint along the way. The disciples asked where they could find enough bread. They had seven loaves and a few fish. Jesus gave thanks, broke the food and gave it to the disciples to distribute. About four thousand men ate, besides women and children, and seven baskets remained. Then he dismissed the crowd and crossed by boat."], "lesson": "The woman keeps her request before Jesus and takes hold of the possibility of mercy in his own illustration. His answer gives weight to the faith of someone the disciples wanted sent away.", "question": "How did the woman answer Jesus' illustration, and what did he commend in her?"},
  {"id": 91039, "title": "Reading the Weather and Missing the Moment", "subtitle": "Discernment about teaching", "text": "Matthew 16:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Pharisees and Sadducees came together and asked Jesus to show them a sign from heaven. Matthew says they meant to test him. Jesus answered with a skill they already used. At evening they could look at the redness of the sky and expect fair weather. In the morning they could see a red and threatening sky and expect a storm. They could read the weather, yet they failed to discern the signs of the times. He gave them the sign of Jonah as his answer and left them.", "When the disciples reached the other side, they had forgotten to take bread. Jesus warned them to beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees. The disciples discussed the warning among themselves and decided it must concern the bread they had left behind.", "Jesus knew what they were saying and asked why they were so occupied with having no bread. He called them people of little faith and directed their memory to the two crowds he had fed. How many baskets had they collected after the five loaves fed the five thousand? How many after the seven loaves fed the four thousand? They had seen how those two shortages ended.", "They then understood what Jesus meant. He had been warning them about the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Remembering what he had done about bread helped them attend to what he was now saying about something else."], "lesson": "Concern about immediate provisions can make it harder to hear a warning on another matter. Jesus calls the disciples to remember what they had already seen him do.", "question": "How did the memory of the feedings help the disciples understand what Jesus meant by leaven?"},
  {"id": 91040, "title": "Peter's Confession and Jesus' Rebuke", "subtitle": "The Christ and the way of the cross", "text": "Matthew 16:13–28", "chapters": [[39, 16]], "start": 13, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus came to the district of Caesarea Philippi and asked his disciples what people were saying about the Son of Man. The answers included John the Baptist, Elijah, Jeremiah and one of the prophets. Then he put the question directly to them. Simon Peter answered that Jesus was the Christ, the Son of the living God.", "Jesus called Simon blessed and said his Father in heaven had revealed this to him. He spoke about Peter, the rock and the church he would build, and said the gates of death would not prevail against it. He spoke of the keys of the kingdom and of binding and loosing. Then he charged the disciples to tell no one that he was the Christ.", "From that time Jesus began telling them what lay ahead. He must go to Jerusalem, suffer at the hands of the elders, chief priests and scribes, be killed and be raised on the third day. Peter took him aside and began to rebuke him. Jesus told him to get behind him and called him a stumbling block, because his concern was with human things rather than the things of God.", "Jesus then spoke to anyone who would come after him. Following him meant denying oneself and taking up a cross. He asked what a person would gain by acquiring the whole world at the cost of his soul. Peter's answer had named Jesus rightly. His objection showed how much he still had to hear about the road Jesus would take."], "lesson": "Peter spoke truly about Jesus' identity and still resisted what Jesus said about his mission. Understanding who Jesus is requires listening when he describes the road he will take.", "question": "What does Peter accept in his confession, and what does he resist a few verses later?"},
  {"id": 91041, "title": "The Voice on the Mountain", "subtitle": "Listening to the beloved Son", "text": "Matthew 17:1–13", "chapters": [[39, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Six days later Jesus took Peter, James and John up a high mountain apart from the others. His appearance changed before them. His face shone like the sun and his garments became bright as light. The disciples saw Moses and Elijah speaking with him.", "Peter said it was good for them to be there. He offered to make three shelters, one for Jesus, one for Moses and one for Elijah. While he was still speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them. A voice from the cloud called Jesus the beloved Son in whom he was well pleased and told them to listen to him. The disciples fell on their faces in fear.", "Jesus came to them, touched them and told them to get up and not be afraid. When they lifted their eyes, they saw Jesus alone. On the way down he told them to speak of the vision to no one until the Son of Man had risen from the dead. What they had seen on the mountain would have to be held alongside what he had been telling them about suffering and rising.", "The disciples asked why the scribes said Elijah must come first. Jesus said Elijah had already come, and people had failed to recognise him and had done to him what they pleased. He said the Son of Man would suffer in a similar way. The disciples understood that he was speaking about John the Baptist."], "lesson": "Peter had a proposal ready while the scene was still unfolding. The Father's instruction places listening to Jesus first.", "question": "What would it mean to obey the voice from the cloud as you read what follows?"},
  {"id": 91042, "title": "A Suffering Boy and a Tax Question", "subtitle": "Dependence and consideration", "text": "Matthew 17:14–27", "chapters": [[39, 17]], "start": 14, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus and the three disciples returned to the crowd, a man came and knelt before him. He asked Jesus to have mercy on his son, who suffered severely and often fell into fire or water. He had brought the boy to the disciples, but they had been unable to heal him. Jesus lamented the unbelief of the generation and asked for the boy to be brought to him. He rebuked the demon, and the boy was healed from that hour.", "The disciples came privately to ask why they had failed. Jesus spoke about their little faith. He pictured faith as small as a mustard seed and spoke of its power to move a mountain. Later, while they were in Galilee, he told them again that the Son of Man would be handed over and killed and would rise on the third day. They were deeply grieved.", "When they came to Capernaum, the collectors of the temple tax asked Peter whether his teacher paid it. Peter said he did. Back in the house, Jesus asked him whether earthly kings collected taxes from their own sons or from others. Peter answered that they collected from others. Jesus said the sons were free.", "He then made provision for the payment so they would not give offence. Peter was to catch a fish and find a coin in its mouth. It would cover the tax for Jesus and Peter. The passage gives Peter a reason for the payment as well as the means of making it."], "lesson": "The healing exposes the disciples' need for faith. The tax conversation shows Jesus considering the effect that exercising a freedom would have on others.", "question": "Why did Jesus choose to pay the tax after explaining that the sons were free?"},
  {"id": 91043, "title": "A Child in the Middle", "subtitle": "The Father's concern for little ones", "text": "Matthew 18:1–14", "chapters": [[39, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["The disciples came to Jesus with a question about who was greatest in the kingdom of heaven. Jesus called a child and set the child among them. Before naming anyone as greatest, he spoke about the change needed to enter the kingdom at all. They had to turn and become like children. Whoever humbled himself like that child was greatest, and whoever welcomed such a child in Jesus' name welcomed Jesus.", "He then turned to the danger of causing one of the little ones who believed in him to stumble. He used severe pictures to show how seriously he took the harm. He spoke of a millstone and the sea, and of removing even a hand, foot or eye if it led a person into sin. The desire to be important gave the disciples no excuse for overlooking the effect of their own conduct.", "Jesus told them not to despise any of the little ones. He spoke of their angels seeing his Father's face. Then he asked them to picture a man with a hundred sheep. If one wandered, the man would leave the others and go to look for the one that had strayed.", "If he found it, he would rejoice over that sheep. Jesus connected the search with his Father's will that none of the little ones should perish. The disciples' question about rank had led them to a child in their midst and then to a shepherd looking for one sheep."], "lesson": "Jesus answers ambition by giving attention to someone the disciples might overlook. The searching shepherd makes the value of one vulnerable person plain.", "question": "Whose welfare does Jesus direct the disciples to consider when they want to discuss rank?"},
  {"id": 91044, "title": "A Private Conversation and an Unpaid Debt", "subtitle": "Seeking restoration and giving forgiveness", "text": "Matthew 18:15–35", "chapters": [[39, 18]], "start": 15, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus gave his disciples a way to speak to a brother who had sinned against them. The first conversation should be private. If the brother listened, the relationship had been recovered. If he did not listen, one or two others should go along so the matter could be established by witnesses. Only after that would it come before the congregation. Jesus also spoke of agreement in prayer and his presence when people gathered in his name.", "Peter then asked how often he ought to forgive a brother who sinned against him. Would seven times be enough? Jesus answered with a much greater number and told of a king who began settling accounts with his servants. One owed him ten thousand talents. As he could not pay, the king ordered that he be sold with his household and possessions. The man pleaded for patience. The king had compassion and cancelled the debt.", "That servant found a fellow servant who owed him a hundred denarii. He took hold of him and demanded payment. The fellow servant pleaded for patience in much the same way, but the first servant had him put in prison. Other servants saw and brought the matter to the king.", "The king summoned the servant and asked why he had failed to give the mercy he had received. He handed him over for punishment until the debt should be paid. Jesus brought the account back to his disciples and the forgiveness they were called to give from the heart."], "lesson": "The first step aims to regain a brother. The parable asks whether a person who has received mercy is passing it on in the way he deals with others.", "question": "How did the first servant's treatment of his fellow servant contradict the mercy he had received?"},
  {"id": 91045, "title": "A Question About Marriage and a Welcome for Children", "subtitle": "Faithfulness and care for families", "text": "Matthew 19:1–15", "chapters": [[39, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus left Galilee and travelled to the region of Judea beyond the Jordan. Crowds followed him, and he healed people there. Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked whether a man could put away his wife for any reason. Jesus took them back to creation, when God made male and female and said the two would become one flesh. He told them to take seriously what God had joined.", "They asked why Moses had directed that a certificate be given when a wife was put away. Jesus said Moses had permitted it because of the hardness of their hearts. He contrasted that permission with what was intended from the beginning and spoke of the gravity of divorce and remarriage. His disciples said that, if the obligations were so demanding, it might be better not to marry.", "Jesus answered that people did not all receive the same course in life. He spoke of different reasons a person might remain unmarried, including a choice for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. He left the saying with those able to receive it.", "Then people brought children for Jesus to lay his hands on and pray for them. The disciples rebuked those who brought them. Jesus told them to let the children come and said the kingdom of heaven belonged to such as these. He laid his hands on them before going on his way."], "lesson": "Jesus brings God's purpose and human hardness of heart into the discussion of marriage. His welcome of children reminds disciples to make room for those who depend on their care.", "question": "What does Jesus give attention to that the disciples were ready to dismiss?"},
  {"id": 91046, "title": "The Man Who Went Away Sad", "subtitle": "When possessions hold the heart", "text": "Matthew 19:16–30", "chapters": [[39, 19]], "start": 16, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["A young man came to Jesus asking what good thing he must do to have eternal life. Jesus directed him towards the commandments. When he asked which ones, Jesus named commands concerning murder, adultery, stealing, false testimony and honour towards parents. He included loving one's neighbour as oneself. The man said he had kept these from his youth and asked what he still lacked.", "Jesus told him that if he wished to be perfect, he should sell his possessions, give to the poor, and come and follow him. He spoke of treasure in heaven. The young man went away sorrowful because he had great possessions. The question he had brought to Jesus had become a decision about what he would carry into the life he was asking for.", "Jesus told the disciples how hard it was for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven. He used the picture of a camel passing through the eye of a needle. The disciples were astonished and asked who could be saved. Jesus looked at them and said that what was impossible for people was possible with God.", "Peter pointed out that they had left everything and followed him. Jesus spoke of what his followers would receive in the renewal and of the inheritance awaiting those who had left things for his name. He ended by saying that many who were first would be last, and the last first. The next story would give that reversal a place in a vineyard."], "lesson": "The man's sorrow reveals how a possession can become something we cannot imagine surrendering. Jesus turns the disciples from confidence in human ability to dependence on God.", "question": "Where does the man's response change, and what does that change reveal?"},
  {"id": 91047, "title": "The Workers Hired at the Last Hour", "subtitle": "Generosity and comparison", "text": "Matthew 20:1–16", "chapters": [[39, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus compared the kingdom of heaven to a householder who went out early to hire workers for his vineyard. He agreed with the first men on a denarius for the day and sent them to work. Later in the morning he saw others standing in the marketplace. He sent them to the vineyard and promised to give what was right. He did the same around noon and again in the afternoon.", "Near the end of the day he found still more men standing there. He asked why they had been idle all day. They answered that no one had hired them. He sent them into his vineyard too. Their time among the vines would be much shorter than that of the men hired first.", "At evening the owner told his steward to call the workers and pay them, beginning with the last hired. The men who had come near the end each received a denarius. Those hired first expected more when they saw this. When they received the same sum, they complained that the others had worked only one hour while they had borne the heat and labour of the day.", "The owner answered one of them that he had paid what they agreed. He could choose to give the last man the same amount, and asked why his generosity provoked resentment. Jesus ended by returning to the reversal he had spoken of before: the last would be first and the first last."], "lesson": "Comparison turned an agreed wage into a grievance. The owner's question invites us to welcome generosity even when someone else receives its benefit.", "question": "What had the first workers been promised, and what made them feel wronged?"},
  {"id": 91048, "title": "A Request for Places and a Cry for Mercy", "subtitle": "Greatness takes the form of service", "text": "Matthew 20:17–34", "chapters": [[39, 20]], "start": 17, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["As Jesus went up to Jerusalem, he took the Twelve aside on the road. He told them the Son of Man would be handed over to the chief priests and scribes, condemned and delivered to the Gentiles. He spoke of mockery, scourging and crucifixion, and said he would rise on the third day.", "Then the mother of the sons of Zebedee came with her sons and asked that they sit at Jesus' right and left in his kingdom. Jesus asked if they could drink his cup. They said they could. He told them they would share in his cup, while the places they requested were for those for whom his Father had prepared them. The other ten were indignant with the brothers.", "Jesus called them together. He described how rulers of the Gentiles exercised authority, then told his followers that greatness among them took the form of service. The Son of Man himself had come to serve and give his life as a ransom for many. His words about what would happen at Jerusalem now had a reason attached to them.", "As they left Jericho, two blind men by the roadside heard Jesus was passing and called for mercy. The crowd tried to silence them. They called louder. Jesus stopped, asked what they wanted and, moved with compassion, touched their eyes. They received sight and followed him. Along the same road where the disciples had heard about service, Jesus had stopped to listen to people the crowd wanted to quiet."], "lesson": "The disciples' dispute about rank is followed by Jesus attending to people a crowd wanted silenced. His conduct shows the kind of greatness he had just described.", "question": "How does Jesus' response to the two men illustrate his teaching about service?"},
  {"id": 91049, "title": "The King Arrives on a Donkey", "subtitle": "Recognising the purpose of worship", "text": "Matthew 21:1–17", "chapters": [[39, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["As Jesus approached Jerusalem, he sent two disciples into a village near the Mount of Olives. They would find a donkey tied there and a colt with her. They were to untie and bring them, saying if challenged that the Lord needed them. They followed his instructions. Matthew connects Jesus' entry with the prophet's picture of a meek king coming to his people riding on a donkey.", "The disciples put garments on the animals, and Jesus rode towards the city. Many spread their garments on the road, while others cut branches from trees and laid them down. The people ahead and behind cried Hosanna to the Son of David. When Jerusalem was stirred and people asked who this was, the crowd identified him as Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee.", "Jesus went into the temple and drove out those buying and selling. He overturned the tables of the money changers and the seats of people selling doves. He spoke of God's house as a house of prayer and said they had made it a den of thieves. Blind and lame people came to him there, and he healed them.", "The chief priests and scribes saw the things he did and heard children crying Hosanna in the temple. They objected. Jesus answered from Scripture about praise coming from the mouths of children. He then left the city and lodged at Bethany."], "lesson": "The entry and the temple visit reveal what matters to this king. He defends the purpose of prayer and receives people who come to him in need.", "question": "What actions and voices does Matthew bring together in the temple after Jesus enters Jerusalem?"},
  {"id": 91050, "title": "The Fig Tree and Two Sons", "subtitle": "Fruit in conduct", "text": "Matthew 21:18–32", "chapters": [[39, 21]], "start": 18, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Returning to the city in the morning, Jesus was hungry. He approached a fig tree by the road and found only leaves. When he told it that it would bear no fruit again, it withered. The disciples wondered how it had happened so suddenly. Jesus answered with teaching about faith and prayer and spoke of what faith could ask of God.", "In the temple, chief priests and elders wanted to know what authority he had and who gave it to him. Jesus asked them a question in return. Did John's baptism come from heaven or from people? They reasoned that if they said it was from heaven, Jesus would ask why they had not believed John. If they said it was from people, they feared the crowd, which regarded John as a prophet. They answered that they did not know.", "Jesus then told them about two sons. Their father asked each to work in the vineyard. The first refused and later changed his mind and went. The second said he would go and did not. Jesus asked which son had done his father's will. They gave the answer themselves: the first.", "Jesus applied it to their response to John. Tax collectors and prostitutes had believed him, while those questioning Jesus still had not changed their minds after seeing that response. The parable gave the leaders a clear example of the change they had refused to make."], "lesson": "The leaders' calculation stands beside a parable that makes obedience concrete. A sincere change of mind can be seen in what a person goes on to do.", "question": "Which son did his father's will, and how does Jesus apply the answer to John's ministry?"},
  {"id": 91051, "title": "The Tenants and the Son", "subtitle": "Answering to the owner", "text": "Matthew 21:33–46", "chapters": [[39, 21]], "start": 33, "end": 46, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus told of a householder who planted a vineyard, set a hedge around it, dug a winepress and built a tower. He leased it to tenants and went away. At harvest he sent servants to receive his fruit. The tenants seized the servants, beating one, killing another and stoning another. The owner sent more servants, and they treated them in the same way.", "Finally the owner sent his son, expecting the tenants to respect him. They recognised him as the heir and saw a chance to take the inheritance for themselves. They seized him, threw him out of the vineyard and killed him. Their conduct made plain what they thought the owner's rights were worth when he was away.", "Jesus asked what the owner would do when he came. His hearers said the owner would judge the tenants severely and lease the vineyard to others who would give him the fruit in season. Jesus then recalled the words of Scripture about the stone rejected by builders becoming the cornerstone, something the Lord had done.", "Jesus spoke of the kingdom being given to people who would produce its fruit. The chief priests and Pharisees understood that he was speaking about them. They wanted to seize him, but held back because the crowds regarded him as a prophet. The men who had answered his question about the vineyard now recognised where he had directed it."], "lesson": "The tenants treated something entrusted to them as their possession. The story presses anyone entrusted with responsibility to consider what fruit belongs to God.", "question": "What do the tenants' responses to the servants and the son reveal about how they viewed the vineyard?"},
  {"id": 91052, "title": "The Wedding Feast", "subtitle": "Taking the invitation seriously", "text": "Matthew 22:1–14", "chapters": [[39, 22]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus told of a king who prepared a wedding feast for his son. When he sent servants to call those already invited, they would not come. He sent others with a fuller announcement. The meal was ready, the animals had been prepared and the guests were to come to the wedding.", "Some made light of the summons and went away, one to his farm and another to his business. Others seized the servants, mistreated them and killed them. The king was angry and judged those responsible. Then he told his servants that the wedding was ready and the first guests had shown themselves unworthy. They were to go to the roads and invite whomever they found.", "The servants gathered people, both bad and good, and the hall filled with guests. When the king came to see them, he noticed a man without a wedding garment. He addressed the man and asked how he had come in without one. The man had no answer. The king ordered his attendants to remove him from the feast.", "Jesus closed by saying that many are called and few chosen. The story carries the reader from people who dismissed the invitation to a full hall with guests drawn from the roads. Even there, the king's question makes the response of a person who has accepted the invitation a matter to consider."], "lesson": "The invitation reaches widely, even beyond the doors first visited. The king's inspection keeps the hearer from treating an invitation as a reason for indifference.", "question": "What different responses to the king do you find in those invited and in the guest questioned inside?"},
  {"id": 91053, "title": "A Coin and a Question About Resurrection", "subtitle": "God's claim and God's power", "text": "Matthew 22:15–33", "chapters": [[39, 22]], "start": 15, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["The Pharisees considered how to trap Jesus in what he said. They sent their disciples with the Herodians. They began with praise for his truthfulness and then asked whether it was lawful to pay tribute to Caesar. Jesus recognised what they were doing and asked them to show him the coin used for the tax.", "They brought him a denarius. He asked whose image and inscription it carried. They answered that they were Caesar's. He told them to give Caesar what belonged to Caesar and God what belonged to God. His hearers marvelled, left him and went away.", "That same day Sadducees, who said there was no resurrection, brought him another question. They described a woman who had been married in turn to seven brothers after each previous husband died, without leaving children. Then the woman died. They asked whose wife she would be in the resurrection. Their question assumed that the arrangements of this life would carry over in the same way.", "Jesus said they had failed to understand both the Scriptures and God's power. He explained that marriage did not continue in that way in the resurrection. Then he appealed to God's words about being the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob and said God was the God of the living. The crowd was astonished at his teaching. Both questions had been framed as traps, and both answers had brought God's authority into view."], "lesson": "Each question tried to confine the answer to its questioner's assumptions. Jesus answered by directing attention to God's claim on people and his power over death.", "question": "Why did Jesus ask about the coin's image before replying to the question about tax?"},
  {"id": 91054, "title": "The Greatest Commandments and David's Lord", "subtitle": "Love at the centre of obedience", "text": "Matthew 22:34–46", "chapters": [[39, 22]], "start": 34, "end": 46, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When the Pharisees heard that Jesus had silenced the Sadducees, they gathered. One of them, a lawyer, asked him which commandment was greatest in the law. Matthew says he asked to test him. Jesus answered by calling for love of the Lord one's God with all the heart, soul and mind. He identified it as the first and greatest commandment.", "He then named a second like it: loving one's neighbour as oneself. He said the law and the prophets hung on these two. The lawyer had asked for one commandment to be named. Jesus gave him the first and showed how closely it was joined to the conduct owed to another person.", "With the Pharisees still gathered, Jesus asked them a question of his own. Whose son was the Christ? They answered that he was David's son. Jesus asked why David, speaking in the Spirit, called him Lord. He cited David's words about the Lord giving that person a place at his right hand until his enemies were put beneath his feet.", "If David called him Lord, Jesus asked, how was he his son? No one was able to answer, and Matthew says they did not dare question him further from that day. The exchange that began with a question about reading the law ends with a question about reading what David said about the Christ."], "lesson": "Love for God and neighbour holds the commands together. Jesus' own question invites the hearers to give closer attention to the identity of the one giving that answer.", "question": "How would the two commands guide you through a decision where your duty to a neighbour is easy to overlook?"},
  {"id": 91055, "title": "The Seats of Honour", "subtitle": "Serving without seeking rank", "text": "Matthew 23:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus turned from the exchanges with his questioners to speak to the crowds and his disciples. He said the scribes and Pharisees sat in Moses' seat. His hearers should pay attention to what they told them to observe, while recognising that their conduct could fail to match their teaching. Jesus pictured them binding heavy burdens and laying them on other people's shoulders without lifting a finger to move them.", "He spoke about the desire to be seen. Some made the cases holding Scripture on their bodies conspicuously broad or made the fringes of their garments wide. They sought the places of honour at feasts and the best seats in synagogues. They enjoyed greetings in the marketplaces and being called Rabbi. Each setting gave people a chance to recognise their importance.", "Jesus then addressed his own disciples. He directed them away from titles that placed them above one another and reminded them that they were brothers with one teacher and one Father in heaven. They were to give Christ his place as their guide. A community hearing this teaching would have to consider how it spoke of those who taught and how those teachers carried themselves.", "Jesus gave them a different way to recognise greatness. The greatest among them would be their servant. He ended by saying that whoever exalted himself would be humbled and whoever humbled himself would be exalted. The teaching begins with burdens carried by ordinary people and ends by asking what those with standing will do for them."], "lesson": "Teaching puts an obligation on the one who teaches. Jesus directs attention to quiet service when titles and public honour begin to matter more.", "question": "Where might a person make a duty heavier for others while declining to help with it?"},
  {"id": 91056, "title": "The Cup, the Tomb and the City", "subtitle": "Justice, mercy and faithfulness", "text": "Matthew 23:13–39", "chapters": [[39, 23]], "start": 13, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus pronounced a series of woes against scribes and Pharisees. He spoke of shutting the kingdom of heaven in people's faces and travelling far to gain a convert without giving him a better way to live. He exposed distinctions they drew between swearing by the temple and its gold, or by the altar and the gift on it. He directed attention back to what made those things holy.", "He spoke of tithes on small herbs alongside larger duties they had neglected: judgement, mercy and faithfulness. He pictured people straining out a tiny gnat and swallowing a camel. Then he compared their outward and inward lives to a cup cleaned outside while greed and excess remained within, and to a tomb made beautiful outside while holding what was unclean.", "Jesus addressed those who decorated the tombs of prophets and said they would never have shared in killing them. He warned that messengers would still be persecuted and killed. The treatment of the people he would send would show whether the honour paid to earlier prophets had changed anything.", "The speech ends in lament. Jesus addressed Jerusalem as the city that killed prophets and stoned those sent to it. He spoke of longing to gather its children like a hen sheltering her brood under her wings, and of their refusal. He spoke of their house being left desolate. The last picture gives the warning a voice of grief."], "lesson": "Outward care can coexist with a neglected inner life. Jesus names justice, mercy and faithfulness plainly, and his lament shows grief over the people he rebukes.", "question": "Which weightier matter did Jesus name that calls for greater attention in your conduct?"},
  {"id": 91057, "title": "The Stones and the Warnings", "subtitle": "Steadiness in troubled times", "text": "Matthew 24:1–28", "chapters": [[39, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus left the temple and the disciples drew his attention to its buildings. He told them a day was coming when one stone would not be left on another. Later he sat on the Mount of Olives, and the disciples approached him privately. They asked when these things would happen and what sign would mark his coming and the end of the age.", "Jesus began by warning them against being deceived. Many would come claiming to be the Christ. There would be wars, reports of wars, famines and earthquakes, yet these would be the beginning of sorrows. His followers would face hatred and betrayal. False prophets would mislead people, and as wrongdoing increased, the love of many would grow cold. He spoke of endurance and of the gospel of the kingdom going out to all nations.", "He then spoke of the desolating sight described by Daniel and the urgency of leaving when it appeared. He pictured a person on a roof with no time to fetch things from the house and someone in a field with no time to return for a garment. He asked his hearers to pray about the circumstances of their flight.", "Finally he warned about reports that the Christ was in the wilderness or in a private room. False signs could make those reports persuasive. He compared the coming of the Son of Man to lightning that crosses the sky. The disciples had asked how to recognise the time. His answer gave them reasons to be discerning under pressure."], "lesson": "Jesus prepares the disciples for pressure and deception. His warnings direct them to endure and keep their love alive when fear makes dramatic claims easy to believe.", "question": "Which dangers in the passage arise within people themselves, as well as around them?"},
  {"id": 91058, "title": "The Unannounced Hour", "subtitle": "Readiness in ordinary responsibilities", "text": "Matthew 24:29–51", "chapters": [[39, 24]], "start": 29, "end": 51, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus went on to describe signs in the heavens after the distress and the coming of the Son of Man with power and glory. He spoke of angels gathering his elect. Then he pointed to a fig tree. When its branches become tender and put out leaves, people recognise that summer is near. His words would remain even though heaven and earth passed away.", "Jesus said the day and hour were unknown. He recalled Noah's time, when people carried on eating, drinking and marrying until the flood came. He pictured people side by side at work and yet met by different outcomes. The usual tasks of the day could give little warning of what was about to interrupt them.", "A householder who knew the hour a thief would come would keep watch. Jesus called his disciples to be ready because the Son of Man would come at an hour they did not expect. He then pictured a servant appointed to give the people of a household their food in season. The master would find that servant blessed if he was still doing the work when he returned.", "Another servant told himself that the master was delayed and used the time to mistreat his fellow servants and indulge himself. His master returned when he did not expect him. Jesus gives the reader two ways of living in the interval: carrying out the responsibility at hand or using the delay as permission to neglect it."], "lesson": "Jesus gives readiness an ordinary shape by describing work that a faithful servant does while waiting. Uncertainty about the hour gives present duties greater weight.", "question": "What is the faithful servant doing when the master returns?"},
  {"id": 91059, "title": "The Lamps at Midnight", "subtitle": "Preparing before the call comes", "text": "Matthew 25:1–13", "chapters": [[39, 25]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus said the kingdom of heaven would be like ten young women who took lamps and went out to meet a bridegroom. Five were wise and five foolish. All took lamps. The wise also took oil in vessels. The difference in what they carried would matter later, when there was little time to put it right.", "The bridegroom took longer than they expected. All of them grew drowsy and slept. At midnight someone cried that the bridegroom was coming and that they should go out to meet him. All ten got up and put their lamps in order. The foolish saw that their lamps were going out and asked the others for some oil.", "The wise answered that there might not be enough for all of them. They told the others to go and buy some. While they were away, the bridegroom arrived. Those who were ready went in with him to the wedding and the door was shut.", "Later the other women came and asked for the door to be opened. The bridegroom answered that he did not know them. Jesus concluded by telling his hearers to watch because they did not know the day or hour. All ten had expected the same bridegroom and all had slept during the delay. Their different outcomes came from what they had brought while there was still time to prepare."], "lesson": "The delay exposed a difference in preparation. Jesus' call to watch asks hearers to take responsibility while there is still time to act.", "question": "What did the delay reveal that was already true when the women set out?"},
  {"id": 91060, "title": "The Servants and the Entrusted Money", "subtitle": "Faithfulness with what is in your hands", "text": "Matthew 25:14–30", "chapters": [[39, 25]], "start": 14, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus told of a man who was going on a journey. He called his servants and entrusted his possessions to them. He gave one five talents, another two and another one, to each according to his ability. Then he left. The two servants who had received more put what they had to work and each gained an equal amount in addition. The third dug a hole and hid his master's money.", "After a long time the master returned to settle accounts. The man with five talents brought five more. The master called him a good and faithful servant and invited him to share his joy. The man with two brought two more and received the same welcome. Their responsibilities had differed, but the master commended each for being faithful with what he had received.", "The last servant came with the single talent. He described his master as a hard man and said he had been afraid. That was why he had hidden the money. He handed it back. The master asked why, given what the servant claimed to know about him, he had not at least entrusted the money to bankers so it could gain interest.", "The master took the talent from him and gave it to the servant with ten. The unprofitable servant was put outside. The account follows what each servant did during the master's absence and what those choices looked like once he returned."], "lesson": "The first two received unequal amounts and the same commendation. The parable turns attention to faithful use of opportunity and exposes the inactivity that fear can excuse.", "question": "What opportunity or responsibility are you tempted to bury because the result is uncertain?"},
  {"id": 91061, "title": "The King Remembers the Least", "subtitle": "Mercy in concrete acts", "text": "Matthew 25:31–46", "chapters": [[39, 25]], "start": 31, "end": 46, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus described the Son of Man coming in glory with the angels and sitting on his throne. The nations would be gathered before him, and he would separate people as a shepherd separates sheep from goats. The king would invite the people on his right to inherit the kingdom prepared for them. He would remember being hungry, thirsty, a stranger, in need of clothing, sick and in prison, and receiving their care.", "Those people would ask when they had seen him in any of those conditions. The king would answer that what they had done to one of the least of his brothers, they had done to him. They had been able to see the need before them and act on it without knowing the significance the king would give their acts.", "He would then address those on his left. He named the same conditions and said he had received no food, drink, welcome, clothing or visit from them. They too would ask when they had seen him in need and failed to serve him. His answer would put the people whose needs they had passed by at the centre of the judgement.", "Jesus ended by speaking of punishment and eternal life. The two groups hear the same list of ordinary needs. Each asks when it met the king. His answer gives weight to what happened when one person had occasion to care for another."], "lesson": "The judgement brings ordinary acts of care into view. Jesus gives weight to needs that can be overlooked because the person in front of us seems unimportant.", "question": "Which act of care named by the king could you offer to someone this week?"},
  {"id": 91062, "title": "An Anointing in Bethany and a Bargain", "subtitle": "Two ways of valuing Jesus", "text": "Matthew 26:1–16", "chapters": [[39, 26]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After Jesus finished teaching, he told the disciples that Passover was two days away and that the Son of Man would be handed over to be crucified. The chief priests, scribes and elders gathered at the house of Caiaphas, the high priest. They considered how to seize Jesus by stealth and kill him. They wanted to avoid doing it during the feast for fear of a disturbance among the people.", "In Bethany Jesus was at the house of Simon the leper. A woman came with an alabaster jar of very costly ointment and poured it on his head while he reclined at the table. The disciples were indignant and called it waste. They said the ointment could have been sold for a large sum and the money given to the poor.", "Jesus asked why they were troubling the woman. He said she had done a good work for him. They would always have poor people with them, while his own time with them was limited. He connected what she had done to his burial and said her act would be remembered wherever the gospel was preached.", "Then Judas Iscariot, one of the Twelve, went to the chief priests and asked what they would give him for handing Jesus over. They agreed on thirty pieces of silver. From then on he looked for an opportunity. Matthew moves directly from a gift whose cost the disciples questioned to the sum one of them accepted for betrayal."], "lesson": "Matthew places a costly gift alongside a price accepted for betrayal. The two scenes ask what our choices reveal about the value we give Jesus.", "question": "How does reading the bargain immediately after the anointing sharpen your understanding of both events?"},
  {"id": 91063, "title": "The Passover Table and Peter's Promise", "subtitle": "The covenant and human weakness", "text": "Matthew 26:17–35", "chapters": [[39, 26]], "start": 17, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["On the first day of the feast of unleavened bread, the disciples asked Jesus where they should prepare the Passover. He sent them into the city to a particular man and told them what to say about keeping the feast at his house. They went and prepared it. When evening came, Jesus sat at the table with the Twelve.", "As they ate, Jesus said that one of them would betray him. They were deeply troubled and began asking one by one whether it was them. He spoke of the one who dipped with him in the dish. The Son of Man would go as Scripture said, but Jesus pronounced woe upon the betrayer. Judas asked whether he was the one, and Jesus answered him.", "While they were eating, Jesus took bread, blessed and broke it and gave it to them to eat, speaking of his body. Then he took a cup, gave thanks and gave it to them. He spoke of his covenant blood poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins. He said he would drink the fruit of the vine with them again in his Father's kingdom.", "They sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus told them they would fall away that night, recalling the words about the shepherd being struck and the sheep scattered. He also spoke of going before them to Galilee after he was raised. Peter insisted he would remain loyal. Jesus said Peter would deny him three times before the rooster crowed. Peter and the others protested."], "lesson": "Jesus gives the cup in a room where betrayal and weakness are already present. Peter's promise invites us to pray for the faithfulness we are quick to claim.", "question": "What does the passage reveal about Jesus' purpose and Peter's confidence in himself?"},
  {"id": 91064, "title": "Prayer and Arrest in Gethsemane", "subtitle": "Obedience in anguish", "text": "Matthew 26:36–56", "chapters": [[39, 26]], "start": 36, "end": 56, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus came with the disciples to Gethsemane and asked them to sit while he went to pray. He took Peter and the two sons of Zebedee farther with him and began to be deeply distressed. He told them his soul was sorrowful to the point of death and asked them to stay and watch with him. Then he went a little farther, fell on his face and asked his Father whether the cup might pass, yielding the matter to his Father's will.", "When he returned, he found the three asleep. He asked Peter whether they could watch with him for one hour and told them to watch and pray against temptation. He went away to pray again, now saying that if the cup could not pass without his drinking it, the Father's will should be done. He found them asleep again and went away a third time to pray.", "By the time he returned, Judas was approaching with a crowd carrying swords and clubs. Judas had arranged a kiss as the sign identifying Jesus. He greeted him and kissed him, and the crowd seized him. One of the people with Jesus drew a sword and struck the high priest's servant, cutting off his ear.", "Jesus told him to put away the sword. He spoke of the help he could ask of his Father and of the Scriptures being fulfilled through what was happening. He reminded the crowd that he had taught openly in the temple. As the arrest went ahead, the disciples left him and fled."], "lesson": "Jesus brings his distress before the Father and yields to him. The disciples' sleep gives urgency to his instruction to watch and pray before the trial reaches them.", "question": "What does Jesus pray for himself, and what does he urge the disciples to pray about?"},
  {"id": 91065, "title": "Inside the Hearing and Outside by the Fire", "subtitle": "Truth under pressure", "text": "Matthew 26:57–75", "chapters": [[39, 26]], "start": 57, "end": 75, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Those who had seized Jesus took him to Caiaphas, where the scribes and elders had gathered. Peter followed at a distance and sat with the servants in the courtyard to see what would happen. Inside, the chief priests and council sought testimony against Jesus to put him to death. Many false witnesses came, and finally two spoke about his words concerning the temple.", "The high priest asked Jesus what answer he had to the witnesses. Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest put him under oath and asked whether he was the Christ, the Son of God. Jesus answered and spoke of the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of power and coming with the clouds. The high priest tore his clothes. The council declared him deserving of death, and people spat on and struck him.", "Outside, a servant girl told Peter that he had been with Jesus of Galilee. Peter denied it before the people there. He went towards the entrance, and another girl said to those nearby that he had been with Jesus of Nazareth. This time he denied it with an oath. Later others said his way of speaking gave him away. Peter began to curse and swear that he did not know the man.", "The rooster crowed. Peter remembered how Jesus had told him he would deny him three times before it crowed. He went out and wept bitterly. Matthew leaves the courtyard with Peter remembering words he had been sure would never come true."], "lesson": "Matthew places Jesus' answer alongside Peter's denials. Peter's tears begin with remembering the word he had dismissed earlier that night.", "question": "What brought Peter to recognise what he had done?"},
  {"id": 91066, "title": "Silver Returned and a Prisoner Released", "subtitle": "Responsibility under pressure", "text": "Matthew 27:1–26", "chapters": [[39, 27]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["In the morning the chief priests and elders agreed to put Jesus to death. They bound him and delivered him to Pilate the governor. When Judas saw that Jesus had been condemned, he brought back the thirty pieces of silver and confessed that he had betrayed innocent blood. The chief priests and elders told him that was his concern. Judas threw the money down in the temple and left. He died by suicide.", "The priests decided the silver could not go into the treasury because it was the price of blood. They used it to buy the potter's field as a burial place for strangers. Matthew relates this to words spoken through the prophet Jeremiah.", "Before Pilate, Jesus was asked whether he was the King of the Jews. He answered that question, then made no reply to the accusations made against him. Pilate was amazed. At the feast the governor customarily released a prisoner of the crowd's choice. He offered them Barabbas or Jesus. While he sat on the judgement seat, his wife sent him a warning based on a troubling dream.", "The chief priests and elders persuaded the crowds to ask for Barabbas and demand Jesus' death. Pilate asked what wrong Jesus had done, but the cries grew louder. Seeing a disturbance forming, he washed his hands in front of the crowd and declared himself innocent of the blood. Then he released Barabbas, had Jesus scourged and handed him over to be crucified."], "lesson": "The priests' reply to Judas and Pilate's gesture both attempt to put responsibility elsewhere. The sequence invites us to face what our decisions actually set in motion.", "question": "What does Pilate do after washing his hands, and why does the order of those actions matter?"},
  {"id": 91067, "title": "The Mocked King on the Cross", "subtitle": "Faithfulness through humiliation", "text": "Matthew 27:27–44", "chapters": [[39, 27]], "start": 27, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["The governor's soldiers took Jesus inside and gathered their company around him. They put a scarlet robe on him, made a crown of thorns and placed a reed in his right hand. Kneeling before him, they mocked him as King of the Jews. They spat on him and struck him. When they had finished, they put his own clothes back on him and led him away.", "On the way they compelled Simon, a man from Cyrene, to carry the cross. At Golgotha, a place whose name Matthew explains as the place of a skull, they offered Jesus a drink that he tasted and refused. They crucified him and cast lots for his clothes. Above his head they set the charge calling him Jesus the King of the Jews. Two others were crucified with him, one on either side.", "People passing by shook their heads and challenged him to save himself. They recalled words about the temple and told him to come down if he was God's Son. Chief priests, scribes and elders also mocked him. They said he had saved others but could not save himself, and demanded that he come down if they were to believe him. They spoke of his trust in God as another reason to taunt him.", "Even the men crucified beside him joined in. The title the soldiers had used in mockery now stood above his head in writing, and different people below were using it to say what they thought he must do."], "lesson": "The mockers treated escape as the proof of who Jesus was. Matthew has already shown Jesus choosing the path that would bring him to this place.", "question": "How do the mockers' challenges sound in light of what Jesus prayed in Gethsemane?"},
  {"id": 91068, "title": "The Torn Curtain and the Sealed Tomb", "subtitle": "Witness in the hour of grief", "text": "Matthew 27:45–66", "chapters": [[39, 27]], "start": 45, "end": 66, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["From noon until mid-afternoon darkness lay over the land. Jesus cried out to God in words some standing there thought called for Elijah. Someone brought him sour wine, and others waited to see whether Elijah would come. Jesus cried out again and died. Matthew describes the temple curtain tearing from top to bottom, an earthquake, rocks splitting and graves opening. He also records that after the resurrection people came out of those graves and appeared in the city.", "The centurion and those watching with him were frightened by what they saw and called Jesus God's Son. Women who had followed him from Galilee and served him were watching from a distance. Matthew names Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joses, and the mother of the sons of Zebedee.", "At evening Joseph of Arimathea, a wealthy man who had become Jesus' disciple, asked Pilate for the body. Pilate ordered it released. Joseph wrapped it in clean linen and laid it in his own new tomb, which had been cut in rock. He rolled a large stone to the entrance and went away. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary remained sitting opposite the tomb.", "The next day the chief priests and Pharisees asked Pilate to secure the tomb. They recalled Jesus' words about rising after three days and feared the disciples might take the body. Pilate told them to make it as secure as they could. They sealed the stone and set a guard."], "lesson": "Joseph and the women remain present after public disgrace and death. The passage gives quiet acts of care and witness a place beside events of great public consequence.", "question": "Who remained close enough to witness the burial, and what did Joseph do?"},
  {"id": 91069, "title": "The Women at the Tomb", "subtitle": "Fear, joy and truthful witness", "text": "Matthew 28:1–15", "chapters": [[39, 28]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After the Sabbath, as the first day of the week was dawning, Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to see the tomb. There was an earthquake. An angel of the Lord came down, rolled back the stone and sat on it. The guards were terrified and became like dead men. The angel addressed the women and told them not to be afraid.", "He knew they were seeking Jesus who had been crucified. Jesus was not there, he said, because he had risen as he had said. The women were invited to see where he had lain. Then they were told to hurry to the disciples with the news and say that Jesus would go ahead of them to Galilee, where they would see him.", "The women left the tomb with fear and great joy and ran to tell the disciples. Jesus met them on the way. They came to him, took hold of his feet and worshipped him. He also told them not to be afraid and sent them to tell his brothers to go to Galilee.", "Meanwhile some of the guards went into the city and told the chief priests what had happened. The leaders consulted and gave them a large sum of money to say the disciples stole the body while they slept. They promised to deal with the governor if necessary. The guards did as they were told. Matthew follows the news along those two roads, to the disciples and to the leaders."], "lesson": "The women carried their message with fear and great joy. The payment to the guards shows the pressure that can surround the telling of what one has witnessed.", "question": "What did the women see and hear before Jesus sent them on their way?"},
  {"id": 91070, "title": "The Last Meeting on the Mountain", "subtitle": "Making disciples with Jesus' presence", "text": "Matthew 28:16–20", "chapters": [[39, 28]], "start": 16, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["The eleven disciples went into Galilee and came to the mountain Jesus had appointed. They had been sent there with a promise that they would see him. When they saw him, they worshipped him. Matthew also records that some doubted. This last scene includes the different responses of people who had come to the same place.", "Jesus came to them and spoke first about his authority. All authority in heaven and on earth had been given to him. That was the basis on which he sent them out. They were to go and make disciples of all nations. The work was to reach people beyond the circle that had stood with him in Galilee.", "He told them to baptise in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. He also told them to teach people to observe all he had commanded. Earlier in the Gospel these disciples had heard his teaching, seen his acts and at times been corrected by him. Now they were being given responsibility to teach others to practise what he had taught.", "Jesus ended with a promise that he would be with them always, to the end of the age. Matthew began by naming Jesus Emmanuel, God with us. The Gospel closes with Jesus assuring the people he sends out that his presence will go with them as they do the work he gave them."], "lesson": "Jesus joins his authority to a command and a promise. Teaching people to practise what he commanded takes sustained attention, and the work rests on his continuing presence.", "question": "Which parts of Jesus' commission require a relationship that continues after a person first hears the message?"},
  {"id": 91071, "title": "The Road, the River and the Wilderness", "subtitle": "The beginning of the good news", "text": "Mark 1:1–13", "chapters": [[40, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Mark opens by calling what follows the beginning of the good news of Jesus Christ, the Son of God. He recalls the prophetic promise of a messenger who would prepare the Lord's way. Then he takes the reader to the wilderness, where John called people to repent and be baptised for the forgiveness of sins. People came out from Judea and Jerusalem, confessed their sins and were baptised in the Jordan.", "John wore camel's hair with a leather belt and ate locusts and wild honey. Mark gives those details and then turns to what John said about the person coming after him. Someone mightier was on the way. John did not regard himself as worthy even to stoop and untie his sandals. He baptised with water, but the coming one would baptise with the Holy Spirit.", "Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee and was baptised by John in the Jordan. Coming up out of the water, he saw the heavens opening and the Spirit descending like a dove. A voice from heaven addressed him as the beloved Son in whom the Father was well pleased.", "The Spirit immediately drove him into the wilderness, where he remained forty days and was tempted by Satan. Mark mentions the wild animals and the angels who ministered to him. He gives no account here of the tempter's words. He carries the reader directly from the Father's declaration at the river to the Son's testing in the wilderness."], "lesson": "The Father's declaration precedes the wilderness. A place of testing can come directly after a clear word of assurance.", "question": "What does Mark tell you about Jesus before he records his first public words?"},
  {"id": 91072, "title": "From the Nets to the Doorway", "subtitle": "Authority that reaches ordinary homes", "text": "Mark 1:14–34", "chapters": [[40, 1]], "start": 14, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["After John was put in prison, Jesus came to Galilee proclaiming God's good news. He said that the time had been fulfilled and God's kingdom had drawn near. People were to repent and believe. Beside the sea he saw Simon and Andrew casting a net and called them to follow him. They left their nets. Farther on, James and John left the boat where they had been mending nets with their father and the hired men.", "They went to Capernaum. On the Sabbath Jesus taught in the synagogue, where people were astonished because he spoke with authority. A man with an unclean spirit cried out and called him the Holy One of God. Jesus ordered the spirit to be silent and come out. It left the man, and the people began asking one another what kind of teaching they had heard.", "Jesus and the four men went from the synagogue to the home of Simon and Andrew. Simon's mother-in-law was lying sick with a fever. They told Jesus about her, and he took her by the hand and lifted her up. The fever left her, and she began serving them.", "At sunset people brought those who were ill or afflicted by demons. Mark describes the town gathered at the door. Jesus healed many and would not allow the demons to speak. The men called from the shore had seen him teach in public and then attend to the needs of a household."], "lesson": "Mark follows Jesus from public teaching to a sickbed in a home. His authority has a personal reach, and the healed woman answers with service.", "question": "What does Jesus do for Simon's mother-in-law, and what is her first recorded response?"},
  {"id": 91073, "title": "The Place of Prayer and the Man Outside", "subtitle": "Compassion guided by purpose", "text": "Mark 1:35–45", "chapters": [[40, 1]], "start": 35, "end": 45, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Before daylight Jesus got up and went out to a solitary place. He prayed there. Simon and his companions went looking for him, and when they found him they said everyone was seeking him. The night before, the town had brought its sick to the door. Now the disciples reported that people still wanted him.", "Jesus said they should go on to the nearby towns so he could preach there as well. He named preaching as the purpose for which he had come out. He travelled through Galilee, preaching in the synagogues and driving out demons. His answer to Simon took them beyond the place where people already knew to seek him.", "A man with leprosy came, knelt and asked to be made clean if Jesus was willing. Jesus had compassion, stretched out his hand and touched him. He said he was willing. The leprosy left him immediately. Jesus charged him to say nothing about it and sent him to show himself to the priest and offer what Moses had commanded.", "The man went out and spread the news freely. As a result Jesus could no longer go openly into a town. He stayed in places outside, and people came to him from every direction. Mark begins the passage with Jesus withdrawing to pray. It ends with him outside the towns again, this time with people knowing where to find him."], "lesson": "The search of the crowd did not settle where Jesus would go. Prayer and the purpose for which he came help us read his decision to move on.", "question": "How did Jesus respond when told that everyone was looking for him?"},
  {"id": 91074, "title": "Through the Roof and Around the Table", "subtitle": "Forgiveness and fellowship", "text": "Mark 2:1–17", "chapters": [[40, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["When Jesus returned to Capernaum, word spread that he was in a house. So many people came that there was no room, even at the door, while he spoke to them. Four men carrying a paralysed man could not bring him through. They opened the roof above Jesus and lowered the man on his bed into the room.", "Jesus saw their faith and addressed the man as a son. He said his sins were forgiven. Some scribes sitting there reasoned inwardly that this was blasphemy, since only God can forgive sins. Jesus knew what they were thinking and asked which was easier to say: that the man's sins were forgiven, or that he should rise and walk. To show his authority to forgive on earth, he told the man to rise, take up his bed and go home.", "The man got up at once and went out in front of them all. The people were amazed and glorified God. Later Jesus went out beside the sea and taught there. Passing a tax booth, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus and called him to follow. Levi stood up and went with him.", "At a meal, many tax collectors and sinners sat with Jesus and his disciples. The scribes and Pharisees questioned the disciples about his company. Jesus heard and answered with the picture of a physician whose work is with the sick. The people who had been waiting to see what his authority meant now had another occasion to see whom he had come to help."], "lesson": "The four men make their faith visible through steady trouble on another person's behalf. Jesus brings the man's need of forgiveness into view before he sends him home walking.", "question": "What do the four men recognise about the need to bring their friend to Jesus?"},
  {"id": 91075, "title": "A Wedding and a Walk Through Grain", "subtitle": "Receiving God's gifts in their purpose", "text": "Mark 2:18–28", "chapters": [[40, 2]], "start": 18, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["John's disciples and the Pharisees were fasting. People asked Jesus why his own disciples were doing differently. Jesus asked them to picture a wedding. As long as the bridegroom was with the wedding guests, they could not fast. The time would come when he was taken away, and then they would fast. His answer gave them a reason to consider the occasion before comparing what the two groups were doing.", "He spoke of someone sewing a patch of new cloth onto an old garment. The new material could pull away from the old and make a worse tear. He gave the same listeners a second picture. New wine put into old skins could burst them, with both wine and skin lost. Fresh skins make room for the new wine.", "On a Sabbath his disciples passed through grainfields and began plucking the heads of grain. The Pharisees asked why they were doing something they considered unlawful on the Sabbath. Jesus asked them whether they had read about David when he and his companions were hungry. David went into the house of God, ate the bread normally reserved for priests and gave some to his companions.", "Jesus said that the Sabbath was made for people and that the Son of Man was Lord of the Sabbath. The first question had been about when it was fitting to fast. The second gave Jesus occasion to speak about the need of hungry people and the purpose of a day God had given them."], "lesson": "Jesus draws attention to the occasion for a practice and the purpose for which God gave it. Those questions can expose needless severity in our treatment of people.", "question": "How does Jesus use David's need to answer the objection over the grain?"},
  {"id": 91076, "title": "A Hand Restored and Twelve Appointed", "subtitle": "Being with Jesus before being sent", "text": "Mark 3:1–19", "chapters": [[40, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus entered a synagogue where a man had a withered hand. Some people watched to see whether Jesus would heal on the Sabbath, hoping to accuse him. Jesus told the man to stand where they could all see him. He then asked whether it was lawful on the Sabbath to do good or harm, to save a life or kill. They remained silent.", "Jesus looked around at them with anger and grieved over their hardness of heart. He told the man to stretch out his hand. The man did, and it was restored. The Pharisees went out and began consulting with the Herodians about how to destroy Jesus. Mark places their plans alongside the healing that had brought the man's need into the open.", "Jesus withdrew to the sea with his disciples. People came from Galilee, Jerusalem and other regions when they heard what he had done. He asked for a boat to be kept ready because the crowd pressed towards him. People with diseases reached to touch him. Unclean spirits cried out that he was God's Son, and he told them not to make him known.", "Then Jesus went up a mountain and called those he wanted. He appointed twelve to be with him and to be sent out to preach and exercise authority over demons. Mark names them, including Simon, whom Jesus called Peter, and James and John, whom he called sons of thunder. The work for which they were appointed began with being with him."], "lesson": "Jesus puts a man's need in the centre of the room. When he appoints the Twelve, he makes being with him part of their calling before their public work.", "question": "What two purposes does Mark name when Jesus appoints the Twelve?"},
  {"id": 91077, "title": "Voices Outside the House", "subtitle": "The family that does God's will", "text": "Mark 3:20–35", "chapters": [[40, 3]], "start": 20, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["When Jesus came into a house, the crowd gathered again, so that he and the disciples could not even eat. People close to him heard what was happening and went out to take charge of him, saying he was beside himself. Scribes who had come from Jerusalem offered a different account. They said he had Beelzebub and drove out demons by the ruler of demons.", "Jesus called them and answered in pictures. How could Satan drive out Satan? A kingdom divided against itself could not stand, and the same was true of a house. A person entering a strong man's house to take his goods must first bind the strong man. Jesus' question required the scribes to reckon with what would happen if their own explanation were true.", "Jesus then spoke about forgiveness and gave a serious warning concerning blasphemy against the Holy Spirit. Mark tells the reader why the warning came in this exchange: they had been saying Jesus had an unclean spirit. Their words concerned the source of something they had seen him do.", "His mother and brothers arrived and stood outside, sending word in for him. The crowd sitting around him told him they were looking for him. Jesus asked who his mother and brothers were, then looked at the people around him. He named whoever did God's will as his brother, sister and mother. The house that had been too crowded for a meal became the setting for that description of his family."], "lesson": "Outsiders were busy explaining Jesus, while people around him were listening to him. The family he names is marked by a response to God's will.", "question": "How does Jesus' picture of a divided house answer the scribes' charge?"},
  {"id": 91078, "title": "The Seed and the Lamp", "subtitle": "Attending to what you hear", "text": "Mark 4:1–25", "chapters": [[40, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus began teaching beside the sea, and the crowd grew so large that he sat in a boat while the people remained on shore. He told them to listen. A sower went out scattering seed. Some fell on the path and the birds ate it. Some fell on shallow, rocky ground and sprang up quickly, then withered in the sun because it had no root. Some fell among thorns and was choked. Seed in good soil grew and yielded a crop.", "Afterwards, when Jesus was with those around him and the Twelve, they asked about the parables. He spoke of the mystery of God's kingdom and recalled the distinction between seeing or hearing and actually taking in what was said. Then he explained the sower's work. The seed was the word.", "The seed on the path pictured people from whom Satan quickly took away what they heard. Rocky ground pictured those who received it gladly until trouble or persecution came. Cares, riches and other desires choked the word in others. In good soil people heard, received and bore fruit. Several people could hear the same words, yet what followed could differ.", "Jesus went on to speak about a lamp, which belongs on a stand, and about things hidden that would be made known. He told them to pay attention to what they heard. The passage begins with a call to listen and returns to it after explaining what can happen to the word."], "lesson": "The parable gives us more to examine than our first response to the word. It asks whether we are making room for it to grow after that first hearing.", "question": "What do the thorns represent, and where could they be choking what you have heard?"},
  {"id": 91079, "title": "A Growing Field and a Quiet Sea", "subtitle": "Faith when you cannot control events", "text": "Mark 4:26–41", "chapters": [[40, 4]], "start": 26, "end": 41, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus compared God's kingdom to a person scattering seed on the ground. He went about the ordinary cycle of sleeping and rising while the seed grew, though he could not explain how. The earth produced first the blade, then the head and then the grain. When the crop was ready, the farmer brought in the harvest. Jesus also spoke of a small mustard seed growing large enough for birds to shelter under its branches.", "That evening Jesus told the disciples to cross to the other side of the lake. They left the crowd and took him in the boat where he had been teaching. Other boats went with them. A great storm arose, and waves beat into the boat until it was filling. Jesus was at the stern asleep on a cushion.", "The disciples woke him and asked whether he cared that they were perishing. He got up and rebuked the wind and told the sea to be still. The wind stopped, and there was a great calm. Then he asked the disciples why they were so afraid and why they had so little faith.", "They were greatly afraid and asked one another who he was, since the wind and sea obeyed him. Earlier they had heard about seed growing while a farmer slept. Now they had watched Jesus rise from sleep and do something they could not do for themselves."], "lesson": "The farmer cannot account for every stage of the growth, and the disciples cannot command the storm. The two scenes ask where trust belongs when events are beyond our control.", "question": "What did the disciples' question assume about Jesus while he was asleep?"},
  {"id": 91080, "title": "The Man Who Wanted to Go With Jesus", "subtitle": "Witness starts at home", "text": "Mark 5:1–20", "chapters": [[40, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["After the crossing, Jesus stepped out of the boat and a man came from among the tombs to meet him. The man lived there and could no longer be held even with chains. He had broken the restraints people put on him. Night and day he cried out and injured himself with stones. When he saw Jesus, he ran and fell before him. Jesus commanded the unclean spirit to come out.", "Asked its name, the spirit answered Legion, because there were many. The spirits begged Jesus to let them enter a herd of pigs feeding nearby. He allowed it. The herd rushed down the steep bank into the water and drowned. The herdsmen fled and told people in the town and countryside what had happened.", "Those who came out saw the man sitting down, clothed and in his right mind. They also heard the account of what had happened to him and to the pigs. They were afraid and asked Jesus to leave their district. When Jesus got into the boat, the man who had been delivered asked to go with him.", "Jesus gave him a different place to serve. He sent him home to his own people to tell them what the Lord had done for him and how he had shown him mercy. The man went through the Decapolis telling what Jesus had done. People marvelled at the report carried by the man who had once lived among the tombs."], "lesson": "The restored man received a task close to home. Telling people who knew his former condition gave them a concrete account of mercy.", "question": "What did Jesus ask the man to tell his own people?"},
  {"id": 91081, "title": "Two Daughters in Need", "subtitle": "Jesus receives the hidden sufferer", "text": "Mark 5:21–43", "chapters": [[40, 5]], "start": 21, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["When Jesus crossed the lake again, a crowd gathered around him. Jairus, a ruler of the synagogue, fell at his feet and pleaded for him to come. His little daughter was near death. Jesus went with him, and the crowd pressed around them on the way.", "Among the people was a woman who had been bleeding for twelve years. She had endured much under several physicians and spent all she had, yet her condition had grown worse. Hearing about Jesus, she reached through the crowd and touched his garment. The bleeding stopped. Jesus stopped too and asked who had touched him. His disciples pointed to the crowd pressing on him, but Jesus looked around. The woman came trembling and told him the whole truth. He called her daughter and sent her away in peace.", "While Jesus was still speaking, people arrived from Jairus's house to say the girl had died. They asked why he should trouble the teacher further. Jesus told Jairus not to be afraid, but to believe, and went on with Peter, James and John. At the house people were weeping loudly. When Jesus spoke of the child as sleeping, they laughed at him.", "Jesus took the girl's parents and the three disciples into the room. Taking her by the hand, he told her to rise. She got up and walked. Jesus gave strict instructions about making the event known and told those there to give her something to eat. The account ends with the people in the room astonished and with an ordinary need for them to attend to."], "lesson": "Jesus addresses one sufferer in a crowd and remembers an ordinary need after raising another. Care can include attention to details others consider too small to notice.", "question": "What did Jesus ask people to do for the girl after she rose?"},
  {"id": 91082, "title": "The Hometown and the Roads Beyond", "subtitle": "Witness amid rejection", "text": "Mark 6:1–13", "chapters": [[40, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus returned to his own country with the disciples. When the Sabbath came, he taught in the synagogue. Many who heard him were astonished and asked where he had received this wisdom and how he did such works. They knew him as the carpenter, Mary's son, and they named his brothers and spoke of his sisters living among them. What they knew of his household shaped the way they heard him, and they took offence.", "Jesus said that a prophet could be without honour in his own country, among his relatives and in his own house. Mark says Jesus laid hands on a few sick people and healed them there, and marvelled at their unbelief. He continued teaching in the villages around them.", "He called the Twelve and began sending them out in pairs. He gave them authority over unclean spirits and instructed them to take a staff but no bread, bag or money. They could wear sandals and were to travel without a spare tunic. When a household received them, they were to stay there until they left that place.", "If people would neither receive nor hear them, they were to leave and shake the dust from their feet as a testimony. The disciples went out and preached repentance, drove out demons and anointed and healed many sick people. The chapter moves from a town where Jesus met offence into villages where others were given a chance to hear."], "lesson": "Rejection in a familiar place did not end the work. Jesus trained the Twelve to minister together and to handle refusals without getting stuck in them.", "question": "What did Jesus instruct the Twelve to do when a place would not receive them?"},
  {"id": 91083, "title": "Herod's Banquet and John's Death", "subtitle": "The cost of pleasing an audience", "text": "Mark 6:14–29", "chapters": [[40, 6]], "start": 14, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Reports about Jesus reached Herod because his name had become widely known. People offered different explanations. Some thought Elijah had come, and others thought Jesus was a prophet like those of earlier times. Herod believed John the Baptist, whom he had beheaded, had risen. Mark then explains what had happened to John.", "Herod had seized and imprisoned him because John told him it was unlawful to have Herodias, the wife of his brother Philip. Herodias wanted to kill John, but could not do so. Herod feared John, knowing him to be a just and holy man, and kept him safe. Even though John's words concerned his own conduct, Herod listened to him gladly.", "An opportunity came at Herod's birthday banquet, where he entertained his officials and leading men of Galilee. Herodias's daughter danced and pleased those at the table. Herod swore to give her whatever she asked, up to half his kingdom. She went out to consult her mother, then returned and asked for John's head on a dish.", "Herod was deeply sorry, yet he gave the order because of his oaths and the guests. John was killed in prison, and what the girl requested was brought to her. She took it to her mother. John's disciples heard, came for his body and laid it in a tomb. The man Herod had listened to was dead because Herod placed another set of voices first."], "lesson": "Listening to a truthful person can coexist with refusing to act on what he says. Herod's decision shows what can happen when approval in a room matters more than conscience.", "question": "How did Herod's opinion of John differ from what he finally chose to do?"},
  {"id": 91084, "title": "Bread for the Crowd and Jesus on the Water", "subtitle": "Remembering the help already given", "text": "Mark 6:30–56", "chapters": [[40, 6]], "start": 30, "end": 56, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["The apostles returned and told Jesus what they had done and taught. There were so many people coming and going that they had no time to eat. Jesus invited them to come apart and rest. They went by boat to a secluded place, but people recognised them, ran there on foot from the towns and arrived ahead of them.", "Jesus saw the crowd and had compassion because they were like sheep without a shepherd. He taught them. Late in the day the disciples wanted to send people out to buy food. Jesus told the disciples to feed them. When they found five loaves and two fish, he had the people sit in groups on the grass, blessed and broke the food and gave it to the disciples to distribute. All ate, and twelve baskets of pieces were gathered.", "Jesus sent the disciples away in the boat while he dismissed the crowd, then went to the mountain to pray. That night he saw them struggling against the wind. He came to them walking on the sea. They thought they saw a spirit and cried out. He told them who he was and spoke courage to them. When he got into the boat the wind stopped.", "Mark connects their astonishment with their failure to understand the loaves. After landing at Gennesaret, people recognised Jesus and brought the sick to him. They asked to touch the edge of his garment, and those who touched it were made well."], "lesson": "A fresh fear can hide the significance of help we have just received. Mark invites readers to hold the feeding and the crossing together as they ask who Jesus is.", "question": "What does Mark say the disciples had failed to understand before the crossing?"},
  {"id": 91085, "title": "The Gift That Excused Neglect", "subtitle": "Looking beneath religious appearances", "text": "Mark 7:1–23", "chapters": [[40, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Pharisees and scribes from Jerusalem gathered around Jesus and saw some disciples eating with unwashed hands. Mark explains the washing customs that lay behind their question. They asked why the disciples did not walk according to the tradition of the elders. Jesus answered by recalling Isaiah's words about people who honoured God with their lips while their hearts were far from him.", "He gave them a concrete example. Moses commanded people to honour father and mother. But someone could declare that the help a parent would have received from him was Corban, meaning a gift devoted to God. In practice the tradition could leave the parent without help. Jesus said that in this way they set aside God's word through what they had handed down.", "Then he called the crowd and told them to listen and understand. Something entering a person from outside could not defile in the way that what came out of a person could. When he entered the house, the disciples asked him to explain.", "Jesus distinguished where food goes from what comes out of the heart. He named such things as evil thoughts, stealing, murder, deceit, envy, slander and pride. These proceed from within and defile. The first question had been about what the disciples did with their hands before eating. Jesus' answer gave the hearers reason to examine what they allowed their hearts to produce."], "lesson": "A religious claim can become a polished excuse for neglecting someone. Jesus makes both the neglected duty and the motives of the heart visible.", "question": "Which command did Jesus say could be set aside through the tradition about gifts?"},
  {"id": 91086, "title": "A Mother's Reply and an Opened Ear", "subtitle": "Mercy in unfamiliar places", "text": "Mark 7:24–37", "chapters": [[40, 7]], "start": 24, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus went to the region of Tyre and Sidon and entered a house. He wanted to keep his presence quiet, but word reached a woman whose daughter had an unclean spirit. The woman was a Greek, Syrophoenician by birth. She came, fell at his feet and begged him to drive the spirit out of her child.", "Jesus spoke about letting the children eat first and said their bread should not be thrown to the dogs. The woman answered from within the picture he had given her: even the dogs under the table ate the children's crumbs. Jesus told her to go because of what she had said. When she arrived home, she found the girl lying on the bed and the spirit gone.", "Jesus travelled on towards the Sea of Galilee through the region of the Decapolis. People brought a man who was deaf and had difficulty speaking. They asked Jesus to lay his hand on him. Jesus took the man away from the crowd, touched his ears and tongue, looked towards heaven and sighed. He told him to be opened. The man's ears opened and he began to speak clearly.", "Jesus charged the people to keep it quiet, but the more he told them, the more widely they spoke about it. They marvelled that he made the deaf hear and those who could not speak speak plainly. In one house a mother received an answer about a child she could not bring there. Beside the lake a man received attention away from the people who brought him."], "lesson": "Jesus hears the mother's reply and deals personally with the man brought by others. People beyond familiar religious boundaries receive his attention.", "question": "What do you learn from the way Jesus handled the man privately before the healing became widely known?"},
  {"id": 91087, "title": "Another Meal and Sight in Stages", "subtitle": "Learning to see what is before you", "text": "Mark 8:1–26", "chapters": [[40, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["A large crowd had been with Jesus for three days and had nothing to eat. He told the disciples he had compassion. Some people had come from far away and might faint if sent home hungry. The disciples asked where bread could be found in that place. Jesus took seven loaves and a few small fish, gave thanks and had them distribute the food. About four thousand ate, and seven baskets were gathered.", "After the crossing, Pharisees came to question Jesus and demanded a sign from heaven to test him. He sighed deeply and asked why that generation wanted a sign. Then he left them and crossed again. The disciples had forgotten bread and had only one loaf in the boat. When Jesus warned them about the leaven of the Pharisees and of Herod, they thought he was speaking about their supplies.", "He asked why they were reasoning about having no bread. He made them remember the numbers of baskets after both feedings. They could answer with twelve and seven. Then he asked if they still did not understand.", "At Bethsaida people brought a blind man to Jesus. He took him by the hand and led him outside the village. After Jesus touched his eyes, the man could see people, but they looked like walking trees. Jesus laid his hands on him again, and he saw clearly. Jesus sent him home, charging him to keep away from the village."], "lesson": "Mark places questions about seeing beside a healing in stages. Honest attention to what we only partly understand can make room for further instruction.", "question": "What do the disciples remember about the baskets, and what do they still need to understand?"},
  {"id": 91088, "title": "The Confession on the Road", "subtitle": "The identity of Jesus and the cost of following", "text": "Mark 8:27–38", "chapters": [[40, 8]], "start": 27, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["As Jesus and the disciples walked towards the villages around Caesarea Philippi, he asked what people said about him. They reported several answers: John the Baptist, Elijah or one of the prophets. Jesus then asked what the disciples themselves said. Peter answered that he was the Christ. Jesus told them to tell no one about him.", "Then he began teaching them plainly that the Son of Man must suffer many things. The elders, chief priests and scribes would reject him. He would be killed and would rise after three days. Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. Jesus turned, saw the other disciples and rebuked Peter in their hearing. Peter had set his mind on human concerns instead of God's.", "Jesus called the crowd and the disciples to hear what following him meant. Anyone who would come after him must deny himself, take up his cross and follow. A person who tried to save his life would lose it. One who lost it for Jesus and for the gospel would save it.", "Jesus asked what it would profit someone to gain the whole world and lose his soul, or what he could give in exchange for it. He spoke of people being ashamed of him and his words, and of the Son of Man coming in his Father's glory. Peter had given the right name on the road. Jesus now explained what allegiance to the Christ would cost."], "lesson": "Peter accepts a title and objects to what Jesus says it will involve. Jesus teaches disciples to examine what they are protecting when his way calls for surrender.", "question": "What words of Jesus provoked Peter's objection?"},
  {"id": 91089, "title": "Glory on the Mountain and Need Below", "subtitle": "Prayer when faith is weak", "text": "Mark 9:1–29", "chapters": [[40, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus told his hearers that some standing there would see God's kingdom come with power before they died. Six days later he took Peter, James and John up a high mountain apart from the others. His appearance changed, and his garments became brilliantly white. Elijah and Moses appeared and spoke with him. Peter suggested making three shelters. Mark says he did not know what to say because they were afraid.", "A cloud overshadowed them and a voice called Jesus the beloved Son and told them to hear him. When they looked around, they saw Jesus alone. On the descent Jesus told them to keep the event to themselves until the Son of Man had risen. They discussed what rising from the dead could mean and asked about Elijah. Jesus spoke about Elijah having come and about the suffering of the Son of Man.", "Below, scribes were disputing with the disciples. A father explained that his boy was tormented by a spirit and that the disciples had been unable to drive it out. He brought the boy to Jesus and asked for help. When Jesus spoke of believing, the father cried out that he believed and asked for help with his unbelief.", "Jesus commanded the spirit to leave. The boy lay so still people said he was dead. Jesus took him by the hand and lifted him up. Later the disciples privately asked why they had failed. Jesus directed them to prayer."], "lesson": "The father's plea gives words to imperfect faith. He brings his uncertainty to Jesus along with his son's need.", "question": "How does the father's plea hold belief and need together?"},
  {"id": 91090, "title": "The Argument They Kept Quiet", "subtitle": "Welcome, humility and care", "text": "Mark 9:30–50", "chapters": [[40, 9]], "start": 30, "end": 50, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus and the disciples passed through Galilee without seeking notice. He was teaching them that the Son of Man would be handed over, killed and raised. They did not understand and were afraid to ask. When they reached a house in Capernaum, Jesus asked what they had been discussing along the road. They kept silent because they had argued about who was greatest.", "He sat down, called the Twelve and told them that anyone who wanted to be first should be last and servant of all. He set a child among them and took the child in his arms. Whoever received such a child in his name received him, and whoever received him received the one who sent him.", "John said they had tried to stop someone who was driving out demons in Jesus' name because he did not follow their group. Jesus told them not to stop him. He spoke of someone giving his followers even a cup of water on account of belonging to Christ, and of the seriousness of causing one of the little ones to stumble.", "Jesus used severe images of giving up a hand, foot or eye to impress on them the danger of what led into sin. He ended by speaking of salt and telling them to be at peace with one another. The group that had been silent about its argument was given something concrete to practise together."], "lesson": "The disciples' argument kept their attention on position. Jesus gave them practical matters to consider, including the welcome they offered and the harm they might cause.", "question": "What did Jesus place before the disciples to give their argument a different direction?"},
  {"id": 91091, "title": "Children at the Door and a Man With Wealth", "subtitle": "Receiving the kingdom with open hands", "text": "Mark 10:1–31", "chapters": [[40, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus went to the region of Judea beyond the Jordan, and crowds gathered for him to teach. Pharisees asked whether a man could put away his wife, trying to test him. Jesus asked what Moses commanded. They referred to the certificate of divorce. He said that permission had been written because of hardness of heart. He went back to creation, when God made male and female and joined two into one flesh. In the house he addressed his disciples again about divorce and remarriage.", "People brought children for Jesus to touch, and the disciples rebuked them. Jesus was indignant and told them to let the children come. The kingdom belonged to such as these, he said, and must be received like a child. He took the children in his arms, laid his hands on them and blessed them.", "As he was setting out, a man ran up, knelt and asked what he must do to inherit eternal life. Jesus named commandments, and the man said he had kept them since youth. Jesus looked at him with love. He told him to sell what he had, give to the poor and follow him. The man went away sorrowful because he owned much.", "Jesus spoke about how hard it was for the wealthy to enter God's kingdom, using the image of a camel and a needle's eye. When the disciples asked who could be saved, he pointed them to what was possible with God. Peter spoke of what they had left. Jesus promised provision with persecutions now, and eternal life in the age to come."], "lesson": "Mark records Jesus' love for the man who walked away. It makes the hard instruction personal and shows that a demand can come from love.", "question": "What does Mark say about the way Jesus looked at the man before giving the instruction?"},
  {"id": 91092, "title": "Two Requests on the Road", "subtitle": "Serving and seeing", "text": "Mark 10:32–52", "chapters": [[40, 10]], "start": 32, "end": 52, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["On the road to Jerusalem Jesus was walking ahead. Those following were afraid, and he took the Twelve aside to tell them what awaited him. He spoke of being handed over, condemned and killed, and of rising again. James and John then approached him with something they wanted him to do. Asked what it was, they requested places beside him in his glory.", "Jesus spoke of the cup he would drink and the baptism he would undergo. The brothers said they were able to share them. When the other ten grew indignant, Jesus gathered the disciples and spoke about how rulers exercise power. Among his followers, greatness would take the form of service. He gave his own mission as the pattern, saying he came to serve and give his life as a ransom for many.", "As they left Jericho, Bartimaeus sat begging by the road. Hearing that Jesus was passing, he cried out to the Son of David for mercy. People rebuked him, but he cried louder. Jesus stopped and told them to call him. The people now encouraged the man to rise. Bartimaeus threw aside his garment and came to Jesus.", "Jesus asked him what he wanted, as he had asked James and John earlier on the road. Bartimaeus asked to see. Jesus told him his faith had made him well. He received his sight and followed Jesus along the way, the same road on which Jesus had been teaching his disciples what following him would involve."], "lesson": "Jesus asks what people want in both scenes. The answers reveal different desires as he goes towards Jerusalem.", "question": "What do James and John ask for, and what does Bartimaeus ask for when Jesus stops?"},
  {"id": 91093, "title": "Branches, a Temple and a Withered Tree", "subtitle": "The fruit of prayer and forgiveness", "text": "Mark 11:1–26", "chapters": [[40, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Near Bethphage and Bethany, Jesus sent two disciples to a village where they would find a colt tied that no one had ridden. They were to bring it and say the Lord needed it if anyone asked. They found the colt, answered the people standing there and brought it to Jesus. He rode towards Jerusalem while people spread garments and branches on the road and cried Hosanna. He entered the temple, looked around and went back to Bethany because it was late.", "Returning the next day, Jesus was hungry. He went to a fig tree with leaves and found no fruit. Mark notes it was not the season for figs. The disciples heard Jesus say that no one would eat fruit from it again. Then they went into Jerusalem.", "In the temple Jesus drove out those buying and selling and overturned the money changers' tables and the sellers' seats. He taught that God's house was to be a house of prayer for all nations and said they had made it a den of thieves. The chief priests and scribes heard and looked for a way to destroy him. He went out of the city at evening.", "Next morning the disciples saw the fig tree withered from the roots. Peter remembered Jesus' words. Jesus told them to have faith in God and spoke about prayer. He also told them to forgive when standing to pray, bringing the lesson back to their own conduct."], "lesson": "Mark places the temple visit between the two encounters with the tree. The sequence invites examination of the fruit of worship, including a willingness to forgive.", "question": "Which instruction about forgiveness accompanies Jesus' teaching about faith and prayer?"},
  {"id": 91094, "title": "An Answer Kept Back", "subtitle": "Being honest about authority", "text": "Mark 11:27–33", "chapters": [[40, 11]], "start": 27, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus and the disciples came again to Jerusalem. As he walked in the temple, the chief priests, scribes and elders approached him. They asked what authority he had to do these things and who had given it to him. Mark has just described his actions in the temple and the leaders looking for a way to destroy him. Now they challenged him to account for what he had done.", "Jesus said he would ask them a question and answer theirs if they answered his. He brought them back to John's baptism. Had it come from heaven or from people? He asked them to say plainly which they believed.", "The leaders discussed it among themselves. If they said it came from heaven, Jesus could ask why they had not believed John. If they said it came from people, they had reason to fear the crowd, because people considered John a prophet. Their discussion centred on how each answer would be received. Mark records no conclusion among them about the source of John's authority.", "They told Jesus they did not know. He replied that he would not tell them by what authority he acted. The question they had brought remained unanswered after they had declined to give an answer of their own."], "lesson": "Concern for position governed their discussion. When an honest answer carries a cost, the passage asks us to notice the temptation to retreat into convenient uncertainty.", "question": "What were the leaders considering when they decided how to answer?"},
  {"id": 91095, "title": "The Vineyard, the Coin and the Living God", "subtitle": "Answering to God", "text": "Mark 12:1–27", "chapters": [[40, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus told of a man who planted a vineyard, prepared it for making wine, built a tower and entrusted it to tenants. When he sent servants to receive some of the fruit, the tenants beat or killed them. At last he sent his beloved son. They recognised the heir and killed him to try to take the inheritance. Jesus asked what the owner would do and spoke of judgement and of the rejected stone becoming the cornerstone. The leaders knew the parable concerned them.", "They sent Pharisees and Herodians to trap him with a question about paying tribute to Caesar. Jesus asked for a coin and asked whose image and inscription it bore. When they said Caesar's, he told them to give Caesar what was Caesar's and God what was God's. They marvelled at him.", "Then Sadducees, who denied resurrection, told of one woman who had been married in succession to seven brothers, each of whom died without leaving children. They asked whose wife she would be in the resurrection. Jesus said they had misunderstood Scripture and God's power. Marriage would not carry over in the way they supposed.", "He took them to the account of God speaking to Moses at the bush and naming himself the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. He spoke of God as the God of the living. Each exchange in the passage returns to a claim that the questioners had treated too lightly."], "lesson": "The passages move from responsibility for what is entrusted to us towards God's claim and power. Jesus reads each question against what the questioners have missed about God.", "question": "How does the owner's claim in the parable help you consider what belongs to God?"},
  {"id": 91096, "title": "The Listening Scribe and the Widow's Coins", "subtitle": "Wholehearted devotion", "text": "Mark 12:28–44", "chapters": [[40, 12]], "start": 28, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["A scribe heard Jesus answer the others well and asked which commandment came first. Jesus began with Israel's confession that the Lord is one and called for love of God with heart, soul, mind and strength. He gave a second command, to love one's neighbour as oneself. The scribe agreed and said that such love mattered more than offerings and sacrifices. Jesus saw that he had answered thoughtfully and told him he was near God's kingdom.", "While teaching in the temple, Jesus asked how the scribes could say the Christ was David's son when David, speaking by the Holy Spirit, called him Lord. He also warned people about scribes who sought public greetings and places of honour, while devouring widows' houses and making long prayers for appearance.", "Jesus then sat opposite the treasury and watched people putting in their money. Many wealthy people put in much. A poor widow came and put in two very small coins. Jesus called the disciples to notice what she had done. He said she had put in more than all the others.", "The others had given from what they had in abundance. She had put in all she had to live on. Mark gives the widow's two coins the last word in a passage about what people say matters most and what Jesus regards as significant in the temple."], "lesson": "The attentive scribe and the widow each receive an individual response from Jesus. He considers the substance of a person's devotion, which a public display or the amount of a gift may conceal.", "question": "What did Jesus notice about the widow's gift that an observer counting coins could miss?"},
  {"id": 91097, "title": "Across From the Temple", "subtitle": "Discernment under pressure", "text": "Mark 13:1–23", "chapters": [[40, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["As Jesus left the temple, one disciple drew his attention to its great stones and buildings. Jesus said a day was coming when one stone would not be left on another. Sitting opposite the temple on the Mount of Olives, Peter, James, John and Andrew asked him privately when it would happen and how they would recognise the time.", "Jesus warned against being misled by people claiming his name. Wars and reports of wars would come, along with earthquakes and famines. These would be beginnings. He told the disciples they would be brought before councils and rulers and would have opportunities to bear witness. In those moments the Holy Spirit would give them what to say. Even families would be divided over their loyalty to him.", "When the desolating sight appeared, those in Judea were to flee. Jesus used the examples of someone on a roof who could not afford to go down for possessions and someone in a field who should not return for a garment. He spoke of people for whom flight would be especially hard and told them to pray that it would not happen in winter.", "He then warned that false Christs and false prophets would appear with convincing signs. His disciples should be alert. They had asked how they would recognise what lay ahead. He supplied warnings they could remember when rumours, fear and oppression made judgement harder."], "lesson": "Jesus answers the question about timing by preparing his followers for discernment and endurance. His promise concerning the Spirit addresses a real moment of vulnerability.", "question": "What instructions does Jesus give for a time when fear and false claims are widespread?"},
  {"id": 91098, "title": "The Doorkeeper's Watch", "subtitle": "Faithfulness while waiting", "text": "Mark 13:24–37", "chapters": [[40, 13]], "start": 24, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus went on speaking about what would follow the distress. The sun would be darkened and the moon would cease to give light. The powers in heaven would be shaken. Then people would see the Son of Man coming in clouds with power and glory. He would send angels to gather his chosen people from the ends of earth and heaven.", "Jesus asked the disciples to learn from a fig tree. Tender branches and new leaves tell people that summer is near. He connected this with recognising what was near when they saw the things he had described. Heaven and earth would pass away, but his words would remain.", "Then he spoke about what they could not calculate. The day or hour was known to the Father. They were to watch. He compared the situation to a man going away from his house. The man gave authority and a particular task to each servant and instructed the doorkeeper to watch.", "The master might come in the evening, at midnight, when the rooster crowed or in the morning. Jesus warned against being found asleep. The last instruction is addressed to all who hear it. Each servant has work to attend to during the wait, and the doorkeeper has a watch to keep even though he cannot schedule the return."], "lesson": "Each servant has work while the master is away. Jesus joins expectancy to attention to the duty already assigned.", "question": "What has the master already given each servant to do before he returns?"},
  {"id": 91099, "title": "The Costly Gift and the Shared Cup", "subtitle": "Devotion in a room of weakness", "text": "Mark 14:1–25", "chapters": [[40, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Passover was approaching, and the chief priests and scribes looked for a way to seize Jesus without stirring up the people. At Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper, a woman came while Jesus reclined at the table. She broke a jar of costly perfume and poured it on his head. Some protested that it could have been sold and the money given to the poor, and they rebuked her.", "Jesus told them to leave her alone. She had done a good work, he said. They could still help the poor, but his time with them was short. She had done what she could and had anointed his body beforehand for burial. Wherever the gospel was proclaimed, her act would be remembered. Judas then went to the leaders to arrange to hand Jesus over. They promised him money, and he began looking for an opportunity.", "When it was time to prepare the Passover, Jesus gave two disciples directions. A man carrying a water jar would lead them to a house. They found the upstairs room furnished and ready and made the preparations there.", "At the meal Jesus said one of the Twelve eating with him would betray him. They were sorrowful and asked in turn if it was them. Jesus took bread, blessed it and gave it to them, speaking of his body. He gave thanks over the cup and spoke of his covenant blood poured out for many. He said he would drink again in God's kingdom."], "lesson": "The anointing is remembered for what the woman did within her reach. At the meal Jesus gives his disciples a way to remember what he is doing for them.", "question": "Why does Jesus link the woman's gift with his burial?"},
  {"id": 91100, "title": "The Sleeping Friends and the Arrest", "subtitle": "Watching and yielding to the Father", "text": "Mark 14:26–52", "chapters": [[40, 14]], "start": 26, "end": 52, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["After singing a hymn, Jesus and the disciples went to the Mount of Olives. He said they would all fall away and recalled the words about the shepherd being struck and the sheep scattered. He promised to go before them to Galilee after he was raised. Peter insisted he would stay faithful, and Jesus told him he would deny him three times before the rooster crowed twice. Peter repeated his promise, and the others said the same.", "At Gethsemane Jesus took Peter, James and John with him. Deeply distressed, he asked them to stay awake. Going farther, he fell to the ground and prayed to his Father that the cup might pass, yielding to his Father's will. He returned to find them asleep. He told Peter to watch and pray because the spirit was willing and the flesh weak.", "Jesus went to pray again and again found them sleeping. When he came back the third time, he told them the hour had come. Judas approached with people sent by the chief priests, scribes and elders. He gave them the agreed sign by greeting Jesus and kissing him. The group laid hands on Jesus. Someone drew a sword and cut off the high priest's servant's ear.", "Jesus reminded them he had taught openly in the temple and spoke of the Scriptures being fulfilled. The disciples left him and fled. Mark also records a young man who escaped when seized by leaving behind the linen cloth he wore."], "lesson": "Jesus had warned the disciples about their weakness before the crowd arrived. The passage shows the need to bring that weakness into prayer while there is time.", "question": "What did Jesus warn Peter about the spirit and the flesh when he found him asleep?"},
  {"id": 91101, "title": "Peter Below in the Courtyard", "subtitle": "Remembering the word after failure", "text": "Mark 14:53–72", "chapters": [[40, 14]], "start": 53, "end": 72, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus was led to the high priest, where the chief priests, elders and scribes gathered. Peter followed at a distance into the courtyard and sat with the servants by the fire. Inside, the council sought testimony that would justify putting Jesus to death. Witnesses came forward, but their accounts did not agree. Even the witnesses who spoke about the temple differed from one another.", "The high priest stood and asked Jesus to answer the accusations. Jesus remained silent. When asked directly whether he was the Christ, the Son of the Blessed, he answered and spoke of the Son of Man seated at the right hand of power and coming with the clouds. The high priest tore his clothes and the council condemned Jesus as deserving death. Some spat on him, covered his face and struck him.", "Below in the courtyard, a servant girl saw Peter warming himself and said he had been with Jesus of Nazareth. Peter denied it and moved out towards the porch. When the girl spoke to others about him, he denied it again. Later those standing nearby insisted he was one of the followers, pointing to his being a Galilean. Peter began to curse and swear that he did not know the man they were talking about.", "The rooster crowed a second time. Peter remembered that Jesus had told him he would deny him three times before it crowed twice. Remembering the words he had earlier contradicted, he broke down and wept."], "lesson": "Remembering Jesus' words allowed Peter to recognise his failure. A word we once resisted can become the beginning of honest repentance.", "question": "What detail brought Jesus' warning back to Peter's mind?"},
  {"id": 91102, "title": "The Rejected Prisoner and the Mock Crown", "subtitle": "Recognising the power of a crowd", "text": "Mark 15:1–20", "chapters": [[40, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Early in the morning the leaders consulted, bound Jesus and took him to Pilate. Pilate asked whether he was the King of the Jews. Jesus answered him, then remained silent when the chief priests brought further charges. Pilate marvelled that he gave no reply.", "At the feast Pilate usually released a prisoner the people requested. Barabbas was in prison with men who had committed murder during an uprising. When the crowd asked for the customary release, Pilate offered to release the King of the Jews. Mark says he knew the chief priests had handed Jesus over out of envy. The priests stirred the crowd to ask for Barabbas instead.", "Pilate asked what he should do with Jesus. They shouted for him to be crucified. He asked what wrong Jesus had done, and they cried out more loudly. Pilate wanted to satisfy the crowd. He released Barabbas, had Jesus scourged and handed him over to be crucified.", "The soldiers took Jesus inside and called together their company. They dressed him in purple, crowned him with thorns and saluted him in mockery as King of the Jews. They struck him, spat on him and knelt before him. Then they removed the purple robe, put his own clothes on him and led him away. The question with which Pilate began had become a title for the soldiers to mock."], "lesson": "Mark gives a reason for Pilate's decision: he wanted to satisfy the crowd. His choice asks what happens when a person uses authority chiefly to preserve approval.", "question": "What did Pilate know about the chief priests' motives, and how did he still decide?"},
  {"id": 91103, "title": "The Centurion, the Women and the Tomb", "subtitle": "Witness and courage at the cross", "text": "Mark 15:21–47", "chapters": [[40, 15]], "start": 21, "end": 47, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["The soldiers compelled Simon of Cyrene, the father of Alexander and Rufus, to carry Jesus' cross. They brought Jesus to Golgotha, offered him wine mixed with myrrh, which he refused, and crucified him. They cast lots for his clothes. The charge above him called him King of the Jews, and two men were crucified beside him.", "Passers-by mocked Jesus and told him to come down. The chief priests and scribes spoke of him saving others and taunted him about saving himself. Those crucified alongside him also reviled him. Darkness lay over the land from noon until the middle of the afternoon. Jesus cried out, and some people thought he was calling Elijah. After another loud cry, Jesus died.", "The temple curtain tore from top to bottom. A centurion who faced Jesus saw how he died and called him God's Son. Women watched from a distance, including Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joses, and Salome. Mark remembers that they had followed and served him in Galilee.", "As evening approached before the Sabbath, Joseph of Arimathea went courageously to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate checked with the centurion that Jesus was dead before releasing it. Joseph wrapped the body in linen, laid it in a tomb cut from rock and rolled a stone across the entrance. The two Marys saw where he was laid."], "lesson": "People in different places bear witness to what happened. Joseph's request took courage, while the women attended closely enough to know where to return.", "question": "Which details does Mark give about the people who saw Jesus die or saw where he was buried?"},
  {"id": 91104, "title": "The Stone Already Rolled Away", "subtitle": "Hope spoken to failing disciples", "text": "Mark 16:1–8", "chapters": [[40, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 8, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["When the Sabbath was past, Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Salome bought spices so that they could anoint Jesus' body. They went to the tomb early on the first day of the week, as the sun was rising. They had a practical question on the way. The stone across the entrance was large, and they wondered who would roll it away for them.", "When they looked up, they saw that it had already been rolled back. They entered the tomb and saw a young man sitting on the right side, dressed in white. They were alarmed. He told them he knew they were looking for Jesus of Nazareth, who had been crucified. He had risen and was no longer there. The young man directed them to the place where Jesus had been laid.", "He then gave them something to do. They were to go and tell the disciples and Peter that Jesus was going ahead of them into Galilee. There they would see him, as he had told them. Naming Peter gives the reader a reason to remember the courtyard where he had denied Jesus.", "The women went out quickly and fled from the tomb, trembling and astonished. Mark says they said nothing to anyone as they fled because they were afraid. They had come bearing spices for the dead. They left with a message about where the disciples would see him alive."], "lesson": "Peter is named when the message is sent to the disciples. Readers who remember his denial can hear the personal care in that small addition.", "question": "What difference does the mention of Peter make after the account of his denial?"},
  {"id": 91105, "title": "The News Carried Outward", "subtitle": "From unbelief to witness", "text": "Mark 16:9–20", "chapters": [[40, 16]], "start": 9, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["In the closing verses printed in the King James Version, the risen Jesus appears first to Mary Magdalene, from whom he had cast out seven demons. She goes to the people who had been with him and finds them mourning and weeping. They hear her report that he is alive and that she has seen him, but they do not believe her.", "Jesus then appears in another form to two of them as they walk into the country. They go back and give their report, and the others do not believe them either. Afterwards Jesus appears to the eleven while they are eating. He reproaches their unbelief and hardness of heart because they had not believed the people who had seen him risen.", "He sends them out into the world to proclaim the gospel to all creation and speaks of the response of belief and baptism. The passage describes signs that would accompany believers. The people Jesus sends now have work to do with the message they themselves had been slow to receive.", "After speaking to them, Jesus is received into heaven and sits at God's right hand. They go out and preach everywhere. The account pictures the Lord working with them and confirming the word. It has moved from the grief of people unable to believe another person's report to those same followers going out to bear witness."], "lesson": "The people commissioned to speak had themselves struggled to believe. Their earlier failure gives context to the patient work of bearing witness to others.", "question": "How do the disciples' first reactions compare with what they do at the close of the passage?"},
  {"id": 91106, "title": "Zechariah Comes Out Silent", "subtitle": "A promise in the years of waiting", "text": "Luke 1:1–25", "chapters": [[41, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Luke tells Theophilus that others have already undertaken to write accounts of these events. The accounts draw on those who saw what happened and served the word from the beginning. Luke has traced the events carefully and wants Theophilus to know the certainty of what he has been taught. The first people he puts before him are an elderly priest and his wife.", "Zechariah and Elisabeth lived in the days of Herod. Both are described as righteous before God and faithful in keeping his commands. They had no child, and both were old. When Zechariah's division was on duty, the lot fell to him to enter the sanctuary and offer incense. The people waited and prayed outside.", "An angel appeared by the altar, and Zechariah was afraid. The angel told him his prayer had been heard. Elisabeth would bear a son named John. The child would bring joy, turn many to the Lord and prepare people for his coming. Zechariah asked how he could know this, pointing to his and Elisabeth's age. The angel identified himself as Gabriel and told him he would be unable to speak until the promise came to pass because he had not believed.", "Outside, the people wondered why he was taking so long. When he emerged, he could only gesture, and they realised he had seen a vision. After completing his service he went home. Elisabeth conceived and kept herself apart for five months. She spoke of the Lord seeing her and taking away the reproach she had borne among her people."], "lesson": "Luke presents the couple as faithful before their prayer is answered. Elisabeth's years of waiting deserve attention alongside the joy that follows.", "question": "How does Luke describe Zechariah and Elisabeth before Gabriel brings the news?"},
  {"id": 91107, "title": "Mary Goes to See Elisabeth", "subtitle": "Believing God's word in uncertain circumstances", "text": "Luke 1:26–56", "chapters": [[41, 1]], "start": 26, "end": 56, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Gabriel was sent to Nazareth in the sixth month of Elisabeth's pregnancy. He came to Mary, who was pledged to Joseph, a man of David's house. His greeting troubled her. He told her she had found favour with God and would have a son named Jesus. The child would be called the Son of the Highest and would reign on David's throne without end.", "Mary asked how this could happen since she had not been with a man. Gabriel spoke of the Holy Spirit coming upon her and the power of the Highest overshadowing her. He also told her that Elisabeth, who had been called barren, was already in her sixth month. Mary placed herself at the Lord's service and accepted that it should happen according to his word.", "Mary went quickly to the hill country and greeted Elisabeth. At the sound of her greeting, Elisabeth's child leaped. Elisabeth was filled with the Holy Spirit and blessed Mary and the child she carried. She wondered at being visited by the mother of her Lord and called Mary blessed for believing what had been spoken to her.", "Mary answered in praise. She spoke of God seeing her low estate, bringing down the powerful, lifting the lowly, filling the hungry and remembering mercy towards Israel. Her words reached back to God's promise to Abraham and his descendants. Mary stayed with Elisabeth about three months before returning home. The private announcement in Nazareth had become an occasion for two women to speak of what God was doing."], "lesson": "Mary's praise places her own experience inside God's mercy to generations. Elisabeth's welcome gives her a place to share what has happened.", "question": "What parts of Mary's song reach beyond her own circumstances?"},
  {"id": 91108, "title": "The Name Written on the Tablet", "subtitle": "Mercy remembered and a way prepared", "text": "Luke 1:57–80", "chapters": [[41, 1]], "start": 57, "end": 80, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["The time came for Elisabeth to give birth, and she had a son. Her neighbours and relatives heard how the Lord had shown her mercy, and they rejoiced with her. On the eighth day they came to circumcise the child. They expected to name him Zechariah after his father, but Elisabeth said his name was to be John. They objected that no one in her family was called by that name.", "They made signs to the child's father to ask what he wanted him called. Zechariah asked for a writing tablet and wrote that his name was John. Everyone marvelled. At once Zechariah could speak again, and he used his first words to bless God. The neighbours were filled with awe, and word of these events spread through the hill country. People wondered what this child would become.", "Filled with the Holy Spirit, Zechariah spoke of God visiting and redeeming his people. He recalled the promises to David and Abraham and the covenant by which God would deliver people to serve him without fear, in holiness and righteousness. The promise concerning his son belonged to a purpose older and wider than the family's joy at his birth.", "Then he addressed the child. John would go before the Lord to prepare his ways and give people knowledge of salvation through forgiveness. Zechariah pictured light dawning upon people in darkness and guiding their feet into peace. Luke ends by telling how the child grew strong in spirit and lived in the wilderness until his public appearance to Israel."], "lesson": "The name marks obedience to what was spoken before the child was born. Zechariah uses his restored speech to bless God and to describe a calling greater than family expectations.", "question": "What does Zechariah say John will do for the people?"},
  {"id": 91109, "title": "The Night the Shepherds Went to Bethlehem", "subtitle": "Good news carried by ordinary witnesses", "text": "Luke 2:1–21", "chapters": [[41, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A decree for registration went out from Caesar Augustus. People travelled to their own towns. Joseph went from Nazareth in Galilee to Bethlehem in Judea because he belonged to David's family. Mary went with him, and while they were there the time came for her to give birth. She wrapped her firstborn son and laid him in a manger because there was no room for them in the lodging place.", "Shepherds were staying in the fields nearby, keeping watch at night. An angel appeared and the glory of the Lord shone around them. They were frightened. The angel told them of good news and great joy: a Saviour, Christ the Lord, had been born in David's town. The sign they were given was a baby wrapped and lying in a manger. A multitude from heaven praised God for what had happened.", "When the angels had gone, the shepherds decided to go to Bethlehem and see what the Lord had made known. They went quickly and found Mary, Joseph and the child lying where the angel had said. They made known what they had been told about him. The people who heard were amazed, while Mary kept these things and pondered them in her heart.", "The shepherds returned to their work glorifying and praising God for what they had heard and seen. Eight days later the child was circumcised and named Jesus, the name given by the angel before he was conceived."], "lesson": "The shepherds act on a sign they can go and check. Mary shows another faithful response by keeping and pondering what she hears.", "question": "What did the shepherds do between hearing the news and telling it to others?"},
  {"id": 91110, "title": "Simeon and Anna See the Child", "subtitle": "Waiting with hope and attention", "text": "Luke 2:22–40", "chapters": [[41, 2]], "start": 22, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["When the days of purification were completed, Mary and Joseph brought Jesus to Jerusalem to present him to the Lord as the law required. Luke records the offering they brought: a pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons. They came to carry out an ordinary duty, and there they met people who had been waiting for something God had promised.", "Simeon was righteous and devout and was waiting for Israel's consolation. The Holy Spirit had told him he would see the Lord's Christ before he died. Led by the Spirit, he came to the temple. When the parents brought Jesus in, Simeon took the child in his arms and blessed God. He said he could depart in peace because he had seen God's salvation, prepared before all peoples, a light for the Gentiles and glory for Israel.", "Jesus' parents marvelled. Simeon blessed them and spoke to Mary of a child appointed for the fall and rising of many and for a sign people would speak against. He told her that a sword would pierce her own soul. The words spoken over the child gave her reason to prepare for grief as well as to give thanks.", "Anna, a prophetess and widow who served in the temple with fasting and prayer, came up at that hour. She thanked God and spoke about the child to people waiting for redemption in Jerusalem. After doing what the law required, the family returned to Nazareth, where the child grew in strength and wisdom under God's grace."], "lesson": "Years of prayer have attuned Simeon and Anna to what God is doing. Simeon's words put joy and coming sorrow before Mary together.", "question": "What had Simeon and Anna been waiting for, and how did each respond?"},
  {"id": 91111, "title": "Three Days Searching for Jesus", "subtitle": "Growing in wisdom and obedience", "text": "Luke 2:41–52", "chapters": [[41, 2]], "start": 41, "end": 52, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus' parents went to Jerusalem every year for Passover. When he was twelve, they went up as usual. After the feast they began the journey home, but Jesus stayed behind in Jerusalem without their knowing it. Thinking he was with the travelling company, they went a day's journey and looked for him among relatives and acquaintances. When they did not find him, they went back to Jerusalem searching for him.", "After three days they found him in the temple, sitting among the teachers. He was listening and asking questions. Those who heard him were astonished at his understanding and answers. Mary and Joseph were also astonished when they saw him, but they had been living through a different part of the story as they looked for him.", "Mary asked why he had treated them in this way and told him how anxiously she and his father had searched. Jesus asked why they had been looking for him and whether they did not know that he must be concerned with his Father's affairs. Luke says they did not understand his words.", "Jesus went back with them to Nazareth and lived in obedience to them. Mary kept these things in her heart. Luke describes Jesus growing in wisdom and stature and in favour with God and people. The account leaves Mary with something she can remember before she can fully explain it, and it leaves Jesus growing within the household to which he returned."], "lesson": "Luke records both Jesus' awareness of his Father and his obedience in the household. Mary keeps what she cannot yet explain and allows time for understanding.", "question": "What does Luke say Jesus did after the conversation at the temple?"},
  {"id": 91112, "title": "What Shall We Do?", "subtitle": "Repentance in daily dealings", "text": "Luke 3:1–20", "chapters": [[41, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Luke names the rulers and high priests of the time before moving to the wilderness, where God's word came to John son of Zechariah. John went through the country around the Jordan preaching a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins. Luke connects his work with Isaiah's voice calling for the Lord's way to be prepared, with rough ground made smooth so that all people might see God's salvation.", "John warned the crowds who came to be baptised. They could not rely on descent from Abraham to escape judgement. They should produce fruit in keeping with repentance. He pictured an axe already laid at the root of trees that failed to bear good fruit. People asked him what they should do.", "His answers addressed the circumstances of the people who asked. Whoever had two coats should share with someone who had none, and whoever had food should do likewise. Tax collectors should take only what was appointed. Soldiers should avoid extortion and false accusation and be content with their wages. The fruit John asked for could be seen in the way people used what they had and treated people in their power.", "People wondered whether John was the Christ. He spoke of someone mightier who would baptise with the Holy Spirit and fire and clear his threshing floor. John continued exhorting the people. He also reproved Herod for his conduct, including his dealings with Herodias. Luke records that Herod added to his wrongs by shutting John in prison."], "lesson": "John answers each group in terms of the power and goods already in its hands. Repentance reaches how people earn, speak, share and exercise authority.", "question": "What would a practical answer like John's sound like in your own work?"},
  {"id": 91113, "title": "The Son at Prayer", "subtitle": "Jesus and the human family", "text": "Luke 3:21–38", "chapters": [[41, 3]], "start": 21, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Luke has just described people coming to John in response to his preaching. Now he turns to Jesus at the river. Jesus also was baptised, and Luke gives particular attention to what happened while he was praying. Heaven opened, and the Holy Spirit descended upon him in bodily form like a dove. A voice from heaven addressed Jesus as the beloved Son in whom the Father was well pleased.", "Luke then tells the reader that Jesus was about thirty years old when he began. People supposed him to be Joseph's son. The account turns from the voice at the baptism to a record of generations, working backwards from Joseph. Luke moves through a succession of names to Zerubbabel and Shealtiel and farther back through Nathan to David.", "David is followed in the backward record by Jesse and earlier generations, including Boaz and Obed, then Judah, Jacob, Isaac and Abraham. Luke continues beyond Abraham through Shem and Noah and through the generations before the flood. The line reaches Seth and Adam, whom the record calls the son of God.", "The passage gives the reader two statements about sonship to hear carefully. At the baptism the Father speaks directly to Jesus. In the genealogy Luke follows Jesus' human family line as it was understood, past the kings and patriarchs of Israel to the beginning of humanity. The next account will take Jesus from the Jordan into the wilderness to face a challenge to the words spoken there."], "lesson": "Luke places a genealogy reaching to Adam beside the Father's declaration. The two passages give the reader a wide horizon for the ministry about to begin.", "question": "How does Luke's decision to trace the line to Adam widen the opening of the story?"},
  {"id": 91114, "title": "Three Tests in the Wilderness", "subtitle": "Obedience in hunger and weakness", "text": "Luke 4:1–13", "chapters": [[41, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus returned from the Jordan full of the Holy Spirit and was led in the wilderness. For forty days he was tempted by the devil. He ate nothing during those days, and when they ended he was hungry. The first suggestion came at the point of that hunger. If he was God's Son, the devil said, he could command a stone to become bread. Jesus answered from Scripture about the food a person needs to live.", "The devil then took Jesus up and showed him the kingdoms of the world in a moment of time. He offered their power and glory in return for worship. Jesus answered that Scripture directs people to worship the Lord their God and serve him alone. The offer of authority came with a demand Jesus refused to meet.", "Next the devil brought him to Jerusalem and placed him on a high point of the temple. He again challenged him to act if he was God's Son, this time by throwing himself down. To support the proposal he quoted words about angels being given charge over him and bearing him up. Jesus answered with the command that a person must not put the Lord his God to the test.", "Having completed the temptations, the devil departed for a time. Luke has placed the test of trust at the temple last. The promise of protection had been presented as a reason to force a display of it. Jesus' answer keeps trust joined to obedience."], "lesson": "Jesus meets both plain temptation and a temptation dressed in biblical words. His answers show how obedience holds Scripture together.", "question": "What is the difference between trusting God's promise and demanding proof of it?"},
  {"id": 91115, "title": "The Reading at Nazareth", "subtitle": "Receiving grace beyond familiar boundaries", "text": "Luke 4:14–30", "chapters": [[41, 4]], "start": 14, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus returned to Galilee in the power of the Spirit. Reports about him spread and he taught in the synagogues. At Nazareth, where he had been brought up, he went to the synagogue on the Sabbath as he usually did. He stood to read and was handed the book of Isaiah. He read about the Spirit of the Lord anointing him to bring good news to the poor, freedom to the oppressed and sight to the blind, and to announce the Lord's acceptable year.", "He closed the book, returned it to the attendant and sat down. With everyone's eyes on him, he said that the Scripture had been fulfilled that day in their hearing. People wondered at his gracious words and asked whether he was Joseph's son.", "Jesus spoke of the proverb about a physician healing himself and of the expectation that he would do in his hometown what they had heard about Capernaum. He recalled two occasions when prophets helped people outside Israel. Elijah was sent to a widow at Sarepta during the famine. In Elisha's time, Naaman the Syrian was cleansed although many in Israel had leprosy.", "The listeners became furious. They drove Jesus out of the town and brought him to the brow of the hill intending to throw him down. He passed through their midst and went away. The people who had begun by marvelling at his gracious words rejected the examples he gave of where God's grace had gone."], "lesson": "Jesus' neighbours are willing to hear gracious words until he speaks of grace reaching outsiders. Their reaction tests how we respond when God's kindness crosses our boundaries.", "question": "Why did the examples of the widow and Naaman matter to Jesus' hearers?"},
  {"id": 91116, "title": "An Open Door and Another Town", "subtitle": "Compassion and a clear purpose", "text": "Luke 4:31–44", "chapters": [[41, 4]], "start": 31, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus went down to Capernaum and taught on the Sabbath. The people were astonished at the authority of his word. A man in the synagogue who had an unclean spirit cried out and called him the Holy One of God. Jesus told the spirit to be silent and come out. It threw the man down in their midst and came out without harming him. Those who saw it spoke to one another about the authority with which Jesus commanded, and news spread through the region.", "Jesus left the synagogue and entered Simon's house. Simon's mother-in-law had a high fever, and people appealed to him on her behalf. He stood over her and rebuked the fever. It left her, and she got up at once and served them. The authority people had heard in the synagogue reached into an ordinary household and relieved someone whose need had been brought to him.", "At sunset people came bringing friends and relatives with many kinds of sickness. Jesus laid his hands on each of them and healed them. Demons came out of many, crying that he was the Christ, the Son of God. He rebuked them and would not let them speak.", "At daybreak Jesus went out to a deserted place. When the people found him, they tried to keep him from leaving. He explained that he must preach God's kingdom to other towns too because he had been sent for this purpose. Luke follows him on to the synagogues where that work continued."], "lesson": "Jesus gives personal attention to sufferers while keeping his larger mission before him. Real need in one place does not cancel the call to serve elsewhere.", "question": "What reason does Jesus give when the people want him to stay?"},
  {"id": 91117, "title": "Nets That Nearly Break", "subtitle": "A call that reaches past unworthiness", "text": "Luke 5:1–16", "chapters": [[41, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["People pressed around Jesus beside the lake of Gennesaret to hear God's word. Two boats were at the shore, and the fishermen were washing their nets. Jesus got into Simon's boat and asked him to put out a little from the land. He sat there and taught the people. When he finished, he told Simon to put out into deeper water and let down the nets.", "Simon explained that they had worked all night and caught nothing, but he said he would lower the net at Jesus' word. They caught so many fish that the net began to break. They signalled their partners in the other boat. Both boats filled until they began to sink. Simon Peter fell at Jesus' knees and asked him to depart, saying he was a sinful man. James and John, who were his partners, shared his astonishment.", "Jesus told Simon not to fear and said that from then on he would catch people. When they brought the boats to land, they left everything and followed him. The catch at the end of the night's work became the occasion for a different calling.", "In one of the towns a man covered with leprosy fell on his face before Jesus. He said that if Jesus was willing, he could make him clean. Jesus stretched out his hand, touched him and cleansed him. He sent the man to the priest with the offering Moses required. The report spread, crowds came for teaching and healing, and Jesus withdrew into deserted places to pray."], "lesson": "Simon and the man with leprosy each approach Jesus conscious of a barrier. Jesus answers one with a calling and the other with a touch.", "question": "Why does Simon's response to the catch go beyond gratitude for fish?"},
  {"id": 91118, "title": "A Man Lowered and a Tax Collector Called", "subtitle": "Forgiveness and a place at the table", "text": "Luke 5:17–39", "chapters": [[41, 5]], "start": 17, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus was teaching, with Pharisees and teachers of the law present from Galilee, Judea and Jerusalem. Some men carried a paralysed man on a bed and tried to bring him in. They could not get through the crowd, so they went onto the roof and lowered him through the tiles into the middle of the room. Jesus saw their faith and told the man his sins were forgiven.", "The scribes and Pharisees asked inwardly who could forgive sins except God. Jesus knew what they were thinking and asked which was easier to say, that the man's sins were forgiven or that he should get up and walk. To show his authority to forgive, he told him to rise, take his bed and go home. He did so in front of them and went away glorifying God. The crowd was amazed.", "Jesus went out and saw a tax collector named Levi sitting at the booth. He called him to follow. Levi left everything, rose and followed him, then made a great feast for him at his house. When people complained about Jesus eating with tax collectors and sinners, he answered with a physician's work among the sick and said he had come to call sinners to repentance.", "A question then arose about fasting. Jesus spoke of a bridegroom, a new patch and wineskins. The people at the feast were hearing how his presence shaped the practices they were accustomed to judging."], "lesson": "Jesus' choice to eat with people others shun belongs to his call to repentance. The healing and the feast both give visible form to his concern for people in need.", "question": "How does Jesus' picture of a physician explain his presence at Levi's table?"},
  {"id": 91119, "title": "A Sabbath Question and a Night of Prayer", "subtitle": "Mercy and the calling of the Twelve", "text": "Luke 6:1–19", "chapters": [[41, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["On a Sabbath Jesus and the disciples passed through grainfields. The disciples plucked heads of grain, rubbed them in their hands and ate. Pharisees asked why they were doing what they considered unlawful. Jesus recalled David eating the bread reserved for priests when he and his men were hungry. He declared the Son of Man Lord of the Sabbath.", "On another Sabbath Jesus taught in a synagogue where a man had a withered right hand. Scribes and Pharisees watched to see if Jesus would heal him so they could accuse him. Knowing their thoughts, Jesus had the man stand in the middle. He asked whether the Sabbath allowed doing good or harm, saving a life or destroying it. He told the man to stretch out his hand, and it was restored. His opponents were furious and discussed what they might do to him.", "In those days Jesus went out to a mountain and spent the whole night praying to God. When day came, he called his disciples and chose twelve of them, naming them apostles. Luke records their names, including Judas Iscariot, who became the betrayer.", "Jesus came down with them and stood on a level place. A large gathering of disciples and people from as far as Jerusalem and the coast of Tyre and Sidon had come to hear him and be healed. People reached out to touch him. The night of prayer and choice of the Twelve were followed by a public gathering full of need."], "lesson": "Luke places a night of prayer before a consequential choice. Public ministry and the calling of fellow workers arise alongside time spent before God.", "question": "What does Jesus do before choosing the Twelve, and how might that shape your own decisions?"},
  {"id": 91120, "title": "The Level Place and Two Foundations", "subtitle": "Mercy that reaches enemies", "text": "Luke 6:20–49", "chapters": [[41, 6]], "start": 20, "end": 49, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus looked at his disciples and spoke of people who were poor, hungry, weeping or rejected for the Son of Man's sake. He told them what they could hope for. He also spoke woes to people already satisfied with riches, food, laughter and public approval. The conditions people could see in the present did not tell the whole story of where their lives were going.", "He told those listening to love their enemies, bless people who cursed them and pray for those who mistreated them. He spoke of giving and lending, and asked what was distinctive about doing good only to those who could do good in return. He directed his hearers to the mercy of their Father and called them to be merciful. He addressed judging, forgiving and giving.", "Then Jesus spoke of a blind person leading another, and of someone trying to remove a speck from a brother's eye while overlooking a beam in his own. He used fruit to show how a tree could be known and speech to show what the heart was full of. He asked why people called him Lord while disregarding what he said.", "He described two houses. A person who heard and obeyed dug deep and laid a foundation on rock. A flood could not shake the house. The person who heard and failed to act built without a foundation, and the flood brought it down."], "lesson": "The risk Jesus describes is hearing strong teaching and leaving ordinary conduct untouched. Mercy towards a person who cannot repay us is one place obedience becomes visible.", "question": "What would doing one instruction from this sermon look like before the week ends?"},
  {"id": 91121, "title": "A Servant and a Widow's Son", "subtitle": "Authority exercised with compassion", "text": "Luke 7:1–17", "chapters": [[41, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["After Jesus finished speaking, he entered Capernaum. A centurion's servant, whom the officer valued, was gravely ill. The centurion sent Jewish elders to ask Jesus to come and heal him. The elders pleaded for him, saying he loved their people and had built their synagogue. Jesus went with them.", "Before he reached the house, the officer sent friends with a message. He did not think himself worthy to have Jesus under his roof or to approach him in person. He knew how an order could be obeyed at a distance, because he himself served under authority and gave orders to soldiers. He trusted that Jesus could speak the word and heal his servant. Jesus marvelled and said he had found no such faith in Israel. The messengers returned to find the servant well.", "Jesus then went to Nain. At the town gate he met a funeral procession. The man who had died was the only son of a widow, and a large company from the town was with her. Jesus saw her, had compassion and told her not to weep.", "He touched the bier and the men carrying it stood still. He told the young man to rise. The man sat up and began to speak, and Jesus gave him back to his mother. Fear came over the people, and they glorified God for visiting his people. News of the event spread."], "lesson": "In Nain Jesus notices a grieving person before anyone asks him for help. The centurion's confidence in his authority is followed by an account of the compassion with which he uses it.", "question": "What about the widow's situation does Luke take care to tell you?"},
  {"id": 91122, "title": "John's Messengers Bring a Question", "subtitle": "Taking questions to Jesus", "text": "Luke 7:18–35", "chapters": [[41, 7]], "start": 18, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["John's disciples reported these things to him. He called two of them and sent them to Jesus with a question. Was he the one who was to come, or should they look for someone else? They reached Jesus and repeated the question. Luke says that at that hour Jesus healed many people, delivered those troubled by evil spirits and gave sight to blind people.", "He told the messengers to return and tell John what they had seen and heard. The blind saw, the lame walked, people with leprosy were cleansed, the deaf heard, the dead were raised and good news was preached to the poor. He added a blessing for the person who did not stumble on account of him.", "After they left, Jesus spoke to the crowds about John. He asked what they had gone into the wilderness to see. John was more than a prophet, the messenger appointed to prepare the way. Luke notes that people who had received John's baptism acknowledged God's justice, while Pharisees and lawyers who had refused it rejected God's counsel for themselves.", "Jesus compared the generation to children complaining in the marketplace when others would neither dance nor mourn. John had lived austerely and been accused of having a demon. The Son of Man ate and drank and was called a glutton and a friend of sinners. Opposite ways of living had given people two excuses for withholding a response."], "lesson": "Jesus answers a serious question with evidence. His picture of children also exposes the habit of rejecting a messenger whatever he does.", "question": "What did Jesus give John's messengers to tell him on returning?"},
  {"id": 91123, "title": "Tears at Simon's Table", "subtitle": "Love that grows from forgiveness", "text": "Luke 7:36–50", "chapters": [[41, 7]], "start": 36, "end": 50, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Simon, a Pharisee, invited Jesus to eat with him. As Jesus reclined at the meal, a woman known in the city as a sinner entered with a jar of ointment. She stood behind him at his feet, weeping. Her tears wet his feet. She wiped them with her hair, kissed them and anointed them. Simon watched and reasoned to himself that a prophet ought to know who was touching him.", "Jesus told Simon he had something to say. Two people owed money to the same lender. One owed five hundred denarii and the other fifty. Neither could pay, and the lender cancelled both debts. Jesus asked which of the two would love him more. Simon supposed it would be the one whose larger debt had been cancelled, and Jesus said he had judged rightly.", "Jesus turned towards the woman and asked Simon whether he saw her. Simon had given him no water for his feet, while she had washed them with tears. He had given him no kiss of greeting, while she kept kissing his feet. He had not anointed Jesus' head with oil, while she had anointed his feet with ointment. Jesus drew a connection between forgiveness and the love made visible in her actions.", "Then he told the woman her sins were forgiven. The other guests wondered who he was to forgive sins. Jesus told her that her faith had saved her and sent her away in peace. Her last words from the table were addressed to her directly, after others had been speaking or thinking about her."], "lesson": "Simon classifies the woman by her past. Jesus makes her actions visible and asks Simon to consider the love that gratitude for mercy can produce.", "question": "Which acts of welcome does Jesus name when he turns Simon's attention to the woman?"},
  {"id": 91124, "title": "Women on the Road, Seed in the Soil", "subtitle": "Receiving the word and doing it", "text": "Luke 8:1–21", "chapters": [[41, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus went through towns and villages announcing the good news of God's kingdom. The Twelve went with him. Luke also names women who had been healed and supported the company from their resources: Mary Magdalene, Joanna the wife of Chuza, Susanna and many others. Their care belongs to the account of how the travelling ministry went forward.", "When people gathered from many towns, Jesus told them of a sower whose seed fell on a path, on rocky ground, among thorns and on good soil. The first seed was trampled and eaten by birds. The shallow ground could not hold moisture, and the thorns choked what grew among them. The good soil brought a crop. Jesus called on those with ears to hear.", "His disciples asked what the parable meant. He said the seed was God's word. Some people heard and lost it. Others received it with joy and fell away when tested. Cares, riches and pleasures kept some from bearing mature fruit. The good soil pictured people who held the word in an honest and good heart and bore fruit with patience.", "He spoke next of placing a lamp where people could see and called for care in how they heard. When his mother and brothers could not reach him through the crowd, he said his family were those who heard God's word and did it. The passage ends by joining hearing to doing once more."], "lesson": "Luke names people whose service made the travelling ministry possible. The teaching that follows calls for a response with the patience to bear fruit.", "question": "What does Jesus say marks the soil that brings fruit to maturity?"},
  {"id": 91125, "title": "Across the Storm to the Tombs", "subtitle": "Faith and testimony", "text": "Luke 8:22–39", "chapters": [[41, 8]], "start": 22, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus got into a boat with his disciples and told them to cross the lake. While they sailed he fell asleep. A storm came down upon the water, the boat began to fill and they were in danger. They woke him, crying that they were perishing. Jesus rebuked the wind and waves, and they stopped. He asked where their faith was. Afraid and astonished, they asked one another who he was, since winds and water obeyed him.", "When they landed opposite Galilee, a man from the town met Jesus. He had long been afflicted by demons, wore no clothes and lived among the tombs. People had kept him bound and watched, but he broke away and was driven into deserted places. As Jesus commanded the spirit to come out, he asked its name. The answer was Legion, because many demons had entered him.", "The demons begged to go into pigs feeding nearby. When Jesus allowed it, the herd rushed down the steep bank into the lake and drowned. The people who came after hearing the herdsmen's report found the man sitting at Jesus' feet, clothed and in his right mind. Fear seized them, and they asked Jesus to leave.", "The man asked to go with him. Jesus sent him home to tell what God had done for him. He went through the town telling what Jesus had done. The people who knew his former condition now had his own account of what had changed."], "lesson": "The restored man's neighbours have known his former condition. Jesus gives him a ministry among those who can see the change for themselves.", "question": "Why might the man's testimony matter particularly in his own home and town?"},
  {"id": 91126, "title": "The Woman Who Touched His Garment", "subtitle": "Care for a person hidden by the crowd", "text": "Luke 8:40–56", "chapters": [[41, 8]], "start": 40, "end": 56, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["When Jesus returned, the crowd welcomed him because they had been waiting for him. Jairus, a ruler of the synagogue, fell at his feet and pleaded with him to come to his house. His only daughter was about twelve and was dying. As Jesus went with him, the crowd pressed closely around him.", "A woman in the crowd had been bleeding for twelve years. She had found no one able to heal her. She came from behind and touched the edge of Jesus' garment, and the bleeding stopped. Jesus asked who had touched him. When people denied it, Peter pointed out that the crowd was pressing around him. Jesus said he knew that power had gone out from him. The woman came trembling, fell before him and told everyone why she had touched him and how she had been healed at once. He called her daughter and sent her away in peace.", "While Jesus was still speaking, someone came from Jairus's house and said the child had died. Jesus told Jairus to be unafraid and believe. At the house he admitted only Peter, John, James and the girl's parents. People were weeping, and they laughed when he spoke of her as sleeping.", "Jesus took the girl by the hand and told her to rise. Her spirit returned and she got up. He directed that she be given food. The parents were astonished, and Jesus told them to keep what happened to themselves."], "lesson": "Jesus gives both sufferers personal attention. The woman's public account allows the crowd to hear the peace he speaks to someone who could have gone away unnoticed.", "question": "What does the woman do after she realises that she cannot remain hidden?"},
  {"id": 91127, "title": "Sending the Twelve and Feeding the Crowd", "subtitle": "Serving from what God provides", "text": "Luke 9:1–17", "chapters": [[41, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus called the Twelve together and gave them authority over demons and diseases. He sent them to proclaim God's kingdom and heal the sick. They were to travel without spare supplies, stay in the house that received them and shake the dust from their feet when a town refused them. They went through the villages preaching and healing.", "Reports of what was happening reached Herod the tetrarch. Some said John had risen, others that Elijah had appeared or that one of the old prophets had risen. Herod knew he had beheaded John. He asked who this could be and wanted to see Jesus. Luke leaves that question with Herod and turns back to the apostles.", "They returned and told Jesus what they had done. He took them away towards Bethsaida, but the crowds found out and followed. Jesus welcomed them, taught them about the kingdom and healed those in need. As the day wore away, the Twelve wanted to send the people into surrounding villages for lodging and food. Jesus told the disciples to feed them.", "They counted five loaves and two fish. At Jesus' direction they seated the people in groups of about fifty. He looked towards heaven, blessed and broke the food and gave it to the disciples to set before the crowd. Everyone ate and was satisfied, and twelve baskets of pieces remained. The apostles who had been sent out without provisions now distributed a meal in a deserted place."], "lesson": "The disciples return from ministry and find another occasion to serve. Jesus welcomes the people who have interrupted their retreat and gives the disciples a part in feeding them.", "question": "How does Jesus respond to the crowd before there is any talk of bread?"},
  {"id": 91128, "title": "Peter's Answer and the Voice From the Cloud", "subtitle": "Hearing Jesus about the road ahead", "text": "Luke 9:18–36", "chapters": [[41, 9]], "start": 18, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus was praying apart from the crowd, with his disciples nearby. He asked who the crowds said he was. They reported that some said John the Baptist, others Elijah or an old prophet risen again. Then he asked the disciples for their own answer. Peter said he was the Christ of God.", "Jesus charged them to keep it to themselves and told them what the Son of Man would face. The elders, chief priests and scribes would reject him, he would be killed and he would rise on the third day. He spoke to all about denying themselves, taking up a cross daily and following him. He asked what a person would gain by winning the world and losing himself.", "About eight days after these sayings, Jesus took Peter, John and James up a mountain to pray. While he prayed, his appearance changed and his clothing became bright. Moses and Elijah appeared in glory and spoke with him about the departure he was to accomplish at Jerusalem. The disciples, heavy with sleep, became fully awake and saw his glory.", "As the men were leaving, Peter suggested three shelters without knowing what he was saying. A cloud overshadowed them, and a voice directed them to hear Jesus, God's beloved Son. Then they saw Jesus alone. They kept silent about what they had seen. Luke has let the disciples hear about Jerusalem twice, once from Jesus himself and once in the conversation on the mountain."], "lesson": "The conversation on the mountain concerns the road Jesus has already described. Glory and the costly work awaiting him belong together in Luke's account.", "question": "What subjects connect Jesus' first prediction with the conversation on the mountain?"},
  {"id": 91129, "title": "From the Mountain Towards Jerusalem", "subtitle": "Humility and a settled purpose", "text": "Luke 9:37–62", "chapters": [[41, 9]], "start": 37, "end": 62, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["The following day, when they came down from the mountain, a crowd met Jesus. A father cried out for help for his only child. A spirit caused the boy great suffering, and the disciples had been unable to drive it out. As the boy came towards Jesus, the spirit threw him down. Jesus rebuked it, healed the boy and gave him back to his father.", "People marvelled, but Jesus told the disciples to listen carefully: the Son of Man would be handed over. They did not understand and were afraid to ask. When an argument arose about who was greatest, Jesus set a child beside him and spoke about receiving the child in his name. John then told him the disciples had tried to stop someone acting in Jesus' name. Jesus told them not to stop him.", "As the time drew near for Jesus to be taken up, he set his face towards Jerusalem. Messengers entered a Samaritan village, but the people would not receive him because he was headed for Jerusalem. James and John asked about calling down fire. Jesus rebuked them, and they went to another village.", "On the road, several people spoke of following him or were called to follow. Jesus spoke about having nowhere to lay his head, the urgency of proclaiming God's kingdom and the danger of looking back after putting a hand to the plough. The journey to Jerusalem had begun, and his words pressed the question of what it meant to go with him."], "lesson": "Several scenes show the disciples using rank, group identity or power to settle questions Jesus handles differently. His settled direction towards Jerusalem gives shape to his replies about following.", "question": "What responses does Jesus correct as the disciples move from the mountain to the road?"},
  {"id": 91130, "title": "The Messengers Return Rejoicing", "subtitle": "Joy rooted in belonging to God", "text": "Luke 10:1–24", "chapters": [[41, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus appointed another group of messengers and sent them in pairs to the places he intended to visit. He told them the harvest was plentiful and the workers few, and asked them to pray for workers. They were going out like lambs among wolves. They were to travel simply, speak peace upon entering a house and remain with those who received them, accepting what was set before them.", "In a town that welcomed them they were to care for the sick and announce that God's kingdom had come near. Where people refused them, they were to tell them plainly and leave. Jesus spoke of the seriousness of such refusal and of how receiving or rejecting the messengers concerned the one who had sent them.", "The messengers returned with joy because even demons had been subject to them in Jesus' name. He spoke of Satan falling and of authority given to them. Then he directed their joy towards a different fact: their names were written in heaven.", "Jesus rejoiced and thanked the Father for revealing things to little children that were hidden from the wise and understanding. He spoke of the Son making the Father known. Turning privately to the disciples, he called their eyes blessed for seeing what prophets and kings had longed to see. The people who set out as workers came back with reason to give thanks for what they themselves had received."], "lesson": "The messengers have good reason to rejoice in what they have seen. Jesus directs them to a more lasting ground for joy than results they can report.", "question": "What reason for rejoicing does Jesus place above the success of their mission?"},
  {"id": 91131, "title": "The Good Samaritan", "subtitle": "Becoming a neighbour through mercy", "text": "Luke 10:25–37", "chapters": [[41, 10]], "start": 25, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A lawyer stood up to test Jesus by asking what he must do to inherit eternal life. Jesus asked what was written in the law and how he read it. The lawyer answered that a person should love God with heart, soul, strength and mind, and love his neighbour as himself. Jesus told him to do this and live. Wanting to justify himself, the lawyer asked who his neighbour was.", "Jesus told him about a man travelling from Jerusalem down to Jericho. Robbers stripped him, wounded him and left him half dead. A priest came down the road and passed on the other side when he saw him. A Levite also came to the place, looked at the man and passed by. The man remained where the robbers had left him.", "Then a Samaritan came along the road. He saw the wounded man and had compassion. He bandaged the wounds, pouring on oil and wine, put the man on his own animal and took him to an inn. There he cared for him. The next day he paid the innkeeper and asked him to continue the care, promising to repay any additional expense on his return.", "Jesus asked which of the three had proved a neighbour to the man who fell among robbers. The lawyer answered that it was the one who showed him mercy. Jesus told him to go and do likewise. The conversation that began by asking where the duty ended closed with a concrete example of what mercy required."], "lesson": "The Samaritan gives the injured man time, transport and money. Jesus directs the lawyer's attention to what it means to act as a neighbour when need is already in front of him.", "question": "Which of the Samaritan's actions required him to give up something of his own?"},
  {"id": 91132, "title": "At Martha's House", "subtitle": "Making room to listen to Jesus", "text": "Luke 10:38–42", "chapters": [[41, 10]], "start": 38, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["As Jesus travelled on, he entered a village, and a woman named Martha welcomed him into her house. Luke has just finished telling of a wounded traveller for whom practical care could not wait. Now he brings the reader into another home and another kind of need. Here the person who has been welcomed has something to say.", "Martha's sister Mary sat at Jesus' feet and listened to his words. Martha was occupied and distracted by much serving. Luke does not give a list of her tasks. He tells us how they affected her and how she brought the matter to Jesus.", "Martha asked whether he cared that Mary had left her to serve alone. She wanted him to tell her sister to help. Jesus addressed Martha by name twice. He said she was anxious and troubled about many things, and told her that Mary had chosen the good part, which would not be taken away from her.", "The account ends with his answer. Martha's welcome has given Jesus a place in the house. Mary's place at his feet shows the attention that a guest's words can require. The question Martha asked gives the reader a way to notice how helpful work can go on until the person being served is scarcely heard."], "lesson": "Martha's welcome is real, and so is the strain she feels. Jesus names the distraction that can crowd out listening even when the work began as hospitality.", "question": "What was Mary doing when Martha asked Jesus to intervene?"},
  {"id": 91133, "title": "Teach Us to Pray", "subtitle": "Asking the Father with confidence", "text": "Luke 11:1–13", "chapters": [[41, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus had been praying in a certain place. When he finished, one disciple asked him to teach them to pray, as John had taught his disciples. Jesus gave them a prayer directed to the Father. It asked for his name to be honoured and his kingdom to come. It brought daily bread, forgiveness and the need for deliverance from temptation into the same prayer. Those asking forgiveness were to remember the debts of people they themselves forgave.", "Jesus then asked them to imagine going to a friend at midnight to ask for three loaves. A traveller had arrived, and the host had nothing to give him. From inside, the friend said the door was shut and his children were already in bed. Jesus said the man would get up and give what was needed because the request kept being made.", "He urged his hearers to ask, seek and knock. The needs people carry into prayer could be spoken plainly and brought again. He then pictured a child asking a parent for food. A parent would understand the difference between a fish and a serpent or between an egg and a scorpion.", "If people who fell short knew how to give good gifts to their children, Jesus said, their heavenly Father would give the Holy Spirit to those who asked him. The passage begins with disciples watching someone pray and ends by giving them a reason to trust the one to whom they pray."], "lesson": "Jesus gives his disciples words for both large hopes and daily needs. The assurance of the Father's goodness gives persistence in prayer a personal basis.", "question": "How does the midnight request prepare you for what Jesus says about the Father?"},
  {"id": 91134, "title": "The Sign and the Lamp", "subtitle": "Taking care how you receive light", "text": "Luke 11:14–36", "chapters": [[41, 11]], "start": 14, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus drove out a demon that had kept a man from speaking. When the man spoke, the crowd marvelled. Some people said Jesus drove out demons by Beelzebub, their ruler. Others wanted a sign from heaven. Jesus knew their thoughts and asked how a kingdom divided against itself could stand. If he drove out demons by the finger of God, then God's kingdom had already come upon them.", "He pictured a strong man guarding his house until someone stronger overpowered him and took away his armour. He also spoke of a spirit that left a person and later returned with seven others, finding its former place swept and put in order. When a woman in the crowd called his mother blessed, Jesus spoke of the blessing of hearing God's word and keeping it.", "As the crowd thickened, Jesus answered the demand for a sign. He called attention to Jonah and to the Ninevites who repented when they heard him. He recalled the queen who travelled to hear Solomon's wisdom. Both examples asked his hearers what they had done with the person already before them.", "He ended with a lamp that belongs where people can see its light. Speaking of the eye as the lamp of the body, he told them to see that the light in them was not darkness. The passage begins with people disputing what they saw and ends with an instruction to attend to how they see."], "lesson": "A demand for another sign can keep a person from facing the evidence already present. Jesus calls attention to the condition of those who are looking.", "question": "What does Jesus say Nineveh did when it had heard Jonah?"},
  {"id": 91135, "title": "The Meal That Exposed Hidden Burdens", "subtitle": "Justice and love alongside religious care", "text": "Luke 11:37–54", "chapters": [[41, 11]], "start": 37, "end": 54, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A Pharisee invited Jesus to eat with him. When Jesus sat down without first washing, the host was surprised. Jesus spoke of people cleaning the outside of a cup and dish while leaving greed and wickedness within. The one who made the outside had also made the inside. The host had noticed a custom at the table, and Jesus directed his attention to matters at work beneath it.", "Jesus spoke of careful tithes on mint, rue and herbs alongside neglect of justice and the love of God. His hearers could attend to the one without leaving the other undone. He mentioned prominent seats and public greetings, then pictured unmarked graves that people walked over without knowing what lay beneath them.", "A lawyer objected that these words insulted his group too. Jesus replied that they placed burdens on people which they would not touch with a finger themselves. He recalled the prophets and messengers who had been killed or persecuted, and spoke of people building their tombs while sharing in the disposition of those who killed them.", "Jesus then charged the lawyers with taking away the key of knowledge. They had not entered, and they obstructed those who were entering. As he left, scribes and Pharisees began pressing him on many subjects, looking for something he might say that they could use against him. The invitation had become an occasion for examining what teachers were doing to the people they were meant to help."], "lesson": "A person can take care over visible duties and leave a neighbour under an unnecessary burden. Jesus connects knowledge and authority with responsibility for what they do to others.", "question": "Which matters did Jesus say were neglected while people paid attention to small tithes?"},
  {"id": 91136, "title": "Barns, Ravens and an Inheritance", "subtitle": "Trust when possessions promise security", "text": "Luke 12:1–34", "chapters": [[41, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A great crowd gathered, pressing upon one another. Jesus began by warning his disciples about the leaven of the Pharisees, which he called hypocrisy. What was concealed would come to light. He told his friends where to place their fear and reminded them that God knew even what became of sparrows. He also spoke of acknowledging him openly and of the Spirit helping them when they had to answer before authorities.", "Someone in the crowd asked him to tell a brother to divide an inheritance. Jesus declined to act as their judge and warned against covetousness. He told of a rich man whose land produced so much that he planned to pull down his barns and build larger ones. He expected to live at ease from what he stored. God told him that his life would be required that night. The man could make plans for his produce without knowing who would use it.", "Jesus turned to the disciples and spoke about food and clothing. Ravens did not sow or store food in barns, yet God fed them. Lilies did not labour or spin, yet Solomon was not clothed like one of them. Their Father knew what they needed.", "He told them to seek God's kingdom and reminded them of the Father's pleasure in giving it to them. He spoke of giving to people in need and of treasure safe from thieves and decay. Where their treasure was, there their hearts would be."], "lesson": "The man in the parable makes plans that centre on his own stores. Jesus gives his disciples a different way to reckon with provision, generosity and the future.", "question": "What did the rich man leave out of the conversation he had with himself?"},
  {"id": 91137, "title": "The Lamps and the Changing Weather", "subtitle": "Faithfulness while there is time", "text": "Luke 12:35–59", "chapters": [[41, 12]], "start": 35, "end": 59, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus pictured servants keeping their lamps lit while waiting for their master to come back from a wedding. Those he found watching would be blessed. Jesus gave the picture an unexpected turn: the master would seat those servants and serve them himself. He also spoke of a householder who would watch if he knew when a thief was coming. The Son of Man would come at an hour people did not expect.", "Peter asked whether the parable was for the disciples or everyone. Jesus described a steward charged with giving the household food at the proper time. A faithful steward would be found doing that work. Another might use the master's delay as permission to mistreat servants and indulge himself. The return would show what each had made of his responsibility. More was required of those who had been entrusted with more.", "Jesus spoke of division that would arise even among members of the same family. His coming would force decisions people in the same household might make differently. He spoke of the distress he himself faced as he approached what he called a baptism.", "Then he turned to the crowd. They could read signs of rain or heat in the weather, yet failed to judge the time they themselves were living in. He pictured two people on their way to court and urged settlement before they reached the judge. The road still gave them time to act."], "lesson": "The faithful steward uses his authority to care for people during the master's absence. Jesus' urgency concerns decisions a hearer can still make today.", "question": "What does the faithful steward give the household, and when does he give it?"},
  {"id": 91138, "title": "A Fig Tree Granted Time and a Woman Set Free", "subtitle": "Repentance and mercy without delay", "text": "Luke 13:1–17", "chapters": [[41, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Some people told Jesus about Galileans whom Pilate had killed while they were offering sacrifices. Jesus asked whether those Galileans were worse sinners than others because they had suffered in that way. He gave another example from Jerusalem, where a tower in Siloam fell and killed eighteen people. In both cases he turned the hearers from judging the victims to considering their own need to repent.", "He told of a man who came looking for fruit on a fig tree in his vineyard and found none. After three years he wanted it cut down. The gardener asked for another year to dig around it and manure it, with a decision to follow if it still bore no fruit. The extra time had a purpose.", "On a Sabbath Jesus was teaching in a synagogue. He saw a woman who had been bent over for eighteen years. He called her, told her she was released from her infirmity and laid his hands on her. She stood upright and glorified God. The synagogue ruler objected to healing on the Sabbath and told people to come on other days.", "Jesus asked whether people untied an ox or donkey to take it to water on the Sabbath. He called the woman a daughter of Abraham whom Satan had bound for eighteen years and asked why she should remain bound that day. His opponents were ashamed, and the crowd rejoiced at what he had done."], "lesson": "More time in the parable comes with attention to bearing fruit. In the synagogue Jesus uses the time at hand to relieve a person's suffering.", "question": "How do the gardener and Jesus each respond to a need that has lasted a long time?"},
  {"id": 91139, "title": "A Narrow Door and a Lament", "subtitle": "Responding while the door is open", "text": "Luke 13:18–35", "chapters": [[41, 13]], "start": 18, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus asked what God's kingdom was like. He compared it to a mustard seed a man put in his garden. It grew until birds lodged in its branches. He compared it also to leaven a woman worked into flour until it had spread through the whole amount. In both pictures the effect became visible after an inconspicuous beginning.", "On his way towards Jerusalem, someone asked whether only a few would be saved. Jesus urged the hearers to strive to enter through the narrow door. He pictured a householder closing it while people outside knocked and appealed to having eaten in his company and heard him teach in their streets. He spoke of people arriving from every direction to sit in God's kingdom and of first and last places being reversed.", "Later some Pharisees warned him that Herod wanted to kill him. Jesus called Herod a fox and spoke of continuing his work of deliverance and healing. He said he must go on towards Jerusalem and spoke of the city in connection with the deaths of prophets.", "Then he addressed Jerusalem with grief. He had longed to gather its children as a hen gathers her brood under her wings, and they had refused. He told them their house was left desolate. The passage closes with the longing of the person whose invitation had been resisted."], "lesson": "Jesus answers speculation with a call to respond. His lament gives us a view of the compassion behind the urgency of his words.", "question": "How does Jesus redirect the question about who will be saved?"},
  {"id": 91140, "title": "Places at the Table and an Open Invitation", "subtitle": "Hospitality that expects no repayment", "text": "Luke 14:1–24", "chapters": [[41, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["On a Sabbath Jesus went to eat in the house of a leading Pharisee. People watched him closely. A man suffering from swelling stood before him. Jesus asked the lawyers and Pharisees whether it was lawful to heal on the Sabbath. They kept silent. He healed the man and sent him away, then asked what they would do if an animal fell into a well on that day. They could not answer.", "Seeing the guests choose places of honour, Jesus described a person who took the best place and had to move when someone more distinguished arrived. He advised taking the lower place and letting the host decide whether to move the guest higher. Then he told the host to invite people who could not repay him, including the poor and people with disabilities.", "A guest spoke of the blessing of eating in God's kingdom. Jesus told of a man who prepared a great supper and sent word when it was ready. The people invited offered excuses. One had bought land, another had bought oxen, and another had married. Each had something else to attend to.", "The host told his servant to bring in people from the streets and lanes. There was still room, so he sent him farther out to bring in more. The meal where people had been choosing the best seats became the setting for a story about a host determined to fill his house with people others might pass by."], "lesson": "Jesus speaks to the people choosing seats and to the people choosing guests. His teaching gives hospitality a purpose beyond rank and repayment.", "question": "Who would receive an invitation if you followed Jesus' advice to the host?"},
  {"id": 91141, "title": "Before You Build the Tower", "subtitle": "Counting the cost of discipleship", "text": "Luke 14:25–35", "chapters": [[41, 14]], "start": 25, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Large crowds were travelling with Jesus. He turned to them and spoke about the terms of following him. He named father, mother, spouse, children, brothers and sisters, and even a person's own life. His words put every close attachment under the claim of discipleship. He spoke of carrying a cross and coming after him.", "He asked them to picture a man who intended to build a tower. The builder would sit down first and reckon whether he had enough to finish it. If he laid the foundation and could go no further, the people who saw it would mock him. His beginning would remain visible while the work he had undertaken stayed unfinished.", "Jesus gave another example, a king considering whether his ten thousand men could meet an approaching army of twenty thousand. If he could not, he would send a delegation to seek terms of peace while the other king was still far away. In both pictures the time to consider what lay ahead came before the decisive step.", "Jesus applied the pictures to what a disciple must be willing to give up. He spoke of salt that had lost its usefulness and could no longer season anything. He closed by calling on those with ears to hear. The crowd had already been walking with him, and he was asking what each person was prepared to do as the journey continued."], "lesson": "Jesus respects his hearers enough to tell them what following will demand. Counting the cost gives substance to the commitment they are considering.", "question": "Why does Jesus speak of the builder before saying what a disciple must be ready to give up?"},
  {"id": 91142, "title": "The Sheep and the Coin", "subtitle": "Joy in finding the lost", "text": "Luke 15:1–10", "chapters": [[41, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Tax collectors and others known as sinners drew near to hear Jesus. Pharisees and scribes complained that he received such people and ate with them. Jesus answered by asking his hearers to consider what they would do if they had a hundred sheep and lost one. A person would leave the ninety-nine where they were and search for the missing sheep until he found it.", "When he found it, he would lay it on his shoulders with joy. At home he would call his friends and neighbours and ask them to rejoice with him. Jesus said there would be joy in heaven over one sinner who repented. A person whom the hearers might regard as an unwelcome guest had a place in that account of joy.", "Jesus then asked about a woman with ten silver coins who lost one. She would light a lamp, sweep the house and search carefully until she found it. Finding it, she would call her friends and neighbours to share her joy. The work of searching takes place inside the house in this picture, with a lamp and a broom.", "Jesus again connected the joy with one person who repented, this time naming joy in the presence of God's angels. Both stories end with the finder inviting others to celebrate. The people who had complained about Jesus receiving sinners were given two pictures of how they might respond when someone lost was found."], "lesson": "Both searchers take the initiative. Jesus uses their joy to challenge people who resented his welcome of those who needed repentance.", "question": "What do the shepherd and the woman both do as soon as they find what was lost?"},
  {"id": 91143, "title": "The Father Goes Out to Both Sons", "subtitle": "Repentance, welcome and resentment", "text": "Luke 15:11–32", "chapters": [[41, 15]], "start": 11, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A father's younger son asked for his share of the estate. The father divided his property between the two sons, and the younger gathered what he had and went to a far country. He wasted it there. When a famine came, he found work feeding pigs and was hungry enough to want their food. No one gave him any. He remembered how well his father's hired servants were fed and resolved to go home, confess his sin and ask to be taken on as a servant.", "While the son was still a long way off, his father saw him and had compassion. He ran to him, embraced him and kissed him. The son began his confession. The father called for the best robe, a ring and shoes, and told the servants to prepare a feast. The son he spoke of as dead was alive again. The one who had been lost had been found.", "The older son was in the field. Coming near the house, he heard music and dancing and asked a servant what had happened. When he heard, he grew angry and refused to go in. His father went out and pleaded with him. The older son recounted his years of service and complained that he had never been given even a young goat for a feast with his friends.", "The father told him that he was always with him and that what remained was his. He returned to the reason for the celebration: the one who had come back was his brother. Jesus leaves the two sons and the father's words before the hearer without saying whether the older son went inside."], "lesson": "The father approaches both sons, each at a different distance from the feast. The older son's words help us notice the resentment that can grow alongside outward service.", "question": "What does the father say to the older son about why the feast is necessary?"},
  {"id": 91144, "title": "The Steward Facing Dismissal", "subtitle": "Stewardship with the future in view", "text": "Luke 16:1–18", "chapters": [[41, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus told his disciples about a rich man whose steward was accused of wasting his possessions. The man called the steward and asked him to give account, telling him that he could no longer hold the position. The steward considered his prospects. He felt unable to dig and ashamed to beg. He wanted people to receive him into their houses once he lost his post.", "He called his master's debtors in one by one. He asked the first what he owed, then told him to write a smaller amount for the oil. He did the same with another man's debt in wheat. The master commended the dishonest steward for acting shrewdly. The steward had recognised that the position he held would not last and acted with his future in mind.", "Jesus spoke of using worldly wealth with an eye to what endures. He made faithfulness in small things a test of faithfulness in greater things and asked who would entrust true riches to someone unfaithful with what belonged to another. He said that a servant could not serve both God and money.", "The Pharisees, who loved money, mocked him. Jesus said they justified themselves before people, but God knew their hearts. He spoke of the law and prophets in relation to the proclamation of the kingdom, affirmed that the law was not lightly set aside and ended with a saying about marriage and divorce."], "lesson": "The steward acts decisively because he knows an accounting is coming. Jesus connects that foresight with faithful conduct in the small responsibilities we already have.", "question": "Which qualities does Jesus demand of his hearers after describing the steward's shrewdness?"},
  {"id": 91145, "title": "The Man at the Gate", "subtitle": "Listening before the opportunity passes", "text": "Luke 16:19–31", "chapters": [[41, 16]], "start": 19, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus described a rich man who dressed in purple and fine linen and feasted splendidly every day. A poor man named Lazarus was laid at his gate, covered with sores and longing to eat the crumbs that fell from the rich man's table. Dogs came and licked his sores. The gate put the two lives close to one another while their circumstances remained far apart.", "Lazarus died and was carried by angels to Abraham's side. The rich man also died and was buried. In torment he looked up and saw Abraham with Lazarus far away. He called for Abraham to send Lazarus to dip the tip of his finger in water and cool his tongue.", "Abraham told him to remember what each had received in life, and said that a great gulf now lay between them which neither could cross. The rich man asked instead for Lazarus to warn his five brothers so they would not come to the same place. Even in this request he imagined Lazarus as someone who could be sent to serve his household.", "Abraham answered that the brothers had Moses and the prophets and should listen to them. The man insisted they would repent if someone went to them from the dead. Abraham said that if they would not hear Moses and the prophets, they would not be persuaded even if someone rose from the dead."], "lesson": "The gate places the rich man's responsibility near enough to see each day. Abraham's reply presses the living reader to act on the instruction already given.", "question": "What opportunity did the rich man have while Lazarus still lay at his gate?"},
  {"id": 91146, "title": "The One Who Returned", "subtitle": "Mercy received with gratitude", "text": "Luke 17:1–19", "chapters": [[41, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus warned his disciples that occasions for sin would come and gave a severe warning to anyone who caused one of the little ones to stumble. He told them to rebuke a brother who sinned and forgive him when he repented, even repeatedly. When the apostles asked for more faith, Jesus gave the picture of faith like a mustard seed. He also spoke of a servant who carried out his duties without treating them as a claim to special thanks.", "On the way to Jerusalem, Jesus passed along the region of Samaria and Galilee. As he entered a village, ten men with leprosy stood at a distance and called to him as Master, asking for mercy. When he saw them, he told them to go and show themselves to the priests. They went, and as they went they were cleansed.", "One of them saw that he had been healed, turned back and glorified God loudly. He fell at Jesus' feet and thanked him. Luke identifies him as a Samaritan. All ten had obeyed the instruction to go. This one returned to acknowledge what he had received.", "Jesus asked where the other nine were and why only this foreigner had come back to give glory to God. He told the man to rise and go, and spoke of the faith by which he had been made well. Luke brings the man back within speaking distance of the person to whom he had first called from far off."], "lesson": "All ten act on Jesus' instruction. One also turns back to acknowledge the mercy received. Gratitude gives us a way to remember who helped us.", "question": "What does the Samaritan do that brings him back to Jesus after the healing?"},
  {"id": 91147, "title": "The Kingdom and the Days of Noah", "subtitle": "Readiness in ordinary life", "text": "Luke 17:20–37", "chapters": [[41, 17]], "start": 20, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Pharisees asked Jesus when God's kingdom would come. He said it did not come in a way that allowed people to point to it here or there. The kingdom was in their midst. Jesus then spoke to his disciples about a time when they would long to see one of the days of the Son of Man.", "He warned them not to follow people who said he was here or there. The day of the Son of Man would be like lightning shining across the sky. First he must suffer and be rejected by that generation. Jesus then turned to the days of Noah, when people were eating, drinking and marrying until Noah entered the ark and the flood came.", "He recalled the days of Lot too. People ate, drank, bought, sold, planted and built. On the day Lot left Sodom, judgement came suddenly. Jesus said the revealing of the Son of Man would come in that way. He told his listeners to remember Lot's wife, and spoke of someone on a roof or in a field needing to leave without turning back for possessions.", "He spoke of people together when one was taken and another left. The disciples asked where, and Jesus replied with an image of birds gathering. The teaching returns the hearer to ordinary places and tasks and asks what readiness would mean in the midst of them."], "lesson": "Daily routines can occupy the whole field of attention. Jesus gives ordinary life a wider horizon by calling people to remember where their deepest loyalties lie.", "question": "Which everyday activities does Jesus name, and what do they reveal about the suddenness of what followed?"},
  {"id": 91148, "title": "The Widow Who Kept Coming", "subtitle": "Prayer without losing heart", "text": "Luke 18:1–8", "chapters": [[41, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 8, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Luke tells why Jesus gave this parable before he tells the story. His hearers should pray and should not lose heart. The person he puts at the centre of it is a widow who has a case against an adversary. The judge in her town fears neither God nor people. She keeps coming to him, asking him to give her justice.", "For a time he will not act. Then he considers the matter privately. He acknowledges that he still does not fear God or respect people. What changes his decision is the widow's repeated visits. He decides to give her justice because she keeps troubling him. The widow wins a hearing from someone who gives her little reason to expect it.", "Jesus asks his hearers to take notice of what that judge said. Then he turns from the judge's motives to God's care for his chosen people who cry to him day and night. He asks whether God will fail to give them justice and assures them that he will act. The parable gives people facing a long wait reason to keep bringing their case to him.", "Jesus ends with a question about whether the Son of Man will find faith on earth when he comes. Luke has already told the reader what kind of conduct the story is meant to encourage. The question puts that conduct before the people who must keep praying during the interval."], "lesson": "Jesus introduces the story as instruction to pray and persevere. The widow's persistence gives shape to a kind of faith that continues through delay.", "question": "What does the judge admit about his motives, and how does Jesus contrast his response with God's care?"},
  {"id": 91149, "title": "Two Prayers and the Children", "subtitle": "Coming to God without a claim to rank", "text": "Luke 18:9–17", "chapters": [[41, 18]], "start": 9, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus told a parable to people who trusted in their own righteousness and looked down on others. Two men went up to the temple to pray. One was a Pharisee and the other a tax collector. The Pharisee stood and thanked God that he was unlike people he regarded as unjust, including the tax collector. He spoke of fasting twice a week and giving tithes from what he received.", "The tax collector stood far off. He would not lift his eyes to heaven. He beat his breast and asked God for mercy as a sinner. His short prayer placed his own condition before God. Jesus said this man went down to his house justified, and spoke of the proud being humbled and the humble being exalted.", "Then people brought even their infants for Jesus to touch. When the disciples saw, they rebuked the people bringing them. Jesus called the children to him and told the disciples to let them come. God's kingdom belonged to such as these. He said that a person must receive it like a child in order to enter it.", "The two scenes give the reader different places to see what it means to come before God. In the temple one man has a record to speak about and another asks for mercy. The children come in the arms of others and are welcomed by Jesus."], "lesson": "The tax collector brings a need before God. Jesus' welcome of the children gives dependence a visible form and challenges those who think they must establish their standing first.", "question": "What is each man relying on as he prays?"},
  {"id": 91150, "title": "Two Men Asking Jesus a Question", "subtitle": "Seeing what stands in the way", "text": "Luke 18:18–43", "chapters": [[41, 18]], "start": 18, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A ruler asked Jesus what he must do to inherit eternal life. Jesus spoke of God's goodness and named commandments concerning adultery, murder, stealing, false testimony and honouring parents. The ruler said he had kept these from his youth. Jesus told him he still lacked one thing. He should sell what he had, distribute it to the poor and follow him. The man became very sorrowful because he was very rich.", "Jesus spoke about the difficulty wealth could bring in entering God's kingdom, using the image of a camel and a needle's eye. When people asked who could be saved, he said what was impossible with people was possible with God. Peter spoke of what the disciples had left, and Jesus answered with a promise about the life of those who leave things for God's kingdom.", "Jesus took the Twelve aside and told them what awaited him in Jerusalem. The prophets' words would be fulfilled. He would be mistreated and killed and would rise. Luke says the disciples did not understand what he was telling them.", "Near Jericho a blind man heard a crowd passing and asked what it meant. Told it was Jesus, he cried out to the Son of David for mercy. People tried to hush him, but he cried louder. Jesus stopped and asked what he wanted. The man asked to see. He received his sight and followed Jesus, glorifying God. The people who saw it praised God as well."], "lesson": "The ruler becomes very sad after hearing what following would cost. The healed man follows and praises God. Their responses prompt reflection on what we do after Jesus answers.", "question": "How does the blind man's response differ from that of the ruler?"},
  {"id": 91151, "title": "Jesus Stops Under Zacchaeus's Tree", "subtitle": "Repentance that makes restitution", "text": "Luke 19:1–10", "chapters": [[41, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus was passing through Jericho. Zacchaeus, a wealthy chief tax collector, wanted to see who he was, but the crowd stood in his way and he was short. He ran ahead and climbed a sycamore tree along the road Jesus would take. From there he could look down as the people came past.", "When Jesus reached the place, he looked up and called Zacchaeus by name. He told him to come down quickly because he must stay at his house that day. Zacchaeus came down at once and received him with joy. The people who saw it began to complain. In their eyes Jesus had gone to be the guest of a sinner.", "Zacchaeus stood and spoke to the Lord about his possessions and his dealings with people. He said he would give half his goods to the poor and restore fourfold if he had taken anything by false accusation. Luke records the amounts. Zacchaeus's words concern things that could be returned, shared and counted. The people who had complained could hear that his response involved the way he used his wealth and the debts of justice that wrongdoing leaves behind.", "Jesus announced that salvation had come to that house and called Zacchaeus a son of Abraham. He then explained the purpose behind the visit: the Son of Man had come to seek and save the lost. Zacchaeus had climbed to get a look at Jesus. By the end of the encounter it was Jesus who had sought him out, spoken his name and entered his house."], "lesson": "Jesus' welcome comes before the public promise of restitution. Zacchaeus then names ways to repair wrongdoing that the people affected can actually experience.", "question": "What does Zacchaeus say he will give to the poor and restore to those he wronged?"},
  {"id": 91152, "title": "The Nobleman and the Money Entrusted", "subtitle": "Using what you have while the king is away", "text": "Luke 19:11–27", "chapters": [[41, 19]], "start": 11, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus was near Jerusalem, and people who heard him supposed God's kingdom would appear immediately. He told them about a nobleman who travelled to a distant country to receive a kingdom and return. Before leaving, he called ten servants, gave them money and told them to put it to work until he came back. Some of his citizens hated him and sent word that they did not want him to reign over them.", "When he returned as king, he called the servants to learn what each had gained. One said the sum entrusted to him had earned ten more. The king commended him for being faithful in a small matter and gave him authority over ten cities. Another reported a gain of five and received authority over five cities.", "Another servant brought back the money he had wrapped in a cloth. He said he had been afraid because he regarded the nobleman as a severe man. The king asked why the servant's own account of him had not at least led him to put the money in a bank so that it could earn interest.", "He ordered the money taken from that servant and given to the one who had ten. He also ordered judgement on the citizens who had rejected his rule. The parable made room for an interval before the king's return, during which his servants already had something to do and his opponents had made their position clear."], "lesson": "Jesus places responsibility inside the waiting period. The servants' work takes place before they can see the nobleman return and settle accounts.", "question": "What does the master ask the servants to do with the time and resources they have?"},
  {"id": 91153, "title": "Praise on the Road and Tears for the City", "subtitle": "Recognising the time of visitation", "text": "Luke 19:28–48", "chapters": [[41, 19]], "start": 28, "end": 48, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus went on towards Jerusalem. Near Bethphage and Bethany by the Mount of Olives, he sent two disciples to fetch a colt no one had ridden. When its owners asked why they were untying it, they said the Lord needed it. They brought it to Jesus, put their garments on it and set him on it. As he rode, people spread their clothes on the road.", "As he came down the Mount of Olives, the multitude of disciples began praising God for the mighty works they had seen. They blessed the king coming in the Lord's name and spoke of peace in heaven. Some Pharisees asked Jesus to rebuke his disciples. He answered that if they kept quiet, the stones would cry out.", "When the city came into view, Jesus wept over it. He spoke of the things that would have brought it peace and said they were now hidden from its eyes. He described enemies surrounding it and the ruin ahead because it had failed to recognise the time of its visitation.", "Jesus went into the temple and drove out those trading there. He recalled its purpose as a house of prayer and said it had been made a den of thieves. He taught there daily. The leaders looked for a way to destroy him, but could find none for the time being, because the people listened attentively to him."], "lesson": "The praise of the disciples and Jesus' grief belong to the same arrival. His tears reveal how deeply he cares about a city that fails to receive what he brings.", "question": "What does Jesus see about Jerusalem that the rejoicing crowd may have missed?"},
  {"id": 91154, "title": "Who Gave You Authority?", "subtitle": "Giving God what belongs to God", "text": "Luke 20:1–26", "chapters": [[41, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["While Jesus taught and proclaimed good news in the temple, chief priests and others asked what authority he had. He asked them in return whether John's baptism came from heaven or from people. They weighed the consequences of answering either way and declared they did not know. Jesus then declined to tell them by what authority he acted.", "He told the people about a man who planted a vineyard, leased it to tenants and went away. The tenants beat or wounded the servants he sent for fruit and sent them away empty. At last the owner sent his beloved son. The tenants recognised him as the heir, threw him out and killed him. Jesus spoke of the owner's coming judgement and asked about the stone rejected by builders that became the cornerstone. The leaders knew the parable concerned them.", "They watched him and sent spies pretending to be sincere, hoping to bring his words to the governor. The spies praised his teaching and asked whether it was lawful to pay tribute to Caesar. Jesus saw what they intended and asked to see a coin. He asked whose image and inscription it carried.", "When they said it was Caesar's, he told them to give Caesar what belonged to Caesar and God what belonged to God. They could not catch him in his words before the people. They marvelled at his answer and kept quiet."], "lesson": "The tenants treat an entrusted vineyard as if it were theirs to seize. The question about the coin gives the hearer another opportunity to consider what belongs to God.", "question": "What do the tenants refuse to acknowledge when the owner sends for fruit?"},
  {"id": 91155, "title": "The Living God and Honour in Public", "subtitle": "Reading Scripture with humility", "text": "Luke 20:27–47", "chapters": [[41, 20]], "start": 27, "end": 47, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Sadducees, who said there was no resurrection, came to Jesus with a question. They quoted the law about a man marrying his brother's widow if the brother died childless. Then they imagined seven brothers who married one woman in succession and each died without children. When the woman died too, whose wife would she be in the resurrection?", "Jesus said that people in this age marry, but those counted worthy of the resurrection do not live under the same conditions. They cannot die any more. He appealed to what Moses wrote about the bush, where the Lord called himself the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. He spoke of God as the God of the living, for all live to him. Some scribes approved of his answer, and people no longer dared question him.", "Jesus then asked how people could say Christ was David's son when David himself, in the Psalms, called him Lord. He spoke of the place at God's right hand until the enemies were made a footstool. The people who had brought a question about reading Moses now heard a question about reading David.", "With the people listening, Jesus warned his disciples about scribes who liked long robes, greetings and seats of honour. He also said they devoured widows' houses and made long prayers for appearance. Their conduct towards vulnerable people mattered more than the respect the crowds were accustomed to giving them."], "lesson": "Jesus gives Scripture careful attention and condemns the use of a religious appearance to hide harm. Humility in interpretation should be joined to honesty in conduct.", "question": "What activity did Jesus warn could be used to cover harm to widows?"},
  {"id": 91156, "title": "Two Small Coins and Great Stones", "subtitle": "Faithfulness when appearances change", "text": "Luke 21:1–19", "chapters": [[41, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus looked up and saw rich people putting gifts into the temple treasury. He also saw a poor widow put in two small coins. He told his hearers she had put in more than all the others. They had given from what they had to spare. She had given all she had to live on.", "Some spoke admiringly of the temple's fine stones and gifts. Jesus told them the days would come when the stones would be thrown down. They asked when it would happen and what sign would come first. He warned against being deceived by people claiming his name or saying the time had arrived. Wars and disturbances should not make them panic.", "Jesus spoke of conflicts between nations, earthquakes, famines and fearful signs. He also told the disciples that, before these things, they would be seized and brought before authorities. That would become an occasion for testimony. He urged them not to decide beforehand how to defend themselves, promising words and wisdom their opponents could not withstand.", "They might be betrayed by relatives or friends and hated on account of his name. Jesus gave them reason to endure. The discussion of the temple's future gives the disciples a way to think about their own conduct if the structures and protections around them begin to fail."], "lesson": "The widow's small gift receives Jesus' notice beside a building that appears permanent. His teaching gives lasting weight to faithfulness in moments others may discount.", "question": "What does Jesus see in the widow's gift that a person admiring the temple might overlook?"},
  {"id": 91157, "title": "Jerusalem Surrounded and the Call to Watch", "subtitle": "Prayer when hearts grow heavy", "text": "Luke 21:20–38", "chapters": [[41, 21]], "start": 20, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus told his hearers that when they saw Jerusalem surrounded by armies, they would know its desolation was near. People in Judea should flee to the mountains, and people inside the city should leave. He spoke of days of distress in which some would die and others be taken captive. Jerusalem would be trampled until the times of the Gentiles were fulfilled.", "He spoke next of signs in the sun, moon and stars, of distress among nations and of people fainting from fear. The Son of Man would be seen coming in a cloud with power and great glory. Jesus told his disciples to lift their heads when these things began because their redemption was drawing near.", "He pointed them to fig trees and other trees. New growth was enough to tell people that summer was near. In the same way, they were to recognise when God's kingdom was near. Jesus said that heaven and earth would pass away, but his words would remain.", "Then he directed his hearers to attend to their own hearts. Excess, drunkenness and the cares of life could weigh them down and leave them unready. They should watch and pray. Luke ends with Jesus teaching in the temple by day and spending the nights at the Mount of Olives. The people came early in the morning to hear him."], "lesson": "The trouble Jesus describes is public, but he identifies a danger within the hearer as well. Prayer and alertness keep cares from occupying the heart entirely.", "question": "Which things does Jesus say can weigh down a person's heart?"},
  {"id": 91158, "title": "The Meal Prepared in Jerusalem", "subtitle": "Receiving and remembering Jesus' gift", "text": "Luke 22:1–23", "chapters": [[41, 22]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Passover drew near. The chief priests and scribes looked for a way to kill Jesus, though they feared the people. Judas Iscariot, one of the Twelve, went to consult them and the officers about handing him over. They agreed to give him money, and he looked for a chance to betray him away from the crowd.", "When the day came for preparing the Passover, Jesus sent Peter and John. They asked where they should prepare it. He told them to enter the city and follow a man carrying a water jar. At the house he entered, they were to ask for the guest room. They found the furnished upper room as Jesus had said and prepared the meal.", "Jesus sat down with the apostles and told them he had earnestly desired to eat this Passover with them before suffering. He spoke of the kingdom to come. He took bread, gave thanks and broke it, and gave it to them as his body given for them, calling them to remember him. After supper he spoke of the cup as the new covenant in his blood poured out for them.", "Then he said the hand of the one betraying him was with him at the table. The Son of Man would go as determined, but he pronounced woe on the one who handed him over. The apostles began asking among themselves who could be about to do such a thing."], "lesson": "Luke places a meal of remembrance in a setting where betrayal is already under way. Jesus speaks of giving himself while those around him are questioning what one of them might do.", "question": "What instructions did Peter and John follow before the meal where Jesus spoke of remembering him?"},
  {"id": 91159, "title": "A Servant at the Table and Prayer on the Mount", "subtitle": "Praying through weakness", "text": "Luke 22:24–46", "chapters": [[41, 22]], "start": 24, "end": 46, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A dispute arose among the apostles about who was greatest. Jesus described kings who exercised authority and were called benefactors. Among his followers, the one who led should be like one who served. He asked them to consider what they saw at a table and spoke of himself being among them as one who served. He also told them they had remained with him in his trials.", "Then he spoke directly to Simon. He said Satan had asked to sift them like wheat and that he had prayed for Simon's faith. When Simon turned back, he was to strengthen his brothers. Peter said he was ready to go to prison and death with him. Jesus told him he would deny knowing him three times before the rooster crowed. He then spoke to the disciples about conditions changing and told them that Scripture concerning him would be fulfilled.", "They went to the Mount of Olives as usual. Jesus told them to pray against temptation and withdrew a short distance. On his knees he asked his Father to take away the cup if he was willing, and yielded to the Father's will. Luke describes an angel strengthening him as he prayed in anguish.", "Jesus found the disciples sleeping from sorrow. He asked why they slept and told them to rise and pray. The warning he had given on arrival was the last thing he said to them there before the arrest began."], "lesson": "Jesus sees the weakness behind Peter's confident words. He also looks ahead to Peter's return and gives him the work of strengthening others.", "question": "What does Jesus ask Peter to do after he has turned back?"},
  {"id": 91160, "title": "A Healed Ear and a Remembered Word", "subtitle": "Mercy and truth at the point of failure", "text": "Luke 22:47–71", "chapters": [[41, 22]], "start": 47, "end": 71, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["While Jesus was still speaking to his disciples, a crowd approached, led by Judas, one of the Twelve. Judas came near to kiss him. Jesus asked whether he was betraying the Son of Man with a kiss. The people around Jesus asked if they should strike with the sword. One struck the high priest's servant and cut off his right ear. Jesus stopped them, touched the man's ear and healed him.", "Jesus addressed the chief priests, officers and elders who had come. Why had they come with weapons as if against a robber, when they had seen him daily in the temple? He spoke of their hour and the power of darkness. They took him to the high priest's house. Peter followed at a distance and sat near a fire in the courtyard.", "A servant girl recognised Peter. He denied knowing Jesus. Two others challenged him in turn, and he denied it again. While he was still speaking the third time, the rooster crowed. Jesus turned and looked at him. Peter remembered what Jesus had said and went outside and wept bitterly.", "The men holding Jesus mocked and struck him. At daybreak the council gathered and questioned him about being the Christ. Jesus spoke of the Son of Man sitting at God's right hand. They asked whether he was God's Son, and he answered. The council said it needed no further testimony."], "lesson": "Jesus heals someone on the side arresting him. Peter receives a look that brings the truth of Jesus' words home to him.", "question": "Which moments in this passage show Jesus giving attention to people around him while he himself is under arrest?"},
  {"id": 91161, "title": "Between Pilate and Herod", "subtitle": "Responsibility for an unjust decision", "text": "Luke 23:1–25", "chapters": [[41, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["The council brought Jesus to Pilate. They accused him of misleading the nation, forbidding tribute to Caesar and claiming to be Christ a king. Pilate asked if he was King of the Jews. Jesus answered him, and Pilate told the leaders and crowd he found no fault in him. They pressed the charge and said his teaching had stirred people from Galilee to Jerusalem.", "When Pilate heard he was a Galilean, he sent him to Herod, who was also in Jerusalem. Herod was glad to see Jesus because he had heard of him and hoped to see a miracle. He questioned him at length, but Jesus gave him no answer. The leaders accused him fiercely. Herod and his soldiers mocked him, dressed him in a splendid robe and sent him back. Luke notes that Pilate and Herod became friends that day.", "Pilate called the leaders and people together. He said that neither he nor Herod had found Jesus guilty of anything deserving death. He proposed punishing and releasing him. The crowd called for Barabbas instead, a man imprisoned for insurrection and murder.", "Pilate addressed them again, wanting to release Jesus. They kept demanding crucifixion. A third time he asked what evil Jesus had done. Their voices prevailed. Pilate released the prisoner they requested and delivered Jesus to their will."], "lesson": "Pilate's own stated finding makes his eventual decision harder to excuse. The sequence asks how far a person will carry a truthful judgement when the people around him insist otherwise.", "question": "What does Pilate say he found, and what does he finally do?"},
  {"id": 91162, "title": "The Road to the Cross and the Man Beside Jesus", "subtitle": "A plea for mercy at the last hour", "text": "Luke 23:26–43", "chapters": [[41, 23]], "start": 26, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["As Jesus was led away, those taking him seized Simon of Cyrene, who was coming in from the country, and put the cross on him to carry behind Jesus. A large crowd followed, including women who wept for him. Jesus turned and spoke to them as daughters of Jerusalem. He told them to weep for themselves and their children, and spoke of hard days ahead.", "Two criminals were led out with him. At the place called Calvary they crucified Jesus with one on either side. Jesus prayed to the Father for forgiveness for the people doing this. Those there divided his clothes by lot. Rulers mocked him for saving others and challenged him to save himself. Soldiers also mocked him. A notice above him named him King of the Jews.", "One criminal joined the taunts and demanded that Jesus save himself and them. The other rebuked him. He acknowledged that the two of them were receiving what their actions deserved and said Jesus had done nothing wrong. Then he asked Jesus to remember him when he came into his kingdom.", "Jesus answered that the man would be with him in paradise that day. Luke leaves both men's voices in the account. They were in the same condition beside Jesus and addressed him differently, and the second received an answer from the one he had asked to remember him."], "lesson": "The second man brings no record of service to commend himself. He acknowledges the truth about his condition and addresses Jesus with a plain request.", "question": "How does the second criminal describe himself and Jesus before making his request?"},
  {"id": 91163, "title": "The Last Prayer and the Borrowed Tomb", "subtitle": "Faithful care in grief", "text": "Luke 23:44–56", "chapters": [[41, 23]], "start": 44, "end": 56, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["About noon darkness came over the land and lasted until the middle of the afternoon. The temple curtain tore. Jesus cried aloud to the Father and committed his spirit into his hands. Then he died. A centurion watching what happened glorified God and called Jesus righteous. People who had gathered to watch went home beating their breasts.", "Those who knew Jesus and the women who had followed from Galilee stood at a distance and saw these things. Luke then introduces Joseph of Arimathea, a good and just man who belonged to the council but had not agreed with its decision or action. He was waiting for God's kingdom.", "Joseph went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. He took it down, wrapped it in linen and laid it in a tomb cut from rock where no one had yet been laid. It was the day of preparation, and the Sabbath was drawing near. The burial had to take place within the time available.", "The women who had come with Jesus from Galilee followed and saw the tomb and how his body was laid. They returned and prepared spices and ointments. Then they rested on the Sabbath according to the commandment. The passage ends with the place of burial known to the women and with their preparations waiting for the next day on which they could come."], "lesson": "Joseph uses what opportunity remains to care for Jesus' body. The women attend to the burial carefully enough to return to the place.", "question": "What does Luke tell you about Joseph's earlier decision and his action after Jesus dies?"},
  {"id": 91164, "title": "The Women Remember His Words", "subtitle": "Hope remembered amid confusion", "text": "Luke 24:1–12", "chapters": [[41, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Early on the first day of the week, the women brought the spices they had prepared to the tomb. They found the stone rolled away. When they went in, they did not find the body of the Lord Jesus and did not know what to make of it. Two men in shining garments stood beside them. The women were afraid and bowed their faces to the ground.", "The men asked why they were looking for the living among the dead. Jesus had risen. They reminded the women what he had told them in Galilee: the Son of Man would be handed over, crucified and rise on the third day. The women remembered his words. They had come to a place they knew and now had to understand it in light of something they had heard before.", "They returned and told the eleven and the rest what had happened. Luke names Mary Magdalene, Joanna and Mary the mother of James, with the other women who brought the news. Their words seemed like nonsense to the apostles, who did not believe them.", "Peter got up and ran to the tomb. He stooped to look and saw the linen cloths by themselves. Then he went away wondering at what had happened. The women's report had been dismissed, yet Peter's visit left him with something he had now seen for himself."], "lesson": "The messengers draw the women's attention to words they have already heard. Those words allow them to understand the empty place before others believe their report.", "question": "Which part of Jesus' earlier teaching do the women remember at the tomb?"},
  {"id": 91165, "title": "A Stranger on the Road to Emmaus", "subtitle": "Reading disappointment in the light of Scripture", "text": "Luke 24:13–35", "chapters": [[41, 24]], "start": 13, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Two disciples were walking from Jerusalem to Emmaus that day, talking over the things that had happened. Jesus drew near and went with them, but they were kept from recognising him. He asked what they were discussing. Cleopas wondered whether the stranger was the only visitor to Jerusalem who had not heard. When Jesus asked what things, they told him about his own ministry, his condemnation and his death. They had hoped he would redeem Israel. They had also heard the women's report about the empty tomb and the angels, but their companions had not seen him.", "Jesus answered by taking them through Moses and the prophets. He showed them how the Christ's suffering and entrance into glory belonged together. They were nearing the village and he appeared ready to go farther. They urged him to stay because evening was coming. He entered and sat down with them.", "At the table he took bread, blessed and broke it, and gave it to them. Their eyes were opened and they recognised him, and he vanished from their sight. They remembered how their hearts had burned while he spoke on the road and opened the Scriptures to them.", "They got up that same hour and went back to Jerusalem. There they found the eleven and their companions gathered and heard that the Lord had risen and appeared to Simon. The two added what had happened on their road and how they had recognised Jesus in the breaking of the bread."], "lesson": "Jesus listens to their account of disappointment, then teaches them to read events through Scripture. Their return to Jerusalem shows what changes when the story they thought had ended opens again.", "question": "What is different in the two disciples' understanding by the time they return to Jerusalem?"},
  {"id": 91166, "title": "An Opened Understanding and the Parting Blessing", "subtitle": "Witness grounded in what Jesus has done", "text": "Luke 24:36–53", "chapters": [[41, 24]], "start": 36, "end": 53, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["While the disciples were talking about what had happened, Jesus stood among them and spoke peace. They were frightened and thought they were seeing a spirit. He asked why doubts had arisen and invited them to see his hands and feet and touch him. While they still marvelled in their joy, he asked whether they had food. They gave him a piece of broiled fish, and he ate before them.", "Then he took their attention back to the words he had spoken while he was with them. The things written about him in the law of Moses, the prophets and the psalms had to be fulfilled. He opened their understanding to the Scriptures and spoke of the Christ suffering and rising on the third day.", "He told them that repentance and forgiveness of sins would be proclaimed in his name to all nations, beginning at Jerusalem. They were witnesses to these things. He spoke of sending what his Father had promised, and told them to remain in the city until they received power from on high. They had a message and a place to begin, and still had to wait.", "He led them out towards Bethany, lifted his hands and blessed them. While blessing them, he parted from them and was carried up into heaven. They worshipped him and returned to Jerusalem with great joy, spending their time in the temple praising and blessing God."], "lesson": "Jesus grounds their witness in what they have seen and in what Scripture explains. He also calls them to wait for what the Father has promised before beginning their work.", "question": "What does Jesus tell the disciples to proclaim, and what does he tell them to wait for?"},
  {"id": 91167, "title": "The Word Comes to Dwell Among Us", "subtitle": "Receiving the One who makes the Father known", "text": "John 1:1–18", "chapters": [[42, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["John opens before the first scene on earth. In the beginning the Word already was. The Word was with God and was God. All things came into being through him. John speaks of life in the Word and of that life as the light of men. The light shines in darkness. The conflict between light and darkness is already present before John names the people who will see it.", "A man named John was sent by God to bear witness to the light so that people might believe through him. He himself was a witness. The true light was coming into the world. The world was made through him, yet it failed to recognise him. He came to his own, and his own did not receive him. To those who did receive him and believe in his name, he gave the right to become children of God.", "The Word became flesh and dwelt among us. John speaks as one who has seen his glory, full of grace and truth. The witness John cries out that the one coming after him ranks before him because he was before him.", "From his fullness people have received grace. John names Moses and the law, then names Jesus Christ in connection with grace and truth. He ends the opening by speaking of the Son making the unseen God known. The person about whom witnesses will speak is already the one through whom the world came into being."], "lesson": "The opening puts God's initiative before any human response. Receiving Jesus begins with recognising who has come and what he makes known.", "question": "What does the opening tell you about the Word before it describes his coming into the world?"},
  {"id": 91168, "title": "A Witness at the Jordan", "subtitle": "Directing attention to Jesus", "text": "John 1:19–34", "chapters": [[42, 1]], "start": 19, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Priests and Levites came from Jerusalem to ask John who he was. He answered plainly that he was not the Christ. They asked whether he was Elijah or the prophet they expected, and he answered no. They needed something to report to those who had sent them. John described himself in Isaiah's words as a voice in the wilderness calling for the Lord's way to be made straight.", "Some sent from the Pharisees asked why he was baptising if he made none of those claims. John said he baptised with water. Among them stood someone they did not know, someone coming after him whose sandal he was unworthy to untie. John gave them an answer about his work by pointing to the person who gave it its purpose.", "The next day John saw Jesus coming towards him and called him the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world. He identified Jesus as the one about whom he had spoken. His baptism with water had served to make this person known to Israel.", "John testified that he saw the Spirit descend like a dove and remain on Jesus. The one who sent him to baptise had told him what to look for: the man on whom the Spirit remained would baptise with the Holy Spirit. John said he had seen and bore witness that this was the Son of God."], "lesson": "John speaks plainly about the limits of his own role. His testimony gives the people a person to look towards beyond the man baptising them.", "question": "What does John say about Jesus that explains why his own work points beyond himself?"},
  {"id": 91169, "title": "Come and See", "subtitle": "The first steps of discipleship", "text": "John 1:35–51", "chapters": [[42, 1]], "start": 35, "end": 51, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["John was standing with two disciples when he saw Jesus walking by. He called him the Lamb of God. The two heard him and followed Jesus. Jesus turned, saw them coming and asked what they were seeking. They called him Teacher and asked where he was staying. He invited them to come and see. They went with him and stayed that day.", "One of the two was Andrew, Simon Peter's brother. Andrew found Simon and told him they had found the Messiah. He brought his brother to Jesus, who looked at him and gave him the name Cephas, which John explains as Peter. The invitation to see where Jesus stayed had already reached a household beyond the original two.", "The next day Jesus went towards Galilee. He found Philip and called him to follow. Philip found Nathanael and said they had found the person spoken of by Moses and the prophets, Jesus of Nazareth. Nathanael questioned whether anything good could come from Nazareth. Philip invited him to come and see.", "Jesus said he had seen Nathanael under the fig tree before Philip called him. Nathanael answered by calling him the Son of God and King of Israel. Jesus promised him greater things, speaking of heaven opened and angels ascending and descending upon the Son of Man. The chapter ends with the promise of more to see."], "lesson": "Philip answers a doubt with an invitation to meet Jesus. The first followers discover more by coming near, listening and bringing others.", "question": "How does Philip respond to Nathanael's objection about Nazareth?"},
  {"id": 91170, "title": "The Jars at the Wedding", "subtitle": "Obedience before the outcome is visible", "text": "John 2:1–12", "chapters": [[42, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["A wedding took place at Cana in Galilee. Jesus' mother was there, and Jesus and his disciples were invited. When the wine ran out, his mother told him about it. Jesus answered that his hour had not yet come. His mother turned to the servants and told them to do whatever he said.", "Six stone jars stood there for the Jewish practice of purification. They were large, and Jesus told the servants to fill them with water. They filled them to the brim. He then told them to draw some out and take it to the steward of the feast. They carried it to him as directed.", "The steward tasted the water that had become wine. He did not know where it came from, although the servants who had drawn the water knew. He called the bridegroom and remarked on what had happened from his point of view. Usually the good wine was served first. In this case the bridegroom had kept the good wine until now.", "John calls this the beginning of Jesus' signs. The steward could recognise what was in the cup without knowing how it came to be there. The servants knew what they had been asked to do. The disciples saw something more in what had happened: Jesus had shown his glory, and they believed in him. After the wedding Jesus went to Capernaum with his mother, brothers and disciples, and they stayed there a few days."], "lesson": "The servants fill the jars before knowing what the steward will taste. John directs the reader past the immediate relief at the wedding to the glory the disciples begin to see.", "question": "Who knew where the wine came from, and what does John say the disciples did in response?"},
  {"id": 91171, "title": "The Temple at Passover", "subtitle": "Worship and the sign Jesus gives", "text": "John 2:13–25", "chapters": [[42, 2]], "start": 13, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Passover was near, and Jesus went up to Jerusalem. In the temple he found people selling cattle, sheep and doves, and money changers seated at their tables. He made a whip of cords and drove them out, along with the sheep and cattle. He scattered the money and overturned the tables. He told the people selling doves to remove them and spoke of his Father's house, which they had made a place of trade.", "His disciples remembered the Scripture about zeal for God's house. The people who challenged him wanted to see a sign to show what authority he had to act. Jesus answered by speaking of destroying the temple and of his raising it in three days. His hearers thought he was speaking of the building and replied that it had taken forty-six years to build.", "John tells the reader that Jesus meant the temple of his body. Later, when Jesus had been raised, his disciples remembered what he had said. They believed both the Scripture and his words. The narrator has carried the reader forward to show how the answer was understood after the event.", "During the feast many believed in his name when they saw the signs he did. Jesus did not entrust himself to them, because he knew people and did not need anyone to tell him what was in them."], "lesson": "Understanding sometimes arrives when an earlier saying is read in light of later events. John tells us the disciples remembered both Scripture and Jesus' words.", "question": "Why did Jesus' hearers and his disciples understand the temple saying differently?"},
  {"id": 91172, "title": "Nicodemus Comes at Night", "subtitle": "New birth and coming into the light", "text": "John 3:1–21", "chapters": [[42, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Nicodemus, a Pharisee and a ruler of the Jews, came to Jesus at night. He addressed him as a teacher from God because of the signs he had seen. Jesus went straight to the need to be born again to see God's kingdom. Nicodemus asked how a grown person could enter his mother's womb and be born a second time.", "Jesus spoke of being born of water and the Spirit. What is born of flesh is flesh, and what is born of the Spirit is spirit. He compared the Spirit's work to the wind. A person hears it without knowing where it comes from or where it goes. Nicodemus asked how these things could happen. Jesus questioned how a teacher of Israel could fail to understand and spoke of testimony that his hearers did not receive.", "The conversation then reached back to Moses lifting up the serpent in the wilderness. Jesus spoke of the Son of Man being lifted up so that believers might have eternal life. The passage goes on to speak of God's love for the world and the giving of his Son so that people might be saved through him.", "John's account ends this exchange with a contrast between loving darkness and coming to the light. Those who do truth come into the light so their deeds can be seen for what they are before God. The man who came with a judgement about Jesus' signs had been asked to consider a change that reached his whole life."], "lesson": "Nicodemus brings knowledge and recognised standing to the conversation. Jesus directs him to life the Spirit gives and to a response that allows deeds to come into the light.", "question": "What makes Nicodemus's first understanding of birth inadequate for what Jesus is saying?"},
  {"id": 91173, "title": "The Friend of the Bridegroom", "subtitle": "Joy when another takes the greater place", "text": "John 3:22–36", "chapters": [[42, 3]], "start": 22, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus and his disciples went into the Judean countryside, where they stayed and baptised. John was also baptising at Aenon near Salim because there was plenty of water there. People kept coming to him. John tells the reader this was before the Baptist was put in prison. For a time both ministries were drawing people.", "A dispute arose about purification. John's disciples came to him with particular news: the man John had testified about across the Jordan was baptising, and people were going to him. John replied that a person could receive only what had been given him from heaven. He reminded them that they themselves had heard him deny that he was the Christ and say that he had been sent ahead of him.", "John pictured a wedding. The bride belonged to the bridegroom. The bridegroom's friend stood nearby and rejoiced when he heard his voice. John described his own joy as complete. He said Jesus must increase and he himself must decrease. The news his disciples brought as a concern was news he could receive with joy.", "The passage goes on to speak of the one who comes from above and bears witness to what he has seen and heard. The Father loves the Son and has given all things into his hand. The conclusion places eternal life with believing in the Son and takes seriously the consequence of refusing him."], "lesson": "John's joy depends on the bridegroom being heard. His disciples' concern gives him a chance to explain success in terms of the work he was given.", "question": "What makes John rejoice in the very news that concerns his followers?"},
  {"id": 91174, "title": "A Conversation Beside Jacob's Well", "subtitle": "Living water and truthful worship", "text": "John 4:1–26", "chapters": [[42, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus left Judea for Galilee and travelled through Samaria. Near Sychar he stopped at Jacob's well, tired from the journey. His disciples went into the town to buy food. A Samaritan woman arrived to draw water. Jesus asked her for a drink. She was surprised: a Jewish man was asking a Samaritan woman to give him water.", "Jesus told her that if she knew God's gift and who was asking her, she would ask him and he would give her living water. She looked at the deep well and asked how he would draw it without a vessel. She brought up Jacob, who had given them the well. Jesus answered that people who drank from this well would thirst again. The water he gave would become in a person a spring rising to eternal life. She asked him for it.", "Jesus told her to call her husband. When she said she had none, he acknowledged the truth of her answer and said she had had five husbands and the man she now had was not her husband. She recognised him as a prophet and asked about the different places where Samaritans and Jews worshipped.", "Jesus spoke of a time when worship of the Father would be bound neither to their mountain nor to Jerusalem. He said that the Father sought worshippers in spirit and in truth. The woman spoke of the Messiah who would come and explain these things. Jesus identified himself to her as the person she was waiting for. The conversation had begun with his request for a drink. He ended it by telling her who was speaking to her."], "lesson": "Jesus speaks about worship to someone whose life and community have made the meeting unusual. He brings truth about her circumstances and the offer of living water into the same conversation.", "question": "How does Jesus answer the question about the place of worship?"},
  {"id": 91175, "title": "The Woman Leaves Her Water Pot", "subtitle": "Witness and a harvest already waiting", "text": "John 4:27–42", "chapters": [[42, 4]], "start": 27, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["The disciples came back from buying food and were surprised to find Jesus speaking with the Samaritan woman. Although they wondered about it, none of them asked why he was talking with her. The woman left her water pot and went into the town. She told people to come and see a man who had told her what she had done, and asked whether he might be the Christ. They left the town and came towards him.", "In the meantime the disciples urged Jesus to eat. He said he had food they knew nothing about, and they wondered whether someone had brought him a meal. He explained that his food was doing the will of the one who sent him and finishing his work. Then he asked them to lift their eyes and look at fields ready for harvest. He spoke of people sowing and reaping and of work the disciples were entering after others had laboured.", "Many Samaritans believed because of the woman's testimony. When they reached Jesus, they asked him to stay. He stayed two days, and many more believed because of what he said himself.", "They told the woman that their conviction now rested on what they had heard for themselves. They spoke of Jesus as the Saviour of the world. The woman had gone from the well with a question and enough confidence to invite people to come. The town's hearing continued after the visit that her testimony had made possible."], "lesson": "The woman gives her neighbours a reason to come. Their own hearing deepens what began with her report.", "question": "What two stages does John describe in the townspeople's response?"},
  {"id": 91176, "title": "The Father Who Went Home on a Word", "subtitle": "Trust before confirmation arrives", "text": "John 4:43–54", "chapters": [[42, 4]], "start": 43, "end": 54, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["After two days Jesus left Samaria for Galilee. The Galileans received him, having seen what he did at the feast in Jerusalem. He returned to Cana, where he had made the water wine. A royal official from Capernaum had a son who was ill. On hearing that Jesus had come into Galilee, he went and asked him to come down and heal his child, who was near death.", "Jesus spoke about people needing to see signs and wonders before they would believe. The father brought the request back to his son. He asked Jesus to come before the boy died. Jesus answered that the man should go because his son lived. The man believed what Jesus told him and set out without having Jesus accompany him to the house.", "While he was on the way down, his servants met him with the news that his son was alive. He asked when the boy had begun to recover. They told him that the fever left the previous day at the seventh hour. The father recognised that it was the same hour in which Jesus had said his son lived.", "The man believed, and so did his household. John calls this the second sign Jesus did after coming from Judea into Galilee. He lets the reader follow the father from his request for Jesus to come with him, to the journey made on Jesus' word, to the moment he could connect that word with news from home."], "lesson": "The father must decide how to act while he is still away from his son. The journey home gives his trust a practical form before he learns the hour of the healing.", "question": "At what point did the father begin to act on what Jesus told him?"},
  {"id": 91177, "title": "The Man Who Carried His Bed", "subtitle": "Receiving help after long waiting", "text": "John 5:1–18", "chapters": [[42, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus went up to Jerusalem for a feast. Near the sheep gate was a pool with five covered porches, where many people with illnesses or disabilities lay. One man had been ill for thirty-eight years. Jesus saw him lying there and knew he had been in that condition a long time. He asked whether he wanted to be made well.", "The man explained the obstacle as he understood it. He had no one to put him in the pool when the water was stirred, and someone else arrived ahead of him. Jesus told him to get up, take up his bed and walk. The man was immediately made well, took his bed and walked. It was the Sabbath.", "The authorities told him it was unlawful to carry his bed that day. He explained that the one who healed him had told him to carry it. They asked who gave the instruction, but he did not know. Jesus had withdrawn into the crowd. Later Jesus found him in the temple and spoke to him about the conduct of his life now that he was well.", "The man told the authorities that it was Jesus who had healed him. They opposed Jesus because he did these things on the Sabbath. Jesus answered that his Father was working and that he was working too. They took this as a claim about his relation to God, and the dispute grew more serious."], "lesson": "A man who has described years of frustrated hope rises at Jesus' word. The dispute that follows shows how easily a healed person can become secondary to an argument.", "question": "What does the man say about his situation before Jesus tells him to rise?"},
  {"id": 91178, "title": "The Witnesses Jesus Names", "subtitle": "Reading Scripture with willingness to come", "text": "John 5:19–47", "chapters": [[42, 5]], "start": 19, "end": 47, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus answered the people questioning him by speaking about the Son's relation to the Father. The Son does what he sees the Father doing. The Father loves him and shows him his works. As the Father raises the dead and gives life, the Son gives life. The Father has also entrusted judgement to him so that people honour the Son as they honour the Father.", "Jesus spoke about the person who hears his word and believes the one who sent him. Such a person has passed from death to life. He spoke of the dead hearing his voice and of a future hour when those in the graves would come out. He said his judgement was just because he sought the will of the Father who sent him.", "Then Jesus set out the witnesses. John bore witness to the truth, and people had been willing for a time to rejoice in his light. The works the Father gave Jesus to do were a greater witness. He also spoke of the Father's own testimony and said his hearers did not have God's word abiding in them, because they did not believe the one he sent.", "Jesus directed them to the Scriptures, which testified about him. They searched them expecting to find life, yet refused to come to him. He asked how they could believe while looking for honour from one another. Finally he named Moses, in whom they hoped, and said Moses had written about him."], "lesson": "Searching Scripture calls for willingness to respond to what it reveals. Jesus connects the hearers' reception of him with the glory they seek from one another.", "question": "Which witnesses does Jesus name, and what response does he find missing?"},
  {"id": 91179, "title": "The Boy's Lunch and the Boat at Night", "subtitle": "Jesus knows the need before the disciples do", "text": "John 6:1–21", "chapters": [[42, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus crossed the Sea of Galilee, and a large crowd followed because people had seen the signs he did for the sick. He went up a mountain and sat with his disciples. Passover was near. Seeing the crowd approach, he asked Philip where they could buy bread for them. John says Jesus knew what he would do and asked to test him.", "Philip reckoned that two hundred denarii would not buy enough for everyone to have even a little. Andrew pointed out a boy with five barley loaves and two small fish and asked what they could do with so little. Jesus told the people to sit down. He gave thanks and distributed the food until they had as much as they wanted. At his direction the disciples gathered twelve baskets of fragments.", "People who saw the sign spoke of Jesus as the prophet who was to come. When he realised they meant to take him and make him king, he withdrew alone to the mountain. The crowd had reached a conclusion about the power that had fed them, and Jesus would not let them turn it into that kind of rule.", "At evening the disciples set out towards Capernaum. It grew dark, the wind blew and the sea rose. They saw Jesus walking towards the boat and were afraid. He spoke to them, told them who he was and said not to fear. They received him, and the boat reached the land they were going to."], "lesson": "Philip's estimate and Andrew's small find are both honest. Jesus has a purpose beyond their calculations and declines the kingship the crowd tries to impose.", "question": "What reasons for fear or concern appear in the feeding and in the crossing?"},
  {"id": 91180, "title": "Bread That Gives Life", "subtitle": "Seeking Jesus beyond a full stomach", "text": "John 6:22–40", "chapters": [[42, 6]], "start": 22, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["The next day people on the far shore remembered seeing the disciples depart without Jesus. Boats had come from Tiberias near the place where they had eaten. When they could find neither Jesus nor the disciples, they crossed to Capernaum to look for him. They found him on the other side and asked when he had come there.", "Jesus told them why they were seeking him. They had eaten the loaves and been satisfied. He urged them to work for food that endures to eternal life, which the Son of Man would give. They asked what they should do to perform God's works. He directed them to believe in the one God sent.", "They asked for a sign and recalled their fathers eating manna in the wilderness. Jesus said the true bread from heaven came from his Father and gave life to the world. The people asked for that bread always. He then called himself the bread of life and spoke of the person who comes to him never hungering and the person who believes never thirsting.", "Jesus said he had come to do his Father's will. He would receive those who came and lose none of what the Father gave him. He spoke of believers having eternal life and being raised on the last day. The conversation had begun with people looking for another meal and had reached what Jesus was promising to give them."], "lesson": "The crowd has received real food and wants more. Jesus uses that need to speak about a deeper hunger and about the life he gives.", "question": "What reason does Jesus give for the crowd's search, and where does he direct it?"},
  {"id": 91181, "title": "When Many Turned Back", "subtitle": "Staying when Jesus' words are hard", "text": "John 6:41–71", "chapters": [[42, 6]], "start": 41, "end": 71, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Some of Jesus' hearers grumbled because he said he was bread that had come down from heaven. They knew him as the son of Joseph and knew his parents. How could he speak that way about where he came from? Jesus told them to stop murmuring. He spoke of the Father drawing people to him and of people hearing and learning from the Father.", "He returned to the manna their fathers ate and to the fact that those people died. He said the bread he would give for the life of the world was his flesh. This led to an argument. In the synagogue at Capernaum he went on speaking of eating his flesh and drinking his blood, of abiding in him and living through him.", "Many disciples called it a hard saying and asked who could hear it. Jesus asked whether it offended them and spoke of the Spirit giving life. He said his words were spirit and life and recognised that some did not believe. Many who had followed him went back and no longer walked with him.", "Jesus asked the Twelve if they also wanted to go away. Peter answered by asking to whom they would go. Jesus had the words of eternal life. Peter spoke of their faith in him, and Jesus answered with a warning concerning one of the Twelve. John names Judas as the one who would betray him."], "lesson": "Peter's reply is a confession about where he has found life. John leaves the departure of many and the future betrayal in view, so that remaining cannot be reduced to standing with the crowd.", "question": "What ground does Peter give for staying with Jesus?"},
  {"id": 91182, "title": "A Journey Made Quietly", "subtitle": "Judging with care", "text": "John 7:1–24", "chapters": [[42, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus stayed in Galilee because opponents in Judea sought to kill him. As the Feast of Tabernacles approached, his brothers urged him to go and show his works openly. They argued that a person seeking public notice would not act in secret. John says his brothers did not believe in him. Jesus answered that his time had not yet come and stayed behind when they left.", "After they had gone, he went up quietly. At the feast people looked for him and discussed him among themselves. Some called him good while others accused him of misleading the people. Fear kept them from speaking openly. About the middle of the feast, Jesus entered the temple and taught. People wondered how he knew what he taught without having studied as they expected.", "Jesus said his teaching came from the one who sent him. A person willing to do God's will would be in a position to consider where the teaching came from. He spoke about seeking the honour of the sender and then challenged people who claimed to have Moses' law while seeking to kill him.", "He brought them back to a work he had done on the Sabbath. They themselves circumcised on the Sabbath when the law required it. Why be angry with him for making a whole person well? He called them to judge fairly instead of stopping at appearances."], "lesson": "Jesus gives little weight to the demand to establish himself by publicity. His instruction about judgement asks listeners to consider the substance of the work they have seen.", "question": "How does Jesus answer the objection about making a man well on the Sabbath?"},
  {"id": 91183, "title": "The Offer to the Thirsty at the Feast", "subtitle": "Hearing before judging", "text": "John 7:25–53", "chapters": [[42, 7]], "start": 25, "end": 53, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Some people in Jerusalem asked whether Jesus was the man the leaders wanted to kill. They saw him speaking openly and wondered why no one stopped him. Others argued about where the Christ would come from. Jesus taught in the temple, saying he had been sent by one whom they did not know. People tried to seize him, but his hour had not come. Many believed and asked whether the Christ would do more signs than Jesus had done.", "The leaders heard what the crowd was saying and sent officers to take him. Jesus spoke of being with them a little longer and then going to the one who sent him. They wondered where he would go that they could not find him.", "On the last great day of the feast, Jesus called those who thirsted to come to him and drink. John explains his words about living water in connection with the Spirit whom believers would receive. The crowd again divided over who Jesus was and where he came from.", "The officers returned without him. Asked why, they said that no one had ever spoken as he did. The Pharisees dismissed the response of the crowd. Nicodemus asked whether their law judged a man before hearing him and learning what he had done. His question drew a rebuke in return, and the gathering broke up."], "lesson": "Nicodemus asks a modest but consequential question about fairness. The passage shows people reaching conclusions while others pause because they have listened.", "question": "What principle does Nicodemus appeal to when the leaders have already reached a judgement?"},
  {"id": 91184, "title": "The Accusers Leave One by One", "subtitle": "Mercy that calls for change", "text": "John 8:1–11", "chapters": [[42, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["In the account printed in the King James Version, Jesus went to the Mount of Olives and returned early to the temple. People came to him, and he sat down to teach. Scribes and Pharisees brought a woman accused of adultery and placed her in the middle of the gathering. They said she had been caught in the act and put a question to Jesus about the penalty in Moses' law.", "John says they were testing him so that they might have something to accuse him of. Jesus stooped and wrote on the ground with his finger. The passage leaves the words he wrote unreported. As the questioners kept asking, Jesus straightened and told the one who was without sin to throw the first stone. Then he stooped and wrote again.", "Those who heard began leaving one by one, starting with the older ones. The woman remained in the middle, and Jesus asked where her accusers had gone and whether anyone had condemned her. She answered that no one had.", "Jesus told her that neither did he condemn her and told her to go and sin no more. The crowd had heard the question posed over her head. The last exchange was with the woman herself, and the instruction she received concerned how she would live after she left."], "lesson": "Jesus brings the accusers' own conduct into view and gives the woman a direct word about her future. The call to change is spoken after the threatening crowd has gone.", "question": "What happens to the accusers after Jesus answers, and what does he say once they have left?"},
  {"id": 91185, "title": "A Light and Two Witnesses", "subtitle": "Listening to the One sent by the Father", "text": "John 8:12–30", "chapters": [[42, 8]], "start": 12, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus spoke again and called himself the light of the world. Whoever followed him would have the light of life. The Pharisees said his testimony could not stand because he gave it about himself. Jesus answered that he knew where he came from and where he was going. They were judging without knowing those things. He appealed to the testimony of the Father who sent him alongside his own.", "They asked where his Father was. He told them that if they knew him, they would know his Father too. John locates the exchange in the temple treasury and says no one seized Jesus because his hour had not yet come.", "Jesus spoke again about going where his hearers could not come. They misunderstood and asked what he meant. He spoke of their belonging to this world and of his coming from above. He warned about dying in their sins and put the question of believing who he was before them. When they asked who he was, he spoke of what he had been telling them and of the truth of the one who sent him.", "Jesus said that when the Son of Man was lifted up, they would know who he was and that he spoke what his Father taught him. John ends this part of the exchange by saying many believed as he spoke."], "lesson": "Jesus grounds his testimony in his relation to the Father. His hearers must attend to who is speaking before they can understand what it means to follow his light.", "question": "How does Jesus answer the objection that he is testifying about himself alone?"},
  {"id": 91186, "title": "The Claim to Be Abraham's Children", "subtitle": "Freedom that begins with truth", "text": "John 8:31–59", "chapters": [[42, 8]], "start": 31, "end": 59, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus told people who had believed him that if they continued in his word, they were truly his disciples. They would know the truth, and the truth would make them free. They appealed to being descended from Abraham and objected to the suggestion that they needed freedom. Jesus spoke of a person who commits sin being its servant. He said that if the Son made them free, they would be free indeed.", "He acknowledged their descent from Abraham and then asked them to consider whether their conduct resembled Abraham's. They sought to kill someone who had told them the truth he heard from God. The discussion went deeper as they claimed God as their Father. Jesus asked why, if that were so, they did not love the one who had come from God.", "Their replies became hostile. They called him a Samaritan and accused him of having a demon. Jesus answered that he honoured his Father and spoke of someone keeping his word and never seeing death. They returned to Abraham and the prophets, who had died, and asked who Jesus made himself out to be.", "He said Abraham had rejoiced to see his day. When they objected that Jesus was not yet fifty, he spoke of himself existing before Abraham. They took up stones, and Jesus went out of the temple. The words about freedom had led to a confrontation over whether they could receive the person offering it."], "lesson": "Jesus puts conduct and willingness to hear beside claims of ancestry. Freedom in this conversation involves receiving a truth the hearers find uncomfortable.", "question": "Why do the hearers object when Jesus speaks to them about becoming free?"},
  {"id": 91187, "title": "A Man Washes at Siloam", "subtitle": "Seeing a person before assigning blame", "text": "John 9:1–23", "chapters": [[42, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["As Jesus passed by, he saw a man who had been blind from birth. The disciples asked whether the man or his parents had sinned for him to be born blind. Jesus turned their attention from assigning blame to the works of God that would be made visible in him. He spoke of doing the work of the one who sent him and called himself the light of the world.", "Jesus made clay, put it on the man's eyes and told him to wash in the pool of Siloam. John explains that the name means Sent. The man went, washed and came back seeing. Neighbours who remembered him begging disputed whether he was the same person. He said that he was.", "They asked how his eyes had been opened. He described what Jesus had done and said, but did not know where Jesus was. They brought him to the Pharisees. It was the Sabbath when Jesus made the clay. Some said a man who did that could not be from God. Others asked how a sinner could do such signs. The man called Jesus a prophet.", "The authorities called his parents to establish whether he had really been born blind. They confirmed that he was their son and had been born blind. Afraid of being put out of the synagogue, they said that he was old enough to answer for himself."], "lesson": "The disciples begin with a question about blame. Jesus addresses the man himself and gives him something to do. The parents' caution shows what public testimony could cost.", "question": "What does the healed man know from experience that the others are disputing?"},
  {"id": 91188, "title": "The Man Cast Out and Found Again", "subtitle": "Truthful witness under pressure", "text": "John 9:24–41", "chapters": [[42, 9]], "start": 24, "end": 41, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["The authorities called the healed man again. They urged him to give glory to God and told him they knew Jesus was a sinner. He said he did not know whether Jesus was a sinner, but he did know that he had been blind and now could see. When they asked again how it happened, he said he had already told them. He asked whether they also wanted to become Jesus' disciples.", "They insulted him and said they were disciples of Moses. They knew God had spoken to Moses, they said, but did not know where Jesus came from. The man found it astonishing that they did not know this when Jesus had opened his eyes. He reasoned from what had happened and said that if Jesus were not from God he could do nothing. They answered that he was born in sins and threw him out.", "Jesus heard that he had been cast out and went to find him. He asked whether he believed in the Son of God. The man asked who that was so he might believe. Jesus told him that he had seen him and that he was the one speaking with him. The man believed and worshipped.", "Jesus spoke of judgement in which those who could not see would see. Some Pharisees asked whether they too were blind. He addressed the responsibility of those who claimed that they could see."], "lesson": "The man speaks from what he can honestly say, even when he is pressured to withdraw it. Jesus seeks him out after the leaders have rejected him.", "question": "What does Jesus do once he hears that the man has been cast out?"},
  {"id": 91189, "title": "The Shepherd Who Knows His Sheep", "subtitle": "Recognising a voice through care", "text": "John 10:1–21", "chapters": [[42, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus described a sheepfold and the difference between people who entered by its door and people who climbed in elsewhere. The shepherd entered through the door. The keeper opened for him, and the sheep heard his voice. He called his own sheep by name and led them out. They followed a voice they knew and would flee from a stranger's voice.", "His hearers did not understand the picture, so he spoke again. He called himself the door of the sheep and spoke of entering, being saved and finding pasture. He contrasted the thief who comes to steal and destroy with his own coming to give life abundantly.", "He then called himself the good shepherd, who gives his life for the sheep. A hired man sees a wolf coming and leaves, because the sheep are not his. Jesus spoke of knowing his sheep and being known by them, and of bringing other sheep so there would be one flock and one shepherd.", "Jesus said he laid down his life willingly and had authority to take it up again. His words divided the hearers. Some said he had a demon and was mad. Others objected that such words were unlike those of someone possessed. They asked whether a demon could open blind eyes. The healing from the previous chapter remained part of what the people had to consider."], "lesson": "The shepherd's authority is visible in his knowledge of the sheep and his willingness to bear danger for them. Jesus extends this care beyond the fold already in view.", "question": "What does the shepherd do when faced with the danger that makes the hireling leave?"},
  {"id": 91190, "title": "A Question on Solomon's Porch", "subtitle": "Trusting the Shepherd's hold", "text": "John 10:22–42", "chapters": [[42, 10]], "start": 22, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["It was winter at the feast of dedication in Jerusalem. Jesus was walking in the temple on Solomon's porch when people gathered around him. They wanted him to say plainly whether he was the Christ. Jesus answered that he had told them and that the works he did in his Father's name testified about him.", "He spoke again about sheep. His own heard his voice, he knew them and they followed him. He gave them eternal life and spoke of their safety in his hand and in the Father's hand. Then he said he and the Father were one. Some of those listening took up stones.", "Jesus asked which of the good works he had shown them from the Father made them want to stone him. They replied that the issue was blasphemy, because they heard him making himself God. He answered from Scripture and returned to the works. Even if they refused his words, he asked them to consider what the works showed about his relation to the Father. They tried to seize him, and he escaped.", "He went across the Jordan to the place where John had first baptised. People came to him there. John had done no sign, they said, yet what he said about Jesus had been true. Many believed. The passage ends where an earlier witness could still be remembered and compared with what they had now seen."], "lesson": "Jesus' promise of safety belongs to the relationship of hearing and following. Beyond Jordan people remember John's testimony and weigh it against what they now see.", "question": "What two actions does Jesus describe on the part of the sheep, and what does he promise them?"},
  {"id": 91191, "title": "The Message From Bethany", "subtitle": "Hope spoken to a grieving sister", "text": "John 11:1–27", "chapters": [[42, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Lazarus of Bethany, the brother of Mary and Martha, became ill. His sisters sent Jesus word that the man he loved was sick. Jesus spoke of God's glory in what would happen. John says Jesus loved Martha, her sister and Lazarus, and also tells us that after hearing the news he stayed two more days where he was.", "When Jesus said they should return to Judea, the disciples remembered that people there had tried to stone him. He spoke about walking in the light and then told them Lazarus was asleep and that he was going to wake him. They took this to mean ordinary sleep. Jesus told them plainly Lazarus had died. Thomas said to the others that they should go too, even if it meant dying with him.", "By the time Jesus arrived, Lazarus had been in the tomb four days. Many people had come to comfort the sisters. Martha heard that Jesus was coming and went to meet him. She told him that if he had been there her brother would not have died, and spoke of trusting what God would give him. Jesus told her her brother would rise.", "Martha understood that as a reference to the last day. Jesus spoke of himself as the resurrection and the life and asked whether she believed. She confessed that he was the Christ, the Son of God who was to come into the world."], "lesson": "Martha speaks her disappointment openly and also listens to Jesus' promise. Her confession is made before she sees anything change at the tomb.", "question": "What does Martha believe about Jesus before Lazarus comes out of the tomb?"},
  {"id": 91192, "title": "Jesus Weeps and Lazarus Comes Out", "subtitle": "Compassion and life at the tomb", "text": "John 11:28–57", "chapters": [[42, 11]], "start": 28, "end": 57, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Martha went to her sister Mary and told her privately that the Teacher was calling for her. Mary got up quickly. The people who had been comforting her thought she was going to the grave and followed. When she reached Jesus, she fell at his feet and told him what Martha had already said: if he had been there, her brother would not have died.", "Jesus saw Mary and the people with her weeping. He was deeply moved and asked where Lazarus had been laid. They invited him to come and see. Jesus wept. Some saw in his tears how much he loved Lazarus. Others wondered why the man who had opened blind eyes had allowed this man to die.", "At the tomb Jesus asked them to remove the stone. Martha objected because her brother had been dead four days. Jesus reminded her of what he had said about seeing God's glory. They took the stone away. He thanked the Father aloud so the people standing there might believe the Father had sent him. Then he called Lazarus by name. Lazarus came out with his hands and feet wrapped and his face covered. Jesus told the people to unbind him and let him go.", "Many who saw believed. Others reported what Jesus had done to the Pharisees. The chief priests and Pharisees convened a council and spoke of what Rome might do if people continued believing in him. From that day they planned his death. Jesus withdrew, and as Passover drew near people wondered whether he would come to the feast."], "lesson": "Jesus' tears have their place beside his authority to call Lazarus. The leaders' reaction shows that evidence can become threatening when people fear what they will lose.", "question": "What two very different responses follow the raising of Lazarus?"},
  {"id": 91193, "title": "Perfume at Supper and Branches on the Road", "subtitle": "Honouring Jesus before the crowds change", "text": "John 12:1–19", "chapters": [[42, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Six days before Passover, Jesus came back to Bethany. The last time John had taken us there, Lazarus had walked out of a tomb with his hands and feet still wrapped. Now he was reclining at supper with Jesus while Martha served. Mary took a pound of costly perfume made from pure nard, poured it on Jesus' feet and wiped them with her hair. Soon everyone in the house could smell what she had done.", "Judas Iscariot put a price on the gift. The perfume could have brought three hundred denarii, he said, and the money could have gone to the poor. John tells us what the others could not see: Judas kept the common purse and used to take from it. Jesus told him to leave Mary alone and spoke of the day of his burial. Lazarus was sitting there alive, and already Jesus was speaking of his own grave.", "News of the supper brought people out from Jerusalem. They wanted to see Jesus and the man he had raised. The chief priests began planning to kill Lazarus too, because people were going over to Jesus on account of him. The man sitting at supper had become a witness by the simple fact that he was alive.", "The next day people took palm branches and went to meet Jesus, acclaiming him as Israel's king. He rode in on a young donkey, as Scripture had said Zion's king would do. Only after Jesus was glorified did the disciples put that Scripture together with what they had seen. For now the crowd kept growing, and the Pharisees could only complain to one another that the whole world seemed to have gone after him."], "lesson": "Mary's gift receives meaning from the approaching burial. The crowds see a public welcome, while John also records what leaders are planning in response to Lazarus.", "question": "What different motives for coming to Bethany or reacting to Lazarus does John describe?"},
  {"id": 91194, "title": "The Grain That Falls Into the Ground", "subtitle": "Fruit through surrender", "text": "John 12:20–50", "chapters": [[42, 12]], "start": 20, "end": 50, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Among the people who had come to worship at the feast were some Greeks. They approached Philip, who was from Bethsaida, and asked to see Jesus. Philip told Andrew, and the two took the request to Jesus. He answered by saying that his hour had come. Then he spoke of something small enough to hold between two fingers. A grain of wheat remains a single grain until it falls into the earth and dies. Only then does it bear much fruit.", "Jesus put his servants into the same picture. They were to follow him, and the Father would honour whoever served him. Then Jesus spoke openly about his own distress. He had come for this hour and asked the Father to glorify his name. A voice answered from heaven. Some in the crowd heard thunder, and others thought an angel had spoken. Jesus said the voice had come for their sake. When he was lifted up, he would draw people to himself. John says this pointed to the kind of death he would die.", "The crowd wondered how the Son of Man could be lifted up if the Christ was to remain forever. Jesus urged them to walk while they had the light. Then he went away and hid himself. John brings in Isaiah to account for the unbelief that had persisted through so many signs. Even among the rulers some believed, yet they feared being put out of the synagogue and kept their belief to themselves.", "Jesus' public words leave a choice before them. To believe him is to believe the One who sent him. He came as light and spoke what the Father had given him, with eternal life in view. The word people heard and refused would still be there to judge them on the last day."], "lesson": "Jesus speaks about fruit when his own death draws near. John also names public approval as something that can restrain a person's confession.", "question": "Why is the picture of the grain appropriate when Jesus says his hour has arrived?"},
  {"id": 91195, "title": "The Towel and the Basin", "subtitle": "Serving from a secure place", "text": "John 13:1–20", "chapters": [[42, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["At supper, before the Passover, Jesus knew his hour had come to leave the world and go to the Father. John says he loved his own to the end. The betrayal was already taking shape. Jesus rose from the meal, took off his outer garment and wrapped a towel around himself. He poured water into a basin and began to wash and dry the disciples' feet.", "When he came to Simon Peter, Peter questioned whether the Lord would wash his feet. Jesus said Peter did not understand what he was doing now but would understand later. Peter refused. Jesus replied that if he did not wash him, Peter had no part with him. Peter then asked for his hands and head as well. Jesus answered with the picture of a person already bathed who needed only his feet washed. He added that they were clean, though not all of them, because he knew who would betray him.", "After washing their feet, Jesus put on his garment and returned to the table. He asked whether they understood what he had done. They were right to call him Teacher and Lord. Because he had washed their feet, they ought to wash one another's feet. He had given them an example to do as he had done.", "Jesus spoke of a servant and the one who sends him. He said the blessing lay in doing what they now knew. He also spoke of the coming betrayal and of receiving the people he would send."], "lesson": "Jesus knows where he comes from and where he is going when he kneels to serve. His example puts a concrete duty before people who call him Lord.", "question": "How does Jesus use the titles Teacher and Lord when he explains his action?"},
  {"id": 91196, "title": "Into the Night", "subtitle": "Love when loyalty is tested", "text": "John 13:21–38", "chapters": [[42, 13]], "start": 21, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["After washing the disciples' feet, Jesus sat among them again. He became troubled and said that one of them would betray him. The disciples looked from face to face, unsure whom he meant. Peter motioned to the disciple reclining close to Jesus, and that disciple leaned back to ask. Jesus answered by telling him to watch who received the piece of bread he was about to dip. He dipped it and gave it to Judas Iscariot.", "John says that after Judas took the bread, Satan entered him. Jesus told him to do quickly what he was doing. The others heard the instruction without catching its meaning. Judas carried the common purse, so they supposed Jesus was sending him to buy something for the feast or give money to the poor. Judas received the bread and went out at once. By then it was night.", "Once Judas had gone, Jesus spoke of the Son of Man being glorified and of God being glorified in him. He told the disciples he would be with them only a little longer. Where he was going they could not come. With that separation ahead, he gave them a command for the life they would share after he left. They were to love one another as he had loved them. That love would show other people whose disciples they were.", "Peter was still thinking about where Jesus meant to go. Jesus told him that he would follow later. Peter wanted to follow now and offered to lay down his life. Jesus took up those very words and asked whether Peter would do so. Before the rooster crowed, Jesus said, Peter would deny him three times."], "lesson": "Jesus gives the command to love in a room marked by betrayal and impending failure. The test of that love will come when loyalty costs more than the disciples expect.", "question": "What does Jesus say will identify his disciples to other people?"},
  {"id": 91197, "title": "A Place in the Father's House", "subtitle": "Trust when the road ahead is unclear", "text": "John 14:1–14", "chapters": [[42, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Peter had just promised to lay down his life, and Jesus had told him what he would do before morning. The rest of the disciples had heard that Jesus was going somewhere they could not yet follow. In that room Jesus told them to trust God and trust him. He spoke of his Father's house, where there was room for them. He was going to prepare a place and would come back to take them to himself. The promise was that they would be where he was.", "Thomas stopped at the difficulty Jesus' words raised for him. If they did not know where Jesus was going, how could they know the way? Jesus answered by speaking of himself as the way, the truth and the life. Anyone coming to the Father comes through him. He told them that in knowing him they were coming to know the Father whom they longed to see.", "Philip asked Jesus to show them the Father. Jesus asked how Philip could still speak that way after spending so much time with him. The Father was in him, and he was in the Father. The words the disciples had heard and the works they had seen belonged together. Even if they struggled with what Jesus said about his relation to the Father, they could look at what he had done.", "Jesus then spoke of work for the people who would remain. The one who believed in him would do the works he did, and greater works, because he was going to the Father. He promised to answer what they asked in his name so that the Father would be glorified in the Son. Their questions had begun with how to follow him. Jesus left them with work to do and a name in which to pray."], "lesson": "Thomas and Philip ask for different kinds of clarity. Jesus answers by drawing them back to the person with whom they have been living and learning.", "question": "How does Jesus answer Philip's request to see the Father?"},
  {"id": 91198, "title": "The Promise of the Comforter", "subtitle": "Peace and obedience in Jesus' absence", "text": "John 14:15–31", "chapters": [[42, 14]], "start": 15, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus had told the disciples that he was going to the Father. Now he spoke about the help they would have in his absence. Their love for him would take shape in keeping his commands, and he would ask the Father to give them another Comforter who would stay with them. He called him the Spirit of truth. The world could not receive him because it neither saw nor knew him. The disciples already knew him, and Jesus said that he would be in them.", "Jesus promised that he would come to them and that they would live because he lived. Then Judas, whom John distinguishes from Judas Iscariot, asked why Jesus would make himself known to his disciples and keep that from the world. Jesus spoke of someone who loves him and keeps his word. The Father would love that person, and the Father and Son would make their home there. What had begun with talk of a place in the Father's house now came close to where a disciple lived.", "Jesus was telling them these things while he was still with them. Afterward, the Father would send the Holy Spirit in his name. The Spirit would teach them and bring back to mind what Jesus had said. Words they could hear in the room that night would come back to them when they needed them in days they had yet to face.", "Jesus left them his peace and called them away from fear. He told them beforehand about his going so that they could believe when it happened. The ruler of this world was coming and had no claim on him. Jesus would do what his Father commanded, so the world could see that he loved the Father. Then he called the others to get up and go with him."], "lesson": "The Spirit's work includes bringing Jesus' words back to mind. His promise of peace sits beside the call to keep those words through what is coming.", "question": "What does Jesus say the Comforter will teach or bring to remembrance?"},
  {"id": 91199, "title": "The Vine and Its Branches", "subtitle": "Fruit that comes from abiding", "text": "John 15:1–17", "chapters": [[42, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus spoke of himself as the true vine and of his Father as the one who tended it. A grower sees which branches are bearing grapes and knows where to cut. Jesus said that the Father removes the branches that bear no fruit and prunes the fruitful ones so they can bear more. He told the disciples they were already clean through the word he had spoken to them. Now they were to remain in him.", "A branch lying on the ground might still have leaves, yet it cannot keep growing grapes once severed from the vine. Jesus put the disciples in the place of those branches. They could bear much fruit by remaining in him, and apart from him they could do nothing. He joined the promise of answered prayer to his words remaining in them. Their fruit would bring glory to the Father and show that they were his disciples.", "Then Jesus spoke about love. The Father had loved him, and he had loved these disciples. They were to remain in this love by keeping his commands, just as he kept his Father's commands. He wanted them to share his joy and have it in full. The command he put before them was to love one another as he had loved them. Soon they would see what he meant when he spoke of giving one's life for one's friends.", "Jesus called them friends because he had shared what he heard from the Father. He reminded them that he had chosen them and given them work to do. They were to go and bear fruit that would last. Before he moved on to what they might expect from the world, he gave the command again to love one another."], "lesson": "A branch receives what sustains its fruit before it produces anything. Jesus gives abiding a concrete setting through his words, obedience and love for others.", "question": "What actions does Jesus connect with remaining in his love?"},
  {"id": 91200, "title": "When the World Hates You", "subtitle": "Witness in the face of opposition", "text": "John 15:18–27", "chapters": [[42, 15]], "start": 18, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus had just told the disciples to love one another. Now he told them how some people would treat them outside that circle. The world's hatred had reached him first, and they could expect it to reach them. If they belonged to the world, it would love them as its own. Jesus had chosen them out of it, and their attachment to him would carry a cost they were soon to feel.", "He asked them to remember something he had already said, that a servant ranks below his master. People who persecuted him would persecute them. Those who kept his word would keep theirs also. The disciples would encounter both responses. Jesus traced the hostility back to people's failure to know the One who sent him. It would come upon the disciples because they carried his name.", "Jesus brought the matter back to what people had actually heard and seen. He had spoken to them and done works among them that no one else had done. After seeing those works, some still hated both him and his Father. Jesus said this fulfilled what was written in their law about being hated without cause. There was a record of what people had been given the chance to receive.", "The disciples would have help when it was their turn to speak. Jesus would send the Comforter from the Father, the Spirit of truth, who would bear witness about him. The disciples would bear witness too. They had been with Jesus from the beginning. They could tell what they had heard and seen for themselves, even when the people listening had already made up their minds."], "lesson": "Opposition is something Jesus prepares his disciples for in advance. He joins their memory of what they have seen to the witness of the Spirit.", "question": "Why does Jesus say these disciples are able to bear witness about him?"},
  {"id": 91201, "title": "The Work of the Spirit of Truth", "subtitle": "Help after Jesus goes", "text": "John 16:1–15", "chapters": [[42, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus told the disciples why he had spoken so plainly about what lay ahead. He wanted to keep them from falling away when it happened. They would be put out of synagogues. A time would come when people who killed them would think they were doing God a service. Jesus said that these people had known neither the Father nor him. When the hour came, he wanted the disciples to remember that he had told them.", "While Jesus was with them, he had not needed to say these things from the beginning. Now he was going back to the One who sent him. The disciples had heard about the dangers and the departure, and sorrow filled their hearts. Jesus told them there was an advantage in his leaving that they could hardly see at the time. If he went, he would send the Comforter to them.", "Jesus described the Spirit's work in terms of sin, righteousness and judgment. The Spirit would show the world its sin in refusing to believe in Jesus. He would show it righteousness because Jesus was going to the Father and the disciples would see him no more. He would show it judgment because the ruler of this world had already been judged.", "There were things Jesus could still tell the disciples that night, but he knew they could not bear them yet. The Spirit of truth would guide them into all the truth. He would speak what he heard, show them what was to come and glorify Jesus by making known what belonged to him. The disciples would still be taught when their teacher was beyond their sight."], "lesson": "The disciples see the loss before them. Jesus also describes help they will receive, including guidance for things they cannot yet bear to hear.", "question": "What does Jesus say the Spirit will do for the disciples that they need after his departure?"},
  {"id": 91202, "title": "A Little While", "subtitle": "Joy after grief and peace in trouble", "text": "John 16:16–33", "chapters": [[42, 16]], "start": 16, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus told the disciples that in a little while they would lose sight of him and that a little while later they would see him again. The words passed among them as questions. What did he mean by a little while? How did that fit with his going to the Father? Jesus could tell that they wanted to ask him. He took up the question himself and told them some of what those two intervals would hold.", "They would weep and lament while the world rejoiced. Then the very sorrow they felt would turn to joy. Jesus asked them to think of a woman in labour. The hour brings real pain, and when the child is born that pain gives way to the joy of another life in the world. The disciples would see Jesus again, their hearts would rejoice, and nobody would be able to take that joy from them.", "Jesus spoke of a day when they would ask the Father in his name. The Father loved them because they loved Jesus and believed he had come from God. Jesus put his journey plainly before them. He had come from the Father into the world and was leaving the world to return to the Father. The disciples said they could now understand him and that they believed.", "Jesus asked whether they believed now. The time had come when they would scatter, each to his own place, and leave him alone. The Father would still be with him. Jesus had told them these things so they could have peace in him. They would have trouble in the world, and he gave them reason to take courage. He had overcome it."], "lesson": "Jesus gives the disciples language for grief that will feel final while it lasts. He also names their coming failure before speaking peace to them.", "question": "What does Jesus say will happen to their sorrow, and what does he tell them about coming trouble?"},
  {"id": 91203, "title": "Jesus Prays for Those Given to Him", "subtitle": "Knowing the Father and being kept in his name", "text": "John 17:1–12", "chapters": [[42, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus finished speaking to the disciples and lifted his eyes to heaven. He told the Father that the hour had come and asked to be glorified so that he might glorify the Father. The Father had given him authority over all people, and he would give eternal life to those the Father had entrusted to him. Jesus spoke of that life as knowing the only true God and knowing Jesus Christ, whom God had sent.", "He looked back over his work among them as work he had brought to completion. Then he asked the Father to glorify him in his presence with the glory he had shared with him before the world existed. The disciples listening to him had known him on the roads and in the houses of their own country. Now they heard him speak of his life with the Father before any of those places existed.", "Jesus turned to the people who had been with him. He had made the Father's name known to them and given them the words the Father gave him. They had received those words and come to believe that the Father sent him. Jesus prayed for them as people who belonged to the Father. He was coming to the Father, and they would remain in the world.", "He asked the Holy Father to keep them in his name so that they could be one. While Jesus had been with them, he had kept them and guarded them. The one destined for destruction had been lost, in fulfilment of Scripture. Judas had gone out into the night. As Jesus was about to go from the others too, he entrusted them to his Father's care."], "lesson": "Jesus speaks of eternal life in terms of knowing God. The prayer gives the disciples' security a place in the Father's care as Jesus' departure approaches.", "question": "How does Jesus describe eternal life as he begins to pray?"},
  {"id": 91204, "title": "A Prayer for Those Who Will Believe", "subtitle": "Unity, truth and a shared witness", "text": "John 17:13–26", "chapters": [[42, 17]], "start": 13, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus kept praying where the disciples could hear him. He wanted them to have his joy in full. He had given them the Father's word, and they had already been marked out by it. The world hated them because they did not belong to it. Jesus asked that the Father keep them from the evil one while they stayed where he had put them. The disciples still had work to do there.", "He asked the Father to make them holy through the truth and said that the Father's word is truth. The Father had sent him into the world, and he had sent the disciples into it in turn. Jesus spoke of dedicating himself for their sake so that they too could be made holy in truth. His prayer joined the work awaiting him that night to the work he was giving them.", "Then he prayed for people who had yet to hear their words. Others would believe through the testimony of those gathered there. Jesus asked that they all be one, as the Father was in him and he was in the Father. Their life together would help the world believe that the Father had sent him. He wanted the world to know that the Father loved them as he loved his Son.", "Jesus asked that those given to him would be with him and see the glory the Father had given him. He spoke of the Father's love for him before the world began. The world had failed to know the Father, and Jesus had made him known to the people who were his. He would go on making the Father's name known, so that this same love would be in them and he would be in them."], "lesson": "Jesus includes later hearers in his prayer. The unity he seeks belongs with truth and mission, because it bears witness to the Father who sent him.", "question": "How does Jesus connect the oneness of believers with what the world may come to know?"},
  {"id": 91205, "title": "The Garden, the Door and the Fire", "subtitle": "Jesus steps forward while Peter draws back", "text": "John 18:1–27", "chapters": [[42, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus crossed the Kidron valley with his disciples and went into a garden. Judas knew where to find him because Jesus had often met his disciples there. He came with soldiers and officers from the chief priests and Pharisees, carrying lanterns, torches and weapons. Jesus knew what was coming and went out to meet them. When they said they were looking for Jesus of Nazareth, he identified himself. They drew back and fell to the ground.", "Jesus asked again whom they were looking for. Once more he identified himself and asked them to let the others go. Peter drew a sword and struck off the right ear of the high priest's servant. John gives the servant's name as Malchus. Jesus told Peter to put the sword away. The Father had given him a cup to drink, and he would drink it. The officers bound him and took him first to Annas, the father-in-law of Caiaphas.", "Peter followed with another disciple who was known to the high priest. That disciple got Peter through the gate, where the servant keeping the door asked whether he belonged to Jesus. Peter denied it. Then he stood with the servants and officers at a charcoal fire they had made because it was cold.", "Inside, Jesus was questioned about his disciples and teaching. He told his questioner to ask those who had heard him speak openly. An officer struck him, and Annas sent him bound to Caiaphas. By the fire Peter denied Jesus again. A relative of Malchus then asked whether he had seen Peter in the garden. Peter denied it a third time, and at once a rooster crowed."], "lesson": "Jesus acts to protect his followers and speaks openly when questioned. John's movement between Jesus and Peter shows two responses under pressure.", "question": "What does Jesus ask for the disciples at the arrest, and what does Peter say about belonging to him?"},
  {"id": 91206, "title": "Pilate Asks About Truth", "subtitle": "Listening when a claim challenges your authority", "text": "John 18:28–40", "chapters": [[42, 18]], "start": 28, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Early in the morning Jesus was taken from Caiaphas to the governor's headquarters. The men who brought him stayed outside so they could eat the Passover without becoming ceremonially unclean. Pilate went out and asked what charge they were bringing. They replied that they would hardly have handed Jesus over if he were innocent. Pilate told them to judge him by their own law. They answered that they had no authority to put anyone to death. John connects this with what Jesus had said about the way he would die.", "Pilate went inside again and called Jesus. He asked whether he was the king of the Jews. Jesus asked where the question had come from. Pilate said that Jesus' own nation and its chief priests had handed him over. He wanted to know what Jesus had done.", "Jesus told him that his kingdom was not from this world. If it were, his servants would have fought to prevent his arrest. Pilate fastened on the word kingdom and asked whether Jesus was a king after all. Jesus spoke of what he had come into the world to do. He had come to bear witness to the truth, and those who belonged to the truth listened to his voice.", "Pilate asked what truth was and went outside again. He said he found no fault in Jesus. Then he brought up the custom of releasing a prisoner at Passover and offered them the man he had just questioned as their king. The people called for Barabbas instead. John tells us that Barabbas was a robber."], "lesson": "Pilate asks a striking question and leaves before John records any answer from Jesus. The scene asks whether our questions leave room to hear what might demand a decision.", "question": "How does Jesus explain the character of his kingdom to Pilate?"},
  {"id": 91207, "title": "The Crown and the Appeal to Caesar", "subtitle": "Authority and accountability", "text": "John 19:1–16", "chapters": [[42, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Pilate had Jesus flogged. The soldiers twisted thorns into a crown and set it on his head. They put a purple robe on him, came up to salute him as king and struck him. Pilate went outside and told the people again that he found no fault in Jesus. Then Jesus came out in the crown and robe. Pilate called on them to look at the man standing before them.", "The chief priests and officers called for crucifixion. Pilate told them to take Jesus themselves, repeating that he found no fault in him. They answered that by their law Jesus ought to die because he had made himself the Son of God. On hearing this, Pilate grew more afraid. He went indoors again and asked Jesus where he came from. Jesus gave him no answer.", "Pilate reminded Jesus that he had the power to release him or crucify him. Jesus replied that Pilate could have no power over him unless it had been given from above. He also said that the one who handed him over bore the greater sin. Pilate continued trying to release him, and those outside turned their charge toward Caesar. If Pilate released a man who called himself king, they said, he was showing himself to be no friend of Caesar.", "Pilate brought Jesus out and sat on the judgment seat at the Stone Pavement, called Gabbatha. It was the day of preparation for Passover. Pilate presented Jesus as their king. The chief priests declared that Caesar was their only king, and Pilate handed Jesus over to be crucified."], "lesson": "Pilate's authority is real enough for his decision to matter. Jesus' words place that authority under a higher accountability.", "question": "What different pressures can you see in Pilate's actions and questions?"},
  {"id": 91208, "title": "The Written Charge and the Finished Work", "subtitle": "Care and faithfulness at the cross", "text": "John 19:17–30", "chapters": [[42, 19]], "start": 17, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus went out carrying the cross to the Place of the Skull, called Golgotha. There the soldiers crucified him between two others. Pilate had an inscription placed over him naming Jesus of Nazareth as king of the Jews. The place was near the city and many could read the charge, which was written in Hebrew, Greek and Latin. The chief priests wanted Pilate to change it to say Jesus had claimed to be king. Pilate let the wording stand.", "The soldiers divided Jesus' clothing into four shares, one for each of them. They came to his tunic and found that it was woven in one piece. They agreed to leave it whole and cast lots for it. John sees in their action the fulfilment of Scripture about dividing someone's garments and casting lots for his clothing.", "Jesus' mother stood near the cross with other women, among them Mary Magdalene. Jesus saw his mother and the disciple he loved standing nearby. He addressed her first and gave her the disciple as a son. Then he gave the disciple Mary as a mother. From that hour the disciple took her into his own home. Even here, Jesus made provision for the people who would go home without him.", "Jesus knew that his work was now complete. To fulfil Scripture he said he was thirsty. There was a jar of sour wine nearby, and they put a sponge soaked in it on hyssop and raised it to his mouth. When he had received it, he said the work was finished. Then he bowed his head and gave up his spirit."], "lesson": "Jesus gives attention to his mother's future while in suffering himself. John records that personal act in the same scene as the public inscription and Jesus' last words.", "question": "What responsibility does Jesus give to the disciple standing near his mother?"},
  {"id": 91209, "title": "A Witness and a Burial Before Sunset", "subtitle": "Courage when public favour has gone", "text": "John 19:31–42", "chapters": [[42, 19]], "start": 31, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["The next day was a Sabbath of special importance. The leaders asked Pilate to have the legs of the crucified men broken and their bodies removed before the Sabbath began. The soldiers broke the legs of the men on either side of Jesus. When they reached him, they found he was already dead. They left his legs untouched, and one soldier pierced his side with a spear. Blood and water came out.", "At this point John pauses over the testimony of the person who saw it. His witness is given so that others may believe. John draws attention to two things that had happened in front of that witness. Jesus' bones had been left whole, and his side had been pierced. He connects both details with Scripture.", "Joseph of Arimathea had followed Jesus secretly because he was afraid. Now he went to Pilate and asked for permission to take away Jesus' body. Pilate agreed. Nicodemus came too, carrying about a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes. John reminds us that Nicodemus was the man who had first gone to Jesus at night. The two men wrapped the body in linen with the spices, following the Jewish burial custom.", "There was a garden near the place of execution and a new tomb in it where nobody had been laid. The day of preparation was drawing to its close, and the tomb was close at hand. Joseph and Nicodemus laid Jesus there. When John takes us back to the tomb, someone else will come looking for him."], "lesson": "Joseph and Nicodemus use their opportunity to care for Jesus after his death. Their actions bring a measure of public courage into lives John earlier describes as cautious.", "question": "How does Nicodemus's action here compare with the circumstances of his first visit in John 3?"},
  {"id": 91210, "title": "Mary Hears Her Name", "subtitle": "Recognition in the place of grief", "text": "John 20:1–18", "chapters": [[42, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Mary Magdalene went to the tomb early on the first day of the week while it was still dark. She saw the stone had been removed and ran to Peter and the disciple Jesus loved. She told them the Lord had been taken out of the tomb and that they did not know where he had been laid. Both men ran there. The other disciple arrived first, but Peter went in ahead of him.", "They saw the linen cloths and the cloth that had covered Jesus' head, lying apart. The other disciple entered and believed. John says they did not yet understand the Scripture that Jesus must rise from the dead. The men went back to their homes.", "Mary remained outside weeping. Looking into the tomb, she saw two angels sitting where the body had been. They asked why she wept, and she told them she did not know where her Lord had been taken. She turned and saw Jesus standing there, but did not recognise him. Thinking he was the gardener, she asked whether he had carried the body away and where he had put it.", "Jesus said her name. She turned and called him Teacher. He sent her to his brethren with a message about his going to his Father and theirs. Mary went to the disciples and told them she had seen the Lord and that he had spoken to her. Her morning had begun with a report about an empty place and ended with testimony to a person she had met."], "lesson": "Mary comes looking for a body and leaves with a message from Jesus. His speaking her name marks the moment recognition comes.", "question": "What does Mary assume at first, and what changes her understanding?"},
  {"id": 91211, "title": "Closed Doors and Thomas's Confession", "subtitle": "Peace for fearful disciples and faith for readers", "text": "John 20:19–31", "chapters": [[42, 20]], "start": 19, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["That evening the disciples were together behind locked doors. Mary Magdalene had brought them her news, and the fear of those outside was still real. Jesus came and stood among them. He greeted them with peace and showed them his hands and side. The wounds belonged to the man they had lost, and they were glad to see him standing there.", "Jesus spoke peace again and told them that he was sending them as the Father had sent him. He breathed on them and told them to receive the Holy Spirit. He gave them responsibility concerning the forgiveness and retention of sins. They were still in the room where they had shut themselves in, and Jesus was already speaking of sending them out.", "Thomas, one of the Twelve, had been absent. When the others told him they had seen the Lord, he said what it would take for him to believe. He wanted to see the nail marks and put his finger in them, and put his hand into Jesus' side. Eight days later the disciples were indoors again. This time Thomas was with them. Jesus stood among them, greeted them with peace and invited Thomas to examine the wounds as he had asked.", "Thomas answered by calling Jesus his Lord and his God. Jesus spoke of the blessing of those who would believe without seeing him. John then turns to the people reading the book. Jesus did many other signs in the disciples' presence. John chose the ones he recorded so readers might believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and have life in his name."], "lesson": "Jesus takes Thomas's stated difficulty seriously and calls him to believe. John then turns towards readers who will encounter the signs through testimony.", "question": "How does John's stated purpose connect Thomas's encounter to your reading of the Gospel?"},
  {"id": 91212, "title": "Breakfast by the Lake", "subtitle": "Care after a night of empty nets", "text": "John 21:1–14", "chapters": [[42, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Seven disciples were together by the Sea of Tiberias. Peter said he was going fishing, and the others went with him. They put out in the boat and worked through the night without a catch. At daybreak Jesus was standing on the shore. From the boat they could see a man there, but they did not know it was him. He called to ask if they had caught anything. They had to tell him they had nothing.", "Jesus told them to cast the net on the right side of the boat. When they did, there were so many fish they could hardly draw it in. The disciple Jesus loved recognised him first and told Peter it was the Lord. Peter pulled on his outer garment and threw himself into the water. The others brought the boat in, dragging the net behind it. They were only about two hundred cubits from shore.", "When they landed, they found a charcoal fire with fish already on it, and bread. Jesus told them to bring some of the fish they had caught. Peter hauled the net ashore. There were a hundred and fifty-three large fish in it, and the net had held. A little earlier, they had called back to the shore to say they had nothing.", "Jesus invited them to eat and gave them bread and fish. Nobody ventured to ask who he was. They knew now. John counts this as the third time Jesus showed himself to the disciples after his resurrection. The meal by the fire came before the conversation Jesus was about to have with Peter."], "lesson": "The risen Jesus meets the disciples in familiar work and provides a meal. John gives time to this quiet welcome before recording what Jesus will ask of Peter.", "question": "What details show that Jesus had prepared for the disciples before they came ashore?"},
  {"id": 91213, "title": "Peter Is Asked to Feed the Sheep", "subtitle": "Love expressed through patient responsibility", "text": "John 21:15–25", "chapters": [[42, 21]], "start": 15, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["After the disciples had eaten beside the lake, Jesus turned to Simon Peter. He asked him whether he loved him more than these. Peter answered by appealing to what Jesus knew: Jesus knew that he loved him. Jesus told him to feed his lambs. A second time Jesus asked about his love, and Peter answered much as before. Jesus gave him the care of his sheep.", "Jesus asked a third time. Peter was grieved at being asked again and replied that the Lord knew all things and knew that he loved him. Jesus again gave him the responsibility of feeding his sheep. The three questions gave Peter room to answer. Each answer was followed by work that required care for others.", "Jesus then spoke of a time when Peter would grow old, stretch out his hands and be taken where he would not wish to go. John explains that this indicated the manner of death by which Peter would glorify God. Jesus told him to follow him.", "Peter turned and saw the disciple Jesus loved following. He asked what would happen to him. Jesus answered that the other disciple's future did not alter Peter's own call to follow. A report spread that the other disciple would not die. John corrects the report by recalling the conditional way Jesus had spoken. The Gospel closes by affirming the disciple's testimony and acknowledging that much more could be written about what Jesus did."], "lesson": "Jesus gives Peter a practical expression for his love: care for the people entrusted to him. When Peter asks about someone else's path, Jesus puts his own responsibility before him again.", "question": "What task follows each question about Peter's love?"},
  {"id": 91214, "title": "From Jerusalem to the Ends of the Earth", "subtitle": "Waiting before setting out", "text": "Acts 1:1–11", "chapters": [[43, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Luke addresses Theophilus again as he begins the second part of his account. His first book told what Jesus began to do and teach up to the day he was taken into heaven. Now Luke goes back over the days between the resurrection and that departure. Jesus showed himself alive to the apostles repeatedly over forty days. They had seen him suffer, and now they had many proofs that he was alive. During those days he spoke to them about the kingdom of God.", "Jesus gave them an instruction that required staying where they were. They were to remain in Jerusalem and wait for what the Father had promised. John had baptised with water. In a few days they would be baptised with the Holy Spirit. The city where Jesus had been crucified was the place from which their work would begin.", "When they came together, the apostles asked whether he would now restore the kingdom to Israel. Jesus told them that the Father had kept the times and seasons under his own authority. Then he spoke of what they would be given. The Holy Spirit would come upon them with power, and they would bear witness to Jesus in Jerusalem, across Judea and Samaria, and to the ends of the earth.", "While they watched, Jesus was lifted up and a cloud took him from sight. The apostles kept looking into heaven. Two men in white stood beside them and asked why they were still looking up. This same Jesus, they said, would return in the way they had seen him go. The apostles had a promise about his return and an instruction about where they were to wait for what came next."], "lesson": "Jesus gives the disciples a responsibility they can prepare for and leaves the timetable they cannot control with the Father.", "question": "What does Jesus tell the disciples to expect, and what work does he give them?"},
  {"id": 91215, "title": "A Place Among the Twelve", "subtitle": "Prayer when a decision must be made", "text": "Acts 1:12–26", "chapters": [[43, 1]], "start": 12, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The apostles came back to Jerusalem from the Mount of Olives. It was a short walk, about the distance allowed on a Sabbath. They went to the upper room where they were staying. Luke names the eleven who were there. The women were with them, as were Mary the mother of Jesus and his brothers. They gave themselves together to prayer while they waited for what Jesus had promised.", "At a gathering of about a hundred and twenty, Peter stood up and spoke about Judas, who had guided the people who arrested Jesus. Judas had been counted among them and had shared in their ministry. Luke records the violent end associated with the field called Aceldama, the Field of Blood. Peter turned to the Psalms, finding there both a word about Judas's place being left desolate and a reason for someone else to take up his office.", "The person chosen had to be able to join the apostles as a witness to Jesus' resurrection. Peter said he should have been with them throughout Jesus' ministry, beginning with John's baptism and continuing to the day Jesus was taken up. The people put forward two men who had been there, Joseph called Barsabas, who was also known as Justus, and Matthias.", "The group prayed to the Lord, who knew what was in every heart, and asked him to show which man he had chosen for the work. They cast lots, and the lot fell to Matthias. He was counted with the eleven apostles. Judas's place among the Twelve was filled as they prepared for the testimony they would soon have to give."], "lesson": "The community takes care over who can bear witness, then acknowledges the limits of what people can see of another person's heart.", "question": "What qualifications does Peter name for the person who will take Judas's place?"},
  {"id": 91216, "title": "The Day the Crowd Heard Its Own Languages", "subtitle": "The Spirit and the good news", "text": "Acts 2:1–21", "chapters": [[43, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When the day of Pentecost came, the believers were together in one place. Suddenly a sound like a rushing wind came from heaven and filled the house where they were sitting. What looked like divided tongues of fire appeared and rested on them. They were filled with the Holy Spirit and began speaking in other languages as the Spirit enabled them.", "Jerusalem was home at that time to devout Jews from many countries. When a crowd gathered, people were bewildered because they heard the speakers in the languages of the places where they had been born. They recognised the speakers as Galileans and wondered how they could hear their own languages. Luke names people from places as far apart as Mesopotamia, Egypt and Rome, all hearing about God's mighty works.", "Some asked one another what it meant. Others mocked and said the speakers had been drinking. Peter stood with the eleven and raised his voice. He told the crowd that it was still early in the morning and that the people were not drunk, as some supposed. Then he turned to the prophet Joel to explain what the crowd had witnessed.", "Joel had spoken of God pouring out his Spirit. Sons and daughters would prophesy, young people would see visions and old people would dream. Servants would be included. Joel's words ended with the promise that whoever called on the Lord's name would be saved. Peter put that promise before people who were already hearing the wonders of God in their own languages."], "lesson": "The Spirit enables the gathered people to hear rather than leaving them to cross the language barrier alone. Peter gives an explanation grounded in Scripture.", "question": "What did the hearers recognise about the languages in which the disciples spoke?"},
  {"id": 91217, "title": "Peter Finishes Speaking and the People Respond", "subtitle": "Repentance and a shared life", "text": "Acts 2:22–47", "chapters": [[43, 2]], "start": 22, "end": 47, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Peter spoke to the people about Jesus of Nazareth. They knew the works God had done through him. Jesus had been handed over and crucified, Peter said, but God raised him. He read David's words about hope beyond the grave and distinguished David, whose tomb was known to them, from the one whose resurrection David foresaw. Peter said he and the others were witnesses. He connected what the crowd now saw and heard with Jesus receiving the promised Spirit from the Father and pouring him out.", "Peter ended by telling the people that God had made the Jesus they crucified both Lord and Christ. They were cut to the heart and asked Peter and the other apostles what they should do. Peter called them to repent and be baptised in Jesus Christ's name for the forgiveness of sins. He spoke of the gift of the Spirit and said the promise reached their children and people far away whom the Lord would call.", "Those who received his message were baptised. About three thousand were added that day. Luke then follows them past the public event. They continued in the apostles' teaching, fellowship, breaking of bread and prayers.", "People shared their possessions to meet needs. They met in the temple and broke bread in homes, eating with gladness and simplicity of heart. As they praised God and received favour among the people, the Lord added to the church. Luke gives the reader a picture of what continued after the day's excitement."], "lesson": "Luke follows the public response with the daily practices that gave it substance. Repentance had consequences for the people's time, possessions and relationships.", "question": "Which habits continued after the people heard Peter and were baptised?"},
  {"id": 91218, "title": "A Man Stands at the Beautiful Gate", "subtitle": "Mercy that directs attention to Jesus", "text": "Acts 3:1–26", "chapters": [[43, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Peter and John were going to the temple at the afternoon hour of prayer. Near the entrance called the Beautiful Gate, a man who had been unable to walk from birth was being carried to his usual place. People laid him there every day so he could ask worshippers for alms. When Peter and John approached, he asked them too. Peter told him to look at them, and the man looked up expecting money.", "Peter had no silver or gold to give him. He called on the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth and told the man to walk. Then he took him by the right hand and lifted him. Strength came into the man's feet and ankles. He stood, walked into the temple with them and began leaping and praising God. People recognised the man they were accustomed to seeing at the gate and came running to the porch called Solomon's.", "As the man held on to Peter and John, Peter asked the crowd why they were staring at them as if their own power or holiness had made him walk. The God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob had glorified Jesus, whom the people had rejected and handed over. They had asked for a murderer and killed the author of life. God had raised him, and the apostles were witnesses. Peter said the man stood before them sound through faith in Jesus' name.", "Peter acknowledged that the people and their rulers had acted in ignorance. He urged them to repent so their sins could be wiped away. He drew together the promises spoken by Moses and the prophets and the blessing God had promised Abraham for all the families of the earth. God had sent his servant to them first, Peter said, to bless them by turning each of them from wrongdoing."], "lesson": "Peter attends to the man and then to the crowd's misunderstanding. The power he exercises is an occasion to direct attention towards Jesus.", "question": "How does Peter answer the crowd's assumption about his power or holiness?"},
  {"id": 91219, "title": "A Council Sees the Man Standing", "subtitle": "Speaking with courage about what you know", "text": "Acts 4:1–22", "chapters": [[43, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Peter and John were still speaking when the priests, the captain of the temple and the Sadducees came up to them. They objected to the apostles teaching the people and proclaiming resurrection through Jesus. Since it was already evening, they put the two men in custody until the next day. The message had already reached many hearers. Luke puts the number of men who believed at about five thousand.", "The next day the rulers, elders and scribes assembled, with Annas, Caiaphas and others of the high priest's family. They set the apostles in the middle and asked by what power or name they had acted. Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, said the man had been made well through Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom they had crucified and God had raised. The stone they had rejected had become the cornerstone. Peter told them that salvation was found in Jesus' name.", "The council saw how freely Peter and John spoke and recognised that they had been with Jesus. The healed man was there too, standing beside them. The council sent the three outside and talked over what they could do. A sign had happened that people throughout Jerusalem knew about. They decided to threaten the apostles and forbid them to speak or teach in Jesus' name.", "Peter and John asked the rulers to judge whether it was right before God to obey them in place of God. They had to speak about what they had seen and heard. After further threats the council released them. The people were praising God for what had happened to the man, who was more than forty years old."], "lesson": "Their answer rests on a testimony they can give honestly. The man standing with them makes the subject of the proceedings visible.", "question": "What evidence does Luke name that the council could see for itself?"},
  {"id": 91220, "title": "The Prayer for Boldness", "subtitle": "Courage and care within the church", "text": "Acts 4:23–37", "chapters": [[43, 4]], "start": 23, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When Peter and John were released, they went back to their own people and told them what the chief priests and elders had said. The believers lifted their voices together in prayer. They began with God as maker of heaven, earth and sea, then recalled the words of David about rulers joining forces against the Lord and his anointed. The opposition they had just heard about had a history they recognised in Scripture.", "They named Herod and Pontius Pilate, the Gentiles and the people of Israel who had gathered against Jesus in that city. They acknowledged that the events had taken place within what God had determined beforehand. Now they asked the Lord to consider the threats and give his servants boldness to speak. They asked him to stretch out his hand to heal and do signs through the name of Jesus.", "When they finished praying, the place where they were gathered shook. They were filled with the Holy Spirit and spoke God's word boldly. Luke then takes the reader from that shared prayer to the way the believers were living together. They were of one heart and soul, and people made what they owned available to one another. The apostles spoke with great power about the resurrection, and great grace was upon them all.", "Those who owned lands or houses sold them and brought the proceeds to the apostles to distribute according to need. Luke names one of them. Joseph was a Levite from Cyprus whom the apostles called Barnabas, meaning son of encouragement. He sold a field and brought the money to the apostles. The gift belonged to the same life Luke had just described, sustained by prayer and attentive to the people who would otherwise go without."], "lesson": "The community asks for courage to carry on with its work. Luke places that prayer beside generosity that answers needs within the same community.", "question": "What do the believers ask God to give them after hearing about the threats?"},
  {"id": 91221, "title": "Ananias and Sapphira's Agreement", "subtitle": "Honesty before God", "text": "Acts 5:1–16", "chapters": [[43, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Luke has just told how Barnabas sold a field and brought the money to the apostles. Now Ananias and his wife Sapphira sold a property of their own. They agreed to keep some of the money back. Ananias brought the rest and laid it before the apostles. Peter asked him why he had allowed Satan to fill his heart so that he lied to the Holy Spirit about the proceeds.", "Peter reminded Ananias that the property had been his before the sale and that the money was still in his power afterward. He could decide what to do with it. The lie he had chosen to tell was a lie to God. When Ananias heard Peter's words, he fell down and died. Young men wrapped his body, carried him out and buried him. The news brought great fear to those who heard it.", "About three hours later Sapphira came in, unaware of what had happened. Peter asked whether the amount Ananias had brought was what they had received for the property. She said it was. Peter asked how they had agreed to put the Spirit of the Lord to the test. The men who had buried Ananias were at the door. Sapphira fell at Peter's feet and died, and they carried her out and buried her beside her husband.", "Fear came over the whole church and everyone who heard the account. Luke goes on to describe signs performed through the apostles and believers gathering at Solomon's Porch. More men and women came to believe. People brought the sick into the streets and laid them on beds and mats in the hope that even Peter's shadow might fall on them. Crowds came from towns around Jerusalem, bringing the sick and those troubled by unclean spirits, and they were healed."], "lesson": "Peter's question makes clear that retaining money was possible. The public appearance of a gift became an occasion for dishonesty about what had been given.", "question": "What freedom did Peter say Ananias had over the property and the proceeds?"},
  {"id": 91222, "title": "The Open Prison and the Unfinished Work", "subtitle": "Obedience after a warning", "text": "Acts 5:17–42", "chapters": [[43, 5]], "start": 17, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The high priest and the Sadducees with him were filled with jealousy. They arrested the apostles and put them in the public prison. During the night an angel of the Lord opened the prison doors and led them out. He directed them to go to the temple and tell the people the words of this life. At daybreak they went to the temple and began teaching.", "The council met and sent for the prisoners. The officers found the doors shut and the guards at their posts, but the prison was empty. Then someone reported that the men they had locked up were teaching in the temple. The captain and officers brought them in without force. They feared that the people might stone them.", "The high priest reminded the apostles that they had been forbidden to teach in Jesus' name. Peter and the others said they must obey God. God had raised Jesus, whom the rulers had hung on a tree, and exalted him as Prince and Saviour to give Israel repentance and forgiveness. The apostles were witnesses, and so was the Holy Spirit. The council was furious and wanted to kill them.", "Gamaliel, a respected teacher of the law, had the apostles sent outside. He recalled how the followers of Theudas and Judas the Galilean had dispersed after their leaders died. He advised the council to leave the apostles alone. If their work came from men, it would fail. If it came from God, the council could find itself fighting God. They took his advice, though they beat the apostles and ordered them to stop speaking in Jesus' name. The apostles left rejoicing that they had suffered for him. They went on teaching about Jesus daily in the temple and in homes."], "lesson": "The opened prison leads straight back to the work that caused their arrest. Their courage is measured by what they continue doing after the council releases them.", "question": "Where do the apostles teach after receiving orders to stop?"},
  {"id": 91223, "title": "The Widows Who Were Overlooked", "subtitle": "Fair service and shared responsibility", "text": "Acts 6:1–15", "chapters": [[43, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The number of disciples was growing, and a complaint came from the Greek-speaking Jews against the Hebrew-speaking Jews. Their widows were being overlooked in the daily distribution. The Twelve called the disciples together. The work of serving at the tables needed people who could give it proper attention, and the apostles needed to keep their attention on prayer and the ministry of the word.", "The Twelve asked the group to choose seven men of good reputation, full of the Holy Spirit and wisdom, whom they could put in charge. The proposal pleased the gathering. They chose Stephen, Philip, Prochorus, Nicanor, Timon, Parmenas and Nicolas, a convert to Judaism from Antioch. They brought the men before the apostles, who prayed and laid their hands on them. Luke records that the word of God spread, the number of disciples in Jerusalem grew greatly and many priests accepted the faith.", "Then Luke stays with Stephen. Full of grace and power, he did wonders and signs among the people. Men from the synagogue associated with the Freedmen and from groups connected with Cyrene, Alexandria, Cilicia and Asia argued with him. They could not withstand the wisdom and the Spirit with which he spoke. They persuaded others to accuse him of speaking against Moses and against God.", "The people, elders and scribes were stirred up. Stephen was seized and brought before the council. False witnesses said he spoke against the holy place and the law. They accused him of saying Jesus of Nazareth would destroy the place and change the customs Moses had handed down. The people sitting in the council fixed their eyes on Stephen. Luke says his face looked like the face of an angel."], "lesson": "The complaint exposes a specific gap in care. The community answers it by giving capable people responsibility and recognising their work publicly.", "question": "What change made it possible to respond to the widows' needs while the apostles continued their work?"},
  {"id": 91224, "title": "Stephen Begins With Abraham and Joseph", "subtitle": "God's presence in unfamiliar places", "text": "Acts 7:1–29", "chapters": [[43, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The high priest asked Stephen whether the charges were true. Stephen began his answer far from Jerusalem. The God of glory had appeared to Abraham in Mesopotamia, before Abraham lived in Haran, and called him to leave his country and relatives. God moved him on to the land where Stephen's hearers now lived. Abraham received no piece of it to call his own at the time, yet God promised the land to his descendants before he even had a child. God also told him they would live as strangers and suffer oppression for four hundred years before coming out to worship him.", "Stephen traced the family from Abraham through Isaac, Jacob and the twelve patriarchs. Joseph's brothers grew jealous and sold him into Egypt. God was with Joseph there, brought him through his troubles and gave him favour and wisdom before Pharaoh. When famine came, the brothers went to Egypt for food. On the second visit Joseph made himself known. He sent for Jacob and the whole family, seventy-five people in Stephen's account. Jacob and the patriarchs died in Egypt, and Stephen speaks of their burial in the ancestral tomb at Shechem.", "As the time of the promise drew near, the people multiplied in Egypt. A new king who had no knowledge of Joseph dealt cruelly with them. Their infants were put out so they would not survive. Moses was born in that period. He was kept for three months in his father's house, then Pharaoh's daughter took him in and raised him as her son. He was educated in the wisdom of Egypt.", "At forty, Moses went to see his own people and defended an Israelite by striking down the Egyptian who was mistreating him. The next day he tried to reconcile two Israelites who were quarrelling. The man doing wrong pushed Moses away and asked who had made him ruler and judge. He knew what Moses had done the previous day. Moses fled to Midian, where he lived as a stranger and had two sons."], "lesson": "Stephen recalls God working through people living far from the places later associated with worship. His history also shows how quickly a brother's help can be refused.", "question": "Where does Stephen say God was with Joseph, and what had brought Joseph there?"},
  {"id": 91225, "title": "The Burning Bush and Stephen's Last Prayer", "subtitle": "Faithful witness and forgiveness", "text": "Acts 7:30–60", "chapters": [[43, 7]], "start": 30, "end": 60, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Forty years passed before Moses saw the flame in the bush in the wilderness near Mount Sinai. As he drew close, he heard the voice of the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. He was told to take off his sandals because the ground was holy. God had seen the suffering of his people and heard them groan. He would send Moses back to Egypt, the very man whom one of his own people had refused as ruler and judge.", "Moses led them out with signs in Egypt, at the Red Sea and in the wilderness. He spoke of the prophet God would raise up from among their people. The people refused to obey Moses, turned back toward Egypt in their hearts and made a calf to worship. Stephen recalled the tent of testimony they carried in the wilderness, which came into the land with Joshua. David sought a dwelling for God and Solomon built the house. Stephen quoted the prophet to remind his hearers that the Most High could never be contained in a house built by human hands.", "Stephen then spoke directly to the council. They resisted the Holy Spirit as their fathers had done. Their fathers persecuted the prophets who foretold the coming of the Righteous One. Now they themselves had betrayed and killed him. They had received the law, and Stephen charged them with failing to keep it.", "The hearers were enraged. Stephen, full of the Holy Spirit, looked up and saw Jesus standing at God's right hand. When he told them what he saw, they covered their ears and rushed at him. They drove him out and stoned him, leaving their coats with a young man named Saul. Stephen asked Jesus to receive his spirit. Then he knelt and asked the Lord to forgive those killing him. After that prayer he died."], "lesson": "Stephen ends a severe speech with a prayer for his attackers. The two actions show that naming wrongdoing and praying for those responsible can belong together.", "question": "What two requests does Stephen make as he is dying?"},
  {"id": 91226, "title": "Scattered People Carry the Word", "subtitle": "A gift beyond purchase", "text": "Acts 8:1–25", "chapters": [[43, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Saul approved of Stephen's death. That day persecution broke out against the church in Jerusalem, and believers were scattered through Judea and Samaria while the apostles remained in the city. Devout men buried Stephen and mourned him deeply. Saul went from house to house, dragging away men and women and sending them to prison. The people forced to leave Jerusalem carried the message with them.", "Philip went to a city in Samaria and proclaimed Christ. The crowds listened as they heard him and saw what happened among them. Unclean spirits came out of people, and many who had been paralysed or unable to walk were healed. Joy spread through the city. For a long time the people had been impressed by a man named Simon, who practised sorcery and presented himself as someone great. Now men and women believed Philip's message about God's kingdom and Jesus Christ and were baptised. Simon was baptised too and followed Philip, amazed at the signs.", "Word reached Jerusalem that Samaria had received God's word, so the apostles sent Peter and John. The two prayed for the believers and laid their hands on them, and they received the Holy Spirit. Simon saw what happened and offered the apostles money. He wanted the power to give the Holy Spirit to anyone on whom he laid his hands.", "Peter told Simon that God's gift could never be bought. His heart was wrong before God, and he needed to turn from his wickedness and pray for forgiveness. Simon asked them to pray that the things Peter had spoken of would not happen to him. Peter and John bore witness and preached in many Samaritan villages on their way back to Jerusalem."], "lesson": "Persecution sends the message beyond Jerusalem. Simon's response shows how easily a person can see God's gift through the habits of an earlier life.", "question": "What does Simon ask to buy, and why does Peter reject the offer?"},
  {"id": 91227, "title": "Philip Meets a Reader on the Road", "subtitle": "Beginning with the question someone is asking", "text": "Acts 8:26–40", "chapters": [[43, 8]], "start": 26, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["An angel of the Lord told Philip to go south to the road from Jerusalem to Gaza. Philip went and saw an Ethiopian official returning from worship in Jerusalem. The man was responsible for the treasury of Candace, queen of the Ethiopians. He was sitting in his chariot reading Isaiah as he travelled.", "The Spirit told Philip to go near and join the chariot. Philip ran alongside and heard the man reading. He asked whether he understood what he was reading. The official answered that he needed someone to guide him and invited Philip to sit with him. The passage described someone led like a sheep to slaughter, silent before the one who shears it. The official asked whether the prophet was speaking about himself or about someone else.", "Philip began with that passage and told him the good news about Jesus. As they went along, they came to water. The official pointed it out and asked what prevented him from being baptised. In the passage as printed in the King James Version, their exchange includes his confession of faith in Jesus Christ as the Son of God. He ordered the chariot to stop, and both men went down into the water. Philip baptised him.", "When they came up, the Spirit took Philip away. The official did not see him again and went on his way rejoicing. Philip was found at Azotus and preached in the towns as he travelled towards Caesarea."], "lesson": "Philip asks a question before giving an explanation. He begins where the man is reading and responds to the question the man actually brings.", "question": "Which question opens the conversation, and which Scripture is the man reading?"},
  {"id": 91228, "title": "Saul Is Led by the Hand", "subtitle": "Grace that changes a persecutor's direction", "text": "Acts 9:1–22", "chapters": [[43, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Saul was still threatening the followers of Jesus. He obtained letters to the synagogues in Damascus so that he could arrest people of the Way and bring them bound to Jerusalem. As he approached Damascus, a light from heaven shone around him. He fell to the ground and heard a voice asking why he was persecuting him. Saul asked who was speaking. The answer was Jesus, the one he was persecuting.", "Saul rose and opened his eyes but could not see. His companions led him by the hand into Damascus. For three days he was without sight and ate and drank nothing. The man who had set out with authority to take others away had to be led into the city himself.", "In Damascus the Lord called a disciple named Ananias and gave him the street and house where he would find Saul. Ananias had heard of the harm Saul had done in Jerusalem and knew why he had come. The Lord told him to go and explained that Saul was chosen to carry his name and would suffer for it. Ananias went into the house, laid his hands on Saul and addressed him as a brother.", "Something like scales fell from Saul's eyes. He could see, rose and was baptised, then took food and regained strength. He spent time with the disciples in Damascus and began preaching that Jesus was the Son of God. Those who heard were astonished to hear that message from the man who had come to arrest people for calling on Jesus' name."], "lesson": "Ananias names a reasonable fear and then obeys. His greeting gives a new relationship practical expression before Saul has built a different reputation.", "question": "What did Ananias know about Saul, and what did he choose to do?"},
  {"id": 91229, "title": "A Basket, a Friend and a Room of Mourners", "subtitle": "The value of help that can be seen", "text": "Acts 9:23–43", "chapters": [[43, 9]], "start": 23, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Saul had been preaching in Damascus that Jesus was the Christ. After many days, some of his opponents plotted to kill him and watched the city gates day and night. Saul learned of the plan. His disciples found another way through the wall. During the night they lowered him in a basket, beyond the reach of the people waiting at the gates.", "In Jerusalem, Saul tried to join the disciples. They knew his reputation and were afraid that his claim to be a disciple was false. Barnabas took him to the apostles and told them how Saul had seen the Lord on the road and spoken boldly in Jesus' name in Damascus. Saul went about with the believers in Jerusalem and spoke openly there too. When the Greek-speaking Jews he disputed with tried to kill him, the believers took him down to Caesarea and sent him on to Tarsus. Luke records a time of peace and growth for the churches across Judea, Galilee and Samaria.", "Peter travelled among the believers and came to Lydda. There he found Aeneas, who had been paralysed and confined to bed for eight years. Peter told him that Jesus Christ healed him and that he should get up and make his bed. Aeneas rose at once. People in Lydda and the plain of Sharon saw him and turned to the Lord.", "In nearby Joppa, a disciple named Tabitha, also called Dorcas, fell ill and died. The widows stood weeping beside Peter when he arrived, showing him garments she had made. Peter sent everyone out, knelt and prayed. He called Tabitha to get up, and she opened her eyes. He gave her his hand and presented her alive to the believers and widows. As news spread and many believed, Peter stayed in Joppa with Simon, a tanner."], "lesson": "The basket, Barnabas's testimony and the garments make care visible. Luke gives attention to service which would otherwise remain in the background.", "question": "What did the widows bring to show Peter about Dorcas's life?"},
  {"id": 91230, "title": "Two Households Receive Instructions", "subtitle": "Letting God correct a settled distinction", "text": "Acts 10:1–23", "chapters": [[43, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Cornelius lived in Caesarea and commanded a hundred soldiers in the Italian regiment. He and his household feared God. He gave generously to the people and prayed regularly. One afternoon he saw an angel come in and call him by name. Frightened, Cornelius asked what it meant. The angel said his prayers and gifts had come before God and told him to send to Joppa for Simon Peter, who was lodging with Simon the tanner by the sea.", "Cornelius called two servants and a devout soldier, told them what had happened and sent them off. The next day, as they approached Joppa, Peter went up on the roof to pray at noon. He grew hungry, and while food was being prepared he fell into a trance. He saw something like a large sheet lowered by its four corners, with animals, creeping things and birds inside.", "A voice told Peter to kill and eat. He protested that he had never eaten anything common or unclean. The voice told him to stop calling unclean what God had made clean. This happened three times and the sheet was taken up. Peter was still wondering what the vision meant when the men from Caesarea reached the gate and asked for him.", "The Spirit told Peter that three men were looking for him and that he should go with them without hesitation. Downstairs, the men explained who Cornelius was and how an angel had told him to send for Peter. Peter brought them in and gave them lodging. The next day he set out with them, accompanied by some of the believers from Joppa."], "lesson": "Peter has to act on the direction before he fully understands his vision. The visitors at the door turn an unfamiliar lesson into a concrete choice.", "question": "How do Cornelius's messengers help Peter begin to see what the vision concerns?"},
  {"id": 91231, "title": "Peter Enters Cornelius's House", "subtitle": "Receiving people whom God receives", "text": "Acts 10:24–48", "chapters": [[43, 10]], "start": 24, "end": 48, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Cornelius was expecting Peter and had called together his relatives and close friends. When Peter came in, Cornelius fell at his feet. Peter raised him and reminded him that he too was a man. Inside, Peter found many people waiting. He explained that God had shown him he should call no person common or unclean. That was why he had come when they sent for him. He asked Cornelius what had led him to do so.", "Cornelius told the people what had happened as he prayed. The man in shining clothing had spoken of his prayers and gifts and told him where to find Peter. He had sent immediately. Now everyone was there in God's presence to hear what Peter had been commanded to say. Peter began by acknowledging what this visit had taught him. God shows no partiality. In every nation he receives those who fear him and do what is right.", "Peter spoke about Jesus of Nazareth, whom God anointed with the Holy Spirit and power. Jesus went about doing good and healing people, and Peter and the other witnesses had seen his work. People had put him to death on a tree, and God had raised him on the third day. Peter spoke as someone who had eaten and drunk with Jesus after he rose. The prophets bore witness that those who believed in him would receive forgiveness through his name.", "While Peter was still speaking, the Holy Spirit fell on those listening. The Jewish believers who had come with him were astonished to hear Gentiles speaking in tongues and praising God. Peter asked who could refuse water to people who had received the Holy Spirit as they had. He ordered them to be baptised, and they asked him to stay a few days."], "lesson": "Peter's understanding changes through meeting people and seeing what God does among them. He lets that evidence govern his welcome.", "question": "What reason does Peter give for baptising these hearers?"},
  {"id": 91232, "title": "Peter Tells the Whole Story", "subtitle": "Listening before drawing a boundary", "text": "Acts 11:1–18", "chapters": [[43, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The news that Gentiles had received God's word reached the apostles and believers in Judea. When Peter went up to Jerusalem, some of the Jewish believers challenged him for going into a house of uncircumcised men and eating with them. Peter began at the beginning and told them what had happened in order. The people who had heard what he did were about to hear what had led him there.", "He described praying in Joppa, seeing the sheet descend and looking closely at the animals and birds on it. He told them what the voice had commanded and how he had answered. Three times he had been told to stop calling unclean what God had cleansed. As soon as the vision ended, the three men from Caesarea arrived. The Spirit directed him to go with them without making a distinction. Six believers went with him and entered the house.", "Peter told them what the man of the house had reported. An angel had instructed him to send for Peter, who would bring a message by which he and his household would be saved. As Peter began to speak, the Holy Spirit fell on them as on the disciples at the beginning. Peter remembered Jesus' words about John's baptism with water and the baptism with the Holy Spirit.", "God had given these people the same gift he had given the disciples who believed in Jesus Christ. Peter asked what right he had to stand in God's way. The objections ceased. The people who had questioned him now glorified God, acknowledging that he had granted the Gentiles repentance leading to life."], "lesson": "Peter answers the objection with the sequence of what happened. The hearers allow that account to change their judgement.", "question": "What event in Peter's account persuaded the hearers that God was at work?"},
  {"id": 91233, "title": "The Church That Grew in Antioch", "subtitle": "Encouragement that grows into practical help", "text": "Acts 11:19–30", "chapters": [[43, 11]], "start": 19, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When persecution followed Stephen's death, believers travelled as far as Phoenicia, Cyprus and Antioch. At first the people who scattered took the message to Jews alone. Some men from Cyprus and Cyrene came to Antioch and also preached the Lord Jesus to Greek-speaking hearers there. The hand of the Lord was with them. Many believed and turned to him, and word of this reached the church in Jerusalem.", "The church sent Barnabas to Antioch. When he arrived, he saw what God's grace had done and was glad. He urged them all to hold to the Lord with a settled purpose. Luke calls Barnabas a good man, full of the Holy Spirit and faith, and many more people were brought to the Lord. There was work enough for another teacher, so Barnabas went to Tarsus to look for Saul.", "When Barnabas found him, he brought him back to Antioch. For a whole year the two met with the church and taught many people. Luke pauses over a name that would travel beyond this city. It was in Antioch that the disciples were first called Christians.", "In those days prophets came down from Jerusalem, and one of them, Agabus, foretold by the Spirit that a severe famine would spread over the inhabited world. Luke says it came in the days of Claudius. The disciples in Antioch decided each to give according to what he could afford, so the believers in Judea would have help. They sent the relief to the elders through Barnabas and Saul. The people who had welcomed teachers from Jerusalem were now sending help to believers in that region."], "lesson": "Barnabas sees what has begun and brings help to strengthen it. The people who receive teaching in Antioch become people who send relief elsewhere.", "question": "How does Antioch respond to the prospect of hardship in Judea?"},
  {"id": 91234, "title": "Peter at the Gate While the Church Prays", "subtitle": "Making room for an unexpected answer", "text": "Acts 12:1–19", "chapters": [[43, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["King Herod laid hands on members of the church and killed James, the brother of John, with the sword. When he saw that this pleased some of the people, he had Peter arrested during the Feast of Unleavened Bread. Four squads of soldiers took turns guarding him. Herod meant to bring him out after Passover. While Peter was held in prison, the church prayed earnestly for him.", "The night before Herod was to bring him out, Peter slept between two soldiers, bound with two chains, with guards at the door. An angel appeared and light shone in the cell. He struck Peter on the side to wake him and told him to get up quickly. The chains fell away. Peter put on his sandals and cloak and followed, thinking he was seeing a vision. They passed two guard posts. The iron gate to the city opened of itself, and the angel left him after they had gone along one street.", "Peter came to himself and went to the house of Mary, the mother of John Mark, where many people were praying. A servant named Rhoda answered his knock. She recognised Peter's voice and was so glad that she ran inside to tell the others without opening the gate. They thought she was out of her mind. When she insisted, they suggested it was his angel. Peter kept knocking until they opened it and saw him.", "Peter motioned for quiet, told them how the Lord had brought him out and asked them to tell James and the other believers. Then he left for another place. In the morning the soldiers could not account for his disappearance. Herod searched for him, questioned the guards and ordered them executed. Then he went down to Caesarea."], "lesson": "Luke leaves James's death and Peter's deliverance in the same account. The house scene also shows people praying in earnest and struggling to believe a report of deliverance.", "question": "What did Rhoda recognise before the others were willing to believe her?"},
  {"id": 91235, "title": "Herod Receives the Crowd's Praise", "subtitle": "The danger of taking God's honour", "text": "Acts 12:20–25", "chapters": [[43, 12]], "start": 20, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Herod was in Caesarea after his search for Peter. Luke now turns to a dispute that had made the king very angry with the people of Tyre and Sidon. The two cities acted together and came to seek peace. They depended on the king's territory for food, so they had reason to settle the quarrel. They secured the help of Blastus, who was in charge of the king's chamber, and made their request.", "On an appointed day Herod put on his royal garments and sat on his throne to address them. Luke leaves the contents of the speech unrecorded. He tells us what the crowd called out when they heard it. They acclaimed what they were hearing as the voice of a god. The king they had come to appease was sitting before them in his royal robes as the acclamation went up.", "Luke says that immediately an angel of the Lord struck Herod because he had failed to give glory to God. Herod was eaten by worms and died. The king who had ordered the death of James and put Peter in prison came to his own end in Caesarea. After recording that death, Luke tells us that the word of God continued to grow and spread.", "Barnabas and Saul had gone to deliver the help the believers in Antioch had gathered for Judea. They completed that service and returned, taking John Mark with them. We have already heard his name in connection with the house where people prayed for Peter. He now goes with the two men who will soon be sent out from Antioch."], "lesson": "The crowd's dependence gives its praise a setting worth noticing. Herod accepts what should be given to God, and Luke contrasts his end with the spread of the word.", "question": "What reason does Luke give for Herod's judgement?"},
  {"id": 91236, "title": "Sent From Antioch to Cyprus", "subtitle": "Service begun in prayer", "text": "Acts 13:1–12", "chapters": [[43, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The church in Antioch had several prophets and teachers. Luke names Barnabas, Simeon called Niger, Lucius of Cyrene, Manaen, who had been brought up with Herod the tetrarch, and Saul. While they worshipped the Lord and fasted, the Holy Spirit told them to set apart Barnabas and Saul for the work to which he had called them. They fasted and prayed, laid their hands on the two men and sent them off.", "Sent out by the Holy Spirit, Barnabas and Saul went down to Seleucia and sailed from there to Cyprus. At Salamis they proclaimed God's word in the Jewish synagogues. John Mark went with them as their assistant. They crossed the island as far as Paphos, where they met a Jewish sorcerer and false prophet called Bar-Jesus. He was with Sergius Paulus, the Roman governor, whom Luke describes as a discerning man.", "The governor called for Barnabas and Saul because he wanted to hear God's word. The sorcerer, also called Elymas, opposed them and tried to turn him away from the faith. Here Luke tells us that Saul was also called Paul. Filled with the Holy Spirit, Paul looked steadily at Elymas and charged him with twisting the straight ways of the Lord. He told him that the Lord's hand was against him and that for a time he would be blind.", "Mist and darkness fell on Elymas at once. He went about looking for someone to lead him by the hand. Having seen what happened, the governor believed. Luke says he was astonished at the teaching of the Lord, the very teaching he had called the visitors to hear."], "lesson": "The journey begins with worship and shared discernment. Opposition does not prevent the governor from hearing the teaching he asked for.", "question": "What was the church doing when the call to set apart Barnabas and Saul came?"},
  {"id": 91237, "title": "Paul Speaks in Pisidian Antioch", "subtitle": "Hearing the promise and answering it", "text": "Acts 13:13–52", "chapters": [[43, 13]], "start": 13, "end": 52, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul and his companions sailed from Paphos to Perga in Pamphylia. John Mark left them there and returned to Jerusalem. Paul and Barnabas continued inland to Pisidian Antioch. On the Sabbath they sat in the synagogue. After the reading from the Law and the Prophets, the synagogue rulers invited them to speak if they had a word of encouragement. Paul stood and motioned for the people to listen.", "He began with the history they knew. God had chosen Israel's ancestors, brought the people out of Egypt, borne with them in the wilderness and given them their land. Paul spoke of the judges, Saul the king and David. From David's descendants God had brought Israel the promised Saviour, Jesus. John had prepared the way by preaching repentance and had pointed to the one who came after him. Paul said the people of Jerusalem and their rulers had fulfilled the prophets' words in condemning Jesus. They laid him in a tomb, and God raised him.", "Jesus was seen for many days by people who had travelled with him from Galilee. Paul announced that God had fulfilled his promise to their ancestors. He drew on the Psalms to show that Jesus, unlike David, rose without seeing decay. Through Jesus, he said, forgiveness was being proclaimed and everyone who believed was justified in ways the law of Moses could never provide. He ended with a warning from the prophets and was asked to speak again the following Sabbath.", "Almost the whole city gathered the next week. Some who saw the crowd grew jealous and contradicted Paul. He and Barnabas said they had brought the message to their own people first and would now turn to the Gentiles. Many Gentiles rejoiced, and the word spread through the region. When opponents stirred up influential people and drove the two men out, they shook the dust from their feet and went to Iconium. The disciples were filled with joy and with the Holy Spirit."], "lesson": "Paul gives his hearers a history in which the promise has reached them. Luke records both the joy of those who receive it and the resistance of those who see others responding.", "question": "What does Paul announce that his hearers can receive through Jesus?"},
  {"id": 91238, "title": "From Applause to Stones and Back to the Churches", "subtitle": "Steadiness when a crowd changes its mind", "text": "Acts 14:1–28", "chapters": [[43, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["In Iconium, Paul and Barnabas spoke in the synagogue and many Jews and Greeks believed. Other hearers stirred up opposition, and the city divided over them. The two stayed a long time and spoke boldly, and the Lord confirmed the message of his grace through signs. When they learned that people on the opposing side planned to mistreat and stone them, they fled to Lystra and Derbe and continued preaching there.", "At Lystra a man who had never walked listened to Paul. Paul saw that he had faith to be healed and called out for him to stand. The man sprang up and walked. The crowd cried out in the Lycaonian language that gods had come down to them. They called Barnabas Zeus and Paul Hermes, since Paul did most of the speaking. The priest of Zeus brought oxen and garlands to the gates to offer a sacrifice with the crowd.", "When Paul and Barnabas heard what was happening, they tore their clothes and rushed in among the people. They told them they were fellow human beings and urged them to turn to the living God. He made heaven and earth and had given them rain, crops and food. Even with those words the two had trouble stopping the sacrifice. Later opponents arrived from Antioch and Iconium and won the crowd over. Paul was stoned and dragged out of the city for dead. When the disciples gathered around him, he got up and went back into the city.", "The next day he and Barnabas went to Derbe and made many disciples. On their return they went back through Lystra, Iconium and Antioch to strengthen the believers. They warned them that entering God's kingdom would involve hardship and appointed elders in each church with prayer and fasting. They passed through Pisidia, preached at Perga and sailed from Attalia. At the Antioch from which they had been sent, they gathered the church and reported what God had done, including how he had opened a door of faith to the Gentiles."], "lesson": "Public praise and public hostility can arise close together. The return visit shows where the missionaries place their responsibility even after rejection.", "question": "What do Paul and Barnabas do for the young churches when they pass through again?"},
  {"id": 91239, "title": "The Dispute Brought to Jerusalem", "subtitle": "Listening to testimony and Scripture", "text": "Acts 15:1–21", "chapters": [[43, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Some men came down from Judea to Antioch and taught that Gentile believers had to be circumcised according to the custom of Moses to be saved. Paul and Barnabas disputed with them at length. The church sent the two men and others to Jerusalem to put the question before the apostles and elders. On the journey they told believers in Phoenicia and Samaria how Gentiles had turned to God, which brought great joy to those who heard.", "In Jerusalem the church, apostles and elders welcomed them. Paul and Barnabas reported what God had done through them. Some believers who belonged to the party of the Pharisees rose and insisted that the Gentiles should be circumcised and told to keep the law of Moses. The apostles and elders gathered to consider it. After much discussion, Peter recalled how God had chosen him to bring the good news to Gentile hearers.", "God knew their hearts and had given them the Holy Spirit as he had given him to the Jewish believers. He had cleansed their hearts by faith. Peter asked why they would put a burden on these disciples that their own ancestors had been unable to bear. They believed that both groups would be saved through the grace of the Lord Jesus. The gathering fell silent and listened to Barnabas and Paul tell of the signs God had done among the Gentiles.", "When they finished, James took up Peter's account and showed how it agreed with the prophets. He recalled the promise of David's fallen dwelling being rebuilt so that others, including Gentiles called by God's name, might seek the Lord. James proposed sending instructions to abstain from what was polluted by idols, sexual immorality, meat from strangled animals and blood. He pointed out that Moses was read every Sabbath in the synagogues."], "lesson": "The gathering hears experience, attends to Scripture and considers a burden people are being asked to bear. Its judgement follows a public exchange.", "question": "What did Peter say God had already done among the Gentile believers?"},
  {"id": 91240, "title": "A Letter Delivered and Two Friends Part Ways", "subtitle": "Careful words when agreement is hard", "text": "Acts 15:22–41", "chapters": [[43, 15]], "start": 22, "end": 41, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The apostles and elders, with the whole church in Jerusalem, chose Judas Barsabas and Silas to go to Antioch with Paul and Barnabas. They carried a letter to the Gentile believers in Antioch, Syria and Cilicia. The letter said that the people who had troubled them had gone out without instructions from Jerusalem. Judas and Silas would confirm in person what the church had decided to write.", "The letter said that it had seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to them to lay only these requirements on the believers. They should abstain from food offered to idols, blood, meat from strangled animals and sexual immorality. When the messengers gathered the believers in Antioch and delivered the letter, the people rejoiced at its encouragement. Judas and Silas, who were prophets, spoke at length to strengthen them. After some time the messengers were dismissed in peace. Paul and Barnabas remained in Antioch, teaching and preaching with many others.", "After a while Paul suggested going back to the towns where they had preached to see how the believers were doing. Barnabas wanted to take John Mark. Paul thought they should leave him behind because he had withdrawn in Pamphylia and had gone no farther with them in the work. The disagreement grew sharp enough that the two men parted company.", "Barnabas took Mark and sailed to Cyprus. Paul chose Silas and left after the believers commended him to the Lord's grace. He travelled through Syria and Cilicia, strengthening the churches. The agreement carried from Jerusalem had brought relief to Antioch. Now the two men who had carried it together were following different routes."], "lesson": "The letter shows the care needed to convey a difficult decision fairly. The personal dispute also shows that devoted workers can part without the work ending.", "question": "How did the Jerusalem church make sure Antioch received its decision and encouragement clearly?"},
  {"id": 91241, "title": "Turned Towards Macedonia", "subtitle": "Guidance and a door opened in Philippi", "text": "Acts 16:1–15", "chapters": [[43, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul came to Derbe and Lystra, the towns he had visited with Barnabas. At Lystra he met Timothy, the son of a Jewish believer and a Greek father. The believers in Lystra and Iconium spoke well of Timothy, and Paul wanted him to come along. He circumcised him because the Jews in those places knew his father was Greek. As the group travelled, they delivered the decisions the apostles and elders had made in Jerusalem. The churches grew stronger in faith and increased in number daily.", "The travellers passed through Phrygia and Galatia after the Holy Spirit kept them from speaking the word in Asia. Near Mysia they tried to go into Bithynia and were prevented again. They passed by Mysia and came down to Troas. During the night Paul saw a man from Macedonia in a vision, standing and pleading for him to come over and help them. Luke now speaks of what we did. The group concluded that God had called them to preach there and sought passage at once.", "They sailed from Troas by way of Samothrace and Neapolis and came to Philippi, a Roman colony in Macedonia. On the Sabbath they went outside the city gate to a riverside place where they expected to find people praying. They sat and spoke to the women who had gathered there.", "One of the women was Lydia, a dealer in purple cloth from Thyatira who worshipped God. As she listened, the Lord opened her heart to attend to Paul's words. She and her household were baptised. Then she urged the travellers to stay at her house if they judged her faithful to the Lord. She prevailed on them, and a journey that had begun with a call for help brought them into a home opened to them."], "lesson": "The group's direction changes through closed routes and a new invitation. The first meeting Luke describes takes place beside a river among people already gathered to pray.", "question": "How does Lydia put hospitality into practice after hearing the message?"},
  {"id": 91242, "title": "Songs at Midnight", "subtitle": "Mercy when the prison door opens", "text": "Acts 16:16–40", "chapters": [[43, 16]], "start": 16, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["A slave girl with a spirit of divination followed Paul and his companions in Philippi for many days. Her owners earned money through her predictions. She kept calling out that the men were servants of the Most High God who were showing the way of salvation. After hearing her repeatedly, Paul commanded the spirit to come out in Jesus' name. When the owners saw that their hope of profit was gone, they took Paul and Silas before the magistrates. The men were beaten and put into the inner prison, their feet secured in stocks.", "At midnight they prayed and sang, and the other prisoners listened. An earthquake shook the foundations. The doors opened and the prisoners' restraints came loose. The jailer woke, saw the open doors and assumed the prisoners had escaped. He drew his sword, but Paul cried out to stop him, saying that everyone was still there.", "The jailer called for a light, came in trembling and brought Paul and Silas out. He asked what he must do to be saved. They told him to believe in the Lord Jesus and spoke the word to his household. That same night he washed their wounds. He and his household were baptised, and he set food before the men he had been guarding.", "In the morning the magistrates sent word to release them. Paul pointed out that they had been beaten publicly without trial although they were Roman citizens. The magistrates came in person and asked them to leave the city. Before leaving, Paul and Silas visited Lydia's house and encouraged the believers gathered there."], "lesson": "Paul pays attention to the jailer's danger when escape seems possible. The man who was guarding them then uses his freedom to care for their wounds.", "question": "What do Paul and the jailer each do to help someone in danger or pain?"},
  {"id": 91243, "title": "Two Towns Hear the Scriptures Opened", "subtitle": "Examining a message carefully", "text": "Acts 17:1–15", "chapters": [[43, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul and Silas passed through Amphipolis and Apollonia and came to Thessalonica. Paul went to the synagogue, as was his custom. Over three Sabbaths he reasoned from the Scriptures, explaining why the Christ had to suffer and rise from the dead. He told them that the Jesus he proclaimed was this Christ. Some of the Jews were persuaded, along with many Greeks who worshipped God and a number of prominent women.", "Others grew jealous. They gathered men from the marketplace, formed a mob and set the city in an uproar. They went to Jason's house looking for Paul and Silas. When they could not find them, they dragged Jason and some of the believers before the city officials. They said the visitors had caused trouble everywhere and that Jason had welcomed them. The charge that most disturbed the city was that these people proclaimed another king, Jesus, in defiance of Caesar's decrees. The officials took security from Jason and the others and let them go.", "The believers sent Paul and Silas away by night to Berea. There the two went to the synagogue. The people received the message eagerly and examined the Scriptures every day to see whether Paul's claims held up. Luke commends the way they listened and looked for themselves. Many believed, including prominent Greek women and men.", "When people from Thessalonica heard that Paul was preaching in Berea too, they came and stirred up the crowds there. The believers sent Paul away toward the sea while Silas and Timothy remained. His escorts took him as far as Athens and left carrying his request that the two join him as soon as they could."], "lesson": "The Bereans combine readiness to hear with steady examination. Their example gives both openness and care a place in studying a new claim.", "question": "What did the Bereans do daily after receiving Paul's message?"},
  {"id": 91244, "title": "An Altar in Athens", "subtitle": "Speaking to people from where they are", "text": "Acts 17:16–34", "chapters": [[43, 17]], "start": 16, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["While Paul waited in Athens, he was troubled by how full the city was of idols. He reasoned in the synagogue with Jews and others who worshipped God. In the marketplace he spoke daily with whoever happened to be there. Epicurean and Stoic philosophers joined the discussion. Some dismissed him as a collector of scraps of talk, and others thought he was introducing foreign gods because he preached Jesus and the resurrection.", "They brought him to the Areopagus and asked what his new teaching meant. Paul began with something he had seen among the city's objects of worship, an altar dedicated to an unknown god. He told them he would speak about the One they worshipped without knowing. The God who made the world and everything in it is Lord of heaven and earth. He gives people life and breath and has no need of anything human hands can supply.", "Paul said that God had made every nation from one human origin and determined their times and boundaries so they might seek him. God was near to each one. Paul quoted their own poets to help them follow his point about being God's offspring. If that was so, an image made from gold, silver or stone by human skill was an inadequate likeness for God.", "God now called people everywhere to repent. Paul said a day had been set when God would judge the world justly through the man he had appointed. He had given assurance of this by raising him from the dead. Some mocked when they heard of resurrection. Others wanted to hear Paul again. A few joined him and believed, including Dionysius of the Areopagus and a woman named Damaris."], "lesson": "Paul notices what his hearers already recognise and moves towards what they have yet to hear. The resurrection remains central even when it divides the audience.", "question": "How does the altar give Paul a starting point for what he says about God?"},
  {"id": 91245, "title": "A Workshop and a Long Stay in Corinth", "subtitle": "Courage for steady work", "text": "Acts 18:1–17", "chapters": [[43, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul left Athens for Corinth. There he met Aquila, a Jew from Pontus, and his wife Priscilla. They had recently come from Italy because Claudius had ordered Jews to leave Rome. They shared Paul's trade as tentmakers, so he stayed and worked with them. On Sabbaths he went to the synagogue and tried to persuade both Jews and Greeks. When Silas and Timothy arrived from Macedonia, Paul devoted himself to testifying that Jesus was the Christ.", "Some of his hearers fiercely opposed him. Paul shook out his clothes and said that from then on he would go to the Gentiles. He moved to the house of a worshipper of God named Justus, right next door to the synagogue. Crispus, the synagogue ruler, believed in the Lord with his whole household. Many Corinthians who heard the message believed and were baptised.", "One night the Lord told Paul in a vision to keep speaking without fear. He was with Paul, and nobody would attack him to do him harm, for he had many people in the city. Paul stayed there a year and six months, teaching the word of God among them. What began with work in a shared trade became a long stay among people learning to follow Christ.", "When Gallio was governor of Achaia, Paul's opponents brought him before the judgment seat. They accused him of persuading people to worship God against the law. Before Paul could answer, Gallio said he would have heard a case involving a crime. Questions about words, names and their own law were for them to settle. He drove them away from the judgment seat. Sosthenes, the synagogue ruler, was beaten in front of it, and Gallio took no notice."], "lesson": "The assurance Paul receives leads to a long period of ordinary teaching. His work in Corinth also has a place for a trade and the companionship of fellow workers.", "question": "What does the Lord tell Paul to keep doing, and how long does Paul remain?"},
  {"id": 91246, "title": "Priscilla and Aquila Hear Apollos", "subtitle": "Correction given with care", "text": "Acts 18:18–28", "chapters": [[43, 18]], "start": 18, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul stayed in Corinth a good while longer before taking leave of the believers and sailing for Syria. Priscilla and Aquila went with him. At Cenchreae Paul had his hair cut because he was under a vow. When they reached Ephesus, Paul went to the synagogue and reasoned with the Jews there. They asked him to stay longer, but he took leave and said he would return if God willed. Priscilla and Aquila remained in Ephesus.", "Paul sailed to Caesarea, went up and greeted the church and then went down to Antioch. After spending some time there, he set out again. He went from place to place in Galatia and Phrygia, strengthening the disciples. While Paul was away, another teacher arrived in Ephesus.", "Apollos was a Jew from Alexandria, an eloquent man with a strong knowledge of the Scriptures. He had been instructed in the way of the Lord. With fervour he spoke and taught accurately what he knew about Jesus, though he knew only John's baptism. Apollos began to speak boldly in the synagogue. Priscilla and Aquila heard him, took him aside and explained the way of God to him more fully.", "When Apollos wanted to cross over to Achaia, the believers encouraged him and wrote to the disciples there asking them to welcome him. On arrival he was a great help to those who had believed through grace. He answered his opponents in public and showed from the Scriptures that Jesus was the Christ. The Scriptures he had brought to Ephesus were still at the heart of what he had to say in Achaia."], "lesson": "Priscilla and Aquila give Apollos the instruction he needs in a setting where he can hear it. His existing gifts then serve more fully.", "question": "How does the couple approach Apollos when they recognise a gap in his understanding?"},
  {"id": 91247, "title": "A Name and a Changed Way of Life", "subtitle": "Repentance with a real cost", "text": "Acts 19:1–20", "chapters": [[43, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["While Apollos was in Corinth, Paul travelled through the inland regions and came to Ephesus. There he met about twelve disciples and asked whether they had received the Holy Spirit when they believed. They had not even heard of the Holy Spirit and knew only John's baptism. Paul explained that John had baptised people who repented and directed them to believe in the one coming after him, Jesus. They were baptised in the name of the Lord Jesus. When Paul laid his hands on them, the Holy Spirit came upon them and they spoke in tongues and prophesied.", "For three months Paul spoke boldly in the synagogue about God's kingdom. When some hardened against the message and spoke against the Way in public, he took the disciples with him and taught daily in the hall of Tyrannus. This continued for two years, and word of the Lord reached Jews and Greeks throughout the province of Asia. God worked extraordinary miracles through Paul. Even cloths that had touched him were carried to the sick, and illnesses and evil spirits left them.", "Some travelling Jewish exorcists tried using the name of the Jesus Paul preached. Seven sons of Sceva were doing this when an evil spirit answered that it knew Jesus and knew about Paul and asked who they were. The man overpowered them, and they fled the house wounded and stripped of their clothes. The account became widely known in Ephesus, and people held Jesus' name in honour.", "Many believers came forward and confessed what they had done. People who had practised magic gathered their books and burned them publicly. The books were valued at fifty thousand pieces of silver. Luke ends the account by saying that the word of the Lord grew and prevailed."], "lesson": "Luke distinguishes a formula borrowed for power from a response that reshapes conduct. The cost of giving up the books makes the change visible.", "question": "What did the people give up when they openly acknowledged their former practices?"},
  {"id": 91248, "title": "The Crowd in the Theatre", "subtitle": "Recognising the interests behind a protest", "text": "Acts 19:21–41", "chapters": [[43, 19]], "start": 21, "end": 41, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul decided to go through Macedonia and Achaia on his way to Jerusalem. After Jerusalem he meant to see Rome. He sent Timothy and Erastus ahead to Macedonia and stayed in Asia for a time. While he was still in Ephesus, a serious disturbance broke out over the Way. Demetrius, a silversmith, made silver shrines of Artemis, also known as Diana. The work brought substantial business to craftsmen in the city.", "Demetrius gathered the workers and reminded them that their income came from this trade. Paul had persuaded people across Ephesus and much of Asia that things made by human hands were no gods. Demetrius warned them that their trade might fall into disrepute and that the great temple of Artemis might lose its standing. The men grew angry and began shouting praise to Artemis. The commotion spread, and people rushed into the theatre, dragging along Gaius and Aristarchus, two Macedonians travelling with Paul.", "Paul wanted to go in among the crowd. The disciples held him back, and some provincial officials who were his friends sent word urging him to stay out. Inside, people shouted different things. Most had no idea why they were there. Some put Alexander forward, but when the crowd realised he was Jewish, they shouted in praise of Artemis for about two hours.", "The city clerk finally quieted them. He said the men they had dragged in had neither robbed temples nor spoken against their goddess. Demetrius and the craftsmen could take any charge to the courts. Other matters could be handled in a lawful assembly. As things stood, the city risked being called to account for a riot it could give no reason for. Then he dismissed the crowd."], "lesson": "The protest begins with a particular financial interest and becomes a gathering many cannot explain. Luke gives a reason to examine what moves us before joining a crowd.", "question": "What concerns did Demetrius raise before the crowd began to assemble?"},
  {"id": 91249, "title": "The Young Man at the Window", "subtitle": "Care in an interrupted gathering", "text": "Acts 20:1–16", "chapters": [[43, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When the uproar in Ephesus had ended, Paul called the disciples together, encouraged them and left for Macedonia. He went through those districts speaking to the believers before coming to Greece, where he spent three months. He was about to sail for Syria when he learned of a plot against him, so he went back through Macedonia. Several companions went ahead to Troas. Luke says the rest sailed from Philippi after the Feast of Unleavened Bread, reached Troas in five days and stayed a week.", "On the first day of the week they gathered to break bread. Paul was leaving the next day and kept speaking until midnight. Many lamps were burning in the upstairs room. A young man named Eutychus was sitting in the window. As Paul went on speaking, Eutychus sank into a deep sleep and fell from the third storey. When people lifted him, he was dead.", "Paul went downstairs, bent over Eutychus and took him in his arms. He told the people to stop being alarmed because the young man was alive. Then Paul went back upstairs, broke bread and ate. He talked with the group until daybreak and left as planned. They took Eutychus away alive and were greatly comforted.", "Paul's companions went ahead by ship to Assos, where Paul, who had gone on foot, joined them. They sailed by way of Mitylene, Chios and Samos to Miletus. Paul decided to pass Ephesus without stopping. He was trying, if possible, to reach Jerusalem by Pentecost."], "lesson": "The gathering stops for a person in danger. Luke remembers the community's comfort alongside the teaching and the journey.", "question": "How does the gathering respond after Eutychus falls?"},
  {"id": 91250, "title": "Paul Says Farewell at Miletus", "subtitle": "Shepherding with humility and vigilance", "text": "Acts 20:17–38", "chapters": [[43, 20]], "start": 17, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["From Miletus Paul sent to Ephesus for the elders of the church. When they came, he asked them to remember how he had lived among them from his first day in Asia. They had seen him serve the Lord humbly, with tears and through danger. He had taught them publicly and from house to house. To Jews and Greeks alike he had urged repentance toward God and faith in the Lord Jesus.", "Now he was bound in spirit for Jerusalem, uncertain what would happen there. The Holy Spirit had warned him in city after city that imprisonment and hardship awaited him. Paul was intent on finishing the work the Lord Jesus had given him, bearing witness to the gospel of God's grace. He told the elders that they would see his face no more. He could say that he had held back none of God's counsel from them.", "Paul told the elders to watch over themselves and the flock in their care. The Holy Spirit had made them overseers to shepherd the church. After his departure, fierce wolves would come in, and even men from their own group would twist the truth to draw disciples after themselves. He asked them to remember how, for three years, he had warned them night and day with tears. Now he entrusted them to God and the word of his grace.", "Paul reminded them that his hands had supplied what he and his companions needed. They too should labour to help the weak, remembering that Jesus called giving more blessed than receiving. When he finished, he knelt and prayed with them. They wept, embraced and kissed him, grieved especially at his saying they would see him no more. Then they went with him to the ship."], "lesson": "Paul puts responsibilities before the elders which his own conduct has illustrated. Their oversight should attend to threats and to people who need support.", "question": "Which patterns from Paul's own work does he ask the elders to remember?"},
  {"id": 91251, "title": "Warnings on the Way to Jerusalem", "subtitle": "Courage with consideration for others", "text": "Acts 21:1–26", "chapters": [[43, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["After the farewell at Miletus, the travellers sailed by way of Cos, Rhodes and Patara. They found a ship for Phoenicia, passed Cyprus and landed at Tyre, where the ship was unloading. They found the disciples and stayed seven days. The disciples, speaking through the Spirit, urged Paul against going to Jerusalem. When it was time to leave, the believers came out with their wives and children. They all knelt on the shore and prayed before the travellers boarded.", "They went on through Ptolemais to Caesarea and stayed with Philip the evangelist, one of the Seven. His four unmarried daughters prophesied. During the visit a prophet named Agabus came from Judea. He took Paul's belt, bound his own feet and hands and said the Holy Spirit had shown him that the owner would be bound in Jerusalem and handed over to the Gentiles. Paul's companions and the local believers pleaded with him to stay away.", "Paul asked why their weeping was breaking his heart. He was ready to be bound in Jerusalem and even to die there for the name of the Lord Jesus. When they could no longer persuade him, they left the outcome to the Lord's will. They went up to Jerusalem, where the believers received them gladly. The next day Paul met James and the elders and told them in detail what God had done among the Gentiles through his ministry.", "The elders glorified God and told Paul that many Jewish believers had heard reports that he taught Jews living among the Gentiles to abandon Moses. They advised him to join four men in their purification and pay their expenses, so people could see how he lived. They also reaffirmed what had been decided about Gentile believers. Paul took the men, purified himself with them and went into the temple to give notice of when the days of purification would end."], "lesson": "Paul faces personal danger and is also willing to take steps that address the concerns of other believers. His resolve leaves room for hearing their counsel.", "question": "What different concerns do Paul's companions and the Jerusalem elders bring to him?"},
  {"id": 91252, "title": "From the Temple Steps Paul Tells His Story", "subtitle": "Witness in the language people understand", "text": "Acts 21:27–22:21", "chapters": [[43, 21], [43, 22]], "start": 27, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When the seven days of purification were almost over, Jews from Asia saw Paul in the temple. They stirred up the crowd by accusing him of teaching against their people, their law and the temple, and of bringing Greeks inside. Luke explains that they had seen Trophimus the Ephesian with Paul in the city and assumed Paul had brought him into the temple. The crowd dragged Paul out, and the gates were shut behind him.", "They were trying to kill him when the Roman commander heard that Jerusalem was in an uproar. Soldiers ran down, and the crowd stopped beating Paul. The commander had him bound with two chains and asked who he was and what he had done. People shouted different answers, so he ordered Paul taken into the barracks. On the steps the soldiers had to carry him because the crowd pressed against them shouting for his death.", "Paul spoke to the commander in Greek and corrected the impression that he was an Egyptian who had led a revolt. Paul said he was a Jew from Tarsus and asked to speak to the people. The commander agreed. Paul motioned from the steps, the crowd quieted, and he addressed them in their own Hebrew speech. He said he had been brought up in Jerusalem, trained under Gamaliel and zealous for God. He had persecuted the Way and set out for Damascus with authority to bring its followers back in chains.", "Near Damascus a great light struck him down, and Jesus identified himself as the one Paul was persecuting. Blinded, Paul was led into the city, where Ananias came, restored his sight and told him he would be a witness. Ananias urged him to be baptised and call on the Lord. Paul then told of praying later in the temple in Jerusalem. Jesus had warned him to leave the city and told him he would send him far away to the Gentiles."], "lesson": "Paul opens with ground his hearers know and explains how his own life changed. He lets the costly part of his calling remain part of the account.", "question": "Which facts about his earlier life does Paul give before he tells what happened near Damascus?"},
  {"id": 91253, "title": "An Unanswered Question About Citizenship", "subtitle": "Using a lawful protection truthfully", "text": "Acts 22:22–30", "chapters": [[43, 22]], "start": 22, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The crowd listened until Paul said that Jesus had sent him to the Gentiles. Then they raised their voices and cried that he should be put to death. People threw off their cloaks and tossed dust into the air. The Roman commander, who had already heard conflicting shouts about Paul on the steps, ordered him taken inside. He meant to find out why the crowd was shouting this way by having Paul questioned under the lash.", "As they stretched Paul out with straps, he spoke to the centurion standing nearby. He asked whether it was lawful to flog a Roman citizen who had never been condemned. The centurion went straight to the commander and told him to consider what he was about to do. Paul was a Roman citizen, and the question he had asked could not be brushed aside.", "The commander came to Paul and asked him directly whether he was a Roman. Paul said he was. The commander said he had paid a large sum for his own citizenship, and Paul replied that he had been born a citizen. Those about to question him withdrew immediately. The commander was afraid when he realised whom he had bound.", "The commander still wanted to learn the charge against Paul. The next day he released him from his bonds and ordered the chief priests and the whole council to assemble. He brought Paul down and set him before them. The planned flogging had ended with a question from the prisoner. Now the people who had accused him would have to speak in a setting where the commander could hear them."], "lesson": "Paul's willingness to suffer leaves room for a truthful appeal to a legal protection. His question gives the officers a chance to stop what they are about to do.", "question": "What fact changes the course of the examination?"},
  {"id": 91254, "title": "The Council Divides Over Resurrection", "subtitle": "Courage for the next place of witness", "text": "Acts 23:1–11", "chapters": [[43, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul looked steadily at the council and said he had lived before God with a good conscience to that day. The high priest Ananias ordered those standing near him to strike him on the mouth. Paul called Ananias a whitewashed wall and asked how a man sitting to judge him by the law could order him struck in breach of it. The people standing nearby asked whether he would speak that way to God's high priest.", "Paul said he had not known that Ananias was the high priest. He acknowledged the command in Scripture against speaking evil of a ruler of the people. Then he took account of the men before him. Some were Sadducees and others Pharisees. Paul called out that he was a Pharisee, a son of Pharisees, and that he was on trial for the hope of the resurrection of the dead.", "The council divided at once. The Sadducees denied resurrection, angels and spirits, while the Pharisees acknowledged them. Some scribes on the Pharisees' side stood up and said they found no wrong in Paul. They raised the possibility that a spirit or an angel had spoken to him. The dispute grew so violent that the Roman commander feared Paul would be torn apart. He ordered soldiers to take him from among them and bring him back into the barracks.", "The following night the Lord stood by Paul and told him to take courage. He had borne witness in Jerusalem, and he must bear witness in Rome as well. Paul was still in the barracks where the soldiers had brought him. By morning others would be making plans for his life, and he already had the Lord's word about where his testimony would go."], "lesson": "The Lord names a future place of witness while Paul is still in custody. The encouragement gives him a direction before he can see how he will get there.", "question": "What does the Lord say about Paul's witness in Jerusalem and in Rome?"},
  {"id": 91255, "title": "A Young Man Hears of the Plot", "subtitle": "The quiet value of reporting danger", "text": "Acts 23:12–35", "chapters": [[43, 23]], "start": 12, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The next morning more than forty men bound themselves by an oath to eat and drink nothing until they had killed Paul. They told the chief priests and elders and asked them to arrange for the commander to bring Paul down again. The request would appear to be for a closer inquiry. The men meant to kill him before he reached the council.", "Paul's sister's son heard about the ambush and went into the barracks to tell him. Paul called a centurion and asked him to take the young man to the commander. The commander took him by the hand, drew him aside and asked privately what he had to report. After hearing the plan, he sent the young man away with instructions to tell nobody that he had reported it.", "The commander called two centurions and ordered an escort of two hundred soldiers, seventy horsemen and two hundred spearmen to be ready by nine that evening. He provided mounts for Paul and sent him to Felix, the governor. In his letter the commander, Claudius Lysias, said the dispute concerned questions of the Jewish law and that he had found no charge deserving death or imprisonment. He explained that he had sent Paul to Felix after learning of the plot and had directed Paul's accusers to make their case there.", "The soldiers took Paul by night as far as Antipatris. The next day the horsemen went on to Caesarea while the rest returned to the barracks. Felix read the letter and asked where Paul was from. When he learned it was Cilicia, he said he would hear Paul once the accusers arrived. Then he ordered Paul kept under guard in Herod's headquarters."], "lesson": "The nephew cannot organise an escort himself. He does what is within reach by bringing information to someone able to act on it.", "question": "What sequence brings the nephew's warning to the commander?"},
  {"id": 91256, "title": "Felix Listens and Delays", "subtitle": "Hearing truth that asks for a response", "text": "Acts 24:1–27", "chapters": [[43, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Five days later the high priest Ananias came to Caesarea with some elders and an advocate named Tertullus. Tertullus praised Felix for the peace he said the governor had brought. Then he charged Paul with stirring up trouble among Jews throughout the world, leading the sect of the Nazarenes and attempting to profane the temple. The others joined in affirming his account.", "At Felix's signal Paul answered. He had been in Jerusalem only twelve days since going there to worship, and nobody had found him arguing in the temple or stirring up a crowd. He acknowledged that he worshipped the God of his ancestors according to the Way. He believed what was written in the Law and the Prophets and hoped for the resurrection of the righteous and the unrighteous. He tried to keep a clear conscience before God and people.", "Paul explained that he had come to bring gifts for his people and present offerings. Some Jews from Asia had found him in the temple after he had purified himself, with no crowd around him. Paul said that those men ought to be present if they had a charge. He asked whether the people who were there could name any wrongdoing beyond his declaring before the council that he was on trial over the resurrection. Felix put off his decision until Lysias might come down. He kept Paul in custody with some freedom and let his friends attend to him.", "Later Felix came with his wife Drusilla, who was Jewish, and heard Paul speak about faith in Christ. When Paul spoke about righteousness, self-control and judgment to come, Felix grew afraid and sent him away, saying he would call again when he found a convenient time. He did send for him often, hoping Paul would offer money. Two years passed, and Felix left him in prison when Festus succeeded him, wishing to gain favour with Paul's accusers."], "lesson": "Felix's alarm shows that the words reached him. His repeated delays show how an opportunity to act can be lost while a person waits for convenience.", "question": "What does Felix do when Paul's teaching alarms him?"},
  {"id": 91257, "title": "Paul Appeals to Caesar", "subtitle": "Making a clear appeal under pressure", "text": "Acts 25:1–27", "chapters": [[43, 25]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Three days after taking up his post, Festus went from Caesarea to Jerusalem. The chief priests and leading men brought their case against Paul and urged Festus to have him transferred. They planned to ambush him on the way. Festus said Paul was being held in Caesarea and invited their leaders to go down and bring any charges there. When Festus returned to Caesarea, he took his seat in court the next day and had Paul brought in.", "Paul's accusers surrounded him and brought serious charges they could not prove. Paul said he had committed no offence against the Jewish law, the temple or Caesar. Festus, wanting to do the accusers a favour, asked Paul whether he would go to Jerusalem for trial. Paul answered that he was already before Caesar's tribunal, where he ought to be tried. If he had committed a crime deserving death, he would accept the penalty. Since the charges had no foundation, nobody could hand him over. He appealed to Caesar, and Festus accepted after consulting his advisers.", "Some days later King Agrippa and Bernice came to greet Festus. Festus described a prisoner left by Felix and charges that had surprised him. The dispute concerned the accusers' religion and a man named Jesus, who had died and whom Paul said was alive. Festus had been at a loss over how to inquire into such matters. Paul had appealed, and Festus was holding him for the emperor. Agrippa asked to hear him.", "The next day Agrippa and Bernice entered the audience hall with great ceremony, accompanied by officers and prominent men of Caesarea. Festus had Paul brought in. He told the gathering that he had found nothing in Paul's conduct deserving death. The appeal required him to send the prisoner to the emperor, and he had no clear charge to write. He hoped the hearing would give him something definite to report."], "lesson": "Paul gives a direct answer about the proper place for his hearing. The appeal provides a route forward when the proposed transfer exposes him to danger.", "question": "What prompts Paul to appeal, and what problem does Festus still face after accepting it?"},
  {"id": 91258, "title": "Paul Speaks Before Agrippa", "subtitle": "Telling the truth about a changed life", "text": "Acts 26:1–32", "chapters": [[43, 26]], "start": 1, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Agrippa gave Paul permission to speak, and Paul stretched out his hand to begin. He was glad to answer before someone familiar with Jewish customs and disputes. He had lived as a Pharisee and now stood on trial over the promise God had made to their ancestors. The twelve tribes served God earnestly in hope of that promise. Paul asked why anyone there thought it incredible that God raises the dead.", "Paul knew what it was to oppose the name of Jesus of Nazareth. He had put believers in prison, supported their execution and pursued them even to foreign cities. He was on such a journey to Damascus when a light brighter than the sun shone around him and his companions at midday. They fell to the ground. Jesus spoke to Paul and told him he was being appointed a servant and witness. He would be sent to open people's eyes, so they could turn from darkness to light and receive forgiveness through faith in Jesus.", "Paul said he obeyed the vision. In Damascus, Jerusalem, Judea and among the Gentiles, he called people to repent, turn to God and show their repentance by what they did. This was what he had been doing when he was seized in the temple. God had helped him to that day. His testimony agreed with Moses and the prophets about the Christ suffering, rising from the dead and bringing light to Israel and the Gentiles.", "Festus interrupted and cried that Paul's learning had driven him mad. Paul answered respectfully and appealed to Agrippa's knowledge of the prophets. Agrippa asked whether Paul expected to persuade him so quickly. Paul said he prayed that everyone listening might become as he was, apart from his chains. After the hearing the officials agreed that Paul had done nothing deserving death or imprisonment. Agrippa told Festus he could have been released if he had not appealed to Caesar."], "lesson": "Paul describes what he once did without excusing it. His account connects the direction of his present life to the word he received.", "question": "What did Paul begin preaching after the encounter he describes to Agrippa?"},
  {"id": 91259, "title": "A Ship Driven Out of Sight of Land", "subtitle": "Hope spoken in danger", "text": "Acts 27:1–26", "chapters": [[43, 27]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When it was decided that they would sail for Italy, Paul and other prisoners were put in the charge of Julius, a centurion of the Augustan cohort. Luke records the journey as one he made himself, and Aristarchus from Thessalonica was with them too. At Sidon, Julius kindly allowed Paul to visit friends who could care for him. Adverse winds took the ship under the shelter of Cyprus. At Myra, Julius transferred the party to an Alexandrian ship bound for Italy.", "They made slow progress and reached Fair Havens on Crete with difficulty. The season was already dangerous for sailing. Paul warned that going on would put the ship, cargo and lives at risk. Julius gave more weight to the pilot and shipowner. Most on board wanted to reach Phoenix, a more suitable harbour where they could spend the winter. When a gentle south wind blew, they weighed anchor and sailed close to Crete.", "A violent wind swept down from the island and drove the ship away. In the shelter of a small island the crew managed with difficulty to secure the boat and bind the ship with ropes. Fearing the sandbanks of Syrtis, they lowered their gear and let the vessel drift. They threw cargo overboard and later threw out the ship's tackle. Days passed without a sight of the sun or stars, and hope of survival faded.", "Paul stood among them after they had gone a long time without food. He reminded them of his warning and urged them to take heart. An angel of the God he served had stood by him that night and told him he must appear before Caesar. God had granted him the lives of all those sailing with him. The ship would be lost, and they would run aground on an island. Paul told them he trusted God to do what he had said."], "lesson": "Paul gives the crew a specific reason for hope while the conditions remain dangerous. His assurance concerns the people on the ship as well as his own future.", "question": "What does Paul tell the people will be lost, and what does he say will be spared?"},
  {"id": 91260, "title": "Bread Before the Shipwreck", "subtitle": "Encouragement joined to practical care", "text": "Acts 27:27–44", "chapters": [[43, 27]], "start": 27, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["On the fourteenth night at sea, the sailors sensed that land was near. Their soundings showed the water was getting shallower. Fearing rocks, they dropped four anchors from the stern and waited for daylight. Some tried to lower the boat under the pretence of setting anchors from the bow. Paul warned the centurion that the men needed to stay aboard, so the soldiers cut the ropes and let the boat go.", "As dawn approached, Paul urged everyone to eat. They had been waiting anxiously without a proper meal. He reminded them that their lives would be spared, took bread, thanked God in front of them and began to eat. The others took courage and ate too. Luke gives their number as 276. Afterwards they lightened the ship by throwing the wheat into the sea.", "At daylight they did not recognise the coast, but they saw a bay with a beach and tried to run the ship ashore. It stuck fast at the bow while the force of the waves broke up the stern. The soldiers proposed killing the prisoners to prevent their escape.", "The centurion wanted to save Paul and stopped that plan. He ordered those who could swim to go first. The rest followed on planks and pieces of the ship. Everyone reached land safely. The promise Paul had spoken in the storm was fulfilled through a series of ordinary decisions, from taking food to giving the prisoners a chance to reach shore."], "lesson": "Paul's confidence in God's promise sits alongside attention to food and to keeping the sailors aboard. The centurion's decision also protects people who cannot protect themselves.", "question": "What practical actions help bring everyone safely to shore?"},
  {"id": 91261, "title": "A Fire on Malta and Friends on the Road", "subtitle": "Receiving care after danger", "text": "Acts 28:1–16", "chapters": [[43, 28]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Once everyone was safely ashore, the travellers learned that the island was Malta. The people there showed them unusual kindness. It was raining and cold, so they lit a fire and welcomed them around it. Paul gathered an armful of sticks. As he laid them on the fire, a viper came out because of the heat and fastened to his hand. The islanders saw it hanging there and took it as a sign that justice had caught up with a murderer who had escaped the sea.", "Paul shook the snake into the fire and suffered no harm. The islanders watched, expecting him to swell up or fall down dead. After waiting a long time and seeing that nothing happened, they changed their minds and called him a god. Soon Luke turns from what people said about Paul to what they did for the travellers.", "Publius, the leading man of the island, welcomed them and cared for them for three days. His father was ill with fever and dysentery. Paul visited him, prayed, laid his hands on him and healed him. Others on the island who were sick came and were cured. The people honoured the travellers and supplied what they needed when they were ready to leave.", "Three months later they sailed in an Alexandrian ship that had spent the winter on the island. They travelled through Syracuse and Rhegium to Puteoli, where fellow believers invited them to stay a week. Others came from Rome to meet them at the Forum of Appius and the Three Taverns. When Paul saw them, he thanked God and took courage. In Rome he was allowed to live on his own with a soldier to guard him."], "lesson": "Hospitality comes first from strangers and later from fellow believers. Luke lets Paul receive help as well as give it.", "question": "What different forms of help do the islanders and the believers offer?"},
  {"id": 91262, "title": "A Door Open in Rome", "subtitle": "Serving within present limits", "text": "Acts 28:17–31", "chapters": [[43, 28]], "start": 17, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Three days after reaching Rome, Paul called together the local Jewish leaders. He told them that he had done nothing against his people or their ancestral customs, yet he had been handed over to the Romans in Jerusalem. The Romans had found no reason to put him to death and wanted to release him. When his accusers objected, he felt compelled to appeal to Caesar. He had brought no charge against his own people. He wanted them to know that he wore his chain because of the hope of Israel.", "The leaders said they had received no letters from Judea about Paul and heard no evil report about him from anyone who had arrived. They wanted to hear what he thought, since they knew that the movement he belonged to was spoken against everywhere. They arranged a day, and more people came to the place where he was staying.", "From morning until evening Paul explained the kingdom of God and tried to persuade them about Jesus from the Law of Moses and the Prophets. Some were persuaded and others refused to believe. As they began to leave in disagreement, Paul recalled Isaiah's words about people who hear without understanding and see without perceiving. Then he told them that God's salvation had been sent to the Gentiles and that they would listen.", "Paul stayed two whole years in his rented lodging. He welcomed everyone who came to see him. The guard and the chain had a place in his life there, and so did the visitors who came to hear him. Luke leaves Paul in that lodging, proclaiming God's kingdom and teaching about the Lord Jesus Christ openly and without hindrance."], "lesson": "Paul uses the place available to him and receives the people who come. Luke ends with the word being taught freely even while Paul's circumstances are restricted.", "question": "What can Paul still do from his rented lodging, and who can come to hear him?"},
  {"id": 91263, "title": "Paul Explains Why He Wants to Come to Rome", "subtitle": "A gospel meant for every hearer", "text": "Romans 1:1–17", "chapters": [[44, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul introduces himself as a servant of Jesus Christ, called to be an apostle and set apart for God's gospel. This good news had been promised beforehand through the prophets in the Scriptures. It concerns God's Son, descended from David according to the flesh and declared Son of God in power through his resurrection from the dead. Through Jesus, Paul says, he received grace and his commission to call people among all nations to the obedience that comes from faith. The believers in Rome belong among those called to Jesus Christ.", "Paul addresses them as people loved by God and called to be saints. He wishes them grace and peace from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ. Before he tells them why he is writing, he thanks God through Jesus for them. Their faith is being spoken of widely. Paul remembers them continually in prayer and asks that, by God's will, a way may finally open for him to visit them.", "He longs to see them in person and share a spiritual gift that will strengthen them. He goes on to put the visit in terms of what he hopes to receive as well. Their faith and his would encourage each other. He has intended to come many times and has been prevented. He still hopes for fruit from his work among them, as among other Gentiles. He feels a duty to Greeks and those outside Greek culture, to the wise and the unwise.", "That debt helps explain his eagerness to preach in Rome. He is unashamed of the gospel because it is God's power for salvation to everyone who believes, to the Jew first and also to the Greek. In it God's righteousness is revealed in connection with faith. Paul gives the thought a line from Scripture that will carry into the rest of the letter. The righteous will live by faith."], "lesson": "Paul writes with a calling to teach and an expectation of receiving encouragement. The people he hopes to visit already have faith from which he expects to benefit.", "question": "What does Paul expect to give and receive if he visits Rome?"},
  {"id": 91264, "title": "When Gratitude Gives Way to Idols", "subtitle": "Honouring the Creator", "text": "Romans 1:18–32", "chapters": [[44, 1]], "start": 18, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has announced the good news as God's power for salvation. Now he shows why people need it. God's wrath is revealed against ungodliness and wrongdoing, Paul says, because people hold down the truth by the way they live. God has made something of himself plain in the things he has made. His eternal power and divine nature can be seen through creation. Paul says people are therefore without excuse.", "The refusal begins with worship and thanksgiving. Although people knew God, they gave him neither the honour nor the thanks due to him. Their reasoning became empty and their hearts darkened. Claiming wisdom, they traded the glory of the immortal God for images of mortal people, birds, animals and creeping things. Paul describes this as exchanging God's truth for a lie and serving created things in place of their Creator.", "Paul repeatedly says that God gave them over to the course they chose. He speaks first of desires through which they dishonoured their bodies. He then describes women exchanging natural relations and men abandoning relations with women and burning in desire for one another. Finally, he says that because people saw no reason to retain the knowledge of God, they were given over to a corrupt mind and conduct that followed from it.", "The final list brings Paul's argument into life with other people. He names envy, murder, quarrelling, deceit, gossip, pride, disobedience to parents and a lack of mercy, among other kinds of wrongdoing. People know God's judgment against such conduct and persist in it, Paul says. They also approve when others do the same. The passage that begins with what people can see of their Creator ends with what they have come to accept among themselves."], "lesson": "Paul begins the account with a refusal to honour and thank God. Gratitude helps keep the gifts of creation in relation to the one who gives them.", "question": "Which changes in conduct does Paul trace back to a change in worship?"},
  {"id": 91265, "title": "The Person Who Judges Is Also Accountable", "subtitle": "Receiving kindness as a call to repent", "text": "Romans 2:1–16", "chapters": [[44, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul turns from the people he has described to the reader who condemns them. The person passing judgment can recognise wrongdoing clearly enough to name it. If he practises the same things, that judgment also tells against him. God's judgment is according to truth, Paul says. Someone who sees another person's guilt and goes on in the same conduct has no reason to expect to escape it.", "Paul asks whether such a person takes God's kindness, patience and willingness to wait lightly. That kindness has a purpose. It leads people toward repentance. A stubborn heart that refuses to repent is storing up wrath for the day when God's righteous judgment will be revealed. God will repay each person according to what that person has done.", "Paul sets out the two outcomes. To those who patiently continue doing good and seek glory, honour and immortality, God will give eternal life. Wrath and trouble await those who reject the truth and obey unrighteousness. Paul names both Jews and Gentiles in speaking of trouble for wrongdoing and of glory, honour and peace for doing good. God judges without favouritism.", "Those who sin without the law will be judged accordingly, and those who sin under the law will be judged by it. Hearing the law does not of itself make a person righteous before God. Gentiles who do what the law requires show its work written on their hearts. Their consciences bear witness, and their thoughts accuse or defend them. Paul brings even these inward matters into view as he mentions the day God will judge people's secrets through Jesus Christ."], "lesson": "God's patience gives time to repent. It is easy to interpret that time as approval when we are occupied with pointing out another person's faults.", "question": "Where is Paul asking his reader to turn attention after condemning someone else?"},
  {"id": 91266, "title": "The Teacher Under His Own Teaching", "subtitle": "Integrity that reaches the heart", "text": "Romans 2:17–29", "chapters": [[44, 2]], "start": 17, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul now addresses the reader who calls himself a Jew, relies on the law and takes pride in his relationship to God. Such a person has been taught to recognise what God approves. He considers himself a guide to the blind and a light to people in darkness, able to teach the foolish and instruct the young. In the law he has a form of knowledge and truth he can pass on.", "Paul turns these claims into questions. Does the one teaching others also teach himself? If he preaches against stealing, does he steal? If he forbids adultery, does he commit it? If he detests idols, does he rob temples? A person can boast in the law while dishonouring God by breaking it. Paul reminds his reader of what Scripture says about God's name being blasphemed among the Gentiles because of such conduct.", "Then Paul takes up circumcision, the outward sign of belonging to the covenant people. It has value if a person keeps the law. Breaking the law makes the sign count as uncircumcision. Paul asks what follows when an uncircumcised person carries out what the law requires. His obedience exposes the disobedience of the person who possesses the written law and bears the sign.", "Paul ends with what God sees inwardly. The circumcision he describes takes place in the heart, by the Spirit, and reaches where a written code and a mark on the body cannot reach. The person who lives this way receives praise from God. The argument has travelled from the honour the teacher claims in public to the life God sees beneath that claim."], "lesson": "The responsibility to teach gives the teacher an additional reason to examine his own life. Public recognition cannot substitute for God's judgement of the heart.", "question": "Which of Paul's questions would help you examine a duty you urge on other people?"},
  {"id": 91267, "title": "Every Mouth Comes to Silence", "subtitle": "Honesty before God", "text": "Romans 3:1–20", "chapters": [[44, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul anticipates what his reader might ask after hearing what he has said about the law and circumcision. What advantage, then, does the Jew have? Paul answers that there is much advantage. To begin with, the Jews were entrusted with the words of God. Some had been unfaithful. Their unfaithfulness could never make God unfaithful. Paul appeals to Scripture to affirm that God is true and will be vindicated when his words are judged.", "A further objection follows. If people's unrighteousness makes God's righteousness more evident, would God be unjust to judge them? Paul rejects the argument, for on those terms God could scarcely judge the world. He also rejects the claim that people should do evil so that good may result. He says some had slanderously attributed such teaching to him, and he calls their condemnation deserved.", "Paul then returns to the question of whether having these privileges puts Jews in a better position before God's judgment. His charge has already included Jews and Gentiles alike under sin. He gathers several lines from Scripture to describe that condition. Nobody is righteous. No one seeks God. Their speech carries deceit and bitterness. Their paths are marked by violence and trouble, and they do not know the way of peace. There is no fear of God before their eyes.", "Paul says the law speaks to those under it. Its words leave every mouth silent and the whole world accountable before God. Nobody will be justified before him by works of the law. Through the law comes knowledge of sin. The Scripture entrusted to this people has brought the argument to a point where every reader needs to hear what God will do."], "lesson": "A genuine privilege can make accountability deeper. The Scripture Paul cites gives people words for their condition before it gives them grounds for hope.", "question": "What does Paul say the law makes known when people stand accountable before God?"},
  {"id": 91268, "title": "The Righteousness God Makes Known", "subtitle": "Grace gives no room for boasting", "text": "Romans 3:21–31", "chapters": [[44, 3]], "start": 21, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has brought the whole world before God with no defence to offer. Now he says God's righteousness has been made known apart from works of the law. The Law and the Prophets themselves bear witness to it. This righteousness comes through faith in Jesus Christ to all who believe. Paul gives the reason he can speak to them together. All have sinned and fall short of God's glory.", "Those who are justified receive it as a gift of grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus. God put Christ forward as an atoning sacrifice through his blood, to be received by faith. In his patience God had passed over sins committed beforehand. Paul says that what God has now done shows his righteousness. He is just, and he justifies the one who has faith in Jesus.", "Paul then asks what has become of boasting. It has been shut out. An arrangement based on what people achieve would leave them something to boast about, but faith gives them no such ground. His conclusion is that a person is justified by faith apart from works of the law. The argument concerns people who have spent their lives under the law as well as those who have lived outside it.", "God is the God of Gentiles as well as of Jews. Since there is one God, he justifies the circumcised by faith and the uncircumcised through faith. Paul anticipates one last question. Does this faith overthrow the law? He says it upholds it. He will now go back to Abraham, whose place in Scripture lets him show how long this truth has been there."], "lesson": "The same grace meets people with different histories. Boasting loses its ground when acceptance depends on what God gives through Christ.", "question": "How does the conclusion about boasting follow from the way Paul describes justification?"},
  {"id": 91269, "title": "Abraham Before the Sign", "subtitle": "Trust in the God who justifies", "text": "Romans 4:1–12", "chapters": [[44, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul takes up Abraham, the ancestor whose story his Jewish readers know well. What did Abraham find? If he had been justified by works, he would have a ground for boasting, Paul says, though he would have none before God. Paul asks the reader to look at what Scripture actually says. Abraham believed God, and it was counted to him as righteousness.", "Paul asks his readers to think about the difference between wages and a gift. If someone works for a wage, the payment is counted as something owed. In Abraham's case, the righteousness was counted through faith. Paul speaks here of the person who trusts the God who justifies the ungodly. He then draws in David, who speaks of the blessedness of the person to whom God counts righteousness apart from works. David describes a person whose wrongdoing is forgiven, whose sins are covered and whose sin the Lord does not count against him.", "Paul asks who can share this blessing. Is it for the circumcised alone? He looks at the order of events in Abraham's life. Abraham's faith was counted to him as righteousness while he was still uncircumcised. Circumcision came later as a sign and seal of the righteousness he already had by faith.", "That order gives Abraham a place in the lives of people who have never borne his outward sign. He is the father of uncircumcised people who believe, so that righteousness may be counted to them also. He is also the father of circumcised people who follow in the footsteps of the faith he had before his circumcision. Paul makes the timing of the sign part of his answer about who may belong to this family of faith."], "lesson": "Paul pays attention to the timing of the sign. That sequence gives people beyond Abraham's physical descendants a place in the story of faith.", "question": "What happened before Abraham received the sign, and why is the order important to Paul?"},
  {"id": 91270, "title": "The Promise Faced by an Old Man", "subtitle": "Hope grounded in the power of God", "text": "Romans 4:13–25", "chapters": [[44, 4]], "start": 13, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul now considers the promise that Abraham and his descendants would inherit the world. It came through the righteousness of faith, apart from the law. If the inheritance depended on keeping the law, faith would be emptied and the promise would lose its force. The law brings wrath where it is broken. Paul says the promise rests on faith so that it depends on grace and is sure to all Abraham's offspring, including those who share his faith without having his people's law.", "Scripture speaks of Abraham as father of many nations. Paul points to the God in whom Abraham believed, the God who gives life to the dead and calls into being things that do not exist. Abraham trusted him when the ordinary grounds for expecting a child had disappeared. He was about a hundred years old and knew the condition of his own body. He also knew that Sarah's womb was past bearing children.", "Abraham went on trusting what God had spoken. He grew strong in faith, gave glory to God and was fully persuaded that God could do what he had promised. This is why his faith was counted to him as righteousness. Paul has brought the reader close enough to Abraham's circumstances to see what gave that faith its footing.", "The words about righteousness being counted were written for later readers too. Righteousness will be counted to those who believe in the God who raised Jesus our Lord from the dead. Jesus was handed over for our trespasses and raised for our justification. At the close of the passage, the God who brings life out of death is the ground of hope for Paul's readers as he was for Abraham."], "lesson": "Abraham's trust takes the facts of his condition seriously. Its ground is what God is able to do.", "question": "What two human limitations does Paul name when he describes Abraham's faith?"},
  {"id": 91271, "title": "Peace After Justification", "subtitle": "Hope in the midst of trouble", "text": "Romans 5:1–11", "chapters": [[44, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul turns to what follows when a person has been justified by faith. Believers have peace with God through the Lord Jesus Christ. Through him they have gained access by faith into the grace in which they stand, and they rejoice in the hope of sharing God's glory. Paul speaks of this as a place already given to them and a hope for which they still wait.", "He has room in that hope for trouble. Suffering produces endurance, endurance produces tested character, and character produces hope. This hope will hold because God's love has been poured into their hearts through the Holy Spirit he has given them. Paul then directs their attention to what that love has already done for them.", "Christ died for the ungodly while they were still weak. Paul asks his readers to consider how seldom someone dies for a righteous person, though someone might dare to die for a good person. God shows his love in that Christ died for us while we were still sinners. Paul puts the death of Christ at the point in the story where people had nothing in their own condition to commend them.", "Now that they have been justified by Christ's blood, Paul says, they can trust that they will be saved from wrath through him. If God reconciled them to himself through his Son's death while they were enemies, how much more will they be saved by his life now that they are reconciled? They rejoice in God through Jesus Christ, through whom they have received reconciliation. What Christ has already done gives Paul his reason for confidence about what lies ahead."], "lesson": "Christian hope in this passage begins with reconciliation God has given. It makes room to speak honestly about suffering while trusting the love that met people in their weakness.", "question": "How does Christ's death for sinners support Paul's confidence about what lies ahead?"},
  {"id": 91272, "title": "Adam and Christ", "subtitle": "Grace that abounds over sin", "text": "Romans 5:12–21", "chapters": [[44, 5]], "start": 12, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul now goes back to Adam to put the work of Christ in view over the length of human history. Sin came into the world through one man, and death came through sin. Death spread to all because all sinned. Sin was in the world before the law was given. Death reigned from Adam to Moses, even over people whose sin differed from Adam's breaking of a direct command. Paul calls Adam a figure of the one who was to come.", "The effect of Adam's trespass helps Paul show the reach of Christ's gift. Many died through one man's trespass. God's grace and the gift that comes by the grace of Jesus Christ have abounded to many. A single trespass brought judgment and condemnation. The gift comes after many trespasses and brings justification. Paul draws attention to how much the gift has answered.", "Death reigned through the one man. Paul says those who receive abundant grace and the gift of righteousness will reign in life through Jesus Christ. He brings the two accounts together again. One trespass brought condemnation for all, and one righteous act brought justification and life. Through one man's disobedience many were made sinners. Through the obedience of the one, many will be made righteous.", "Paul says the law entered so that the trespass would increase. As sin increased, grace abounded all the more. Sin had reigned in death. Grace now reigns through righteousness leading to eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord. This leaves the reader with a question Paul will take up next. What does this abundance of grace mean for the way believers live now?"], "lesson": "Paul places humanity's condition inside a larger account of what Christ has done. The gift is described as greater than the ruin it answers.", "question": "What changes when Paul moves from what entered through Adam to what comes through Christ?"},
  {"id": 91273, "title": "Buried and Raised With Christ", "subtitle": "Living in the newness of life", "text": "Romans 6:1–14", "chapters": [[44, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul anticipates a question about his words on abundant grace. Should believers continue in sin so that grace will increase? He rejects the thought and asks how those who died to sin can go on living in it. To explain what he means by dying, he brings his readers back to their baptism. Those baptised into Christ Jesus were baptised into his death. They were buried with him so that, as Christ was raised through the Father's glory, they too might walk in newness of life.", "If they have been united with Christ in a death like his, Paul says, they will share in his resurrection. Their old self was crucified with him so that the body ruled by sin would lose its power and they would no longer serve sin as slaves. The person who has died has been freed from sin's claim. Paul expects those who died with Christ to live with him.", "Christ, having risen, will never die again. Death no longer rules over him. He died to sin once for all and now lives to God. Paul asks his readers to reckon themselves in the light of what is true of Christ. They are to count themselves dead to sin and alive to God in Christ Jesus. What they think about their life in him bears on what they do with it.", "Paul therefore tells them to refuse sin's rule over their mortal bodies and its demands on their conduct. They are to present themselves to God as people brought from death to life and offer their bodily powers as instruments of righteousness. Sin will no longer rule them, Paul says, because they live under grace. He has brought the question about grace down to what they will do with their hands, their speech and everything else they can offer to God."], "lesson": "Grace gives believers reason to change whom they obey. Paul asks them to offer their actual capacities to God in the life they now live.", "question": "What would presenting one part of your daily life as an instrument of righteousness involve?"},
  {"id": 91274, "title": "The Master You Obey", "subtitle": "Freedom that bears the fruit of holiness", "text": "Romans 6:15–23", "chapters": [[44, 6]], "start": 15, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul raises a second question about grace. Since believers are under grace rather than under the law, may they sin? He rejects the idea. He asks his readers to think about what it means to present themselves to someone for obedience. They become servants of the one they obey. Sin leads to death, and obedience leads to righteousness. The freedom he has been describing has a direction that can be seen in daily conduct.", "Paul thanks God as he looks back on what has changed for his readers. They once served sin. Then they obeyed from the heart the teaching to which they had been entrusted. Having been freed from sin, they became servants of righteousness. Paul says he is speaking in ordinary human terms because of their weakness. The familiar picture of service helps them see what their allegiance means.", "They once placed their bodies at the service of impurity and lawlessness, and lawlessness led to more of the same. Now they are to put themselves at the service of righteousness, which leads to holiness. Paul asks them to look back at the fruit of the things they are now ashamed of. They were free from the service of righteousness at the time, but the end of those things was death.", "Now they have been freed from sin and have become servants of God. Their fruit is holiness, and the end is eternal life. Paul brings the two courses to their conclusion in a sentence. Sin pays its wages in death. God gives eternal life as a gift through Jesus Christ our Lord."], "lesson": "Paul asks his hearers to compare where two kinds of obedience lead. Choices that look isolated can form habits that direct an entire life.", "question": "What fruit and conclusion does Paul associate with service to God?"},
  {"id": 91275, "title": "The Command That Reveals Coveting", "subtitle": "Recognising what the law brings to light", "text": "Romans 7:1–13", "chapters": [[44, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["At the end of the previous passage, Paul described people who had been freed from sin and now served God. He returns to the question of the law and asks his readers to think about something a law can only do while a person lives. A married woman is bound by law to her husband while he is alive. If she belongs to another man during that time, the law calls her an adulteress. If her husband dies, the bond ends, and she is free to marry again. The point of the example is that death changes the reach of an existing claim.", "In the case of Paul's readers, it is they who have died to the law through the body of Christ. They now belong to the one who was raised from the dead, so that they may bear fruit for God. Paul looks back on the former life and says sinful passions, aroused through the law, were at work in their bodies and bore fruit for death. Having died to what held them, they now serve in the new way of the Spirit. The change reaches beyond how they understand a written command. It concerns the person to whom they belong and the life their service produces.", "This raises an awkward question. If the law could stir up sinful passions, was the law itself sinful? Paul rejects the idea. He takes a command that reaches into the thoughts people may keep to themselves. He says that he would not have recognised coveting if the law had not forbidden it. Sin took the command as an opportunity and produced desires of every kind. Paul describes himself as once alive apart from the law. When the command came, sin sprang to life and he died.", "Even the command that held out life proved to bring death in his case. Paul says sin seized the opportunity, deceived him and killed him through the command. The law remains holy, and the command is holy, righteous and good. Did something good itself bring him death? Paul puts the responsibility on sin, which used something good to produce death. The command exposes sin in its true character. The example began with a rule whose claim ends at death and comes to rest on another question altogether, what can bring a person under sin into a life fruitful for God?"], "lesson": "A good command can uncover a desire we had allowed to pass unnoticed. Paul distinguishes what the command shows from the sin that takes advantage of it.", "question": "Which command does Paul use to explain how he came to recognise coveting?"},
  {"id": 91276, "title": "The Good I Want to Do", "subtitle": "Taking inward conflict seriously", "text": "Romans 7:14–25", "chapters": [[44, 7]], "start": 14, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has already called the command holy and good. He now says the law is spiritual, then puts his own condition beside it. In the first person he describes someone of flesh, sold under sin, who finds his actions hard to understand. He does the very thing he hates, while the thing he wants to do remains undone. There is a kind of agreement with the law even in that confession. By hating the wrong he does, he acknowledges that the law which forbids it is good.", "Paul draws attention to sin dwelling within him. He says he finds no good thing dwelling in his flesh. The desire to do right is present, yet he cannot carry it through. He returns to the same experience from another direction. The good he wants stays out of reach, and the evil he wants to leave alone is what he actually does. His repeated description makes the gap plain. A person can see the goodness of the command, sincerely desire to obey it and still discover another power at work in his conduct.", "Paul uses the word law again for the pattern he keeps finding. When he wants to do good, evil is close at hand. In his inward being he delights in God's law. In his bodily members he sees another law waging war against the law of his mind and taking him captive to sin. The image changes from a person whose actions puzzle him to a prisoner who can see what holds him. The agreement of his mind with God's law has not ended the struggle he describes.", "At that point he cries out over his wretched condition and asks who will deliver him from this body of death. He answers with thanksgiving to God through Jesus Christ our Lord. The closing sentence holds both parts of his experience in view. With his mind he serves God's law, and with his flesh he serves the law of sin. Paul has followed the trouble all the way from the command he approves to the captivity he cannot break by wishing. His thanks directs the reader toward the deliverance he will take up as he begins to speak about life in the Spirit."], "lesson": "Honest confession can name the gap between conviction and conduct. Paul's cry places hope of deliverance with God through Jesus Christ.", "question": "What tension is expressed between what the speaker approves and what he actually does?"},
  {"id": 91277, "title": "The Spirit and the Cry of Adoption", "subtitle": "Living as children who call on the Father", "text": "Romans 8:1–17", "chapters": [[44, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has just cried out for deliverance and given thanks through Jesus Christ. Now he tells the people who are in Christ Jesus that there is no condemnation for them. The law of the Spirit of life in Christ has set them free from the law of sin and death. The law could not overcome the weakness of the flesh. God acted by sending his own Son in the likeness of sinful flesh and for sin, and judged sin in the flesh. Paul's purpose in following that thought is practical. The righteous requirement of the law is to be fulfilled in people who walk according to the Spirit.", "Paul then asks his readers to consider what occupies their minds. People living according to the flesh set their minds on what the flesh wants, and people living according to the Spirit set their minds on what the Spirit desires. One course ends in death, while the mind set on the Spirit has life and peace. The mind ruled by the flesh is hostile to God and cannot submit to his law. Paul speaks that plainly before turning to the people in whom God's Spirit dwells. Anyone who lacks Christ's Spirit does not belong to him.", "Paul sets the body's mortality beside the life that comes through righteousness. The God who raised Jesus from the dead will give life even to their mortal bodies through his Spirit who dwells in them. That promise bears on how they live before their bodies are raised. They owe the flesh no obedience. If they live by it they will die. If by the Spirit they put to death the deeds of the body, they will live. Paul says those led by God's Spirit are God's children.", "He then brings them from the fear of slavery to the language of a household. They have received the Spirit of adoption, by whom they cry to God as Abba, Father. The Spirit bears witness with their own spirit that they are God's children. Children are also heirs, heirs of God and fellow heirs with Christ. Paul includes suffering with Christ in the life of those who will share his glory. The believer who calls on God as Father has a place in that family even while the suffering is still real."], "lesson": "The Spirit leads believers into a family relationship with obligations and assurance. They can address God as Father even in circumstances that carry a cost.", "question": "How does adoption change the way believers approach God according to Paul?"},
  {"id": 91278, "title": "Creation Groans and Love Holds", "subtitle": "Hope when words are difficult", "text": "Romans 8:18–39", "chapters": [[44, 8]], "start": 18, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has just spoken of suffering with Christ and sharing his glory. Now he weighs those two things. The sufferings of the present time are small beside the glory to come. His view widens from the believer to creation itself. Creation has been subjected to frustration and waits for God's children to be revealed, in hope of sharing their freedom from decay. Paul likens its groaning to the pains of childbirth. Believers who already have the firstfruits of the Spirit groan too, waiting for adoption in its fullness, the redemption of their bodies. They wait patiently for what they cannot yet see.", "The Spirit helps them in their weakness. At times they do not know how to pray as they should, and the Spirit himself intercedes with groans beyond speech. God searches hearts and knows the mind of the Spirit, whose intercession for believers accords with God's will. Paul goes on to speak of all things working together for the good of those who love God and are called according to his purpose. They are to be conformed to the image of his Son, the firstborn among many brothers and sisters. Paul traces God's work from foreknowledge and purpose through calling and justification to glory.", "He then puts several questions to his readers. If God is for them, who can stand against them? God gave up his own Son for them. They can trust him to give what they need with him. Who can make a charge stick against the people God has chosen, when God himself justifies them? Who can condemn them? Christ died, was raised and is at God's right hand interceding for them. Paul has given them two pictures of intercession in the same passage, one in their weakness and one at God's right hand.", "He asks whether trouble, distress, persecution, hunger, want, danger or the sword can separate them from Christ's love. Scripture itself speaks of God's people facing death. Even in such circumstances, Paul says, they conquer through the one who loved them. He ends with what he is persuaded of. Neither death nor life, spiritual powers, the present or the future, height or depth, nor anything else in creation can separate them from God's love in Christ Jesus their Lord."], "lesson": "Paul gives groaning and assurance space in the same argument. Weakness in prayer is met by the Spirit's help and by confidence in God's love.", "question": "Where does Paul direct a believer who is too weak to know how to pray?"},
  {"id": 91279, "title": "Paul Grieves for Israel", "subtitle": "Mercy and the faithfulness of God", "text": "Romans 9:1–29", "chapters": [[44, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["After speaking of the love from which nothing can separate believers, Paul tells the truth about a grief he cannot put aside. He says his conscience bears witness in the Holy Spirit. He carries great sorrow for his own people, the Israelites, and says he could wish himself accursed and cut off from Christ for their sake. He names what God has given them. The adoption and the glory, the covenants, the law, the worship and the promises are theirs. The patriarchs belong to them, and from their people came the Christ according to the flesh.", "Has God's word failed? Paul answers by returning to the family stories in Scripture. He distinguishes between physical descent from Israel and belonging to Israel in the sense his argument now requires. Abraham had more than one son, and God said that his offspring would be named through Isaac. Paul recalls the promise that Sarah would have a son. Then he turns to Rebekah, whose two children had the same father, Isaac. Before the twins were born or had done good or evil, she was told that the elder would serve the younger. Paul connects this with God's purpose in calling and with what Scripture later says about Jacob and Esau.", "Paul knows the objection this raises. Is there injustice with God? He cites God's words to Moses about showing mercy and compassion as he chooses. Mercy depends on God, beyond any human willing or effort. Paul brings in Pharaoh as another example. Scripture says God raised him up so that his power might be shown and his name proclaimed throughout the earth. Paul says God has mercy on whom he wills and hardens whom he wills.", "The objection grows sharper. Why does God still find fault? Paul asks the questioner to consider the relation of clay to its potter. A potter can make vessels for different purposes out of the same lump. Paul speaks of God's patience in bearing vessels of wrath and of the riches of his glory made known to vessels of mercy. Those called include Gentiles as well as Jews. Hosea speaks of people once called 'not my people' becoming God's people. Isaiah speaks of a remnant of Israel being saved and of the Lord leaving survivors. Paul has answered the question of God's faithfulness by going back to the very Scriptures entrusted to the people for whom he grieves."], "lesson": "Paul's argument begins with grief for real people. His appeal to mercy calls for humility when considering God's freedom and the limits of human claims.", "question": "What privileges does Paul remember before asking how God's promise stands?"},
  {"id": 91280, "title": "The Message Comes Near", "subtitle": "Righteousness received by faith and proclaimed", "text": "Romans 9:30–10:21", "chapters": [[44, 9], [44, 10]], "start": 30, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul brings the argument back to a difference he wants his readers to see. Gentiles who had never pursued righteousness attained it through faith. Israel pursued a law of righteousness and failed to reach its goal, because they pursued it as though it depended on works. Paul says they stumbled over a stone, and brings together words from Isaiah about the stone set in Zion and the security of the one who believes. Then he speaks of his own prayer to God that Israel might be saved. He acknowledges their zeal for God and says it lacks knowledge. Seeking to establish their own righteousness, they failed to submit to God's righteousness. Christ is the end of the law for righteousness to everyone who believes.", "Paul sets the righteousness described in the law beside the way the righteousness of faith speaks. He draws on Moses' language about going up or going down to obtain the word. The hearer has no need to ascend to bring Christ down or descend to bring him up from the dead. The word is already near, in the mouth and in the heart. Paul identifies it as the message of faith he proclaims. The person who confesses Jesus as Lord and believes in the heart that God raised him from the dead will be saved. The same Lord is Lord of all, rich toward everyone who calls on him.", "Then Paul asks how someone comes to call on the Lord. Calling follows believing. Believing depends on hearing, and hearing depends on someone proclaiming the message. A preacher needs to be sent. Isaiah's words about the welcome given to messengers of good news belong here, and so does Isaiah's question about who believed what they heard. The message goes out, and hearers give different answers. Faith comes through hearing the word about Christ.", "Paul asks whether Israel had heard and whether they had understood. He turns to the Scriptures again. Moses spoke of Israel being provoked by people who were no nation. Isaiah spoke of God being found by people who had never sought him. Of Israel he speaks of God stretching out his hands all day toward a disobedient and contrary people. Paul's prayer at the beginning of the passage gives these words their setting. He is speaking about people he longs to see call on the Lord."], "lesson": "Paul's concern for his people becomes prayer and an argument for proclamation. The sequence makes the work of bringing the message to others a practical responsibility.", "question": "What links does Paul name between someone being sent and a hearer calling on the Lord?"},
  {"id": 91281, "title": "Unexpected Branches on an Olive Tree", "subtitle": "Humility after being welcomed", "text": "Romans 11:1–24", "chapters": [[44, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul asks whether God has rejected his people. His own life gives the first answer. He too is an Israelite, descended from Abraham and from the tribe of Benjamin. He reminds his readers of Elijah, who complained to God that Israel had killed the prophets and torn down the altars. Elijah thought he alone had survived and that his life was in danger. God told him that he had kept seven thousand people who had never bowed to Baal. Paul sees a remnant in his own time too, chosen by grace. Grace would lose its meaning if the choice depended on works.", "Israel, Paul says, failed to obtain what it was seeking. The chosen obtained it, and the others were hardened. He cites Scripture about unseeing eyes, unhearing ears and a table that becomes a snare. Then he asks whether they stumbled so as to fall beyond recovery. Through their trespass salvation has come to the Gentiles, which in turn is meant to move Israel to jealousy. If their loss has brought riches to the world, Paul asks his readers to consider what their fullness would mean. He takes his ministry to the Gentiles seriously in part because he hopes to stir his own people and save some of them.", "Paul pictures an olive tree. If the root is holy, the branches are holy. Some of the branches have been broken off, and a shoot from a wild olive has been grafted among the others and now shares the nourishment of the root. Paul tells his Gentile readers to think carefully about any claim to superiority. The root supports the grafted branch. If the branch replies that others were removed to make room for it, Paul points back to what happened. They were broken off through unbelief, and the grafted branch stands by faith. That is reason for reverence.", "Paul asks them to consider God's kindness and severity. God has been kind to them, and they are to continue in that kindness. The warning against pride matters because God can cut off the grafted branches too. And if those who were broken off do not continue in unbelief, God is able to graft them in again. If a wild branch could be grafted into a cultivated tree, Paul says, how much more readily could the natural branches be grafted back into their own olive tree?"], "lesson": "The image gives people included through faith a reason for gratitude and reverence. It also keeps open a possibility they may be tempted to dismiss in others.", "question": "How does Paul's reminder about the root answer the temptation to boast?"},
  {"id": 91282, "title": "The Argument Ends in Praise", "subtitle": "A place for humility before God's wisdom", "text": "Romans 11:25–36", "chapters": [[44, 11]], "start": 25, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul tells his Gentile readers that he wants them to understand a mystery so they will have less reason to think themselves wise. A hardening has come upon Israel in part until the fullness of the Gentiles comes in. In this way, Paul says, all Israel will be saved. He draws on Scripture about a deliverer coming from Zion, turning ungodliness away from Jacob, and God keeping his covenant by taking away sins. Paul has just spoken about branches that God can graft back into their own tree. Here he states the hope in terms of God's promise.", "Paul keeps two facts in view at once. With regard to the gospel, these people are enemies for the Gentiles' sake. With regard to election, they are beloved for the sake of their ancestors. God's gifts and calling are irrevocable. The same people who have opposed the gospel are still the people about whom Paul can speak of God's love and calling. He asks the Gentiles to remember that they too have a history of disobedience.", "They once disobeyed God and have now received mercy through Israel's disobedience. Israel is now disobedient and can also receive mercy in connection with the mercy shown to the Gentiles. Paul says God has shut all up in disobedience in order to have mercy on all. Neither group, then, can take its own record of obedience as the reason it has been shown mercy. The question of how one group came to receive the message has brought both groups back to the same need.", "At this point Paul's words turn to praise. He speaks of the depth of God's riches, wisdom and knowledge. God's judgments are beyond searching out, and his ways are beyond tracing. Paul asks who has known God's mind, who has served as his adviser, or who has first given him something that God now owes back. All things come from God, hold together through him and come to their end in him. Paul gives him glory forever. The next thing he will ask of his readers begins with the mercies he has spent these chapters tracing."], "lesson": "Paul has reasoned carefully and still ends in worship. The passage shows the place of humility after patient study of a difficult subject.", "question": "What reasons for humility does Paul give before he turns to praise?"},
  {"id": 91283, "title": "A Living Sacrifice in Ordinary Relationships", "subtitle": "Love that takes visible form", "text": "Romans 12:1–21", "chapters": [[44, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul appeals to God's mercies and asks believers to offer their bodies and be transformed by a renewed mind. He compares different gifts to members of one body. He then describes love in practice: honouring others, sharing with people in need, persevering in prayer, blessing persecutors and giving food or drink to an enemy."], "lesson": "The offering of the body becomes visible in the way people use gifts and respond to others. In Paul's examples, even hostility becomes an occasion to do good.", "question": "Which of Paul's instructions gives you a way to act towards someone with whom you are in conflict?"},
  {"id": 91284, "title": "Duties to Authorities and to Neighbours", "subtitle": "Paying what is owed and walking in the light", "text": "Romans 13:1–14", "chapters": [[44, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul discusses duties towards governing authorities and says to render taxes, respect and honour as due. He turns to the continuing duty of love, which seeks no harm to a neighbour and fulfils the law. Using the image of night passing and day drawing near, he urges his hearers to put on Christ."], "lesson": "Paul gives particular duties their place and then describes love as a debt that keeps making claims on us. The approaching day gives urgency to daily conduct.", "question": "How does Paul connect love of neighbour with the commands he names?"},
  {"id": 91285, "title": "Different Tables Within One Fellowship", "subtitle": "Making room for conscience", "text": "Romans 14:1–23", "chapters": [[44, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Some believers eat freely and others abstain. Some distinguish days, and others regard days alike. Paul tells them to receive one another and remember that each answers to the Lord. He asks people to consider whether their conduct causes another to stumble and to pursue peace and what builds up the fellowship."], "lesson": "Paul gives weight to both conscience and the effect of freedom on another person. A choice can be permitted and still be unhelpful in a particular company.", "question": "Why might a believer freely give up something at a shared meal?"},
  {"id": 91286, "title": "The Strong Bear With the Weak", "subtitle": "Welcome patterned on Christ", "text": "Romans 15:1–13", "chapters": [[44, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul asks the strong to bear with those who struggle and to seek their neighbours' good. He points to Christ's example and speaks of Scripture giving endurance and hope. He asks believers to receive one another as Christ received them. He then gathers Scriptures about the Gentiles sharing in praise."], "lesson": "Welcome is work someone has to do, especially where strengths and convictions differ. Paul gives it a common purpose in the glory of God.", "question": "How does Paul's instruction to the strong follow from what he says about Christ?"},
  {"id": 91287, "title": "A Collection, a Journey and a Request for Prayer", "subtitle": "Serving across distances", "text": "Romans 15:14–33", "chapters": [[44, 15]], "start": 14, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul explains his calling among the Gentiles. He hopes to visit Rome on his way to Spain, but first he is carrying a contribution from Macedonia and Achaia to poor believers in Jerusalem. He asks the Romans to join him in prayer for the journey and for the gift to be received."], "lesson": "Paul sees practical support as part of fellowship across communities. He also asks believers who may never travel with him to share in the work through prayer.", "question": "What work takes Paul to Jerusalem before his hoped-for visit to Rome?"},
  {"id": 91288, "title": "The People Who Carried the Work", "subtitle": "Recognising service by name", "text": "Romans 16:1–27", "chapters": [[44, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul commends Phoebe and asks the Romans to help her. He greets Priscilla and Aquila and many others who have worked, hosted churches or shared danger. He warns against people who cause division and sends greetings from his companions, including Tertius, who writes down the letter. The letter ends in praise to God."], "lesson": "Paul remembers people by name and sometimes tells what their work cost. The greetings put the earlier teaching about one body into the life of particular households and workers.", "question": "Which acts of service are made visible in the greetings Paul sends?"},
  {"id": 91289, "title": "A Household Reports a Division", "subtitle": "The church belongs to Christ", "text": "1 Corinthians 1:1–17", "chapters": [[45, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul thanks God for the gifts of the church at Corinth. Then he says people from Chloe's household have reported quarrels. Some claim Paul, Apollos, Cephas or Christ as their party. He asks whether Christ is divided and whether Paul was crucified for them. He remembers the few people he baptised and returns to his commission to preach."], "lesson": "Paul can give thanks for a congregation and still name a damaging habit plainly. He asks where the church's allegiance belongs before addressing its factions.", "question": "Which of Paul's questions exposes the weakness of building a party around a leader?"},
  {"id": 91290, "title": "The Word of the Cross", "subtitle": "Where boasting comes to an end", "text": "1 Corinthians 1:18–31", "chapters": [[45, 1]], "start": 18, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul says the message of the cross appears foolish to some and is God's power to those being saved. He describes different demands for signs and wisdom, then points to Christ crucified. He asks the Corinthians to consider their calling: many lacked the standing that attracts public honour. God gives them reason to boast in the Lord."], "lesson": "Paul connects the message of the cross with the kind of people God calls. Together they question the church's confidence in social status and persuasive display.", "question": "Why does Paul ask the Corinthians to consider who they were when they were called?"},
  {"id": 91291, "title": "What the Spirit Makes Known", "subtitle": "Depending on God in teaching", "text": "1 Corinthians 2:1–16", "chapters": [[45, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul recalls coming in weakness and concentrating on Jesus Christ crucified. He wanted the Corinthians' faith to rest in God's power. He describes wisdom God reveals through the Spirit, who searches even God's deep things. Those who have received the Spirit can understand things freely given by God."], "lesson": "Paul cares about what supports faith after a speaker leaves. The Spirit's work gives teachers and hearers a reason to approach spiritual matters with humility.", "question": "What did Paul want their faith to rest on?"},
  {"id": 91292, "title": "The Field and the Building", "subtitle": "Working together under God", "text": "1 Corinthians 3:1–23", "chapters": [[45, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul says jealousy and factions show the Corinthians' immaturity. He planted, Apollos watered and God gave growth. He then compares the church to a building with Jesus Christ as its foundation. Each builder's work will be tested. The community is God's temple, and no one should boast in human leaders."], "lesson": "The field image gives distinct tasks a place in one work. The building image asks people to consider the quality of what they add to the foundation.", "question": "What was within Paul's and Apollos's responsibility, and what belonged to God?"},
  {"id": 91293, "title": "A Steward Answers to His Master", "subtitle": "Faithfulness when opinions differ", "text": "1 Corinthians 4:1–21", "chapters": [[45, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul says ministers should be regarded as servants entrusted with God's mysteries. A steward must be faithful, and the Lord will disclose hidden motives. Paul contrasts the Corinthians' satisfaction with the hardships of the apostles. As a father in the gospel, he warns them, sends Timothy to remind them of his ways and prepares to visit."], "lesson": "Paul tells the church to hold its verdicts with restraint. He also gives an example people can examine in his life and in Timothy's account of it.", "question": "What does Paul say the Lord will bring to light when he comes?"},
  {"id": 91294, "title": "A Case the Church Had Tolerated", "subtitle": "Facing harm within the fellowship", "text": "1 Corinthians 5:1–13", "chapters": [[45, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul addresses a case of sexual immorality the congregation has allowed while remaining proud. He calls them to act and uses the image of leaven spreading through dough. He clarifies a former instruction about fellowship, distinguishing the church's responsibility for its members from God's judgement of outsiders."], "lesson": "The leaven illustration makes the congregation's response part of the issue. Paul treats shared responsibility and holiness as matters that require action.", "question": "How does Paul explain the scope of the judgement he asks the church to make?"},
  {"id": 91295, "title": "Disputes and the Use of the Body", "subtitle": "Belonging to Christ in everyday decisions", "text": "1 Corinthians 6:1–20", "chapters": [[45, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul challenges believers who take disputes with one another before outside courts. He asks whether they would accept a wrong and recalls the change God has made in them. He turns to sexual conduct and describes the body as belonging to the Lord and as a temple of the Holy Spirit. They were bought at a price."], "lesson": "The chapter puts the rights people claim alongside the obligations of belonging to Christ. Paul asks whether a choice serves God's glory in the body and in the fellowship.", "question": "How does belonging to Christ change Paul's answer to the claim that something is permissible?"},
  {"id": 91296, "title": "Households and Their Different Callings", "subtitle": "Faithfulness in present responsibilities", "text": "1 Corinthians 7:1–24", "chapters": [[45, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul answers questions about marriage, singleness and mixed households. He speaks of mutual duties and makes room for different gifts. Where an unbelieving spouse consents to remain, the believer should not dismiss the spouse. He then considers circumcision and slavery as circumstances in which people may have been called."], "lesson": "Paul applies the call of God to concrete situations. Faith can be practised within existing obligations while room remains for changes the passage permits.", "question": "What does Paul say about a believing spouse whose unbelieving spouse is willing to remain?"},
  {"id": 91297, "title": "Choices in a Time of Distress", "subtitle": "Making decisions with devotion in view", "text": "1 Corinthians 7:25–40", "chapters": [[45, 7]], "start": 25, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul offers a judgement about unmarried people in view of the present distress and the shortness of time. He considers the real concerns that marriage brings and the opportunity for undivided attention to the Lord. He distinguishes his advice from a command, gives people room to marry and addresses a widow's freedom to marry in the Lord."], "lesson": "Paul explains what makes his counsel fitting to the situation. His concern is that people can attend to the Lord while facing the responsibilities of the choice they make.", "question": "What reason does Paul give for the advice he offers in this part of the chapter?"},
  {"id": 91298, "title": "A Meal That Could Wound a Conscience", "subtitle": "Love governs what knowledge permits", "text": "1 Corinthians 8:1–13", "chapters": [[45, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["The Corinthians ask about food offered to idols. Paul affirms that there is one God, while recognising that some believers still associate the food with their former worship. An example set by someone confident may lead another to act against conscience. Paul is willing to give up meat if it causes a brother to stumble."], "lesson": "Knowledge can describe what a person is free to do. Love also considers what someone else is likely to learn from watching that choice.", "question": "Why does Paul give weight to the conscience of the person who sees another believer eating?"},
  {"id": 91299, "title": "A Right Paul Chooses to Lay Aside", "subtitle": "Serving the gospel ahead of advantage", "text": "1 Corinthians 9:1–18", "chapters": [[45, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul defends his apostleship and argues that workers have a right to material support. He uses examples from military service, farming, the law and temple service. He says the Lord has provided for gospel workers to live from their work. Paul has chosen to forego the right in Corinth so that he will place no obstacle in the way of the gospel."], "lesson": "Paul can recognise a genuine right and still decide that its exercise would hinder his work in a particular setting. The reason for restraint is the good of the people hearing the gospel.", "question": "What right is Paul defending, and why does he choose to set it aside?"},
  {"id": 91300, "title": "The Runner and the Finish", "subtitle": "Discipline for the sake of others", "text": "1 Corinthians 9:19–27", "chapters": [[45, 9]], "start": 19, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Though free, Paul makes himself a servant to many. He describes adapting his approach to Jews, Gentiles and the weak so that he might win people. He compares the life to a runner and a boxer who trains deliberately. He applies the discipline to himself as someone who preaches to others."], "lesson": "Paul's flexibility has a purpose and boundaries. He exercises restraint in his own conduct so that his life does not contradict the message he gives.", "question": "What does the athlete image add to Paul's explanation of becoming a servant to many?"},
  {"id": 91301, "title": "Lessons From Israel in the Wilderness", "subtitle": "Receiving privileges with humility", "text": "1 Corinthians 10:1–22", "chapters": [[45, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul recalls Israel passing through the sea, eating and drinking in the wilderness. He names instances of craving, idolatry, immorality, testing and grumbling. The accounts warn people who think they stand securely. God is faithful in temptation and provides a way to endure. Paul urges the church to flee idolatry and considers what participation at a table means."], "lesson": "Shared privileges gave Israel real gifts and still left room for disobedience. Paul asks believers to learn from the old events before overestimating their own strength.", "question": "How does Paul place God's faithfulness beside the warning against confidence in oneself?"},
  {"id": 91302, "title": "At Someone Else's Table", "subtitle": "Seeking another person's good", "text": "1 Corinthians 10:23–33", "chapters": [[45, 10]], "start": 23, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul discusses meat sold in the market and food served by an unbelieving host. He gives room to eat without an inquiry, then addresses the case where someone explicitly identifies the food as an offering. He asks hearers to consider conscience, seek another's good and do ordinary things such as eating and drinking to God's glory."], "lesson": "The same food can be received in one circumstance and declined in another. Paul directs his readers to attend to the people present and the meaning the action has for them.", "question": "What new information at a meal changes the decision Paul gives?"},
  {"id": 91303, "title": "Honour in Prayer and Prophecy", "subtitle": "Considering others in public worship", "text": "1 Corinthians 11:1–16", "chapters": [[45, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul commends the holding of traditions and discusses honour, head coverings and the conduct of men and women who pray or prophesy. He reasons from creation and from the way their actions are understood. He also says that man and woman are interdependent in the Lord and that all things are from God."], "lesson": "This passage asks worshippers to consider the honour their conduct communicates. Paul's reminder of mutual dependence gives humility a place in the discussion.", "question": "How does the statement that all things are from God shape the way you read the surrounding instructions?"},
  {"id": 91304, "title": "When One Eats and Another Goes Hungry", "subtitle": "Recognising the body at the Lord's Supper", "text": "1 Corinthians 11:17–34", "chapters": [[45, 11]], "start": 17, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul hears that divisions show themselves when the church gathers to eat. Some are hungry while others have gone ahead. He recounts what he received about Jesus giving the bread and cup and calls people to examine themselves. He asks them to wait for one another when they come together."], "lesson": "The meal proclaims Jesus' death while the congregation's conduct can humiliate people with little to bring. Paul addresses the way they treat one another as part of taking the supper seriously.", "question": "What change does Paul give the church for the next time it gathers to eat?"},
  {"id": 91305, "title": "Many Members in One Body", "subtitle": "Giving honour to the overlooked", "text": "1 Corinthians 12:1–31", "chapters": [[45, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul names different gifts, forms of service and activities that come from the same Spirit, Lord and God. He compares the church to a body. A foot belongs even if it is not a hand, and an eye cannot dismiss the hand. Members regarded as weaker are necessary. When one suffers or is honoured, all share in it."], "lesson": "The body image questions both the person who feels dispensable and the person who thinks others are dispensable. God gives each member a place in a shared life.", "question": "Who might be overlooked in your fellowship, and how does Paul's picture change the way you see that person's contribution?"},
  {"id": 91306, "title": "The More Excellent Way", "subtitle": "Love gives gifts their purpose", "text": "1 Corinthians 13:1–13", "chapters": [[45, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul imagines impressive speech, knowledge, faith, giving and sacrifice without love. He then describes love through its patience, kindness, restraint and endurance. Gifts such as prophecy and partial knowledge have their appointed limit. Faith, hope and love remain, and Paul calls love the greatest."], "lesson": "Paul measures gifts by the character in which they are exercised. Love becomes visible in how people respond to irritation, wrongdoing and partial understanding.", "question": "Which description of love would most change the way you exercise a gift you already have?"},
  {"id": 91307, "title": "Words People Can Understand", "subtitle": "Using gifts to build up hearers", "text": "1 Corinthians 14:1–25", "chapters": [[45, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul tells the church to pursue love and desire spiritual gifts. He distinguishes speaking in a tongue from speech that the congregation can understand and receive. Using musical sounds and the example of prayer, he asks how people can respond to words they cannot follow. He also considers what a visitor hears in the gathering."], "lesson": "A person using a gift should consider the one trying to listen. Interpretation and intelligible speech help other people take part rather than remain observers.", "question": "How does Paul's example of someone saying Amen show why understanding matters?"},
  {"id": 91308, "title": "Room for Others to Speak", "subtitle": "Order that serves the congregation", "text": "1 Corinthians 14:26–40", "chapters": [[45, 14]], "start": 26, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul pictures people arriving with a psalm, teaching, revelation or a tongue. He sets limits, calls for interpretation and asks others to weigh what prophets say. He addresses speech and silence in the gathered church. His governing instruction is that everything should build up the church and be done decently and in order."], "lesson": "Order makes room for contributions to be heard and tested. Paul asks people with something to offer to consider the benefit of the whole gathering.", "question": "Which instructions in the passage help a gathering receive and evaluate what someone says?"},
  {"id": 91309, "title": "The Gospel Paul Received and Passed On", "subtitle": "Faith rests on a risen Christ", "text": "1 Corinthians 15:1–19", "chapters": [[45, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul recalls the message he delivered: Christ died for sins, was buried, rose and appeared to many, including Paul. He describes grace at work in his own calling after persecuting the church. Then he asks what follows if the dead are never raised. If Christ is still dead, their faith and proclamation are empty."], "lesson": "Paul places his own experience inside a testimony others can also give. The resurrection is part of what the gospel announces and of what believers trust.", "question": "What would be lost from Paul's message if the claim that Christ rose were removed?"},
  {"id": 91310, "title": "The Firstfruits and the Last Enemy", "subtitle": "Hope that changes present conduct", "text": "1 Corinthians 15:20–34", "chapters": [[45, 15]], "start": 20, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul calls the risen Christ the firstfruits of those who have died. He relates Christ's resurrection to Adam, the order of resurrection and the defeat of every enemy, with death last. He asks why people put themselves in danger if there is no resurrection and urges the Corinthians to wake up to righteous living."], "lesson": "The hope Paul describes changes how sacrifice and discipline look in the present. His discussion of the future leads to an immediate call to live differently.", "question": "Why does Paul connect his exposure to danger with the truth of the resurrection?"},
  {"id": 91311, "title": "A Seed Goes Into the Ground", "subtitle": "The hope of a changed body", "text": "1 Corinthians 15:35–58", "chapters": [[45, 15]], "start": 35, "end": 58, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul answers questions about how the dead are raised and what body they will have. A seed is sown and God gives it a body. Paul describes decay giving way to incorruption and the mortal clothed with immortality. He closes with thanksgiving for victory through Jesus and urges steady work in the Lord."], "lesson": "Paul's image gives continuity and change a place in the same hope. The chapter ends by giving ordinary faithful work a reason to continue.", "question": "How does the resurrection hope support Paul's final instruction about work in the Lord?"},
  {"id": 91312, "title": "Setting Something Aside and Greeting Fellow Workers", "subtitle": "Preparing generosity in advance", "text": "1 Corinthians 16:1–24", "chapters": [[45, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul gives directions for the collection, asking people to set something aside weekly as they prosper. He explains his travel plans, asks the church to receive Timothy without fear and mentions Apollos. He remembers people devoted to serving believers and sends greetings. He tells the church to do everything in love."], "lesson": "Advance preparation gives generosity a dependable form. The closing greetings also ask the church to notice the people who carry its work.", "question": "Why does Paul ask people to put something aside before he arrives?"},
  {"id": 91313, "title": "Comfort Received and Passed On", "subtitle": "Learning dependence in trouble", "text": "2 Corinthians 1:1–11", "chapters": [[46, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul greets the church and blesses the Father of mercies. He speaks of receiving comfort in affliction so that he can comfort others. He remembers a distress in Asia that felt beyond his strength. It taught him to rely on God who raises the dead. He asks the church to share in the work through prayer."], "lesson": "Paul gives the people a glimpse of trouble he could not manage alone. The comfort he received becomes something he can pass on to others.", "question": "What does Paul say his distress taught him about where to place his confidence?"},
  {"id": 91314, "title": "A Changed Visit and a Call to Forgive", "subtitle": "Correction that seeks restoration", "text": "2 Corinthians 1:12–2:11", "chapters": [[46, 1], [46, 2]], "start": 12, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul explains that he changed his plans to spare the Corinthians another painful visit. He recalls writing in tears so they might know his love. Speaking of someone who had caused sorrow, Paul says the punishment by the majority is sufficient. He urges the church to forgive, comfort and confirm its love for the person."], "lesson": "Paul is concerned with the damage that can follow once correction has done its work. Restoration includes words and actions that assure a person of the community's love.", "question": "Why does Paul say the congregation should comfort the person it had corrected?"},
  {"id": 91315, "title": "An Open Door and a Letter on Hearts", "subtitle": "God makes people sufficient for service", "text": "2 Corinthians 2:12–3:6", "chapters": [[46, 2], [46, 3]], "start": 12, "end": 6, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul finds an open door at Troas but has no rest because he has not found Titus. He goes on to Macedonia. He describes God spreading the knowledge of Christ through his servants. The Corinthians themselves are a letter written by the Spirit. Paul locates his capacity for the ministry of the new covenant in God."], "lesson": "Paul's concern for Titus is part of the life in which he serves. His confidence about his work rests on what God does in people.", "question": "Why does Paul call the Corinthians a letter, and who has written it?"},
  {"id": 91316, "title": "The Veil Lifted", "subtitle": "Transformation in the Lord's presence", "text": "2 Corinthians 3:7–18", "chapters": [[46, 3]], "start": 7, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul recalls the glory on Moses' face and compares it with the glory of the Spirit's ministry. He speaks of a veil when the old covenant is read and of its removal when a person turns to the Lord. With unveiled faces believers behold the Lord's glory and are changed into his likeness by the Spirit."], "lesson": "The removal of the veil makes possible a way of seeing that leads to change. Paul connects transformation with turning to the Lord and the work of the Spirit.", "question": "What does Paul say happens when a person turns to the Lord?"},
  {"id": 91317, "title": "Treasure in Earthen Vessels", "subtitle": "Light and hope carried in weakness", "text": "2 Corinthians 4:1–18", "chapters": [[46, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul speaks of proclaiming Jesus Christ as Lord with openness and describes God shining in hearts. He compares the treasure to something carried in fragile jars. He lists pressures that have not destroyed the workers and connects their suffering with the life of Jesus becoming visible. He fixes his attention on what endures beyond what can be seen."], "lesson": "Paul lets his hearers see both the treasure and the fragility of those carrying it. Weakness gives room for the source of the power to become evident.", "question": "Why is the image of an earthen vessel fitting for the hardships Paul describes?"},
  {"id": 91318, "title": "A Tent, a Home and a Message of Reconciliation", "subtitle": "Living for the One who died and rose", "text": "2 Corinthians 5:1–21", "chapters": [[46, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul compares the present body to a tent and speaks of a dwelling from God. He longs for life to swallow up mortality and aims to please the Lord as all will appear before Christ's judgement seat. Christ's love presses him towards a new way of regarding people. God reconciles through Christ and entrusts his servants with the message of reconciliation."], "lesson": "Paul's future hope gives direction to present conduct. A person who has received reconciliation can bring its invitation to others.", "question": "How does Paul connect Christ's death and resurrection with the purpose for which people now live?"},
  {"id": 91319, "title": "An Open Heart and a Call to Holiness", "subtitle": "Integrity under hardship and pressure", "text": "2 Corinthians 6:1–7:1", "chapters": [[46, 6], [46, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 1, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul urges the church to receive grace with a present response. He describes ministry through hardship, patience, truth and apparently contradictory experiences such as sorrow with joy. He asks the Corinthians to open their hearts. Then he calls for care about binding alliances and recalls God's promise to dwell among his people. On the strength of those promises he calls them to pursue holiness."], "lesson": "The apostle asks for relational openness while taking loyalty to God seriously. Both concerns are responses to the God who has drawn near.", "question": "What qualities does Paul put beside the hardships he lists as marks of his ministry?"},
  {"id": 91320, "title": "Titus Brings News From Corinth", "subtitle": "Sorrow that leads to a changed response", "text": "2 Corinthians 7:2–16", "chapters": [[46, 7]], "start": 2, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul asks the Corinthians to receive him and recalls his concern in Macedonia. Titus arrives and brings comfort with news of their response. Paul had grieved over causing pain by his letter. He now rejoices that their sorrow led to repentance and describes the earnestness it produced. Titus himself is encouraged by their reception."], "lesson": "The sorrow is judged by what it produces. Paul also notices the comfort brought by an honest report of a relationship being repaired.", "question": "What outcomes does Paul name when he describes the Corinthians' sorrow?"},
  {"id": 91321, "title": "The Macedonians Ask to Take Part", "subtitle": "Giving willingly and handling gifts honestly", "text": "2 Corinthians 8:1–24", "chapters": [[46, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul tells how churches in Macedonia gave generously amid hardship and begged to share in the relief work. He urges Corinth to complete what it had begun and recalls Christ's grace. He explains giving according to what one has and sends approved companions with Titus so the gift will be handled honourably before God and people."], "lesson": "Paul pairs willingness to give with careful handling of the money. Generosity is strengthened when others can see that it is being administered honestly.", "question": "Why does Paul arrange for more than one trusted person to accompany the collection?"},
  {"id": 91322, "title": "A Gift Ready Before the Visitors Arrive", "subtitle": "Cheerful generosity and thanksgiving", "text": "2 Corinthians 9:1–15", "chapters": [[46, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul sends people ahead so the promised gift will be ready. He compares giving to sowing and asks each person to give as decided in the heart. God supplies what is needed for good work. Beyond meeting needs, the gift will lead people to give thanks and pray for those who gave."], "lesson": "Advance preparation can make room for a gift given freely. Paul sees the result in both material provision and gratitude to God.", "question": "What effects does Paul expect this gift to have after the recipients receive it?"},
  {"id": 91323, "title": "Letters, Presence and the Limits of Boasting", "subtitle": "Letting the Lord decide what is commendable", "text": "2 Corinthians 10:1–18", "chapters": [[46, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul answers people who call him bold in letters and weak in person. He describes the character of his conflict and says his conduct will match what he writes. He challenges people who measure themselves by one another and describes the sphere of work God has given him. Commendation comes from the Lord."], "lesson": "Comparing ourselves with a circle of admirers can make almost any claim seem justified. Paul directs attention to the work actually entrusted to him and to the Lord's judgement.", "question": "What different standards of commendation does Paul put before the Corinthians?"},
  {"id": 91324, "title": "A Different Message and Impressive Messengers", "subtitle": "Discernment when a speaker looks convincing", "text": "2 Corinthians 11:1–15", "chapters": [[46, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul fears the Corinthians will be drawn away from devotion to Christ. He names the danger of receiving another Jesus, spirit or gospel. He explains why he preached without charge in Corinth and warns that deceptive workers can present themselves attractively, recalling that even Satan can appear as an angel of light."], "lesson": "An impressive appearance is one thing a hearer can see. Paul asks the church to attend to the message and to the direction in which it moves their loyalty.", "question": "What three things does Paul ask the Corinthians to notice if the message they receive has changed?"},
  {"id": 91325, "title": "The Experiences Paul Would Rather Mention", "subtitle": "Strength measured by care in hardship", "text": "2 Corinthians 11:16–33", "chapters": [[46, 11]], "start": 16, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul reluctantly answers boasting by listing hardships: labour, imprisonment, beatings, journeys, dangers and hunger. He adds the daily concern he carries for the churches. If he must boast, he will speak of things that show weakness. He recalls escaping Damascus by being lowered in a basket through an opening in the wall."], "lesson": "Paul ends with an escape that depended on help and offers little occasion for display. His concern for the churches remains a daily pressure within the list.", "question": "Why is the escape in a basket a fitting end to this kind of boasting?"},
  {"id": 91326, "title": "The Thorn and the Answer Paul Receives", "subtitle": "Receiving grace in weakness", "text": "2 Corinthians 12:1–21", "chapters": [[46, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul describes a man in Christ receiving extraordinary revelations, then speaks of his own thorn in the flesh. Three times he asks for it to depart. The Lord answers that his grace is sufficient and his power is made perfect in weakness. Paul later returns to his concern for the Corinthians and his fear of finding unrepented sins when he visits."], "lesson": "The Lord answers Paul's repeated prayer with a promise about the grace available to him. Paul's concern for the church continues while he lives with the weakness.", "question": "What does Paul ask to be removed, and what assurance does he receive?"},
  {"id": 91327, "title": "Before the Third Visit", "subtitle": "Examining yourself and seeking restoration", "text": "2 Corinthians 13:1–14", "chapters": [[46, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul says he is coming again and that matters will be established by witnesses. The Corinthians seek proof that Christ speaks through him, and he asks them to examine themselves. He prays that they may do what is right, even if he appears weak. He closes with calls to restoration and peace and a blessing."], "lesson": "Paul's authority has an end: building people up. His prayer gives their good conduct priority over winning the contest about his reputation.", "question": "What does Paul ask the Corinthians to examine while they are asking him for proof?"},
  {"id": 91328, "title": "An Opening That Goes Straight to the Trouble", "subtitle": "Holding to the gospel received", "text": "Galatians 1:1–10", "chapters": [[47, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["After greeting the churches, Paul says he is astonished at their movement towards a different gospel. Some are troubling them and distorting the message of Christ. He says that even he or an angel would stand condemned for proclaiming a different message. He asks whether his aim is to please people or serve Christ."], "lesson": "Paul puts himself under the same test as any other messenger. The identity of a speaker does not settle whether a message is faithful.", "question": "Whom does Paul include in his warning about proclaiming a different gospel?"},
  {"id": 91329, "title": "The Persecutor Becomes a Preacher", "subtitle": "Grace that changes an established direction", "text": "Galatians 1:11–24", "chapters": [[47, 1]], "start": 11, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul recalls his former zeal and the harm he did to the church. God called him by grace and revealed his Son so Paul would preach among the Gentiles. He describes where he travelled and when he met Peter and James. The churches of Judea heard that the former persecutor now preached the faith he once attacked, and they glorified God."], "lesson": "Paul acknowledges the harm of his earlier life. The story he tells gives the churches reason to glorify God for the direction in which grace turned him.", "question": "What was the report about Paul that reached the churches of Judea?"},
  {"id": 91330, "title": "Titus Goes With Paul to Jerusalem", "subtitle": "Fellowship without adding a burden", "text": "Galatians 2:1–10", "chapters": [[47, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul goes to Jerusalem with Barnabas and Titus and sets the message he preaches before recognised leaders. Titus, a Greek, is not compelled to be circumcised. The leaders recognise Paul's calling among the Gentiles and give him and Barnabas the right hand of fellowship. They ask him to remember the poor, which he is eager to do."], "lesson": "The meeting recognises distinct fields of work and a shared fellowship. Remembering the poor remains a point of practical agreement.", "question": "What do the leaders recognise about Paul's work, and what request do they make?"},
  {"id": 91331, "title": "Peter Leaves the Table", "subtitle": "Living consistently with justification by faith", "text": "Galatians 2:11–21", "chapters": [[47, 2]], "start": 11, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["At Antioch Peter eats with Gentile believers. When certain people arrive, he withdraws out of fear, and others follow. Paul confronts him publicly because his conduct conflicts with the gospel. Paul explains that a person is justified through faith in Christ, and speaks of his own life as lived by faith in the Son of God who loved and gave himself for him."], "lesson": "Peter's retreat affects other people and puts a new condition on fellowship in practice. Paul connects the shared table with the ground on which believers are accepted.", "question": "What changed Peter's conduct, and why did Paul regard it as a gospel issue?"},
  {"id": 91332, "title": "The Question About Receiving the Spirit", "subtitle": "Beginning and continuing by faith", "text": "Galatians 3:1–14", "chapters": [[47, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul asks the Galatians how they received the Spirit and whether they now expect to finish by human effort. He recalls Abraham believing God and the promise of blessing to the nations. He explains the curse of failing to do all the law and speaks of Christ redeeming people so the blessing and promise of the Spirit might reach them."], "lesson": "The question turns the Galatians towards how their new life began. Paul uses Abraham to show that receiving by faith belongs inside God's long purpose.", "question": "What experience does Paul ask the Galatians to remember before speaking about Abraham?"},
  {"id": 91333, "title": "The Promise, the Law and the Heirs", "subtitle": "Belonging to Christ across familiar boundaries", "text": "Galatians 3:15–29", "chapters": [[47, 3]], "start": 15, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul argues that the law given later does not cancel God's earlier promise. He explains its temporary role, using the image of a guardian until Christ. Through faith believers are God's children and have put on Christ. Differences such as Jew and Greek, slave and free, male and female cannot take away their shared standing as heirs in him."], "lesson": "Paul's argument about the timing of the promise reaches real differences in the congregation. Their inheritance rests on belonging to Christ.", "question": "What role does Paul give the law in relation to the promise and to the coming of Christ?"},
  {"id": 91334, "title": "An Heir Learns to Call God Father", "subtitle": "Receiving sonship and caring for others", "text": "Galatians 4:1–20", "chapters": [[47, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul compares an underage heir with a servant under guardians. When the time comes, God sends his Son and gives believers adoption. The Spirit of the Son cries to the Father. Paul then asks why the Galatians are turning back to bondage. He recalls their former welcome and says he longs for Christ to be formed in them."], "lesson": "Paul's concern for the churches is personal. His image of adoption gives them a reason to face the habits that pull them back towards servitude.", "question": "How does Paul describe the difference in the heir's relationship to the household after the appointed time?"},
  {"id": 91335, "title": "Hagar and Sarah in Paul's Argument", "subtitle": "Understanding how Paul uses the promise", "text": "Galatians 4:21–31", "chapters": [[47, 4]], "start": 21, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul recalls Abraham's two sons, one born to a slave woman and the other to a free woman. He explicitly uses the account as an allegory for two covenants and develops the contrast between bondage and promise. He quotes Isaiah and identifies believers with the children of promise, like Isaac."], "lesson": "Paul tells the reader what kind of use he is making of the Genesis account. Following his argument carefully keeps the lesson tied to the freedom and promise he is discussing.", "question": "Which features of the two births does Paul use to develop his comparison?"},
  {"id": 91336, "title": "Freedom Put to Work Through Love", "subtitle": "Serving without returning to bondage", "text": "Galatians 5:1–15", "chapters": [[47, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul urges the Galatians to stand in the freedom Christ has given. He warns of relying on circumcision for justification and asks what interrupted their obedience. Then he explains what freedom is for: serving one another in love. He recalls the command to love a neighbour and warns against consuming one another in quarrels."], "lesson": "Paul treats freedom as an opportunity for service. The life of the congregation will show whether people use that freedom for their neighbour's good.", "question": "What use of freedom does Paul recommend when people might use it for themselves?"},
  {"id": 91337, "title": "What Grows When the Spirit Leads", "subtitle": "Recognising character by its fruit", "text": "Galatians 5:16–26", "chapters": [[47, 5]], "start": 16, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul describes the conflict between the flesh and the Spirit. He names actions that damage people and fellowship, including enmity, jealousy and drunkenness. Then he describes the Spirit's fruit, such as love, joy, peace, gentleness and self-control. He calls those who live by the Spirit to walk accordingly and avoid provoking or envying one another."], "lesson": "Fruit can be observed in relationships over time. Paul's closing words give people a place to examine whether their conduct is consistent with the Spirit whose life they profess.", "question": "Which examples of the flesh and the Spirit's fruit would show up first in a disagreement?"},
  {"id": 91338, "title": "Bearing Burdens and Sowing for the Future", "subtitle": "Gentleness, endurance and the cross", "text": "Galatians 6:1–18", "chapters": [[47, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul tells those who are spiritual to restore a person caught in wrongdoing with gentleness and self-watchfulness. He calls believers to bear one another's burdens, examine their own work and persist in doing good. With his closing words he warns about the desire to make a public show and names Christ's cross as his ground for boasting."], "lesson": "Gentle restoration takes another person's burden seriously while recognising our own vulnerability. Paul's picture of sowing gives patient goodness a longer horizon than immediate results.", "question": "How does Paul join the duty to help another person with responsibility for one's own conduct?"},
  {"id": 91339, "title": "Blessed in Christ", "subtitle": "Grace gives God's people a home and a future", "text": "Ephesians 1:1–14", "chapters": [[48, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul blesses God for choosing a people in Christ and adopting them as his children. He speaks of forgiveness through Christ's blood and of God's purpose to bring all things together in him. Those who hear and believe the gospel receive the promised Spirit as the pledge of their inheritance."], "lesson": "Paul starts with gifts God has already given. Remembering those gifts can give steadiness to a life otherwise governed by the need to prove its worth.", "question": "Which gifts does Paul associate with the Father, with Christ and with the promised Spirit?"},
  {"id": 91340, "title": "A Prayer for Opened Eyes", "subtitle": "Knowing the hope of God's calling", "text": "Ephesians 1:15–23", "chapters": [[48, 1]], "start": 15, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["After hearing of their faith and love, Paul gives thanks and prays that the believers will know God better. He asks that they understand the hope of their calling and the greatness of God's power towards believers. He points to that power in raising Christ and setting him above every authority as head of the church."], "lesson": "Paul prays for people who already believe. He wants them to grasp more fully what God's action means for their future and for the church.", "question": "What three things does Paul want the believers to recognise more clearly?"},
  {"id": 91341, "title": "From Death to a Life of Good Works", "subtitle": "Grace gives rise to obedience", "text": "Ephesians 2:1–10", "chapters": [[48, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul recalls a former life governed by sin and disobedience. Then he describes God, rich in mercy, making people alive with Christ. Salvation is received by grace through faith and leaves no ground for boasting. Those saved are God's workmanship, created in Christ for good works God has prepared."], "lesson": "Good works have a clear place after the gift of salvation. Paul gives a reason to pursue them without turning them into a claim on God.", "question": "Where do good works belong in the sequence Paul sets out?"},
  {"id": 91342, "title": "Those Who Were Far Off Come Near", "subtitle": "Peace that makes room for others", "text": "Ephesians 2:11–22", "chapters": [[48, 2]], "start": 11, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul asks Gentile believers to remember when they were strangers to Israel's covenants and without hope. Christ brings them near through his blood and makes peace between divided peoples. Both have access to the Father through one Spirit. Paul pictures them as members of God's household and stones in a temple where God dwells."], "lesson": "Peace with God changes the standing of people towards one another. The household image makes that change a matter of belonging, rather than mere tolerance.", "question": "What changes for those Paul first describes as strangers?"},
  {"id": 91343, "title": "Paul Names the Mystery", "subtitle": "Sharing the promise across former boundaries", "text": "Ephesians 3:1–13", "chapters": [[48, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Writing as a prisoner, Paul explains the grace given to him to proclaim Christ among the Gentiles. He names the mystery plainly: through the gospel Gentiles are fellow heirs and members of the same body. God's wisdom is made known through the church. Paul asks his readers to take courage in the face of his sufferings for them."], "lesson": "The mystery becomes visible in people who now share a body and an inheritance. Paul sees that shared life as worth the trouble his ministry has brought him.", "question": "Which three expressions does Paul use to describe Gentile believers' place in God's people?"},
  {"id": 91344, "title": "Rooted and Grounded in Love", "subtitle": "Praying for strength in the inner person", "text": "Ephesians 3:14–21", "chapters": [[48, 3]], "start": 14, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul bows before the Father and prays that believers will be strengthened through the Spirit and that Christ will dwell in their hearts through faith. He asks that they grasp, together with all the saints, the dimensions of Christ's love. He ends by giving glory to God, whose power can do more than they ask or think."], "lesson": "Paul asks for strength to receive and comprehend love. The words 'with all the saints' give the fellowship a part in learning what that love is like.", "question": "What does Paul ask God to strengthen the believers to understand?"},
  {"id": 91345, "title": "One Body Learning to Grow", "subtitle": "Unity and maturity through shared service", "text": "Ephesians 4:1–16", "chapters": [[48, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul calls believers to walk with humility, patience and care for unity. He names what they share: body, Spirit, hope, Lord, faith, baptism and Father. Christ gives different people to equip the church for service. As each part does its work, the body grows in maturity, speaking the truth in love."], "lesson": "Unity gives different gifts room to serve a common purpose. Paul measures growth by the maturity of the whole body as its members help each other.", "question": "How do truth and love work together in Paul's picture of a growing church?"},
  {"id": 91346, "title": "Putting Off the Old Way", "subtitle": "Practising truth and kindness", "text": "Ephesians 4:17–32", "chapters": [[48, 4]], "start": 17, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul tells believers to put off their former way of life, be renewed in mind and put on the new person. He then names what this looks like: truthful speech, dealing promptly with anger, honest work that enables sharing and words that build people up. He calls them to forgive as God has forgiven them in Christ."], "lesson": "Paul gives each rejection of a harmful habit a positive direction. Honest work, for instance, gives a person something to share with someone in need.", "question": "Choose one old habit and the action Paul gives in its place. What would that change look like today?"},
  {"id": 91347, "title": "Walking in Love, Light and Wisdom", "subtitle": "Making good use of the time", "text": "Ephesians 5:1–21", "chapters": [[48, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul asks God's children to imitate him by walking in love as Christ did. He then speaks of speech and conduct fitting for people who belong to the light and urges them to discern what pleases the Lord. He tells them to use the time wisely and be filled with the Spirit, giving thanks and speaking to one another in song."], "lesson": "Paul's images of walking give direction to repeated choices. Spirit-filled life is shown in what the community says and sings and in its gratitude.", "question": "What habits does Paul place alongside his instruction to be filled with the Spirit?"},
  {"id": 91348, "title": "Faithfulness Within the Household", "subtitle": "Answering to Christ in close relationships", "text": "Ephesians 5:22–6:9", "chapters": [[48, 5], [48, 6]], "start": 22, "end": 9, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul addresses wives and husbands, using Christ and the church to explain the seriousness of marital love. He tells husbands to love with the care Christ gives the church. He speaks to children and parents, then to servants and masters. He tells masters to stop threatening and remember that in heaven they have the same Master, who shows no partiality."], "lesson": "The passage places duties on people with greater authority as well as on those under it. Christ's care sets a demanding measure for how power is used.", "question": "What responsibilities does Paul place on husbands, fathers and masters?"},
  {"id": 91349, "title": "Standing Firm and Praying for Others", "subtitle": "Strength supplied by the Lord", "text": "Ephesians 6:10–24", "chapters": [[48, 6]], "start": 10, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul gives an image of armour for a struggle involving spiritual powers. He names truth, righteousness, readiness with the gospel, faith, salvation and God's word. He calls for prayer for all believers and asks for boldness to speak as a prisoner. Tychicus is sent to give news and encourage their hearts."], "lesson": "The image leads into watchful prayer for other people. Paul himself asks the church to pray for his speech, showing how believers can support one another in the struggle.", "question": "What does Paul ask the church to pray for him as he closes the letter?"},
  {"id": 91350, "title": "A Prisoner Gives Thanks for His Partners", "subtitle": "Praying for love with discernment", "text": "Philippians 1:1–11", "chapters": [[49, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul and Timothy greet the church at Philippi, and Paul gives thanks for its partnership in the gospel from the first day. He trusts that God will complete the work he began. He prays that their love will grow with knowledge and discernment, so that they can recognise what matters and bear righteous fruit through Christ."], "lesson": "Paul prays for a love that learns how to make good judgements. His gratitude for past fellowship gives substance to his prayer for their growth.", "question": "What does Paul ask to grow alongside love?"},
  {"id": 91351, "title": "Chains Open a Door for the Gospel", "subtitle": "Courage and purpose under restrictions", "text": "Philippians 1:12–30", "chapters": [[49, 1]], "start": 12, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul reports that his imprisonment has helped make Christ known and encouraged others to speak. Some preach from goodwill and some from rivalry. Paul rejoices that Christ is proclaimed. He considers whether he will live or die and sees continuing life as an opportunity for fruitful service. He asks the church to stand together in the face of opposition."], "lesson": "Paul explains what remains possible in a situation he cannot control. His concern for the church gives him a reason to welcome further work if it is given to him.", "question": "What effects of his imprisonment does Paul want the church to understand?"},
  {"id": 91352, "title": "Christ Takes the Place of a Servant", "subtitle": "Humility that gives others attention", "text": "Philippians 2:1–11", "chapters": [[49, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul asks the church to share one purpose and look to the interests of others. He points to Christ, who took a servant's form and humbled himself to death on a cross. God exalted him, and every knee will bow and every tongue confess that Jesus Christ is Lord."], "lesson": "Paul places Christ's conduct inside a request about relationships. The dignity of the one who stoops to serve makes rivalry over rank harder to defend.", "question": "What connection does Paul make between Christ's humility and how believers treat each other?"},
  {"id": 91353, "title": "Two Fellow Workers Worth Receiving", "subtitle": "Obedience and honour in ordinary service", "text": "Philippians 2:12–30", "chapters": [[49, 2]], "start": 12, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul calls the church to work out its salvation with reverence because God works in it. He asks the believers to act without grumbling and to hold to the word of life. He then explains his plans for Timothy, who genuinely cares for them, and Epaphroditus, who came close to death in service. He asks them to honour such people."], "lesson": "The two workers make the preceding appeal practical. Care for others and costly service deserve recognition even when they receive little public attention.", "question": "What qualities make Paul commend Timothy and Epaphroditus?"},
  {"id": 91354, "title": "What Paul Once Counted as Gain", "subtitle": "Knowing Christ and pressing forward", "text": "Philippians 3:1–21", "chapters": [[49, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul lists reasons he once had for confidence in his religious standing and performance. He counts those gains as loss beside knowing Christ and receiving righteousness through faith. He longs to know Christ's resurrection and share his sufferings. Describing himself as still pressing forward, he calls the church to live as people whose citizenship is in heaven."], "lesson": "Paul's past achievements could have supplied a continuing object of pride. He gives them a new value in light of the one he is seeking to know.", "question": "What does Paul now want to gain or know after setting aside his former grounds for confidence?"},
  {"id": 91355, "title": "A Plea for Two Fellow Workers", "subtitle": "Reconciliation, prayer and disciplined thought", "text": "Philippians 4:1–9", "chapters": [[49, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 9, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul urges Euodias and Syntyche to agree in the Lord and asks another person to help them. He remembers that both worked beside him. He calls the church to rejoice, make requests known to God with thanks, think about what is true and good, and practise what they have learned."], "lesson": "Paul remembers both women's service while addressing a current disagreement. Peace can involve the help of others, the discipline of prayer and attention to what is worth dwelling on.", "question": "How does Paul ask the church to help the two women while remembering their previous service?"},
  {"id": 91356, "title": "The Gift That Reached Paul Again", "subtitle": "Contentment and generosity", "text": "Philippians 4:10–23", "chapters": [[49, 4]], "start": 10, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul rejoices that the Philippians have renewed their practical care for him. He has learned to live with abundance and with need through Christ's strength. He remembers their repeated support, welcomes the gift brought by Epaphroditus and describes it as pleasing to God. He trusts God to supply what they need."], "lesson": "Paul's contentment allows him to receive the gift gratefully without making his well-being depend on it. The church's support is a practical expression of fellowship.", "question": "Which changing circumstances does Paul say he has learned to face?"},
  {"id": 91357, "title": "News Brought by Epaphras", "subtitle": "A growing life that gives thanks", "text": "Colossians 1:1–14", "chapters": [[50, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul and Timothy greet the Colossians. Paul gives thanks for their faith and love and recalls how they learned the gospel from Epaphras. He prays that they will know God's will, bear fruit, endure patiently and thank the Father who rescued them from darkness and brought them into his Son's kingdom."], "lesson": "The prayer joins knowledge to a way of walking. Paul expects understanding to show up in fruitful work, endurance and gratitude.", "question": "What does Paul pray their knowledge of God's will will produce?"},
  {"id": 91358, "title": "Christ Holds All Things Together", "subtitle": "Remaining grounded in reconciliation", "text": "Colossians 1:15–29", "chapters": [[50, 1]], "start": 15, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul describes Christ as the image of the invisible God, the one through whom all things were created and the head of the church. Through his death God reconciles those once alienated. Paul calls them to remain grounded in faith. He describes his own work of making known Christ among them and presenting people mature in him."], "lesson": "The greatness of Christ gives weight to the peace he makes. Paul's labour seeks a settled maturity in the people who have received that peace.", "question": "How does Paul's description of Christ's place in creation and in the church shape what he says about reconciliation?"},
  {"id": 91359, "title": "Rooted in the One They Received", "subtitle": "Living from Christ's sufficiency", "text": "Colossians 2:1–15", "chapters": [[50, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul wants believers to be knit together in love and attain a settled knowledge of Christ. He urges them to walk in the one they received and warns against deceptive teaching. Fullness dwells in Christ. Paul speaks of believers being buried and raised with him, forgiven and released from the record that stood against them."], "lesson": "Paul measures new teaching against what believers already have in Christ. Their roots are to grow deeper in him while their gratitude grows.", "question": "What reasons does Paul give for remaining rooted in Christ?"},
  {"id": 91360, "title": "Shadows and the Life Hidden With Christ", "subtitle": "Choosing the direction of your attention", "text": "Colossians 2:16–3:4", "chapters": [[50, 2], [50, 3]], "start": 16, "end": 4, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul tells believers not to let others judge them over food, drink or religious days. He warns against practices that look wise while drawing people away from the head. Having died and risen with Christ, they are to seek what is above. Their life is hidden with Christ and will be revealed when he appears."], "lesson": "Paul answers pressure from religious regulations with a clearer account of where believers' lives are held. That belonging gives shape to what they seek.", "question": "What does Paul say is hidden now, and what will happen when Christ appears?"},
  {"id": 91361, "title": "Getting Dressed for a Changed Life", "subtitle": "Love binds the new habits together", "text": "Colossians 3:5–17", "chapters": [[50, 3]], "start": 5, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul names destructive desires and practices to put away, including greed, anger and lying. He then describes what to put on as God's beloved people: compassion, humility, patience, forgiveness and love. He calls them to let Christ's peace rule, let his word dwell among them and do everything with thanksgiving."], "lesson": "Paul's clothing image helps people name practical habits to take up each day. Forgiveness and truthful speech give the change a form other people can recognise.", "question": "Which instruction in this passage would change the way you respond to an irritation at home or at work?"},
  {"id": 91362, "title": "The Lord of the Home and the Open Door", "subtitle": "Doing your work with accountability and grace", "text": "Colossians 3:18–4:6", "chapters": [[50, 3], [50, 4]], "start": 18, "end": 6, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul addresses relationships between spouses, parents and children, and servants and masters. Those in power have duties, and everyone ultimately answers to the Lord. He asks the church to continue in prayer and to pray for a door for the word. He urges wisdom towards outsiders and speech seasoned with grace."], "lesson": "The Lord's authority reaches the way people carry out everyday duties and the way they speak outside the fellowship. Prayer draws both concerns into their shared life.", "question": "What does Paul request for his own witness, and what way of speaking does he ask of them?"},
  {"id": 91363, "title": "Letters and Greetings Travelling Together", "subtitle": "Remembering people whose work is easily missed", "text": "Colossians 4:7–18", "chapters": [[50, 4]], "start": 7, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Tychicus and Onesimus will bring news. Paul names fellow prisoners, workers, hosts and messengers, and says Epaphras wrestles in prayer for the believers' maturity. He asks that letters be exchanged between churches and tells Archippus to attend to his ministry. Paul closes by asking them to remember his chains."], "lesson": "The list shows how letters, hospitality, prayer and personal news sustain churches at a distance. Paul's brief instruction to Archippus places a particular responsibility in his hands.", "question": "What does Epaphras pray for, and what does Paul ask Archippus to attend to?"},
  {"id": 91364, "title": "From Idols to the Living God", "subtitle": "A changed life becomes a public witness", "text": "1 Thessalonians 1:1–10", "chapters": [[51, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul, Silvanus and Timothy remember the Thessalonians' work of faith, labour of love and endurance of hope. The church received the word amid affliction with joy from the Spirit. Its faith became known in other places. People report how the believers turned from idols to serve God and wait for Jesus."], "lesson": "Paul describes conversion through a new service and a new hope. Both became visible beyond the congregation itself.", "question": "What changes did people report about the Thessalonians after they received the message?"},
  {"id": 91365, "title": "The Manner in Which They Came", "subtitle": "Gentle care and a life worth imitating", "text": "1 Thessalonians 2:1–12", "chapters": [[51, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul recalls coming after mistreatment at Philippi and speaking with boldness. He denies using flattery or seeking advantage. He compares his care to a nursing mother and to a father encouraging his children. He reminds them that he worked to avoid burdening them and shared his life as well as the gospel."], "lesson": "The images of family give Paul's bold preaching a personal setting. His conduct lets the hearers examine how he cared for them while he spoke.", "question": "What details show how Paul sought to avoid taking advantage of those he taught?"},
  {"id": 91366, "title": "Timothy Returns With Good News", "subtitle": "Strengthening faith during separation", "text": "1 Thessalonians 2:13–3:13", "chapters": [[51, 2], [51, 3]], "start": 13, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul gives thanks that the church received the message as God's word despite suffering. Unable to return, he sends Timothy to strengthen the believers and learn how they are faring. Timothy brings news of their faith and love. Paul is comforted and prays that they will increase in love and be established in holiness."], "lesson": "The separation leads to a practical act of care. Timothy's report then gives Paul fresh grounds for gratitude and prayer.", "question": "Why did Paul send Timothy, and what about his report encouraged him?"},
  {"id": 91367, "title": "Holiness and a Quiet, Responsible Life", "subtitle": "Respecting others in conduct and work", "text": "1 Thessalonians 4:1–12", "chapters": [[51, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul urges the believers to grow in the way they live before God. He speaks about sexual holiness and warns against wronging another person. He commends their love for fellow believers and calls them to grow in it. He urges them to live quietly, attend to their responsibilities and work with their hands."], "lesson": "Paul joins holiness to the treatment of other people. Daily work and respect for others help give their witness credibility outside the church.", "question": "Which ordinary habits does Paul name when he speaks of their conduct towards outsiders?"},
  {"id": 91368, "title": "Grieving With Hope", "subtitle": "Comfort grounded in resurrection", "text": "1 Thessalonians 4:13–18", "chapters": [[51, 4]], "start": 13, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul addresses concern for believers who have died. Because Jesus died and rose, those who have died in him are included in the hope of his coming. He says the dead in Christ will rise, and those alive will be gathered with them to meet the Lord. He tells the church to comfort one another with these words."], "lesson": "Paul takes grief seriously and gives it a source of hope. The teaching is given to a community that can speak comfort to people who mourn.", "question": "What assurance does Paul give about the place of believers who have already died?"},
  {"id": 91369, "title": "Children of the Day", "subtitle": "Readiness through faith, love and hope", "text": "1 Thessalonians 5:1–11", "chapters": [[51, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul says the day of the Lord comes unexpectedly, like a thief at night. He calls the believers children of light and urges alertness and sobriety. Faith and love are pictured as a breastplate and hope of salvation as a helmet. He directs them to continue encouraging and building one another up."], "lesson": "Readiness has a communal shape. Paul gives faith, love and hope work to do while people wait for a day whose timing they cannot control.", "question": "How does Paul's description of readiness affect the way believers should treat one another?"},
  {"id": 91370, "title": "Helping People According to Their Need", "subtitle": "Patient care, prayer and discernment", "text": "1 Thessalonians 5:12–28", "chapters": [[51, 5]], "start": 12, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul asks the church to respect those who work among them and be at peace. He distinguishes between warning the unruly, encouraging the fainthearted and supporting the weak. He calls for patience with everyone. He ends with instructions about prayer, thanksgiving and weighing what is heard, and prays for God to sanctify them wholly."], "lesson": "Patient care pays attention to what a person actually needs. Paul's final prayer puts the church's growth in the hands of the God who calls them.", "question": "How do the different instructions in verse 14 help you respond wisely to people in different situations?"},
  {"id": 91371, "title": "Faith Growing Under Pressure", "subtitle": "Endurance while justice remains with God", "text": "2 Thessalonians 1:1–12", "chapters": [[52, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul, Silvanus and Timothy give thanks for the growth of the church's faith and love during persecution. They speak of God's righteous judgement and of relief when Jesus is revealed. Paul prays that God will fulfil the believers' good purposes and work of faith so that Jesus is glorified in them."], "lesson": "Paul notices growth that suffering has not erased. The promise of just judgement supports his prayer that the believers' present work may continue.", "question": "What qualities does Paul see growing in the church while it faces persecution?"},
  {"id": 91372, "title": "A Report That Shook the Church", "subtitle": "Discernment when a claim sounds urgent", "text": "2 Thessalonians 2:1–12", "chapters": [[52, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul asks the believers not to be shaken by claims, even a letter presented as coming from him, that the day of the Lord has arrived. He recalls what he taught about rebellion and the disclosure of the lawless one. He describes deceptive signs and the eventual defeat of the lawless one by the Lord Jesus."], "lesson": "An urgent claim can borrow the authority of a trusted messenger. Paul asks the church to remember what it received and to love the truth while weighing the claim.", "question": "What kinds of messages did Paul say might have unsettled the believers?"},
  {"id": 91373, "title": "Stand Firm in What You Received", "subtitle": "Encouragement for settled obedience", "text": "2 Thessalonians 2:13–17", "chapters": [[52, 2]], "start": 13, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul gives thanks for the believers' calling through the gospel and speaks of sanctification by the Spirit and belief of the truth. He urges the church to hold to what it received by word or letter. He asks Jesus and the Father to comfort their hearts and establish their words and works."], "lesson": "Paul's response to confusion includes encouragement as well as correction. A settled heart is meant to show itself in dependable speech and work.", "question": "What two areas does Paul ask God to establish after encouraging them to stand firm?"},
  {"id": 91374, "title": "Praying for the Word and Working Quietly", "subtitle": "Perseverance with responsible care", "text": "2 Thessalonians 3:1–18", "chapters": [[52, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul asks for prayer that the message will spread. He reminds the church of the example he set by working and addresses those who refuse to work and interfere in others' affairs. He urges them to work quietly. When correction is necessary, Paul tells the church to admonish the person as a brother. He closes with peace and grace."], "lesson": "Paul's instruction concerns refusal to work. Correction addresses the conduct while maintaining a relationship in which a person can hear and respond.", "question": "What distinction does Paul make between warning someone and treating that person as an enemy?"},
  {"id": 91375, "title": "A Charge to Stay in Ephesus", "subtitle": "Teaching that produces love", "text": "1 Timothy 1:1–11", "chapters": [[53, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul reminds Timothy why he asked him to remain in Ephesus. Some people were teaching differently and becoming absorbed in stories and genealogies that generated disputes. Paul says the aim of the charge is love from a pure heart, a good conscience and sincere faith. He explains that the law has a proper use in addressing conduct contrary to sound teaching."], "lesson": "A teacher can ask what a discussion is producing. Paul gives Timothy a measure that reaches the heart and the conduct of the people who listen.", "question": "What three things does Paul connect with the love his charge is meant to produce?"},
  {"id": 91376, "title": "Mercy Given to a Former Persecutor", "subtitle": "Remembering grace while defending the faith", "text": "1 Timothy 1:12–20", "chapters": [[53, 1]], "start": 12, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul thanks Christ for strengthening and appointing him to serve. He recalls speaking against Christ and persecuting believers before receiving mercy. His life becomes an example of Christ's patience. After giving glory to God, Paul charges Timothy to hold faith and a good conscience and names two men whose course shows what can happen when these are put aside."], "lesson": "Paul speaks of receiving mercy before instructing Timothy about faithfulness. The truth he guards is the same truth that gave him a future after his former conduct.", "question": "What does Paul say his own experience shows to people who will later believe?"},
  {"id": 91377, "title": "Prayer for People in Authority", "subtitle": "Looking beyond familiar circles in prayer", "text": "1 Timothy 2:1–7", "chapters": [[53, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 7, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul asks that petitions, prayers and thanksgivings be made for all people, including kings and others in authority. He connects that prayer with the possibility of a quiet and godly life. He says God desires people to be saved and to know the truth, and points to Christ Jesus as the one mediator who gave himself as a ransom."], "lesson": "Paul gives the congregation a wide field for prayer. A person in authority can be included even when the congregation's influence over that person's decisions is small.", "question": "How does Paul's description of Christ's work help explain the breadth of the prayer he requests?"},
  {"id": 91378, "title": "Prayer, Modesty and Learning", "subtitle": "Conduct fitted to worship", "text": "1 Timothy 2:8–15", "chapters": [[53, 2]], "start": 8, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul directs men to pray without anger or quarrelling and urges women to give attention to modesty and good works. He calls for a woman to learn quietly and says he does not permit a woman to teach or exercise authority over a man. In making his case he refers to Adam and Eve. The passage closes with a reference to childbearing and continuance in faith, love, holiness and self-control."], "lesson": "Paul treats the manner of prayer, dress and learning as matters that shape gathered worship. The closing virtues deserve attention alongside the disputed details of the passage.", "question": "Which instructions in this passage address what worshippers bring into the gathering through their conduct?"},
  {"id": 91379, "title": "The Character of Those Who Serve", "subtitle": "Trustworthy conduct in God's household", "text": "1 Timothy 3:1–16", "chapters": [[53, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul describes the qualifications for overseers and deacons. He gives weight to reputation, self-control, hospitality, the management of one's household and faithfulness in service. He explains that he is writing so Timothy will know how people should conduct themselves in God's household. The chapter closes with a confession concerning Christ and the mystery of godliness."], "lesson": "Paul asks the church to look across a person's life when entrusting responsibility. A visible gift needs the support of habits that other people can trust.", "question": "Which qualifications would be recognisable to people who know a prospective leader outside a church meeting?"},
  {"id": 91380, "title": "Give Attention to Reading and Teaching", "subtitle": "Training for godliness in public and private", "text": "1 Timothy 4:1–16", "chapters": [[53, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul warns that some will turn towards teachings that forbid marriage and certain foods. He says God's gifts are to be received with thanksgiving. He tells Timothy to train himself for godliness, set an example in speech and life, devote himself to Scripture reading and teaching, and attend to the gift he has received. Timothy is to watch his life and his teaching together."], "lesson": "Paul gives a young leader several ways to become credible through conduct. Steady attention to Scripture and to his own life belongs to his care of others.", "question": "What two things does Paul tell Timothy to pay close attention to at the end of the chapter?"},
  {"id": 91381, "title": "Care for Widows and Respect Within the Church", "subtitle": "Making help dependable and responsible", "text": "1 Timothy 5:1–16", "chapters": [[53, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul asks Timothy to approach older and younger people as family and to honour widows in real need. He distinguishes those who have relatives able to care for them from those left alone. He gives instructions for enrolling widows and asks families to shoulder responsibilities they can carry, so the church can assist those without support."], "lesson": "Care becomes dependable when people identify the needs and duties that are actually present. Paul gives a reason for asking households to take responsibility where they can.", "question": "How does Paul's instruction to households affect what the church is able to do for widows who are alone?"},
  {"id": 91382, "title": "Honour, Evidence and Impartial Judgement", "subtitle": "Careful decisions about leaders", "text": "1 Timothy 5:17–25", "chapters": [[53, 5]], "start": 17, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul calls for honour for elders who lead well, especially those who labour in teaching. He requires witnesses before receiving a charge against an elder and asks Timothy to act without partiality. He warns against laying hands on someone hastily. After personal advice about Timothy's health, he observes that conduct sometimes becomes clear only with time."], "lesson": "Receiving an accusation carefully and treating people impartially are both necessary. The warning against haste gives Timothy time to learn what a first impression cannot show.", "question": "What safeguards does Paul place around receiving accusations and entrusting responsibility?"},
  {"id": 91383, "title": "When Godliness Is Treated as Gain", "subtitle": "Contentment that guards the heart", "text": "1 Timothy 6:1–10", "chapters": [[53, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul addresses servants in relation to their masters and then warns about teachers whose arguments produce quarrels and whose interest in godliness is financial. He speaks of the gain of godliness with contentment and reminds readers what people bring into life and take from it. The desire to be rich can lead people into many troubles."], "lesson": "The passage asks what someone hopes to gain through religion. Contentment gives a person a basis for refusing a profitable course that damages faith and relationships.", "question": "What desires and outcomes does Paul connect with the pursuit of riches?"},
  {"id": 91384, "title": "The Good Confession and the Open Hand", "subtitle": "Keeping what has been entrusted", "text": "1 Timothy 6:11–21", "chapters": [[53, 6]], "start": 11, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul tells Timothy to pursue righteousness, godliness, faith, love, endurance and gentleness. He recalls Timothy's confession and charges him before God and Christ to keep the command. The wealthy are to place hope in God, be rich in good works and share. Paul closes by telling Timothy to guard what has been entrusted to him."], "lesson": "Paul gives Timothy qualities to pursue and the wealthy a way to put their resources to work. Both forms of faithfulness are visible in choices made over time.", "question": "What does Paul tell people with wealth to do with it, and where are they to place their hope?"},
  {"id": 91385, "title": "Paul Remembers Timothy's Tears", "subtitle": "Courage drawn from grace and fellowship", "text": "2 Timothy 1:1–18", "chapters": [[54, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul remembers Timothy's tears and the sincere faith he saw in Timothy's grandmother Lois and mother Eunice. He asks Timothy to stir up his gift and to share in suffering for the gospel according to God's power. Paul tells him to guard what was entrusted through the Holy Spirit. He remembers Onesiphorus, who sought him out in Rome and refreshed him."], "lesson": "Paul encourages Timothy by naming people who passed on faith and people who assisted a prisoner. Their acts give courage a human shape alongside God's promise of power.", "question": "What did Onesiphorus do that showed he was willing to be associated with Paul?"},
  {"id": 91386, "title": "Passing the Work to Faithful People", "subtitle": "Endurance for a trust that goes beyond you", "text": "2 Timothy 2:1–13", "chapters": [[54, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul tells Timothy to be strong in grace and entrust what he has heard to faithful people able to teach others. He uses images of a soldier, an athlete and a farmer to speak of discipline and patience. He directs Timothy to remember the risen Jesus, and says that although he himself is bound, God's word is free."], "lesson": "Timothy receives something he is expected to pass on. The images remind him that work spanning generations takes concentration, honest practice and patience.", "question": "What qualities should Timothy look for in the people to whom he passes on the teaching?"},
  {"id": 91387, "title": "A Workman Who Handles the Word Carefully", "subtitle": "Gentleness when correction is needed", "text": "2 Timothy 2:14–26", "chapters": [[54, 2]], "start": 14, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul warns about quarrels over words and tells Timothy to handle the word of truth accurately. He names teaching about the resurrection that has upset some believers. Using vessels in a house as an image, he calls Timothy to a clean life. The Lord's servant must teach patiently and correct opponents gently in hope that they will repent."], "lesson": "Accuracy and gentleness belong together in Paul's instructions. The teacher's purpose is that a person may come to recognise truth and change.", "question": "What does Paul say the Lord's servant should do instead of entering a quarrel?"},
  {"id": 91388, "title": "What Timothy Has Known Since Childhood", "subtitle": "Scripture equips a faithful life", "text": "2 Timothy 3:1–17", "chapters": [[54, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul describes difficult times when people may keep an outward form of godliness while denying its power. He reminds Timothy of the conduct and suffering he has witnessed in Paul. Timothy is to continue in what he learned and remember the sacred writings he has known since childhood. Scripture teaches, reproves, corrects and trains so that God's servant is equipped for good work."], "lesson": "Paul points Timothy to trusted teaching, lived example and Scripture. He describes what Scripture does to equip a person for the conduct God requires.", "question": "Which of the four uses of Scripture named here speaks most directly to a need in your life?"},
  {"id": 91389, "title": "Preach the Word and Finish the Course", "subtitle": "Faithfulness when hearers prefer something else", "text": "2 Timothy 4:1–8", "chapters": [[54, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 8, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul solemnly charges Timothy to preach, correct and encourage with patience. People will gather teachers who say what they want to hear. Timothy is to remain watchful, endure hardship and fulfil his ministry. Paul speaks of his own departure as near and looks back on a fight fought, a course finished and faith kept."], "lesson": "Paul puts the immediate duty of teaching alongside a whole life of service. Timothy can measure success by fulfilling the work entrusted to him through changes in the audience.", "question": "What does Paul ask Timothy to keep doing when people turn towards messages they prefer?"},
  {"id": 91390, "title": "Come Before Winter", "subtitle": "Remembering the needs of a faithful worker", "text": "2 Timothy 4:9–22", "chapters": [[54, 4]], "start": 9, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul asks Timothy to come soon. He tells him who has left and who remains, asks him to bring Mark and requests a cloak, books and parchments. He recalls facing his first defence without human support and says the Lord stood with him. He sends greetings, asks Timothy to come before winter and closes with grace."], "lesson": "The letter leaves Paul with ordinary needs and difficult memories alongside confidence in the Lord. The request for Mark also makes room for a worker to be useful after earlier trouble.", "question": "What concrete acts of help does Paul ask Timothy to bring with him?"},
  {"id": 91391, "title": "The Work Left to Titus in Crete", "subtitle": "Choosing people whose lives support their teaching", "text": "Titus 1:1–9", "chapters": [[55, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 9, "kind": "story", "category": "Titus", "story": ["Paul greets Titus and reminds him why he left him in Crete: to put unfinished matters in order and appoint elders in each town. He describes their household conduct, self-control, hospitality and love of what is good. They must hold firmly to the trustworthy message so they can encourage with sound teaching and answer those who oppose it."], "lesson": "Paul links the authority to correct others with qualities visible in a person's daily life. The same person must be able to welcome, teach and exercise restraint.", "question": "Which qualities concern an elder's conduct, and which concern the handling of teaching?"},
  {"id": 91392, "title": "When Teaching Upsets Households", "subtitle": "Correction with the hope of sound faith", "text": "Titus 1:10–16", "chapters": [[55, 1]], "start": 10, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Titus", "story": ["Paul says some people in Crete are misleading households for gain. He quotes a severe local saying and calls on Titus to rebuke the people concerned so they may become sound in faith. He warns about fables and human commands and observes that people can profess to know God while denying him by their deeds."], "lesson": "The stated aim of correction is soundness in faith. Paul judges a profession by what a person actually does and notices who is affected when teaching goes wrong.", "question": "What harm was spreading through the households, and what result did Paul seek through correction?"},
  {"id": 91393, "title": "Grace Teaches People How to Live", "subtitle": "Making sound teaching visible", "text": "Titus 2:1–15", "chapters": [[55, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Titus", "story": ["Paul tells Titus to teach what fits sound doctrine and addresses older and younger people in different responsibilities. Titus must model good work and sound speech. Paul asks servants to show trustworthiness. He then explains that God's grace has appeared, training people to live with self-control and godliness as they await Christ and become eager for good works."], "lesson": "Paul describes grace as a teacher of daily conduct. The ordinary duties listed earlier are places where people can see what that teaching produces.", "question": "What does Paul say grace has appeared to teach people to leave behind and to practise?"},
  {"id": 91394, "title": "What God Did When We Were Foolish", "subtitle": "Mercy becomes a reason for gentleness", "text": "Titus 3:1–8", "chapters": [[55, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 8, "kind": "story", "category": "Titus", "story": ["Paul asks believers to be ready for good work, considerate in public life and gentle towards others. He reminds them of their own former foolishness and hostility. God saved them through his mercy, renewal by the Holy Spirit and grace through Jesus Christ. Paul asks those who believe God to be careful to devote themselves to good works."], "lesson": "Remembering what mercy found in us can change how we speak to others. Paul places gentleness beside a clear account of the grace believers received.", "question": "How does remembering what believers once were support Paul's instruction about treating others?"},
  {"id": 91395, "title": "Leaving Unfruitful Disputes and Meeting Real Needs", "subtitle": "Giving useful work your attention", "text": "Titus 3:9–15", "chapters": [[55, 3]], "start": 9, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Titus", "story": ["Paul tells Titus to avoid quarrels and arguments that accomplish little. He gives instructions for dealing with a divisive person after repeated warnings. Then he turns to travel plans and asks Titus to help Zenas and Apollos on their way. Believers should learn to maintain good works that meet pressing needs."], "lesson": "The closing turns quickly from unproductive arguments to named people with practical needs. Paul gives Titus a choice about where to spend time and effort.", "question": "What help does Paul want Titus to give Zenas and Apollos for their journey?"},
  {"id": 91396, "title": "Paul Begins With the Good He Has Heard", "subtitle": "Recognising love before making an appeal", "text": "Philemon 1:1–7", "chapters": [[56, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 7, "kind": "story", "category": "Philemon", "story": ["Paul greets Philemon, Apphia, Archippus and the church meeting in the house. He gives thanks for Philemon's love and faith and prays that their fellowship will become effective as they recognise the good they have in Christ. He says the hearts of the saints have been refreshed through Philemon."], "lesson": "Paul names evidence of grace before he raises a difficult matter. His thanksgiving is specific enough to show that his appeal takes Philemon's earlier conduct seriously.", "question": "What does Paul say Philemon has already done for fellow believers?"},
  {"id": 91397, "title": "Receive Onesimus as You Would Receive Me", "subtitle": "Love changes how a person is welcomed", "text": "Philemon 1:8–25", "chapters": [[56, 1]], "start": 8, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Philemon", "story": ["From imprisonment Paul appeals for Onesimus, who has become dear to him. He sends him back and says he wanted Philemon's good deed to be willing. He asks Philemon to receive Onesimus as more than a slave, as a beloved brother, and to welcome him as he would Paul. If there is wrong or debt, Paul offers to take it on himself. He closes with greetings and plans for a possible visit."], "lesson": "Paul puts his relationship with both men to work in the appeal. Calling Onesimus a brother gives Philemon a concrete way to reconsider how he will receive him.", "question": "What does Paul ask Philemon to do if Onesimus owes him anything, and what kind of welcome does he request?"}
];
